"hadiths[1148]{hadithnumber,text}:"
6889,"(6889) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Ṭāhir ibn Muḥammad al-Zubayrī informed us: My father informed us: Sufyān narrated to us from Sulaymān al-Taymī from Abī Mijlaz that a funeral procession passed by Ibn ʿAbbās and al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with them both). One of them stood, and the other did not stand. So one of them said: ""Did the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) not stand?"" The other said: ""Yes, but then he sat."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
6890,"(6890) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Hishām ibn Bahrām al-Madāʾinī informed us: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl informed us: Abū al-Asbāṭ al-Ḥārithī informed us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān ibn Junādah ibn Abī Umayyah from his father from his grandfather from ʿUbādah ibn al-Ṣāmit who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to stand for the funeral procession until it was placed in the grave. Then a Jewish scholar passed by and said: 'This is what we do.' So the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) sat and said: 'Sit, and oppose them.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]"
6891,"(6891) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Shahryār informed us: Yūsuf ibn Salmān informed us: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated it with its like, except that he did not mention ""in the grave.""

Al-Bukhārī said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sulaymān ibn Junādah ibn Abī Umayyah from his father is not supported in his hadith. Abū Saʿd informed us: Abū Aḥmad informed us: al-Junaydī informed us: al-Bukhārī informed us, and he mentioned it. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]"
6892,"(6892) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us: Ibn Wahb informed me: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim related that al-Qāsim used to walk in front of the funeral procession and would sit before it was placed down, and he would not stand for it. He used to narrate from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that she said: ""The people of the Jāhiliyyah used to stand for it when they saw it and would say: 'You were among your family, you were among your family.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Bukhārī]"
6893,"(6893) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Narsī informed us: Shabābah ibn Sawwār al-Fazārī informed me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ḥanẓalah ibn al-Rāhib ibn al-Ghassīl informed us: Asīd ibn ʿAlī informed us from his father ʿAlī ibn ʿUbayd from Abī Usayd al-Sāʿidī who said: ""A man from Banū Sāʿidah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, my parents have passed away. Is there any part of their righteousness that I may maintain with them after their death?' He said: 'Yes, four things: praying upon them, seeking forgiveness for them, fulfilling their pledges after their death, honoring their friends, and maintaining the ties of kinship with those with whom you have no kinship except through them.' The man said: 'How abundant and excellent this is, O Messenger of Allah!' He said: 'Then act upon it, for it will reach them.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]"
6894,"(6894) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Asīd ibn ʿĀṣim informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us from Sufyān.

(Ḥ) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us by dictation: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw informed us: Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd informed us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us: Sufyān informed us from Sālim ibn Abī Ḥafṣah who said: I heard Abū Ḥāzim say: ""I was present on the day al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) died, and I saw al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) saying to Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, poking him in the neck: 'Go forward, for if it were not a Sunnah, you would not have been given precedence.' There was some disagreement between them. So Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: 'Are you competing over the grave of your Prophet's son in which you will bury him, when I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""Whoever loves them both has loved me, and whoever hates them both has hated me""?'"" [Ḥasan for other than it. Narrated by al-Ḥākim]"
6895,"(6895) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: Qabīṣah informed us: Sufyān informed us from Abī al-Jiḥāf from Ismāʿīl ibn Rajāʾ al-Zubaydī who said: ""I was informed by one who witnessed al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī when al-Ḥasan died, and he was saying to Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ: 'Go forward, for if it were not a Sunnah, you would not have been given precedence.'"" As for the narration regarding it from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ḥasan for other than it]"
6896,"(6896) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Kāmil ibn Khalaf ibn Shajarah al-Qāḍī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: ʿAwn ibn Salām informed us: Sawwār ibn Muṣʿab narrated to us from Mujāld from al-Shaʿbī that when Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with her) died, ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) buried her at night and took Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) by his upper arms, meaning, and gave him precedence in the funeral prayer over her.

It was narrated with this chain. However, the authentic narration is from Ibn Shihāb al-Zuḥrī from ʿUrwah from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) in the story of the inheritance, that Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), lived for six months after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). When she passed away, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with them both) buried her at night and did not inform Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) led the funeral prayer over her. [Munkar]"
6897,"(6897) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq, the faqih, informed us — Ahmad ibn Ibrahim informed us — Ibn Bukayr informed us — al-Layth narrated to me from 'Uqayl from Ibn Shihab, and he mentioned it.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his *Sahih* from Ibn Bukayr. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari]"
6898,"(6898) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us — Ibn 'Uthman, meaning 'Abdan, informed us — Abu Hamzah al-Sukkari narrated from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib from Muharib ibn Dithar, who said: The Mother of the Believers passed away — I think he meant Maymunah, may Allah be pleased with her — and she bequeathed that Sa'id ibn Zayd should lead the funeral prayer over her.

And Sufyan al-Thawri narrated from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib from Muharib ibn Dithar that Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, bequeathed that someone other than the imam should lead the funeral prayer over her, and this is more correct. [Da'if]"
6899,"(6899) Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us — Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Hamid informed us — Sharik narrated from Abu Ishaq that 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud bequeathed: ""When I die, let al-Zubayr ibn al-'Awwam lead the funeral prayer over me."" [Very Da'if]"
6900,"(6900) And Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us — Sulayman ibn Harb informed us — Khalid ibn al-Harith informed us — Awf narrated from Khuza'i, a descendant of 'Abd Allah ibn Mughaffal, who said: 'Abd Allah ibn Mughaffal bequeathed, saying: ""Let my companions attend to me, and let Ibn Ziyad not lead the funeral prayer over me."" He said: So Abu Barazah and 'A'idh ibn 'Amr and some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, took charge of him."
6901,"(6901) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us — Musaddad informed us — Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated from Ibn Jurayj, who said: 'Ata' informed me from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Today a righteous slave, As-hamah, has passed away. So stand and pray over him."" He stood and led us in prayer, and we prayed over him.

Muslim narrated it in his *Sahih* from Muhammad ibn Hatim from Yahya al-Qattan, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari]"
6902,"(6902) And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us — Isma'il ibn Ishaq and Ziyad ibn al-Khalil both said — Musaddad informed us — Abu 'Awanah narrated from Qatadah from 'Ata' from Jabir that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, prayed over the Negashī, and I was in the second or third row.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his *Sahih* from Musaddad. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari]"
6903,"(6903) Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Muhammad, the faqih, informed us — Abu Muhammad Yahya ibn Mansur informed us — Abu 'Amr al-Mustamli informed us.

(And) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Abu 'Amr al-Mustamli, Muhammad ibn Shadhhan al-Bandafazki, and Ibrahim ibn Ishaq al-Anmati informed us — they said: al-Hasan ibn 'Isa informed us — Ibn al-Mubarak informed us — Salamah ibn Abi Muti' narrated from Ayyub from Abu Qilabah from 'Abd Allah ibn Yazid, the foster-child of 'A'ishah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""There is no deceased person over whom a community of Muslims numbering one hundred prays, all of them interceding for him, except that their intercession is accepted for him.""

Salamah said: I narrated it to Shu'ayb ibn al-Habbab, and he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated it to me from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him.

Muslim narrated it in his *Sahih* from al-Hasan ibn 'Isa. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
6904,"(6904) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abu Dawud informed us — al-Walid ibn Shuja' al-Sakuni informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — Abu Sakhr narrated from Sharik ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Namir from Kurayb from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: I heard the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, saying: ""There is no Muslim who dies and over whose funeral bier forty men stand, who associate nothing with Allah, except that their intercession is accepted for him.""

Muslim narrated it in his *Sahih* from Harun ibn Sa'id, al-Walid ibn Shuja', and others. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
6905,"(6905) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ahmad al-Mahbubi in Marw informed us — Sa'id ibn Mas'ud informed us — Yazid ibn Harun informed us — Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us.

(And) Abu Tahir, the faqih, informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us — Abu al-Azhar informed us — Wahb ibn Jarir informed us — my father informed us — he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Mursad ibn 'Abd Allah from Malik ibn Hubayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""There is no Muslim man over whom three rows of Muslims pray, seeking forgiveness for him, except that it becomes obligatory (i.e., Paradise becomes obligable for him)."" So when Malik would pray over a funeral — meaning when the people were few — he would say: ""Arrange them in three rows, then pray over it.""

This is the wording of the hadith of Jarir ibn Hazim. In the narration of Yazid ibn Harun: ""except that forgiveness is granted to him."" [Da'if. Narrated by Ibn Majah]"
6906,"(6906) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-'Ataridi informed us — Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated from Salamah ibn Nubayt from his father Nubayt ibn Sharit al-Ashja'i from Salim ibn 'Ubayd, who was among the People of the Bench (Ahl al-Suffah), who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, when he had passed away, then he came out and it was said to him: ""Has the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, passed away?"" He said: ""Yes."" So they knew that as he had said. It was said: ""And will he be prayed over? How will he be prayed over?"" He said: ""They will come in groups and pray."" So they knew that as he had said. They said: ""Will he be buried? And where?"" He said: ""Where Allah took his spirit, and He did not take his spirit except in a pure place."" So they knew that as he had said. [Hasan]"
6907,"(6907) Abu 'Abd Allah and Abu Sa'id both narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Yunus narrated from Ibn Ishaq: al-Husayn ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Abbas narrated to me from 'Ikrimah from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: When the funeral prayer was performed upon the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, the men were entered and they prayed over him without an imam, in scattered groups, until they had finished. Then the women were entered and they prayed over him. Then the children were entered and they prayed over him. Then the servants were entered and they prayed over him in scattered groups — no one led them in prayer over the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.

Al-Shafi'i, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: That was due to the immense station of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — may my father and mother be his ransom — and their mutual reluctance that any one of them should assume the imamate in the prayer over him, so they prayed over him time after time.

Abu Sa'id narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us: al-Rabi' narrated to us, saying: al-Shafi'i mentioned it."
6908,"(6908) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn Salih ibn Hani' narrated to us: al-Husayn ibn al-Hasan ibn Muhajir narrated to us: Abu al-Tahir and Harun ibn Sa'id both narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb narrated to me: 'Amr ibn al-Harith narrated to me from 'Umarah ibn Ghaziyyah from Ishaq ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Talhah from his father, that Abu Talhah invited the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to pray over 'Umayr ibn Abi Talhah when he had passed away. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and prayed over him in their house. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stepped forward, and Abu Talhah was behind him, and Umm Sulaym was behind Abu Talhah, and there was no one else with them."
6909,"(6909) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah — (H) — and Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir narrated to us: my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Hannad ibn al-Sarri narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani from al-Sha'bi from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A man passed away whom the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to visit [during illness], and they buried him at night. When morning came, they informed him of his death. He ﷺ said: ""What prevented you from informing me?"" They said: ""It was night, and it was dark, and we disliked to distress you."" So he ﷺ came to his grave and prayed over him. The wording of the two narrations is identical.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad from Abu Mu'awiyah, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Shaybani in an abridged form."
6910,"(6910) Abu Muhammad Janah ibn Nudhayr ibn Janah al-Muharibi in Kufah narrated to us: Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duwayhim narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hazim narrated to us: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Ta'ifi narrated to me: 'Amr ibn Dinar — he informed me, or I heard — Jabir saying: People saw a fire in the cemetery, so they went to it and found the Messenger of Allah ﷺ in a grave, and he was saying: ""Bring me your companion."" And he was the one who used to raise his voice with the remembrance of Allah.

And it has been narrated to us from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, that that was at night and that he had a lamp with him."
6911,"(6911) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi 'Isa narrated to us: Mu'alla ibn Asad narrated to us: Wahb — (H) — and Abu 'Amr al-Adib narrated to us: Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili narrated to me: Abu Ya'la narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Narsi and 'Abd al-A'la ibn Hammad both narrated to us: Wahb ibn Khalid narrated to us: Hisham from his father from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: She visited Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and mentioned a hadith. Then she said that he asked: ""On which day did the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pass away?"" I said: ""On Monday."" He said: ""I hope there is a night between me and [my death]."" She said: He did not pass away until the evening of Tuesday, and he was buried before morning came. She said: He asked: ""In how many garments was the Messenger of Allah ﷺ shrouded?"" I said: ""In three white Yemeni cloths, in which there was no shirt nor turban."" He looked at a garment in which he had been ill, which had a trace of saffron or wars dye, and said: ""Wash this garment of mine and add two more garments to it, and shroud me in them."" I said: ""This is worn out."" He said: ""The living have a greater right to the new than the deceased; it is only for the one who is washed [i.e., the deceased]."" This is the wording of the hadith of al-'Abbas. Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Mu'alla ibn Asad. And it has been narrated to us from 'A'ishah that the Prophet ﷺ was buried at night. And it has been narrated to us from Ibn 'Abbas that Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, was buried at night.

And it has been narrated to us from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, that he was buried after the late evening prayer. And it has been narrated to us in the Book of Prayer from Abu Hurayrah that he prayed over 'A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, when they had prayed the morning prayer."
6912,"(6912) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us: Abu al-'Abbas narrated to us: al-Rabi' narrated to us: al-Shafi'i narrated to us: a trustworthy man from the people of Madinah with a chain of narration that I do not recall, that the funeral prayer was performed over 'Aqil ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, when the sun had turned yellow shortly before sunset, and they did not wait for the sun to set."
6913,"(6913) Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Muzakka in Nishapur and Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Hasan ibn Ishaq al-Bazzaz in Baghdad narrated to us from the original of his hearing, saying: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Fakihi narrated to us: Abu Yahya ibn Abi Masar'ah narrated to us: Abu 'Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri' narrated to us: Musa ibn 'Ulayy said: I heard my father saying: I heard 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir — meaning al-Juhani — saying: There were three hours in which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to forbid us from praying during them or burying our dead during them: when the sun is rising until it has fully risen; when the sun is at its zenith until it begins to decline; and when the sun is inclining toward setting until it sets. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Ibn Wahb from Musa ibn 'Ulayy ibn Rabah, as has been mentioned above. And Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated from Musa ibn 'Ulayy ibn Rabah from his father from 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade [praying] at three hours — he mentioned it with its meaning and added — he said: I said to 'Uqbah: ""Is burial done at night?"" He said: ""Yes, Abu Bakr was buried at night."""
6914,(6914) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Adnan informed him: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us: Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Muhammad ibn al-Minhal narrated to us: Yazid ibn Zuray' narrated to us: Rawh ibn al-Qasim narrated — and he mentioned it.
6915,"(6915) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakka narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Malik narrated from Muhammad ibn Abi Harmalah: that Zaynab bint Abi Salamah passed away while Tariq was the governor of Madinah. Her bier was brought after the morning prayer and was placed in al-Baqi'. He said: And Tariq used to pray the morning prayer in the darkness [i.e., while it was still dark]. Ibn Abi Harmalah said: I heard 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar saying to her family: ""Either you pray over your bier now, or you leave it until the sun rises."""
6916,"(6916) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr all narrated to us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Hajjaj narrated to me: Ibn Jurayj narrated to me: Ziyad informed me that 'Ali informed him: that a bier was placed in the cemetery of the people of Basrah when the sun had turned yellow, and he did not pray over it until the sun had set. Then Abu Barzah al-Munadi called for the prayer, and he led them in the evening prayer. Among the people were Anas ibn Malik and Abu Barzah, one of the Ansar from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. Then they prayed over the bier."
6917,"(6917) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Narsī informed us, Ḥajjāj narrated, and Ibn Jurayj said: Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrating: that the Prophet (ﷺ) delivered a sermon one day and mentioned one of his Companions who had passed away. He was shrouded in a shroud that was not of good quality, and he was buried at night. The Prophet (ﷺ) rebuked against burying a person at night until the funeral prayer had been performed over him, unless they were compelled to do so. And the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""When any one of you shrouds his brother, let him make his shroud good.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
6918,"(6918) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī informed us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Khālid ibn Makhlad informed us, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr informed me, al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from his father from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) noticed the absence of a black woman who used to pick up rags and sticks from the mosque. He said: ""Where is so-and-so?"" They said: ""She has died."" He said: ""Why did you not inform me?"" They said: ""She died at night and was buried, and we disliked to wake you up."" So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) went to her grave and prayed over her, and said: ""When any one of the Muslims dies, do not refrain from informing me.""

[Ḥasan. Ibn Khuzaymah]"
6919,"(6919) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Shaybānī informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar narrated — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghazrah al-Ghifārī informed us, Jaʿfar — meaning Ibn ʿAwn — from Ibn Jurayj from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that he prayed over nine funeral biers of men and women. He placed the men closer to the imam and the women closer to the qiblah, and he arranged them in a single row. He said: And the bier of Umm Kulthūm bint ʿAlī, the wife of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with them), and her son — who was called Zayd ibn ʿUmar — were placed, and the imam that day was Saʿīd ibn al-ʿĀṣ, and among the people that day were Ibn ʿAbbās, Abū Hurayrah, Abū Saʿīd, and Abū Qatādah. He said: The boy was placed closer to the imam. A man said: I disapproved of that, so I looked at Ibn ʿAbbās, Abū Hurayrah, Abū Saʿīd, and Abū Qatādah (may Allah be pleased with them) and said: What is this? They said: It is the Sunnah.

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. And in the narration of Abū Zakariyyā: that Ibn ʿUmar prayed over nine funeral biers altogether, and he said regarding Umm Kulthūm and her son: they were placed together, and the rest is the same.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Nasāʾī, Abū Dāwūd]"
6920,"(6920) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Yazīd ibn Khālid ibn Mawhab al-Ramlī informed us, Ibn Wahb narrated from Ibn Jurayj from Yaḥyā ibn Ṣubayḥ, who said: ʿAmmār, the freedman of al-Ḥārith ibn Nawfal, informed me: that he witnessed the funeral of Umm Kulthūm and her son, and the boy was placed closer to the imam. I disapproved of that, and among the people were Ibn ʿAbbās, Abū Saʿīd, Abū Qatādah, and Abū Hurayrah. They said: This is the Sunnah.

It was also narrated by Ḥammād ibn Salamah from ʿAmmār ibn Abī ʿAmmār, without mentioning the manner of placement. He said: And among the people were al-Ḥasan, al-Ḥusayn, Abū Hurayrah, Ibn ʿUmar, and approximately eighty of the Companions of Muḥammad (ﷺ).

And al-Shaʿbī narrated it, mentioning the manner of placement similarly, and he mentioned that the imam was Ibn ʿUmar, and he did not mention the question.

He said: And behind him were Ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah, al-Ḥusayn, and Ibn ʿAbbās. And in another narration: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar.

And we have narrated regarding this from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, and Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ (may Allah be pleased with them all).

[Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Abū Dāwūd narrated it; ʿAbd al-Razzāq reported it]"
6921,"(6921) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā: that Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, during the plague that struck al-Shām, where a great many people died, used to pray over the funeral biers of men and women together — the men closer to him and the women closer to the qiblah — and he would place their heads level with the knees of the men.

[Ḍaʿīf. ʿAbd al-Razzāq reported it]"
6922,"(6922) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us, Hammām informed us, Abū Ghālib narrated: I witnessed Anas praying over a man, and he stood at the head of the bier. Then a woman from Quraysh was brought, and he prayed over her, standing near the middle of the bier. Among those who attended his funeral was al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Ziyād al-ʿAdawī. When he saw the difference in his standing, he said: ""O Abū Ḥamzah, did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stand for the woman and the man as you have stood?"" He said: ""Yes."" Then al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Ziyād turned to us and said: ""Remember this well.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭayālisī reported it]"
6923,"(6923) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd — that is, al-Sijistānī — informed us, Dāwūd ibn Muʿādh informed us, ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Nāfiʿ Abū Ghālib, who said: I was in the lane of al-Mirbad when a funeral bier passed by with many people accompanying it. They said: ""It is the funeral of ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmayr."" So I followed it. When the bier was placed down, Anas stood and prayed over it, and I was behind him with nothing between me and him. He stood at its head and pronounced four takbīrs, neither lengthening them nor rushing them. Then he went to sit, and they said: ""O Abū Ḥamzah, the woman is an Anṣārī woman."" So they brought her near, and upon her was a green bier-cover. He stood at her waist and prayed over her in a manner similar to his prayer over the man. Then he sat down, and al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Ziyād said: ""O Abū Ḥamzah, did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pray over the funeral as you have prayed — pronouncing four takbīrs over it, and standing at the head of the man and the waist of the woman?"" He said: ""Yes,"" and he mentioned the ḥadīth.

Abū Ghālib said: I asked about the practice of Anas in his standing for the woman at her waist, and they informed me that it was only because there were no bier-covers (at that time), so the imam would stand at her waist to screen her from the people.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd]"
6924,"(6924) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Kāmil al-Qāḍī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Saʿd al-ʿAwfī informed us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us, Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah, and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us, ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿid informed us from Ḥusayn ibn Dhakwān, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah narrated to me from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: I prayed behind the Prophet (ﷺ), and he prayed over Umm Kaʿb, who had died while in a state of postpartum bleeding. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood for the prayer over her at her middle.

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿImrān ibn Maysarah from ʿAbd al-Wārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
6925,"(6925) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥalīm ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Maymūn informed us, Abū al-Muwajjah informed us, ʿAbdān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh informed us, Layth ibn Saʿd informed me, Ibn Shihāb informed me from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to combine two of the martyrs of Uḥud in a single garment, then he would say: ""Which of them had memorized more of the Qurʾān?"" And when one of them was indicated, he would place him forward in the grave. And he said: ""I shall be a witness over these people on the Day of Resurrection."" And he ordered that they be buried with their blood, and he did not pray over them, nor did he wash them.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Muqātil from Ibn al-Mubārak, with some abridgment, and he narrated it in full from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf and Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
6926,"(6926) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us, Ḥabbān narrated from Ibn al-Mubārak, who said: And al-Awzāʿī informed us from al-Zuhrī from Jābir: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say regarding the martyrs of Uḥud: ""Which of these had memorized more of the Qurʾān?"" And when a man was indicated, he would place him forward in the grave before his companion. Jābir said: So my father and my uncle were shrouded in a single striped cloak.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Muqātil from Ibn al-Mubārak, incorporated within the first chain of narration. He said: And Sulaymān ibn Kathīr said: al-Zuhrī narrated to me, and he said: Someone who heard Jābir narrated to me. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
6927,"(6927) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdād; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us; al-Firyābī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl from Hishām ibn ʿĀmir, who said:

On the day of Uḥud, the people complained to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa ālihi wa sallam, about the severity of the wounds, saying, ""O Messenger of Allah, the digging of a separate grave for each person is too difficult for us."" He said, ""Dig deep, do it well, and bury two or three in a single grave."" They asked, ""O Messenger of Allah, then whom should we place first?"" He said, ""The one who has the most Qurʾān."" He (Hishām) said: ""My father was buried as the third of three in a grave.""

It has also been narrated from him from Saʿd ibn Hishām from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
6928,"(6928) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb from Ḥumayd from Saʿd ibn Hishām ibn ʿĀmir from his father, who said:

The wounds were severe on the day of Uḥud, and the people complained to the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, about their wounds. He said, ""Dig, make it spacious, do it well, and bury two or three in a single grave, and place foremost the one who has the most Qurʾān.""

It was also narrated by ʿAbd al-Wārith from Ayyūb from Ḥumayd from Abū al-Dahmaʾ from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd.]"
6929,"(6929) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed him; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Ayyūb from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl from Abū al-Dahmaʾ from Hishām ibn ʿĀmir that the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, said: ""Dig, make it spacious, do it well, and bury two or three (in a single grave), and place foremost the one who has the most Qurʾān. So my father was placed ahead of two men.""

Al-Qāḍī said: The father of Hishām ibn ʿĀmir was killed on the day of Uḥud. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd.]"
6930,"(6930) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Mūsā narrated from ʿAwn ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib from his mother, Umm Jaʿfar, daughter of Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar, and from ʿUmārah ibn Muhājir from Umm Jaʿfar, that Fāṭimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, said:

""O Asmāʾ, I find repugnant what is done to women — a cloth is thrown over the woman and it describes her shape."" Asmāʾ said, ""O daughter of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, shall I not show you something I saw in the land of Abyssinia?"" She then called for fresh palm fronds, bent them into a frame, and then placed a cloth over them. Fāṭimah, raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā, said, ""How beautiful and excellent this is! By this the man is distinguished from the woman. When I die, you and ʿAlī, raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu, wash me, and do not let anyone enter upon me."" When she passed away, raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā, ʿĀʾishah, raḍiyallāhu ʿanhā, came to enter, but Asmāʾ said, ""Do not enter."" So she complained to Abū Bakr, saying, ""This Khathʿamī woman is standing between me and the daughter of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, and she has made for her something like the bridal howdah."" Abū Bakr, raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu, came and stood at the door and said, ""O Asmāʾ, what prompted you to prevent the wives of the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, from entering upon the daughter of the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, and you have made for her something like the bridal howdah?"" She said, ""She commanded me not to let anyone enter upon her, and I showed her what I had made while she was alive, and she instructed me to do this for her."" Abū Bakr, raḍiyallāhu ʿanhu, said, ""Then do as she commanded you."" Then he departed, and ʿAlī and Asmāʾ, raḍiyallāhu ʿanhumā, washed her. [Munkar. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym in al-Ḥilyah.]"
6931,"(6931) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us among others; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī informed us; Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated; he said: I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from Saʿid ibn al-Musayyab from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, announced the death of al-Najāshī to the people on the day he died, and went out with them to the muṣallā, arranged them in rows, and pronounced four takbīrs.

The wording is from the ḥadīth of al-Shāfiʿī and ʿAbdullāh. In the narration of Yaḥyā: ""He went out to the muṣallā and pronounced four takbīrs.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī.]"
6932,"(6932) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us; Yaḥyā narrated; al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl from Ibn Shihāb from Saʿīd and from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah that he said:

The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, announced to us the death of al-Najāshī, the ruler of Abyssinia, on the day he died, and said, ""Seek forgiveness for your brother.""

Ibn Shihāb said: And Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab narrated to me that Abū Hurayrah narrated to him that the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, arranged them in rows at the muṣallā and pronounced four takbīrs.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī.]"
6933,"(6933) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīh al-Muzzakī informed us; they said: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us; Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us; Salīm ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Mīnāʾ narrated from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh that the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, prayed over Aṣḥamah al-Najāshī and pronounced four takbīrs over him.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Sinān from Salīm, and he (al-Bukhārī) also narrated it, as did Muslim, from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Yazīd ibn Hārūn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
6934,"(6934) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Shuʿbah narrated to us; Sulaymān al-Shaybānī narrated from al-Shaʿbī, who said:

The one who was present with the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, informed me that he came to a wide grave, arranged them in rows, stepped forward, prayed over it, and pronounced four takbīrs. Sulaymān said: I said, ""O Abū ʿAmr, who narrated this to you?"" He said: Ibn ʿAbbās.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Shaybānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī.]"
6935,"(6935) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Hushaym narrated from ʿUthmān ibn Ḥakīm; Khārijah ibn Zayd informed us; his paternal uncle Yazīd ibn Thābit informed him that the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, prayed over a grave and pronounced four takbīrs over it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
6936,"(6936) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us; Abū Bakr — meaning Ibn Abī Shaybah — narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn from al-Zuhrī from Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl from his father that the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, prayed over the grave of a woman and pronounced four takbīrs.

Thus narrated Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn. But the correct narration is that of Mālik and those who followed him, as a mursal narration without the mention of his father in it.

It was also narrated by al-Awzāʿī from al-Zuhrī from Abū Umāmah that one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, informed him — and that will be addressed, in shāʾ Allāh taʿālā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
6937,"(6937) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Sarī ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Qabīṣah narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us, from Abū Yaʿfūr, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Awfā, who said: ""I was present when he (ʿAlī) offered the funeral prayer and pronounced four takbīrs. Then he stood for a while, that is, he made supplication, and then he said: 'Do you think I would pronounce five takbīrs?' They said: 'No.' He said: 'Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pronounce four takbīrs.'""

It was also narrated by Ibrāhīm al-Hajarī from Ibn Abī Awfā with the same meaning, except that he said: ""They said: 'We have seen that.' He said: 'I would not do so, for indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to pronounce four takbīrs, then he would remain standing for as long as Allah willed.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
6938,"(6938) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us: Ibrāhīm al-Hajarī narrated to us, and he mentioned it in a story he related from Ibn Abī Awfā.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Mājah.]"
6939,"(6939) Abū Ḥāmid ibn Abī al-ʿAbbās al-Zawzanī narrated to us: Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us: Faḍl ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Simsār narrated to us: Abū ʿUbaydah al-Ḥaddād narrated to us, from ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿUṭayy, from Ubayy, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The angels prayed over Ādam (peace be upon him) and pronounced four takbīrs over him, and they said: 'This is your way, O children of Ādam.'""

It was also narrated from ʿUthmān ibn Saʿd with his chain of transmission as a mawqūf narration from Ubayy ibn Kaʿb.

[Munkar. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
6940,"(6940) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Isḥāq al-Saylaḥīnī narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah informed us, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Pray over your dead, by night and by day, with four takbīrs, equally.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Mājah.]"
6941,"(6941) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to me: ʿAmr ibn Murrah informed me — he heard Ibn Abī Laylā say: ""Zayd ibn Arqam (may Allah be pleased with him) used to lead us in funeral prayers and would pronounce four takbīrs. One day he pronounced five takbīrs, so he was asked about that. He said: 'Indeed, the Prophet (ﷺ) pronounced five takbīrs.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
6942,"(6942) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh — Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ṣanʿānī — narrated to us in Makkah: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us: Ibn ʿUyaynah informed us, from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maʿqil, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed over Sahl ibn Ḥunayf and pronounced six takbīrs over him. Then he turned toward us and said: 'He was among the people of Badr.'""

It was also narrated by Ibn ʿUyaynah from Ibn al-Aṣbahānī and others, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maʿqil, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him).

[Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Bukhārī.]"
6943,"(6943) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid informed us, from Mūsā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd, that ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed over Abū Qatādah and pronounced seven takbīrs over him, and he was a Badrī.""

This is how it was narrated, but it is an error, because Abū Qatādah (may Allah be pleased with him) survived ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) for a long time.

And it has been narrated to us from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he pronounced four takbīrs over Yazīd ibn al-Mukaffif.

[Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
6944,"(6944) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Abū Hishām narrated to us: Ḥafṣ narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Salʿ, from ʿAbd Khayr, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), that he used to pronounce six takbīrs over the people of Badr, five takbīrs over the Companions of Muḥammad (ﷺ), and four takbīrs over the rest of the people.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Dār Quṭnī.]"
6945,"(6945) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us: Dāwūd — meaning Ibn Abī Hind — informed us, from ʿĀmir, from ʿAlqamah, who said: ""I said to Ibn Masʿud (may Allah be pleased with him): 'The companions of Muʿādh arrived from al-Shām and pronounced five takbīrs over one of their deceased.' Ibn Masʿud said: 'There is no fixed limit to the takbīrs over the deceased. Pronounce as many takbīrs as the imām pronounces. When the imām turns away, then turn away.'""

[Ḥasan.]"
6946,"(6946) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad — Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Baghawī — narrated to us in Baghdād: ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd narrated to us: Shuʿbah informed us, from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, who said: ""I heard Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib narrate from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: 'All of that — both four and five — did occur. Then we reached a consensus upon four takbīrs for the funeral prayer.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
6947,"(6947) Abu 'Abd Allah, the hafiz, and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, Usayd ibn 'Asim narrated to us, al-Husayn ibn Hafs narrated to us, from Sufyan, he said: 'Amir ibn Shaqiyy al-Asadiyy narrated to me, from Abu Wa'il, he said: They used to pronounce the takbir during the era of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ seven times, five times, and six times — or he said: four times. So 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, gathered the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and each man reported what he had seen. Then 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, united them upon four takbirs, like the longer prayer.

Waki' also narrated it from Sufyan, and he said: ""Four"" instead of ""six.""

And in what Waki' narrated from Mis'ar, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Iyyash al-Shaybani, from Ibrahim, he said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ gathered in the house of Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, and they unanimously agreed that the takbir over the funeral is four.

[Saahiha (authentic) according to others. Reported by 'Abd al-Razzaq.]"
6948,"(6948) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us, Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adiyy al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Ya'la narrated to us, 'Uqbah ibn Mukram Abu Mukram al-Hilali narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from al-Nadr Abu 'Umar, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, he said: The last funeral over which the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed, he pronounced four takbirs over it.

Al-Nadr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman Abu 'Umar al-Khazzaz is alone in reporting this from 'Ikrimah, and he is weak (da'if). This wording has been reported from other sources, all of them weak, except that the gathering of the majority of the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, upon four takbirs is evidence for that. And Allah knows best.

[Baatil (false). Reported by al-Tabaranī.]"
6949,"(6949) And Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us, Abu Tahir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadabadi informed us, Abu Ahmad Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us, Ya'la — meaning Ibn 'Ubayd al-Tanafisi — narrated to us, Isma'il — and he is Abu Khalid — narrated to us, from 'Amir, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abza narrated to me, he said: I prayed behind 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, over Zaynab, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, and he pronounced four takbirs. Then he sent word to the wives of the Prophet ﷺ asking who would enter her into her grave. And 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was pleased that they should enter her into her grave, so they sent word to him, may Allah be pleased with them, that those who had seen her during her lifetime should enter her into her grave. He said: ""They have spoken the truth.""

[Saahiha (authentic).]"
6950,"(6950) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Mis'ar narrated to us, from 'Umayr ibn Sa'id Abu Yahya al-Nakha'i, he said: I prayed behind 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, over Ibn al-Mukaffif, and he pronounced four takbirs over him. Then he came to his grave and said: ""O Allah, Your servant and the son of Your servant has come down to You, and You are the best of those to whom one descends. O Allah, widen his entrance and forgive his sin, for we know nothing but good of him, and You know him best.""

[Saahiha (authentic). Reported by Ibn al-Ja'd.]"
6951,"(6951) And Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Adam narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mis'ar ibn Kidam, from Thabit ibn 'Ubayd, he said: I prayed with Zayd ibn Thabit over his mother, and he pronounced four takbirs over her.

And it has been narrated to us from al-Sha'bi, from Zayd ibn Thabit, that he pronounced four takbirs over his mother and did not envy her for any good.

[Saahiha (authentic). Reported by 'Abd al-Razzaq.]"
6952,"(6952) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us, 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us, Ya'qub ibn Sufyan narrated to us, Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Razin — the seller of pomegranates — narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Ibn 'Umar prayed over Zayd ibn 'Umar and his mother, Umm Kulthum bint 'Ali, placing the man on the side of the imam and the woman behind him. He prayed over both of them with four takbirs, and behind him were Ibn al-Hanafiyyah, al-Husayn ibn 'Ali, and Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both.

And among those from whom it has been narrated from the Companions that he pronounced four takbirs are: 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, al-Bara' ibn 'Azib, Abu Hurayrah, and 'Uqbah ibn 'Amir, may Allah be pleased with them.

[Saahiha (authentic).]"
6953,"(6953) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Wasiti narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Aban al-Warraq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ya'la narrated to us, from Abu Farwah Yazid ibn Sinan, from Zayd — he is ibn Abi Unaysah — from al-Zuhri, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: When the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a funeral, he would raise his hands at the first takbir, then place his right hand upon his left hand.

Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi reported it in his book from al-Qasim ibn Dinar from Isma'il ibn Aban.

And it was also narrated by al-Hasan ibn Hammad Sajadah from Yahya ibn Ya'la. If he preserved it, then it is among the unique narrations of Yazid ibn Sinan.

[Da'if (weak). Reported by al-Tirmidhi.]"
6954,"(6954) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki narrated to us, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us, al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us, al-Shafi'i informed us, Ibrahim ibn Sa'd narrated to us, from his father, from Talhah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Awf, he said: I prayed behind Ibn 'Abbas over a funeral, and he recited the Opening of the Book (al-Fatihah). When I gave the salam, I asked him about that, and he said: ""It is a sunnah and a right (haqq).""

Ibrahim ibn Hamzah narrated it from Ibrahim ibn Sa'd, and he said in the hadith: ""He recited the Opening of the Book and a surah."" But the mention of the surah in it is not preserved.

[Saahiha (authentic). Reported by al-Shafi'i.]"
6955,"(6955) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us, Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Kathir narrated to us, Sufyan ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Talhah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Awf, he said: I prayed with Ibn 'Abbas over a funeral, and he recited the Opening of the Book, and he said: ""It is from the sunnah.""

Al-Bukhari reported it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Kathir.

[Saahiha (authentic). Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
6956,"(6956) And Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Umar al-Muqri' ibn al-Hamiyyi, may Allah have mercy on him, in Baghdad informed us, Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih narrated to us, 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Bishr ibn 'Umar narrated to us, Shu'bah narrated to us, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Talhah ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Awf, he said: I prayed behind Ibn 'Abbas over a funeral, and I heard him reciting the Opening of the Book. When he turned away, I asked him, and he said: ""It is a sunnah and a right (haqq)"" — and perhaps he said: ""It is a sunnah"" and did not mention ""a right.""

Al-Bukhari extracted it from the hadith of Ghundar from Shu'bah, incorporated into the first hadith.

And it has been reported from another source from Ibn 'Abbas.

[Saahiha (authentic). Reported by al-Bukhari.]"
6957,"(6957) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, who said: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās recite Sūrat al-Fātiḥah aloud over the funeral bier and say: ""I did so only that you may know it is a Sunnah."" [Hasan. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī]"
6958,"(6958) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: The Prophet ﷺ pronounced four takbīrs over the deceased and recited Umm al-Qur'ān after the first takbīr. [Very weak. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī]"
6959,"(6959) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Muṭarrif ibn Māzin narrated from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī, who said: Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl informed me that a man from among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ informed him: ""The Sunnah in the funeral prayer is that the imām should pronounce the takbīr, then recite Sūrat al-Fātiḥah after the first takbīr silently to himself, then send ṣalāh upon the Prophet ﷺ, and devote the supplications during the takbīrs to the deceased — he should not recite anything in any of them — then he should make the salām silently to himself."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī]"
6960,"(6960) He said: Muṭarrif ibn Māzin also narrated to us from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī, who said: Muḥammad al-Fihrī narrated to me from al-Ḥaḥḥāq ibn Qays that he said the same as Abū Umāmah had said.

And al-Ḥajjāj ibn Abī Manīʿ narrated from his grandfather — who was ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Ziyād al-Ruṣāfī — from al-Zuhrī from Abū Umāmah from a man among the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ. Thus the narration of Muṭarrif was strengthened regarding the mention of al-Fātiḥah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Nasāʾī]"
6961,"(6961) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Abū al-Azhar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb — who is Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd — narrated to us: My father narrated to us from Ibn Isḥāq: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me from Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf from ʿUbayd ibn al-Sabbāq, who said: Sahl ibn Ḥunayf led us in a funeral prayer. When he pronounced the first takbīr, he recited Umm al-Qur'ān such that those behind him could hear. He then continued his takbīrs until only one takbīr remained, at which point he made the tashahhud of the prayer, then pronounced the takbīr and departed.

And it has been narrated to us from Ibn Masʿūd and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ regarding the recitation of al-Fātiḥah in the funeral prayer. [Ḥasan. al-Dāraquṭnī]"
6962,"(6962) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad al-Tājir informed me: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAsqalānī narrated to us: Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: Ibn Wahb informed me: Yūnus narrated from Ibn Shihāb, who said: Abū Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf — who was among the senior Anṣār and their scholars, and among the sons of those who witnessed Badr alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — informed me that men from among the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed him regarding the funeral prayer: that the imām should pronounce the takbīr, then send ṣalāh upon the Prophet ﷺ, and devote the supplication in the three takbīrs, then make a light salām when he departs. And the Sunnah is that those behind him do the same as their imām did.

Al-Zuhrī said: Abū Umāmah and Ibn al-Musayyab narrated this to me while he listened, and he did not deny it.

Ibn Shihāb said: I mentioned what Abū Umāmah had informed me of regarding the Sunnah in the prayer over the deceased to Muḥammad ibn Suwayd, and he said: ""I too heard al-Ḍaḥḥāq ibn Qays narrating from Ḥabīb ibn Maslamah regarding a prayer he performed over the deceased, similar to what Abū Umāmah narrated to us."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ḥākim]"
6963,"(6963) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlāʾ ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Isfarāʾinī narrated therein: Bishr ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Nājiyah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd from Saʿīd al-Maqburī from Abū Hurayrah, who said: He asked ʿUbadah ibn al-Ṣāmit about the prayer over the deceased. He said: ""By Allah, I shall inform you. When you begin, you pronounce the takbīr, then you send ṣalāh upon the Prophet ﷺ, and you say: 'O Allah, Your servant, so-and-so, used not to associate anything with You — You know best about him. If he was righteous, increase his righteousness, and if he was sinful, overlook his sins. O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward, and do not lead us astray after him.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
6964,"(6964) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Yaḥyā al-Ḥarrānī narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""When you pray over the deceased, make your supplication sincerely for him."" [Ḥasan. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
6965,"(6965) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿUmar ibn al-Ḥammāmī al-Muqriʾ, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated in Baghdad: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Sulmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Faqīh narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from Ḥabīb ibn ʿUbayd from Jubayr ibn Nufayr al-Ḥaḍramī, who said: I heard ʿAwf ibn Mālik say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over a funeral bier, and I memorized from his supplication, as he said: ""O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, grant him well-being, and pardon him. Honor his resting place, widen his entrance, wash him with water, snow, and hail, and purify him of sins as a white garment is cleansed of filth. Replace his home with a better home than his, a better family than his, and a better spouse than his. Admit him into Paradise, and protect him from the punishment of the grave and from the punishment of the Fire."" Until I wished I were that deceased. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
6966,"(6966) Abū al-Ḥasan informed us: Aḥmad ibn Sulmān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Abū Ṣāliḥ narrated to me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr from his father from ʿAwf from the Prophet ﷺ, similar to this hadith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim]"
6967,"(6967) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Aḥmad al-Jurjānī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Ḥarmalah narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to me, and he mentioned the hadith with both chains of narration in their entirety.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Saʿīd, from Ibn Wahb, and he said: ""or from the punishment of the Fire."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
6968,"(6968) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us: My grandfather, Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī, narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Abū Ḥamzah al-Ḥimṣī narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from his father, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik, who said:

I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) over a funeral, and I understood from his prayer over him:

""O Allah, forgive him, have mercy on him, pardon him, grant him well-being, honour his resting place, expand his entrance, wash him with water, snow, and hail, and purify him of sins as a white garment is purified of filth. O Allah, replace his home with a better home than his, a spouse better than his spouse, and a family better than his family, and protect him from the trial of the grave and the punishment of the Fire.""

ʿAwf said: ""I wished that I were the deceased.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and others."
6969,"(6969) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Yazīd narrated to us: My father narrated to me. He said: I heard al-Awzāʿī say: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me, saying: Abū Ibrāhīm — a man from Banī ʿAbd al-Ashhal — narrated to me, saying: My father narrated to me that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say in the prayer over the deceased:

""O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, those present and those absent, our males and our females, our young and our old.""

Al-Awzāʿī said: And Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān this hadith, and he said: ""And whomsoever You keep alive among us, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomsoever You cause to die among us, cause him to die upon Īmān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Tirmidhī]"
6970,"(6970) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Sūsī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Tanūkhī narrated to us: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us. He said: Al-Awzāʿī narrated to me, and he mentioned the hadith with both chains of narration in their entirety, similar to it, except that he said at its beginning:

""O Allah, forgive our first and our last, our living and our dead.""

This is the authentic version: the hadith of Abū Ibrāhīm al-Ashhalī is connected (mawṣūl), while the hadith of Abū Salamah is disconnected (mursal).

Hishām al-Dastawāʾī and Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from the Prophet (ﷺ) as a mursal narration.

And Ḥiqal ibn Ziyād and Shuʿayb ibn Isḥāq narrated it from al-Awzāʿī with his chain, from Abū Hurayrah, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Tirmidhī]"
6971,"(6971) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawīh narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Shabīb al-Maʿmarī narrated to us: al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā narrated to us: Ḥiqal ibn Ziyād narrated to us, from al-Awzāʿī. He said: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to me: Abū Salamah narrated to me, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), when he prayed over a funeral, would say:

""O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, those present and those absent, our young and our old, our males and our females. O Allah, whomsoever You keep alive among us, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomsoever You cause to die among us, cause him to die upon Īmān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Abū Dāwūd]"
6972,"(6972) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Marwān al-Raqqī narrated to us: Shuʿayb ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, from al-Awzāʿī, and he mentioned it in a similar manner as a connected narration, except that he said — from ʿUrwah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed over a funeral and mentioned the word ""Īmān"" at the beginning and ""Islām"" at the end, and he added:

""O Allah, do not deprive us of his reward, and do not lead us astray after him.""

ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Abū Dāwūd]"
6973,"(6973) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sinān al-Qazzāz narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Yūnus ibn al-Qāsim al-Yamāmī narrated to us: ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr. He said: Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me, saying: I asked ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her): ""How was the prayer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) over the deceased?"" She said: ""He would say:

'O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, our males and our females, those absent and those present, our young and our old. O Allah, whomsoever You keep alive among us, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomsoever You cause to die among us, cause him to die upon Īmān.'""

Hammām ibn Yaḥyā narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah, from his father, and from Yaḥyā, from Abū Salamah, with the addition, without mentioning Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by al-Nasāʾī]"
6974,"(6974) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed him: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us, from Hammām: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah, from his father, that he witnessed the Prophet (ﷺ) praying over a deceased person. He said: ""I heard him say:

'O Allah, forgive our living and our dead, those present and those absent, our young and our old, our males and our females.'""

He said: ""And Abū Salamah added to this statement: 'And whomsoever You keep alive among us, keep him alive upon Islam, and whomsoever You cause to die among us, cause him to die upon Īmān.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Aḥmad]"
6975,"(6975) It has been narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say in the prayer over the funeral — and he mentioned its meaning.

Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlūsā al-Asadābādhī informed him: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīh ibn Māsī al-Bazzāz narrated to me: Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Baṣrī informed us: Abū ʿUmar al-Ḍarīr — Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar — informed us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, and he mentioned it with its meaning.

Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī said — according to what reached me from him: ""I asked Muḥammad, meaning al-Bukhārī, about this topic and said: 'Which of the narrations from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr is most authentic regarding the prayer over the deceased?' He said: 'The most authentic thing in it is the hadith of Abū Ibrāhīm al-Ashhalī from his father, and his father was a Companion, though the name of Abū Ibrāhīm is not known.' Abū ʿĪsā said: 'I said to him: Then is he the one who is said to be ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah?' He denied that he could be ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Qatādah.

Abū Qatādah — he was a Sulamī — and this one is an Ashhalī."" Muḥammad said: ""And the hadith of Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah, ʿĀʾishah, and Abū Qatādah in this topic is not preserved. And the most authentic thing in this topic is the hadith of ʿAwf ibn Mālik."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by Aḥmad]"
6976,"(6976) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd al-Khalīl ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Bustī al-Qāḍī — who came to us in Naysābūr — narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Aḥmad ibn al-Muẓaffar al-Bakrī informed us: Ibn Abī Khaythamah informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us: ʿUqbah ibn Sayyār, Abū al-Julās, narrated to us, from ʿAlī ibn Shimākh, who said:

""I was present when Marwān asked Abū Hurayrah: 'How did you hear the Prophet (ﷺ) praying over the funeral?' He said: 'He would say:

""O Allah, You are her Lord, You created her, You guided her to Islam, and You took her soul, and You know best her secrets and what she disclosed. We have come as intercessors, so forgive her.""'""

Shuʿbah contradicted him in its chain, and the narration of ʿAbd al-Wārith is more authentic. [Ḍaʿīf — Abū Dāwūd]"
6977,"(6977) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal al-Qattan informed us — 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us — Abu al-Walid informed us — Shu'bah narrated from Julas — he said: I heard 'Uthman ibn Shimas saying: Sa'id ibn al-'As sent me to Madinah, and I was with Marwan. Abu Hurayrah passed by and said: ""Relate some of your hadiths, O Abu Hurayrah!"" Then he went on his way. Then he turned back and we said: ""Now he will ask him."" He said: ""How did you hear the Messenger of Allah ﷺ supplicating over the funeral?"" He said: ""You are the one who created it, or you created it…"" — and he mentioned something similar, except that he said: ""You know its secret and its public aspect.""

Abu Balj corroborated it: Yahya ibn Abi Sulaym. [Weak — Ahmad]"
6978,"(6978) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal informed us — 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us — Ya'qub informed us — Ibn Raja' informed us — Zā'idah informed me — Yahya ibn Abi Sulaym said: I heard Julas narrating — he said: Marwan asked Abu Hurayrah: ""How did you hear the Prophet ﷺ?"" And the correct narration is that of 'Abd al-Warith ibn Sa'id. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
6979,"(6979) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Faddal informed us — 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far informed us — Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us — Sa'id ibn Mansur informed us — Isma'il ibn Ibrahim informed us — Ziyad ibn Mikhraq narrated from 'Uqbah ibn Sayyar — from a man — who said: We were sitting with Abu Hurayrah when Marwan stood before him and said: ""O Abu Hurayrah, you continue to narrate hadiths that we do not recognize!"" Then he departed. Then he returned to him and said: ""O Abu Hurayrah, what is the prayer over the deceased?"" He said: ""Along with what you just said."" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""We used to say: Allahumma Anta Rabbuhā — 'O Allah, You are its Lord.'"" [Weak]"
6980,"(6980) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed us — 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi informed us — Sa'id ibn Abi Maryam informed us — Musa ibn Ya'qub al-Zam'i informed me — Sharhabil ibn Sa'd said: I was present when 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas led us in the funeral prayer at al-Abwa'. He pronounced the takbir, then recited Umm al-Qur'an (Surah al-Fatihah) raising his voice with it, then he sent blessings upon the Prophet ﷺ, then he said: … Then he pronounced three takbirs, then he turned away and said: ""O people, I did not recite it over it except so that you would know that it is a sunnah.""

The Sheikh said: And in the supplication of the funeral prayer, there are many hadiths from the Prophet ﷺ, then from 'Umar, 'Ali, Ibn 'Umar, Abu Hurayrah, and others — may Allah be pleased with them. And there is nothing fixed (mu'aqqat) regarding the supplication, and in what we have mentioned there is sufficiency. And by Allah is success. [Weak — al-Hakim]"
6981,"(6981) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us — they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us — Ibrahim ibn Marzuq informed us — Wahb ibn Jarir informed us — Shu'bah narrated from al-Hajjari, meaning Ibrahim — from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa, who said: His daughter passed away, so he went out in her funeral procession on a mule behind the bier, and the women began to recite elegies. 'Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa said: ""Do not recite elegies, for the Messenger of Allah ﷺ forbade elegies. Rather, let any one of you pour forth from her tears what she wishes."" Then he prayed over her and pronounced four takbirs, and after the fourth takbir he stood for a duration between two takbirs, seeking forgiveness for her and supplicating. Then he said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ used to do thus."" [Weak — Ahmad]"
6982,"(6982) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr ibn Abi Darim al-Hafiz in Kufah informed us — 'Abdullah ibn Ghanam ibn Hafs ibn Ghiyath said — my father narrated to me from his father from Abu al-'Anbas from his father from Abu Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over a funeral, pronounced four takbirs over it, and gave one salam.

And it has been narrated to us from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib in a mursal form: that the Prophet ﷺ gave one salam over the funeral. [Weak — al-Hakim]"
6983,"(6983) Abu Hazim al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz informed us — Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ishaq ibn Khuzaymah informed us — Muhammad ibn Rafi' informed us — Yazid ibn Harun informed us — al-Hajjaj ibn Arta'ah narrated from 'Umayr ibn Sa'id, who said: I prayed behind 'Ali ibn Abi Talib — may Allah be pleased with him — over the funeral of Yazid ibn Mukaffaf, and he pronounced four takbirs and gave one salam. [Weak]"
6984,"(6984) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us — Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us — 'Abbas ibn Muhammad informed us — Qabisah informed us — Sufyan narrated from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar — from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""One salam"" — meaning in the funeral prayer. [Sahih]"
6985,"(6985) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas, al-Asamm, informed us — Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us — 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us — al-'Umari narrated from Nafi' — from Ibn 'Umar: that when he prayed over a funeral, he would give one salam on his right side. [Sahih for other than it]"
6986,(6986) Abu al-Hasan 'Ala' ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sa'id al-Mihrajani informed us there — Abu Sahl Bishr ibn Ahmad informed us — Hamzah ibn Muhammad al-Katib informed us — Nu'aym ibn Hammad informed us — Ibn al-Mubarak narrated from Ziyadah ibn Qudamah — from Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir — from Mujahid — from Ibn 'Abbas: that he would give one salam over the funeral. [Weak — Ibn Abi Shaybah]
6987,"(6987) Nu'aym narrated to us — Khālid ibn Yazīd ibn Abī Mālik narrated to us, from his father, who said: I saw Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ (may Allah be pleased with him), the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), offering one salām over the funeral (janāzah).

This was also narrated from Jābir ibn 'Abdullāh, Anas ibn Mālik, Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, and others. [Ḥasan for other than its chain]"
6988,"(6988) Abū Ḥāmid ibn Abī al-'Abbās al-Zawzanī informed us — Abū Bakr al-Shāfi'ī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Maslamah narrated to us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us — Sharīk reported from Ibrāhīm al-Hajarī, who said: 'Abdullāh ibn Abī Awfā led us in the funeral prayer of his daughter. He pronounced four takbīrs, then remained silent for a while, so much so that we thought he would pronounce a fifth takbīr. Then he gave salām to his right and to his left. When we turned away, we said to him: ""What is this?"" He said: ""I will not add anything beyond what I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do"" — or he said: ""Thus did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) do."" Then he mounted his riding animal and said to the boy: ""Where am I?"" He said: ""Ahead of the funeral."" He said: ""Did I not forbid you?"" — and he had gone blind (i.e., his sight had been lost). [Ḍa'īf]"
6989,"(6989) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn 'Alī al-Rāzī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Zāhir ibn Aḥmad narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Ziyād al-Naysābūrī narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Sa'd al-Zuhrī narrated to us — Sa'īd ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us — Mūsā ibn A'yan narrated to us, from Khālid ibn Yazīd Abī 'Abd al-Raḥīm, from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah, from Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, from 'Alqamah and al-Aswad, from 'Abdullāh, who said: There are three practices that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to perform which the people have abandoned. One of them is giving salām over the funeral (janāzah) in the manner of the salām in the (regular) prayer. [Ḥasan]"
6990,"(6990) Abū 'Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr both informed us, saying: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān narrated to us — 'Amr ibn Muḥammad al-'Anqazī narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Ibrāhīm ibn Muhājir, from Mujāhid, from Ibn 'Abbās, that he used to give a light (khafīf) salām in the funeral prayer. [Ḍa'īf]"
6991,"(6991) 'Alī ibn Aḥmad ibn 'Abdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Jābir narrated to us — Abū Kāmil narrated to us — 'Abd al-Wahhāb narrated to us — Ma'mar narrated, from al-Zuhrī, who said: I heard Sahl ibn Sa'd al-Sā'idī say to Sa'īd: It is from the Sunnah of the prayer over the deceased that one should pronounce takbīr, then send ṣalawāt upon the Prophet (ﷺ), then make earnest supplication for the deceased, then give salām within himself (silently).

It was also narrated from 'Abd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād, from Ma'mar; but in my view this is an error, and the correct narration is the one narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf. [Munkar]"
6992,"(6992) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Ja'far al-Muzakkī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik reported from Nāfi', from 'Abdullāh ibn 'Umar, that when he prayed over the funeral (janā'iz), he would give salām loudly enough for those near him to hear. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik]"
6993,"(6993) 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn 'Abdullāh ibn Khamīrah al-Harawī narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Najdah al-Qurashī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn 'Abdullāh ibn Numayr narrated to us — Ibn Idrīs narrated to us, from 'Ubaydullāh (i.e., Ibn 'Umar), from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he would raise his hands at every takbīr of the funeral prayer, and also when he stood between the two rak'ahs — meaning in the obligatory (prescribed) prayer.

It is also mentioned from Anas ibn Mālik that he would raise his hands every time he pronounced takbīr over the funeral.

Al-Shāfi'ī said: And it has reached me from Sa'īd ibn al-Musayyab and 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr something similar to that.

The Shaykh said: And it has been narrated to us from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, 'Aṭā' ibn Abī Rabāḥ, 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Azīz, al-Ḥasan, and Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn. [Ṣaḥīḥ. A group of trustworthy narrators]"
6994,There is no hadith under this chapter.
6995,"(6994) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us — 'Abdullāh ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — 'Ubaydullāh ibn Mūsā informed us — al-'Alā' ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated, from al-Ḥakam, from Ḥanash, who said: Sahl ibn Ḥunayf passed away, and he was brought to al-Raḥbah, so 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed over him. When we reached the graveyard, Qarazah ibn Ka'b met us, along with a group of his people — or a group of the Anṣār. They said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, we did not witness the prayer over him."" He said: ""Pray over him."" So he prayed with them, and their imām was Qarazah ibn Ka'b. [Ḍa'īf]"
6996,"(6995) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us — 'Abdullāh ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — 'Abdullāh ibn Rajā' informed us — Zā'idah narrated, from Abī Isḥāq, from 'Alqamah ibn Murthad, who said: 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him) prayed over Yazīd ibn al-Mukaffaf al-Nakha'ī. Then Qarazah ibn Ka'b and his companions came after the burial, and he commanded them to pray over him. [Ḍa'īf]"
6997,"(6996) Abu Nasr ibn Qatadah informed us — Abu 'Amr ibn Najayd informed us — Abu Muslim informed us — Abu 'Asim narrated to us — from Sufyan — from Shabib ibn Gharqadah — from al-Mustazill: that 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed over a funeral after it had already been prayed over. [Da'if]"
6998,"(6997) Abu al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us — 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us — Ya'qub narrated to us — ibn 'Uthman informed us — 'Abd Allah, meaning ibn al-Mubarak, informed us — Sharik narrated to us — from Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah — from 'Amr ibn Murrah — from Khaythamah: that Abu Musa prayed over al-Harith ibn Qays al-Ju'fi after the funeral prayer had already been performed over him; he caught up with them at al-Jabban. [Da'if]"
6999,"(6998) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said — Abu al-'Abbas, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, narrated to us — Harun ibn Sulayman narrated to us — 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us — from Harb ibn Shaddad — from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir: that Anas ibn Sirin narrated to him that Anas ibn Malik came to a funeral over which the prayer had already been performed, and the bier was set down, so he prayed facing the bier. [Sahih]"
7000,"(6999) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said — Abu al-'Abbas, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, narrated to us — Ibrahim ibn Marzuq narrated to us — Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — from al-Shaybani. And Abu al-Hasan, 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri', informed us — al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us — Yusuf ibn Ya'qub narrated to us — Sulayman ibn Harb narrated to us — Shu'bah narrated to us — from Sulayman al-Shaybani — from al-Sha'bi, who said: ""Someone who passed by with your Prophet ﷺ over a scattered grave informed me."" He said: ""He led us in prayer and we lined up behind him."" He said: ""We said: 'O Abu 'Amr, who narrated this to you?' He said: 'Ibn 'Abbas.'"" The wording is that of the hadith of Sulayman. In the narration of Wahb, it is: ""Someone who saw the Prophet ﷺ informed me: that he came upon a scattered grave and prayed over it, leading them in prayer."" I said: ""Who narrated this to you?"" He said: ""Ibn 'Abbas."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Sulayman ibn Harb, and Muslim narrated it from two other routes from Shu'bah. [Sahih — al-Bukhari]"
7001,"(7000) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili narrated to us — 'Imran, meaning ibn Musa, narrated to us — 'Uthman, meaning ibn Abi Shaybah, narrated to us — Jarir narrated to us — from al-Shaybani — from al-Sha'bi — from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over a man after he had been buried by night. He and his companions stood, and he had been asked about him, so he said: ""Who is this?"" They said: ""He was buried only last night,"" so he prayed over him. [Sahih — al-Bukhari]"
7002,"(7001) And Abu 'Amr informed us — Abu Bakr informed me — Abu Ya'la narrated to us — Abu Khaythamah narrated to us — Jarir narrated to us with that, except that he said: ""So they prayed over him."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and said: ""So they prayed over him."" And Muslim narrated it from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim from Jarir. [Sahih — previously mentioned]"
7003,"(7002) Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Adib al-Bistami informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me — al-Hasan, meaning ibn Sufyan, narrated to us — Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr al-Hamdani narrated to us — Ibn Idris narrated to us — from al-Shaybani — from al-Sha'bi, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon a fresh grave and prayed over it, and they lined up behind him. He pronounced four takbirs. I said to 'Amir: ""Who narrated this to you?"" He said: ""A trustworthy one — one who witnessed it — 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas."" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr. This is a hadith that Sufyan al-Thawri, 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad, Zay'idah ibn Qudamah, Hushaym ibn Bashir, Abu Mu'awiyah al-Darir, and others narrated from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani in a manner similar to this narration. However, Huryam ibn Sufyan differed from them, narrating from al-Shaybani and saying in the hadith: ""after his death by three [nights]."" [Sahih — Muslim]"
7004,"(7003) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us — 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us — Ibn Sa'id and al-Qadi al-Mahamili both narrated to us — al-Hasan ibn Yunus ibn al-Zayyat narrated to us — Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated to us — Huryam ibn Sufyan narrated to us — from al-Shaybani — from al-Sha'bi — from Ibn 'Abbas: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over a deceased after his death by three [nights]. It was also narrated from Isma'il ibn Zakariyya from al-Shaybani with his chain of narration: ""He prayed over a grave after it had been buried for two nights."" We have mentioned this in al-Khilafiyyat. [Shadhdh — Dar al-Qutni]"
7005,"(7004) And Bishr ibn Adam narrated from Abu 'Asim — from Sufyan — from al-Shaybani — from al-Sha'bi — from Ibn 'Abbas: that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a grave after a month. Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Harith al-Asbahani informed us — 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us — Ibn Sa'id narrated to us — Bishr ibn Adam narrated to us — Abu 'Asim narrated to us, and he mentioned it. 'Ali said: Bishr ibn Adam is singular in this, and others disagreed with him from Abu 'Asim. [Sahih Munkar — Dar al-Qutni]"
7006,"(7005) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both said — Abu al-'Abbas, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, narrated to us — al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us — Abu 'Asim al-Nabil narrated to us — from Sufyan — from al-Shaybani — from al-Sha'bi — from Ibn 'Abbas: that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a grave after it had been buried. And thus did Waki', 'Abd al-Razzaq, al-Firyabi, and the group narrate from Sufyan. And we have previously narrated the hadith from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid and Abu Hasin from al-Sha'bi without the mention of this addition. As for the hadith of Isma'il — [Sahih — Dar al-Qutni]"
7007,"(7006) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya and Abu Ahmad — 'Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Mihrajanī and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr — narrated to us: they said, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Marzūq al-Basrī in Egypt narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Ismā'īl ibn Abī Khāld, from al-Sha'bī, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came upon a solitary grave and prayed over it, and we prayed along with him.

Muslim narrated it in *al-Sahīh* from Ishāq ibn Ibrahim and others, from Wahb.

As for the hadith of Abī Husayn [its chain is authentic; its documentation has preceded]."
7008,"(7007) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Dāwūd al-'Alawī informed us: Abu Hāmid Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Hafiz narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hafs ibn 'Abdullah narrated to me: my father narrated to me: Ibrahim ibn Tahmān narrated to us, from Abī Husayn, from al-Sha'bī, from Ibn 'Abbās: that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a grave after the deceased had been buried. [Its chain is authentic; its documentation has preceded.]"
7009,"(7008) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: 'Alī ibn 'Īsā ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Nadr al-Jārūdī narrated to us: Abu Ghassān Muhammad ibn 'Amr al-Rāzī — Zunayj — narrated to us: Yahya ibn al-Durays narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Tahmān narrated to us, mentioning it with the same meaning. Muslim narrated it in *al-Sahīh* from Zunayj, Abī Ghassān.

Likewise, Muhammad ibn Himyir and Kinānah ibn Jabalah narrated it from Ibrahim ibn Tahmān, from Abī Husayn. And it has also been narrated from Ibrahim, from Abī Ishāq al-Shaybānī. [Its chain is authentic; it has been documented previously.]"
7010,"(7009) Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-'Alawī informed us: Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Hafs ibn 'Abdullah narrated to me: my father narrated to me: Ibrahim ibn Tahmān narrated to us, from Sulaymān al-Shaybānī, from 'Āmir al-Sha'bī, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when he passed by a recently dug grave. He said: ""Whose grave is this?"" It was said: ""The grave of so-and-so."" He then dismounted, arranged his Companions behind him in rows, and prayed over it, and I was among those who prayed over it — as though he had heard the narration from both directions entirely. And concerning this, [similar narrations] have been reported from Anas ibn Mālik and Abī Hurayrah and others, from the Prophet ﷺ. [Its chain is authentic; it has been documented previously.]"
7011,"(7010) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us: Ja'far al-Tayālisī narrated to us: Yahya ibn Ma'īn and Ahmad ibn Hanbal and Khalaf ibn Sālim said: Ghundar narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Habīb ibn al-Shahīd, from Thābit al-Bunānī, from Anas ibn Mālik: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over a grave of a woman after she had been buried.

Muslim narrated it in *al-Sahīh* from Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn 'Ar'arah, from Ghundar, in an abbreviated form: that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a grave only. [Its chain is authentic; Muslim narrated it.]"
7012,"(7011) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qādī and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr informed us: they said, Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Khālid ibn Khidāsh narrated to us — (h) — and 'Alī ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdān informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffār narrated to us: Mu'ādh ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us: Khālid ibn Khidāsh narrated to us: Hammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Thābit, from Anas: that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a grave in which a burial was taking place and said: ""Whose grave is this?"" They said: ""The grave of so-and-so."" He said: ""Why did you not inform me?"" He said: they considered the matter insignificant and trivial. So he prayed over it after it had been buried and said: ""These graves are filled with darkness upon their occupants, and indeed, Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, illuminates them by means of my prayer over them.""

And Thābit also narrated it from Abī Rāfi', from Abī Hurayrah, and it is preserved from both directions. [Its chain is authentic; Muslim.]"
7013,"(7012) Abu al-Khayr Jāmi' ibn Ahmad al-Wakīl al-Muhammadābādī — from his original source of audition — narrated to us: Abu Tāhir Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Muhammadābādī narrated to us: 'Uthmān ibn Sa'īd al-Dārimī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Harb narrated to us: Hammād narrated to us, from Thābit, from Abī Rāfi', from Abī Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him: that a black woman — or a man — used to sweep the mosque. The Prophet ﷺ missed him and asked about him. They said: ""He died."" He said: ""Why did you not inform me? Show me his grave."" So they showed him [the grave], and he prayed over it.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in *al-Sahīh* from Sulaymān ibn Harb and others. [Its chain is authentic; al-Bukhārī.]"
7014,"(7013) Abu al-Khayr Jāmi' ibn Ahmad narrated to us: Abu Tāhir al-Muhammadābādī narrated to us: 'Uthmān al-Dārimī narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Hammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, mentioning it with its chain and its meaning.

He added, as though they had considered her matter — or his matter — insignificant, and he said: ""Show me her grave."" So he came to her grave and prayed over it. Then he said: ""Indeed, these graves are filled with darkness upon their occupants, and indeed, Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, illuminates them by means of my prayer over them.""

Muslim narrated it in *al-Sahīh* from Abī Kāmil, from Hammād ibn Zayd, and he mentioned this addition. [Its chain is authentic; al-Bukhārī.]"
7015,"(7014) And Abu Sālih ibn Abī Tāhir al-'Anbarī informed us: my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansūr al-Qādī narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us: Ahmad ibn 'Abdah al-Dabbī and 'Abdullah ibn Mu'āwiyah al-Jumahī both said: Hammād ibn Zayd informed us: Thābit al-Bunānī narrated to us, from Abī Rāfi', from Abī Hurayrah: that a black woman used to sweep the mosque. She died, and the Prophet ﷺ missed her. He asked about her after some days, and it was said to her: ""She has died."" He said: ""Would that you had informed me."" So he came to her grave and prayed over it.

Ibn 'Abdah added in his hadith: and Hammād informed us — Thābit said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, these graves are filled with darkness upon their occupants, and indeed, Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, illuminates them by means of my prayer over them."" [Its chain is authentic; it has been documented before.]"
7016,"(7015) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishāq narrated to us: 'Affān ibn Muslim narrated to us: Hammād ibn Zayd narrated to us: Thābit narrated to us, from Abī Rāfi', from Abī Hurayrah: that a person who used to sweep the mosque — a black person — he said — died, and the Prophet ﷺ missed her and said: ""What has become of the person who used to sweep the mosque?"" It was said: ""He died."" He said: ""Why did you not inform me about him?"" They said: ""It was night."" He said: ""Show me her grave."" He said: so he came to the grave and prayed over it. Then Thābit said at that point, or in another hadith: ""Indeed, these graves are filled with darkness upon their occupants, and indeed, Allah, the Exalted, illuminates them by means of my prayer over them.""

And what prevails upon the heart is that this addition is not in the narration of Abī Rāfi' from Abī Hurayrah; rather, it may be a mursal [disconnected] narration from Thābit, from the Prophet ﷺ — just as Ahmad ibn 'Abdah and those who followed him narrated it, or from Thābit, from Anas, from the Prophet ﷺ — just as Khālid ibn Khidāsh narrated it. And other than Hammād narrated it from Thābit, from Abī Rāfi', and did not mention it. [Its chain is authentic; it has been documented before.]"
7017,"(7016) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Allāh narrated to us; my father narrated to me; Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated from al-Ḥajjāj — meaning Ibn al-Ḥajjāj — from Yūnus, from Thābit al-Bunānī, from Abū Rāfiʿ, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: Indeed, there was a man who used to follow the impurities of the mosque and pick them up. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ missed him and said, ""What has happened to so-and-so?"" It was said, ""He has died."" So those of his Companions whom Allah willed went forth, and he commanded them to form rows. Then he stepped forward and prayed over him with them.

It is also narrated from Ḥammād ibn Wāqid, from Thābit al-Bunānī, from Abū Rāfiʿ, from Abū Hurayrah: that the Prophet ﷺ prayed over a grave after three days. [Sound. It has been mentioned before it.]"
7018,"(7017) Jāmiʿ ibn Aḥmad informed us; Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us; ʿUthmān al-Dārimī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿĀṣim al-Kūfī — from the family of Mālik ibn Mighwal — narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Wāqid al-Ṣaffār narrated it. And this Ḥammād ibn Wāqid is weak.

And this time specification is not correct at all. What is correct is only what some of the narrators mentioned from Ḥammād ibn Zayd: that he was asked about it after some days. And in some narrations it is mentioned that he mentioned it on the same day. And in this regard, something has been narrated from Yazīd ibn Thābit, the brother of Zayd ibn Thābt, may Allah be pleased with both of them, from the Prophet ﷺ. And Yazīd ibn Thābit witnessed Badr, whereas Zayd did not witness it. [Denounced (Munkar)]"
7019,"(7018) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʪsā al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us; ʿAmr — meaning Ibn ʿAwn — narrated from Hushaym, from ʿUthmān ibn Ḥakīm, from Khārijah ibn Zayd, from his uncle Yazīd ibn Thābit, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to al-Baqīʿ and he saw a new grave, so he asked about it. It was mentioned to him, and he recognized it. He said, ""Why did you not inform me?"" It was said, ""O Messenger of Allah, you were sleeping, and we disliked to disturb you."" He said, ""Do not do that. I would not know when one of you has died, so long as I am among you, unless you inform me, for my prayer over him is a mercy."" Then he went to the grave and prayed over it, and we formed rows behind him, and he pronounced four takbīrs. And in this regard, something has been narrated from ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah and Buraydah from the Prophet ﷺ. [Sound. Its meaning has been referenced.]"
7020,"(7019) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us; Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to me; al-Awzāʿī narrated to me; Ibn Shihāb informed me from Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf al-Anṣārī that one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ informed him: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, may Allah bless him and grant him peace, used to visit the sick among the poor Muslims and their weak ones, and he would follow their funerals, and no one would pray over them other than him. And that a poor woman from the people of al-ʿĀlīyah had been ill for a long time, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ would ask about her from those of her neighbors who were present, and he would instruct them not to bury her if anything happened to her, so that he might pray over her. That woman died at night, so they carried her and brought her with the funerals — or he said, to the place of the funerals — near the mosque of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ might pray over her as he had instructed them. But they found that he had slept after the ʿIshāʾ prayer, and they disliked to wake the Messenger of Allah ﷺ from his sleep, so they prayed over her and then departed with her. When morning came, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ asked about her from those of her neighbors who were present, and they informed him of the matter and that they had disliked to wake the Messenger of Allah ﷺ for her. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to them, ""Why did you do that? Go."" So they went with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until they stood at her grave, and they formed rows behind the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as they form rows for the funeral prayer. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over her and pronounced four takbīrs as one pronounces takbīrs over the dead. [Sound. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
7021,"(7020) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; the brother of Khaṭṭāb narrated to us; Ibn Ḥumayd narrated to us; Mihrān ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Abū Sinān Saʿīd ibn Sinān al-Shaybānī narrated from ʿAlqamah ibn Marthad, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father: that the Prophet ﷺ passed by a newly buried grave, and Abū Bakr was with him. He said, ""Whose grave is this?"" Abū Bakr said, ""O Messenger of Allah, this is the grave of Umm Miḥjan, who used to be devoted to picking up impurities from the mosque."" He said, ""Why did you not inform me?"" They said, ""You were sleeping, and we disliked to disturb you."" He said, ""Do not do that, for my prayer over your dead is a light for them in their graves."" He said: So he had his Companions form rows, and he prayed over her. Abū Sinān said: I presented this hadith to ʿAmr ibn Murrah, and he said: Indeed, Abū Mūsā and his companions prayed over a grave after it had been buried, and he said, ""The people have preceded us in praying over him."" [Weak. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7022,"(7021) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Naṣr ibn ʿAlī narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Hishām al-Dastawāʾī narrated from Qatādah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prayed over Umm Saʿd one month after her death.

And similarly, Ibn Abī ʿArūbah narrated it from Qatādah, and it is a sound mursal narration. [Weak. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7023,"(7022) And Suwayd ibn Saʿīd narrated it from Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ, from Shuʿbah, from Qatādah, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""This and this are equal in blood-money"" — meaning the little finger and the thumb. It was said to him, ""Why did you not pray over Umm Saʿd?"" So he prayed over her after a month had passed, for the Prophet ﷺ had been absent.

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and ʿImrān al-Sakhtiyānī both said: Suwayd ibn Saʿīd narrated it.

And this statement regarding his prayer over Umm Saʿd in this chain of narration is narrated uniquely by Suwayd ibn Saʿīd. The well-known narration from Qatādah is from Ibn al-Musayyab from the Prophet ﷺ as a mursal narration, as has preceded.

And in what Abū Dāwūd reported from Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal: it was said to Aḥmad that Suwayd narrated it from Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ. He said, ""Do not narrate such a thing."" [Sound but denounced (Munkar). Narrated by Ibn ʿAdī.]"
7024,"(7023) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated to me; Abū Muḥammad ibn Maʿbad ibn Abī Qatādah informed me: that al-Barāʾ ibn Maʿrūr was the first to face the Qiblah, and he was one of the seventy chiefs (nuqabāʾ). He arrived in Madīnah before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ emigrated, and he began praying toward the Qiblah. When death approached him, he bequeathed one-third of his wealth to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to spend wherever he willed. He said, ""Direct my face in my grave toward the Qiblah."" The Prophet ﷺ arrived after a year and prayed over him, he and his Companions, and he returned one-third of his inheritance to his children.

Thus I found it in my book, and the correct version is ""after a month."" And Allah knows best. And this is a mursal narration.

And we have narrated it in this book from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Qatādah, from his father, from his grandfather, as a connected narration, without the time specification. [Weak.]"
7025,"(7024) Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū al-Nuʿmān narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr died at al-Ṣifāḥ or near it, so we carried him on the shoulders of men until we buried him in Makkah. ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, arrived after his death and said, ""Where is the grave of my brother?"" She went to it and prayed over it. Others added: ""After a month."" [Sound. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7026,"(7025) And Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, who said: Ibn ʿUmar arrived three months after the death of ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar, so he went to his grave and prayed over it. [Sound.]"
7027,"(7026) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi — informed us, they both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri informed us: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'd informed us: from his father, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Ibn Shihab, who said: Abu Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman and Ibn al-Musayyib both informed me that Abu Hurayrah informed them that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) announced to them the death of the Negus, the ruler of Abyssinia, on the very day he died, and said: ""Seek forgiveness for your brother."" Ibn Shihab said: And Ibn al-Musayyib informed me that Abu Hurayrah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) arranged them in rows in the prayer place, then prayed over him and pronounced four takbirs. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb from Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim with both chains of narration, and Muslim narrated it from al-Hulwani and al-Naqid from Ya'qub. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
7028,"(7027) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Hamid — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya ibn Bital al-Bazzaz — informed us: Yahya ibn al-Rabi' al-Makki informed us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', from Jabir, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""A righteous man has died today, so pray over Ashamah."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Abu al-Rabi' from Sufyan, and Muslim reported it from another route from Ibn Jurayj. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari]"
7029,"(7028) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib informed us: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn Ata' informed us: Sa'id informed us, from Qatadah, from 'Ata', from Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah, that when the death of the Negus reached the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he said: ""Pray over your brother who has died in a land other than your own."" He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed over him and arranged us in rows. Jabir said: I was in the second or third row. He said: And the name of the Negus was Ashamah. Al-Bukhari reported it from another route from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Ubaydah, in abridged form. [Sahih. Muslim, Al-Bukhari]"
7030,"(7029) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir — the son of the daughter of Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi — informed us: My grandfather informed us: Ahmad ibn Salamah informed us: 'Amr ibn Zurarah informed us: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim informed us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from 'Imran ibn Husayn (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, your brother has died, so stand and pray over him"" — meaning the Negus. Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Ali ibn Hujr from Isma'il. And Yahya ibn Abi Kathir narrated it from Abu Qilabah, adding: He said: He arranged us behind him as rows are arranged for the deceased, and we prayed over him as one prays over the deceased. [Sahih. Muslim]"
7031,"(7030) Abu Bakr ibn Fwarak informed it to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us: Yunus ibn Habib informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Harb ibn Shaddad informed us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, who mentioned it with its meaning and with an addition. And the Negus was a Muslim, and in the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ), as we have narrated, there is evidence for that. [Sahih. Ahmad]"
7032,"(7031) And in the hadith of Abu Burdah ibn Abi Musa al-Ash'ari, from his father, regarding the story of the arrival of Ja'far ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) in the land of Abyssinia and his entering upon the Negus, and his informing him of the matter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and what he says about 'Isa ibn Maryam, and his amazement at that, then his saying: ""Welcome to you and to the one from whom you have come. For I bear witness that he is indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and that he is the one whom 'Isa ibn Maryam gave glad tidings of. Were it not for the kingdom that I possess, I would come to him so that I might carry his sandals."" Al-Khalq ibn 'Ali al-Mu'addhin informed it to us: Abu Bakr ibn Khanb informed us: al-Hasan ibn Salam al-Sawwaq informed us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Musa informed us: Israil informed us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Burdah, from his father, who mentioned the story, and in it was the statement of the Negus that I have related. [Da'if. Abu Dawud]"
7033,"(7032) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf informed us, from his original book: Abu Sa'id ibn al-A'rabi informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad al-Za'faran'i informed us: Yazid ibn Harun informed us: al-'Ala' — Abu Muhammad al-Thaqafi — said: I heard Anas ibn Malik say: We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at Tabuk when the sun rose with a radiance, brilliance, and light such as I had never seen it rise with before. Jibril came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""O Jibril, why do I see the sun today rising with a radiance, light, and brilliance such as I have never seen it rise with before?"" He said: ""That is because Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Laythi died in Madinah today, so Allah, the Almighty and Exalted, sent to him seventy thousand angels who pray over him."" He said: ""And for what reason?"" He said: ""He used to recite {Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad} abundantly by night and by day, in his walking, his standing, and his sitting. Shall I, O Messenger of Allah, draw the earth near for you so that you may pray over him?"" He said: ""Yes."" So he prayed over him, then returned. Al-'Ala' — this is Ibn Zayd, also said to be Ibn Zaydil — narrates from Anas ibn Malik with munkar narrations. Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us: Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us: al-Junaidi informed us: al-Bukhari said: al-'Ala' ibn Zayd, Abu Muhammad al-Thaqafi, from Anas — Yazid ibn Harun narrated from him a munkar hadith. The Sheikh said: And this hadith has been narrated from another route from Anas. [Munkar. Narrated by Abu Ya'la]"
7034,"(7033) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan informed us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi informed us: 'Uthman ibn al-Haytham informed us: Mahbub ibn Hilal informed us, from Ibn Abi Maymunah — meaning 'Ata' — from Anas ibn Malik, who said: Jibril (peace be upon him) descended and said: ""O Muhammad, Mu'awiyah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Muzani has died. Would you like to pray over him?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: Jibril (peace be upon him) struck with his wing, and no tree or mound remained but that it gave way, and his bier was raised until he could see it, and he prayed over him. Behind him were two rows of angels, each row consisting of seventy thousand angels. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to Jibril (peace be upon him): ""O Jibril, by what did he attain this station?"" He said: ""By his love of {Qul Huwa Allahu Ahad} and his recitation of it, coming and going, standing and sitting."" Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us: Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz said: Mahbub ibn Hilal al-Muzani, from 'Ata' ibn Abi Maymunah, from Anas — Jibril (peace be upon him) descended. He is not followed in this. I heard Ibn Hammad mention it from al-Bukhari. [Munkar. Narrated by Abu Ya'la]"
7035,"(7034) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh informed us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us: al-Humaydi informed us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Muhammad informed us, from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Hamzah — I think — from 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she ordered that Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) be brought through the mosque so that she could pray over him, but the people disapproved of that. So 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""How quickly the people have forgotten! The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never prayed over Suhayl ibn al-Bayda' except in the mosque."" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Ishaq ibn Ibrahim and 'Ali ibn Hujr from 'Abd al-'Aziz, without the phrase ""I think."" And he also reported it from the hadith of Wuhaib, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Hamzah. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim]"
7036,"(7035) And Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us: Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us: Ibn 'Uthman — meaning 'Abdan — informed us: 'Abd Allah — meaning Ibn al-Mubarak — informed us: Musa ibn 'Uqbah informed me: 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Hamzah informed me that 'Abbad ibn 'Abd Allah ibn al-Zubayr informed him that 'A'ishah and some of the wives of the Prophet (ﷺ) — may Allah be pleased with them — ordered that the bier of Sa'd ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) be brought to them, so it was brought through the mosque. He said: It was made to stop at the chambers, and they prayed over him. Then word reached 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) that some people had disapproved of that and said: ""This is an innovation! The bier was never brought into the mosque."" She said: ""How quickly the people hasten to condemn that of which they have no knowledge! They have reproached us for having the bier of Sa'd brought into the mosque, yet the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never prayed over Suhayl ibn al-Bayda' except inside the mosque."" And he also reported it from the hadith of Wuhaib, from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from 'Abd al-Wahid ibn Hamzah. [Sahih. Muslim]"
7037,"(7036) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfi' narrated to us; Ibn Abī Fudayk narrated to us; al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn 'Uthmān informed us, from Abī al-Naḍr, from Abī Salamah ibn 'Abd al-Raḥmān: that when Sa'd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ passed away, 'Ā'ishah said: ""Bring him into the mosque so that I may pray over him."" This was objected to, so she said: ""By Allah, indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) prayed over the two sons of Bayḍā'—Suhayl and his brother—in the mosque.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfi' and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
7038,"(7037) Abū al-Ḥusayn 'Alī ibn Muḥammad ibn 'Abd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad; Abū Ja'far al-Razzāz informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām narrated to us; Ismā'īl ibn Abān al-Ghānawī narrated to us; Hishām narrated, from 'Urwah, from 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) left neither a dīnār nor a dirham, and he was buried on the night of Tuesday, and the funeral prayer was performed over him in the mosque.""

Ismā'īl al-Ghānawī is matrūk (abandoned). [Bāṭil]"
7039,"(7038) Sufyān al-Thawrī also narrated it from Hishām ibn 'Urwah, from his father, that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was prayed over in the mosque.

Abū Bakr al-Aṣbahānī informed us of it; Abū Naṣr al-'Irāqī informed us; Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; 'Alī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; 'Abd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it. [Ḍa'īf — narrated by 'Abd al-Razzāq]"
7040,"(7039) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Ja'far narrated to us; Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Wuhayb narrated, from 'Ubayd Allāh—meaning Ibn 'Umar—from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar: that 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was prayed over in the mosque, and Ṣuhayb led the funeral prayer over him.

As for the hadith which… [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by Mālik]"
7041,"(7040) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Ja'far al-Ḥaffār informed us in Baghdad; al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn 'Ayyāsh informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mujashshir narrated to us; Wakī' narrated, from Ibn Abī Dhi'b. And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us; Ma'mar and al-Thawrī both narrated, from Ibn Abī Dhi'b, from Ṣāliḥ, the freed slave of al-Taw'amah, from Abū Hurayrah: that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever prays over a funeral in the mosque, there is nothing against him.""

Ṣāliḥ said: ""I saw a funeral bier placed in the mosque, and I saw Abū Hurayrah—when he found no place except in the mosque—turn away and not pray over it.""

The wording is from the hadith of Abū Ṭāhir, and the statement of Ṣāliḥ is not in the narration of Hilāl. This is a hadith narrated by a group from Ibn Abī Dhi'b, from Ṣāliḥ, the freed slave of al-Taw'amah, and it is counted among the unique narrations of Ṣāliḥ. The hadith of 'Ā'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) is more authentic than it.

Ṣāliḥ, the freed slave of al-Taw'amah, is differed upon regarding his probity; Mālik ibn Anas used to declare him weak. And Allah knows best. [Munkar — narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7042,"(7041) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated to us; Ismā'īl ibn Abī Khālid narrated, from 'Āmir, who said: ""'Alī, al-Faḍl, and Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them) washed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and they placed him in his grave."" He said: ""And Marḥab—or Ibn Abī Marḥab—narrated to me that they also brought 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Awf (may Allah be pleased with him) in with them. When 'Alī (may Allah be pleased with him) finished, he said: 'A man is most entitled to be close to his family.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7043,"(7042) And Abū 'Alī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ ibn Sufyān informed us; Sufyān narrated, from Khālid, from al-Sha'bī, from Abī Marḥab: that 'Abd al-Raḥmān ibn 'Awf descended into the grave of the Prophet (ﷺ). He said: ""It is as though I am looking at them—four of them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7044,"(7043) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; 'Abd Allāh ibn Ja'far informed us; Ya'qūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Wuhayb narrated, from 'Ubayd Allāh—meaning Ibn 'Umar—from Nāfi', from Ibn 'Umar: that 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was prayed over in the mosque, and Ṣuhayb led the funeral prayer over him.

Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn 'Alī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us in Kufah; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghurzah narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muslim al-Ṭā'ifī narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Dīnār narrated, from Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh, who said: ""People saw a fire in the graveyard, so they came to it, and there was the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the grave, and he was saying: 'Hand me your companion.' And he was the man who raised his voice in remembrance (dhikr)."" [Ḍa'īf — narrated by Aḥmad]"
7045,"(7044) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr both said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated, from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: Ḥusayn ibn 'Abd Allāh narrated to me, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbās, who said: ""Those who descended into the grave of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) were 'Alī ibn Abī Ṭālib, al-Faḍl ibn al-'Abbās, Qutham ibn al-'Abbās, and Shuqrān, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) (may Allah be pleased with them all). Aws ibn Khawlī had said to 'Alī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him): 'O 'Alī, I adjure you by Allah and our share of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' So he said to him: 'Descend.' So he descended with the people, and they were five."" The Sheikh said: ""And Shuqrān is Ṣāliḥ, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ); his nickname (laqab) was Shuqrān."" [Ḍa'īf — narrated by Aḥmad]"
7046,"(7045) Abū 'Abd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn 'Alī ibn Duḥaym al-Shaybānī narrated to us in Kufah; Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghurzah narrated to us; al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Muslim al-Ṭā'ifī narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Dīnār narrated, from Jābir ibn 'Abd Allāh, who said: ""People saw a fire in the graveyard, so they came to it, and there was the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the grave, and he was saying: 'Hand me your companion.' And he was the man who raised his voice in remembrance (dhikr)."" [Ḍa'īf — narrated by Aḥmad]"
7047,"(7046) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Fulayḥ narrated to us from Hilāl ibn ʿAlī ibn Usāmah.

(Ḥ) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Sinān narrated to us; Fulayḥ informed us; Hilāl narrated from Anas, who said: We were present at the funeral of a daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was sitting at the grave. I saw his eyes shedding tears. He said: ""Is there among you anyone who has not been intimate with his wife tonight?"" Abū Ṭalḥah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I [have not], O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Then go down into her grave."" So he went down into her grave. And Yūnus said: ""Is there among you any man…""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Sinān. Al-Bukhārī said: And Ibn al-Mubārak said from Fulayḥ: ""I think he meant sin."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī]"
7048,"(7047) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it; al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us; Abū al-Muwajjih informed us; ʿAbdān informed us; ʿAbdullāh informed us; Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated from Hilāl ibn ʿAlī from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: We were present at the funeral of a daughter of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was sitting at the grave. I saw his eyes shedding tears. He said: ""Is there among you any man who has not been intimate with his wife tonight?"" Abū Ṭalḥah and Abū Dharr both said: ""I [have not], O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Then go down into her grave."" Fulayḥ said: ""I understood that he meant sin."""
7049,"(7048) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khāld from al-Shaʿbī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abzā: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) performed four takbīrs over Zaynab bint Jaḥsh, then he sent a message to the wives of the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""Who will enter her into her grave?"" They said: ""The one who used to visit her during her lifetime.""

And it has been narrated to us from Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd from Ismāʿīl, who added in it: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wished to enter her into her grave, but when they said what they said, he said: ""You have spoken the truth."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — Bukhārī]"
7050,"(7049) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Baghawī narrated to us; Muḥriz ibn ʿAwn narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUqbah narrated from ʿAlī ibn Badhīmah al-Jazarī from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) covered the grave of Saʿd with his garment.

I do not preserve this except from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā ibn ʿUqbah ibn Abī al-ʿAyzār, and he is weak. [Very weak]"
7051,"(7050) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us; Zuhayr narrated from Abū Isḥāq: that he was present at the funeral of al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd refused to spread a garment over him, saying: ""He is a man."" Abū Isḥāq said: ""And ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd had seen the Prophet (peace be upon him).""

This is a sound isnād, even though it is mawqūf. A group narrated it from Abū Isḥāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Narrated by Ibn Saʿd]"
7052,"(7051) ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam narrated from a man from the people of Kūfah from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him): that he came to them while we were burying a deceased person, and a garment had been spread over his grave. He pulled the garment away from the grave and said: ""This is done only for women.""

Ibn ʿAbdān informed us of it; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Bayān al-Muqriʾ narrated to us; ʿĀrim narrated to us; al-Ṣaʿq ibn Ḥazn narrated from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥakam, and he mentioned it. It is in the sense of munqaṭiʿ due to the ignorance of the man from the people of Kūfah. [Weak — Narrated by Abū Yūsuf al-Qāḍī]"
7053,"(7052) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq, who said: Al-Ḥārith bequeathed that ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd should lead the funeral prayer over him, so he prayed over him, then he entered him into the grave from the side of the feet of the grave and said: ""This is from the Sunnah.""

This is a sound isnād. And since he said ""This is from the Sunnah,"" it became equivalent to a musnad narration.

And this statement has been narrated to us from Ibn ʿUmar and Anas ibn Mālik."
7054,(7053) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Muslim ibn Khālid and others narrated from Ibn Jurayj from ʿImrān ibn Mūsā: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was drawn out from the side of his head. [Very weak — Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]
7055,"(7054) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; al-Thiqah narrated from ʿUmar ibn ʿAṭāʾ from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was drawn out from the side of his head. [Very weak — Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
7056,"(7055) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; some of our companions narrated from Abū al-Zinād, Rabīʿah, and Abū al-Naḍr — there being no disagreement among them in this: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) was drawn out from the side of his head, as were Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both).

The Shaykh said: This is the well-known practice among the people of the Ḥijāz. [Very weak — Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
7057,"(7056) And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Baghawī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us, Abū Burdah narrated to us in his house, ʿAlqamah ibn Murthad narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) was brought in from the direction of the qiblah, a grave (laḥd) was dug for him, and bricks were set in place over it.""

And this Abū Burdah is ʿAmr ibn Yazīd al-Tamīmī al-Kūfī, and he is weak (ḍaʿīf) in ḥadīth; Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn and others declared him weak.

[Weak. Reported by Ibn ʿAdī.]"
7058,"(7057) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us, al-Haytham ibn Sahl al-Tustarī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn al-Yamān narrated to us, al-Minhāl ibn Khalīfah narrated to us, from Ḥajjāj ibn Arṭāh, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) entered a grave at night, and a lamp was lit for him. He was taken from the direction of the qiblah, and four takbīrs were said over it. Then he said: 'May Allah have mercy on you, if you were one who was tender-hearted and a reciter of the Qurʾān.'""

This chain of narration is weak (ḍaʿīf). It has also been reported through another weak chain from Ibn Masʿʿūd. As for what al-Shāfiʿī mentioned, it is more well-known in the land of the Ḥijāz, and the later generations (al-khalaf) took it from the earlier generations (al-salaf), so it is more deserving of being followed. And Allah knows best.

[Munkar. Reported by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7059,"(7058) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us, Hammām narrated to us. And Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Hammām narrated to us, from Qatādah, from Abū al-Ṣiddīq, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet (ﷺ), when he placed the deceased in the grave, would say: ""Bismillāh wa ʿalā sunnat Rasūlillāh (ﷺ)"" — ""In the name of Allah and upon the way of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""

And this is the wording of Muslim.

[Munkar. Reported by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7060,"(7059) And Wakīʿ narrated it from Hammām with his chain, and he said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'When you place your dead in their graves, say: Bismillāh wa ʿalā sunnat Rasūlillāh — In the name of Allah and upon the way of the Messenger of Allah.'""

Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of it, ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to me, Mūsā ibn Hārūn narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated to us, Hammām narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

This ḥadīth is uniquely raised (marfūʿ) by Hammām ibn Yaḥyā with this chain, and he is trustworthy (thiqah).

However, Shuʿbah and Hishām al-Dastawāʾī narrated it from Qatādah as a stopped narration (mawqūf) traced to Ibn ʿUmar.

[Munkar. Reported by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7061,"(7060) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us, Abū Muslim informed us, Muslim — meaning Ibn Ibrāhīm — narrated to us, Hishām narrated to us. And Aḥmad informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, ʿAmr informed us, Shuʿbah narrated to us — both of them from Qatādah, from Abū al-Ṣiddīq. Shuʿbah said in his ḥadīth: ""I witnessed Ibn ʿUmar when he placed a deceased person in his grave, and he said: 'Bismillāh wa ʿalā millat Rasūlillāh (ﷺ)' — 'In the name of Allah and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).'"" Hishām said in his ḥadīth: ""Ibn ʿUmar, when he placed the deceased, would say: 'Bismillāh wa ʿalā sunnat Rasūlillāh (ﷺ)' — 'In the name of Allah and upon the way of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).'""

It has also been reported through another chain from Ibn ʿUmar as a raised narration (marfūʿ) with additional wording, but it is weak (ḍaʿīf).

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Nasāʾī.]"
7062,"(7061) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿĀṣim narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Kalbī — Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — narrated to us, Idrīs ibn Ṣabīḥ al-Awdī narrated to us, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, who said: ""I was present with ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar at a funeral. When he placed the deceased in the laḥd, he said: 'Bismillāh wa fī sabīlillāh wa ʿalā millat Rasūlillāh (ﷺ)' — 'In the name of Allah, in the cause of Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' Then when he began setting the bricks over the laḥd, he said: 'O Allah, protect it from the Shayṭān, from the punishment of the grave, and from the punishment of the Fire.' Then when he leveled the earth over it, he stood at the side of the grave and said: 'O Allah, keep the earth away from its body, elevate its spirit, and grant it Your pleasure.' I said to Ibn ʿUmar: 'Is this something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), or is it something you said from your own opinion?' He said: 'Indeed, I would be capable of saying it on my own, but rather I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).'""

Abū Aḥmad said: ""Thus said Idrīs ibn Ṣabīḥ al-Awdī, when in fact he is Idrīs ibn Yazīd al-Awdī. And I do not know of anyone who narrates it other than Ḥammād ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and he is one who narrated very little.""

[Munkar. Reported by Ibn Mājah.]"
7063,"(7062) And Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Rajāʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us, and he mentioned it with its like, and he said: ""Idrīs ibn Yazīd al-Awdī."" And it has been narrated to us from al-Bayāḍī from the Prophet (ﷺ) with the meaning of the narration of Wakīʿ from Hammām, except that he said: ""Wa billāh wa ʿalā millat Rasūlillāh (ﷺ)"" — ""And by Allah, and upon the religion of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""

[Weak. Reported by Ibn Mājah.]"
7064,"(7063) ʿUmayr ibn Saʿīd al-Nakhaʿī said: ""I witnessed ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him) when he placed a deceased person in his grave, and he said: 'O Allah, Your servant, the son of Your servant, has come to You, and You are the best of those in whom one takes refuge. We know nothing but good of him, and You are the most knowing of him. He used to bear witness that there is no god but Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah. So forgive his sin and expand for him his entrance.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7065,"(7064) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Kathīr ibn Mudrik, that ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say when he leveled the earth over the deceased: ""O Allah, his family, his wealth, and his kin have surrendered him to You, and his sin is great, so forgive him.""

[Weak. Reported by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7066,"(7065) Hānīʾ, the freedman of ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, said: ""ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) would weep when he stood over a grave until his beard became wet. It was said to him: 'You are reminded of Paradise and the Fire and you do not weep, yet you weep because of this?' He said: 'Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""The grave is the first station of the Hereafter. Whoever is saved from it, what comes after it is easier than it. And whoever is not saved from it, what comes after it is more severe than it.""'"" ʿUthmān said: ""I have never seen any sight more dreadful than the grave."" ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""And when the Prophet (ﷺ) finished burying the deceased, he would say: 'Ask forgiveness for your deceased one and pray for him to be made firm, for indeed he is now being questioned.'""

Others added in it from Hishām: ""He stood over it and said: 'Ask forgiveness…'"" And he attributed his statement ""I have never seen any sight"" to the Prophet (ﷺ).

[Ḥasan. Reported by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7067,"(7066) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān informed us, ʿAbdullāh informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us — he said: I heard Ibn Abī Mulaykah saying: I saw ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās when he finished with the burial of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Sāʾib; the people stood up and departed, but Ibn ʿAbbās remained standing over him and supplicated for him.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7068,"(7067) And it has been narrated to us from ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) that he said to his son ʿAbdullāh: ""When I die, let no wailing woman accompany me, nor any fire. When you have buried me, then smooth the earth over me gently. When you have finished with my grave, remain around my grave for the duration it takes for a camel to be slaughtered and its meat to be distributed, for I shall find comfort in your presence until I know what reply I am giving to the messengers of my Lord.""
Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr informed us, Abū ʿĀṣim informed us, Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ informed us, Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb informed me from Ibn Shimāsah al-Mahriyy — he said: We were present with ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ as he was in the throes of death, and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7069,"(7068) Abū ʿAbdillāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad informed us — he said: I asked Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn about recitation at the grave, and he said: Mubashshir ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ḥalabī informed us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-ʿAlāʾ ibn al-Lajlāj, from his father, that he said to his sons: ""When you place me in my grave, set me in the niche (laḥd) and say: 'In the name of Allah and upon the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' Then smooth the earth over me gently, and recite at my head the beginning of Sūrat al-Baqarah and its end, for I saw Ibn ʿUmar considering this to be recommended (mustaḥabb).""
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn al-ʿAsākir.]"
7070,"(7069) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd ibn Aḥmad al-Zawzanī informed us, Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Maʿmar. And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā al-Balkhī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us from Thābit, from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ʿaqr in Islam.""
ʿAbd al-Razzāq said: They used to slaughter a cow or something at the grave.
Al-Zawzanī did not mention the statement of ʿAbd al-Razzāq.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7071,"(7070) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us, Ibn Abī Maryam informed us, al-Firyābī informed us, Sufyān narrated from al-Aswad ibn Qays, from Nubayḥ, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: On the day of Uḥud, the slain were carried to be buried in al-Baqīʿ, when the announcer of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called out: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commands you to bury the slain in their places (where they fell)."" This was after my mother had carried my father and my uncle to bury them in al-Baqīʿ, so they were returned.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7072,"(7071) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Ṣubḥ informed us, Abū Mushir informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah informed me, ʿUrwah ibn Ruwaym narrated that Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) was killed by a stallion and said: ""Bury me behind the river."" Then he said: ""Bury me where I have passed away.""
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn ʿAsākir.]"
7073,"(7072) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Abū Nuʿaym informed us, Sufyān narrated from Manṣūr ibn Ṣafiyyah, from his mother, who said: A brother of ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) died in the valley of Ḥabashah and was carried from his place. We came to offer condolences to her, and she said: ""I feel nothing in my heart, nor does anything grieve me within myself, except that I wish he had been buried in his place.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
7074,"(7073) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUthmān informed us, ʿAbdullāh — that is, Ibn al-Mubārak — informed us, Dāwūd ibn Qays informed me, my mother informed me and said: Saʿd ibn Abī Wāqqāṣ (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) died in al-ʿAqīq. Dāwūd said: It is approximately ten miles away. She said: I saw him carried on the necks of men until he was brought and entered through the gate of the house of Marwān into the mosque. He was placed at the apartments of the Prophet (ﷺ), in the courtyard of the chambers. The imām led the funeral prayer over him, and we prayed over him following the imām's prayer.
[Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
7075,"(7074) He said: And ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak informed us, Yūnus narrated from al-Zuhrī, who said: Saʿd ibn Abī Wāqqāṣ (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) was carried from al-ʿAqīq to al-Madīnah, and Usāmah ibn Zayd (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā) was carried from al-Jurf.
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Saʿd.]"
7076,"(7075) And Abū al-Ḥusayn informed us, ʿAbdullāh informed us, Yaʿqūb informed us, Ibn ʿUthmān informed us, ʿAbdullāh informed us, Nāfiʿ ibn ʿUmar narrated from Ibn Abī Mulaykah that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā) died in al-Ḥabashī, at a distance of some miles from Makkah, and Ibn Ṣafwān transported him to Makkah.
It has been narrated from Ayyūb, from Ibn Abī Mulaykah, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā) died in al-Ṣifāḥ or near it, and we carried him on the necks of men until we buried him in Makkah.
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn al-ʿAsākir.]"
7077,"(7076) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq al-ʿAṭṭār narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: A man was buried alongside my father on the day of Uḥud, but I did not feel at ease until I exhumed him and buried him separately.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Saʿīd ibn ʿĀmir, and he also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim from ʿAṭāʾ, from Jābir, who said: ""I exhumed him after six months, and he was as he had been placed [in the grave], except for his ear."" Some scholars narrate it as: ""except near his ear."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7078,"(7077) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Saʿīd ibn Yazīd Abū Maslamah, from Abū Naḍrah, from Jābir, who said: ""My father was buried with a man, and I felt uneasy about that, so I exhumed him after six months. I found nothing changed about him except for some hairs in his beard that were on the side facing the earth."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7079,"(7078) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, who said: ""I would not like to be buried in al-Baqīʿ, for being buried elsewhere is more beloved to me. It is only one of two men: either an oppressor, in which case I would not like to be in his vicinity, or a righteous person, in which case I would not like his bones to be disturbed on my account.""

He said: And Mālik informed us that it reached him from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: ""Breaking the bone of a deceased person is like breaking the bone of a living person.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said, meaning in terms of sin. The Shaykh said: And this ḥadīth has also been narrated in a connected, raised (mawṣūl marfūʿ) chain. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7080,"(7079) Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmash, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated to us, Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us in the year thirty-five and three hundred, and Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq ibn Hammām ibn Nāfiʿ al-Ḥimyarī narrated to us, Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated to us, Saʿd ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Breaking the bone of a deceased person is like breaking it while he is alive."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7081,"(7080) Abū Ṭāhir narrated to us, Abū Ṭāhir narrated to us, Aḥmad narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from Saʿd ibn Saʿīd, the brother of Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, from the Prophet ﷺ, a ḥadīth similar to that of Dāwūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd.]"
7082,"(7081) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī narrated to us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us on more than one occasion, Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Breaking the bone of a deceased person is like breaking it while he is alive."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7083,"(7082) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Ḥuṣayn, from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn, who said: I saw ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, before he was struck [with his fatal wound] in Madīnah, and he mentioned the ḥadīth about his assassination, in which he said to ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar: ""Go to ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers, and say: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb sends you greetings of peace — and do not say 'Commander of the Faithful,' for today I am not the Commander of the Faithful — and say: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb seeks permission to be buried alongside his two companions."" He said: So he greeted her and sought permission, then entered upon her and found her sitting and weeping. He said: ""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb sends you greetings of peace and seeks permission to be buried alongside his two companions."" She said: ""I had intended it for myself, but today I shall give preference to him over myself."" He said: Then he came, and when he arrived, it was said: ""This is ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, he has come."" He said: ""Lift me up."" So a man supported him against himself, and he said: ""What news do you bring?"" He said: ""That which you love, O Commander of the Faithful — she has given permission."" He said: ""Praise be to Allah! Nothing was more important to me than that resting place. When I am taken [in death], carry me, then greet her and say: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb seeks permission. If she grants you permission, then take me in, and if she turns me away, then take me back to the Muslim cemetery."" He mentioned the ḥadīth. He said: When he passed away, we went out with him and set out walking. ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar greeted her and said: ""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb seeks permission."" She said: ""Bring him in."" So he was brought in and placed there alongside his two companions.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7084,"(7083) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, that a shaykh from the people of Shām informed him that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, buried a woman from the People of the Book who was carrying a Muslim child in her womb, in the Muslim cemetery. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7085,"(7084) Abū Zakariyyā narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, Ibn Jurayj narrated from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā, from Wāthilah ibn al-Asqaʿ, that he buried a Christian woman who was carrying a Muslim child in her womb, in a cemetery that was neither the cemetery of the Christians nor that of the Muslims. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7086,"(7085) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Muthannā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, saying: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd say: ʿAmrah informed me, saying: I heard ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: When the news of the killing of Ibn Ḥārithah, Jaʿfar, and Ibn Rawāḥah, may Allah be pleased with them, reached the Prophet ﷺ, he sat down, and grief was visible in him, while I was looking through the crack of the door — that is, the opening of the door. A man came to him and said: ""The women of Jaʿfar are weeping excessively."" He told him to forbid them from doing so. The man went away, then came a second time and said: ""They have not obeyed him."" He said: ""Forbid them."" Then he came a third time and said: ""By Allah, they have overcome us, O Messenger of Allah!"" She said: He said — as she claimed — ""Then throw earth into their mouths."" I said: ""May Allah humiliate your nose! You did not do what the Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded you, and you did not spare the Messenger of Allah ﷺ the trouble.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7087,"(7086) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Muḥammad ibn Kathīr informed us, Sulaymān ibn Kathīr narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""When Zayd ibn Ḥārishah, Jaʿfar, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah (may Allah be pleased with them) were killed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) sat in the mosque, and grief was visible on his face."" She said: ""And he mentioned a lengthy account."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī]"
7088,"(7087) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways informed me, Qays, Abū ʿUmārah, the mawlā of Sawdah bint Saʿd, the mawlā of Banū Sāʿidah from among the Anṣār, narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm al-Anṣārī, from his father, from his grandfather, that he heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""Whoever visits a sick person remains in the mercy of Allah; when he sits beside him, he becomes immersed therein. And when he rises from his side, he continues to wade through it until he returns to the place from whence he departed. And whoever consoles his believing brother regarding a calamity, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, shall clothe him with garments of honour on the Day of Resurrection."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī]"
7089,"(7088) Abū Manṣūr al-Ẓufar ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAlawī informed us by dictation, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ādamī informed us in Baghdad, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd ibn Nāṣiḥ al-Naḥwī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim informed us, Muḥammad ibn Sūqah narrated from Ibrāhīm, from al-Aswad, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever consoles an afflicted person shall have a reward equal to his."" ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim is the sole narrator of this, and it is one of the matters for which he has been criticized. It has also been narrated through other chains, and Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī]"
7090,"(7089) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdad, Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbaydullāh al-Munādī informed us, Yūnus ibn Muḥammad informed us, Khālid ibn Mayṣarah, Abū Ḥātim, who used to reside in Makkah, narrated from Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrāh, from his father, who narrated the hadith regarding the account of a man who had two young sons who used to come to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), and his son had passed away. Grief over him prevented him from attending the gathering. The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) met him and asked about his son. He informed him that he had passed away. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) then consoled him and said: ""O so-and-so, which is more beloved to you: that you enjoy his company for the duration of your life, or that whenever you come tomorrow to any gate of Paradise, you find him there having preceded you to it and opened it for you?"" He said: ""O Prophet of Allah, rather, that he precede me to the gates of Paradise is more beloved to me."" He said: ""Then that shall be for you."" A man from among the Anṣār then stood and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, may Allah make me your ransom — is this specifically for this person, or for any Muslim whose child passes away, this shall be for him?"" He said: ""Rather, for any Muslim whose child passes away, this shall be for him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī]"
7091,"(7090) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ṣaffār informed us, Abū Ismāʿīl Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl informed us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us, Nāfiʿ ibn Yazīd informed us, Rabiʿah ibn Sayf informed me, Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥublī narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, who said: ""We buried a man with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him). When we returned and reached his gate, there was a woman approaching — we did not think he recognized her. He said: 'O Fāṭimah, from where have you come?' She said: 'I have come from the people of this house. I showed mercy to them regarding their deceased and consoled them.' He said: 'Perhaps you reached with them the al-Kudā.' She said: 'I seek refuge in Allah that I should reach with them the al-Kudā, when I have heard you mention about it what you mention.' He said: 'Had you reached with them the al-Kudā, you would not have seen Paradise until the grandfather of your father sees it.'"" And al-Kudā refers to the graveyards. [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]"
7092,"(7091) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, among others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) passed away and the consolers came, they heard a speaker saying: 'Indeed, in Allah there is consolation from every calamity, a replacement for every loss, and attainment for everything that has passed. So place your trust in Allah and hope in Him, for truly the one afflicted is the one deprived of the reward.'"" (T) Its meaning has also been narrated through another chain from Jaʿfar, from his father, from Jābir, and through another chain from Anas ibn Mālik, though its chains contain weakness, and Allah knows best. [Very weak. Narrated by al-Ḥākim]"
7093,"(7092) Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed us in Baghdad, al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Mujashshir informed us, Wakīʿ informed us, ʿImrān ibn Zāʾidah narrated from Ḥusayn ibn Abī ʿĀʾishah, from Abī Khālid, meaning al-Wālibī, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) consoled a man and said: ""May Allah have mercy on you and reward you."" And this is a mursal narration. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7094,"(7093) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Tamīm al-Ḥanẓalī informed us in Baghdad, ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqāshī informed us, Abū ʿĀṣim informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Khālid ibn Sārah informed me, and Ibn Jurayj also narrated from him, that my father narrated to me that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar said: ""If only you could have seen me, Quthm, and ʿUbaydullāh, the two sons of al-ʿAbbās, playing when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) passed by on his mount and said: 'Lift this one to me.' So he placed me in front of him. Then he said to Quthm: 'Lift this one to me.' So he placed him behind him. He would not have felt shy before his uncle al-ʿAbbās to carry Quthm and leave ʿUbaydullāh. Then he stroked my head three times, and each time he stroked it he said: 'O Allah, replace Jaʿfar among his children.'"" I said to ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar: ""What did Quthm do?"" He said: ""He was martyred."" I said to ʿAbdullāh: ""Allah and His Messenger knew best what was good."" He said: ""Yes, indeed."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
7095,"(7094) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Abī Saʿīd ibn Sakhtawayh al-Isfarāʾīnī informed us in Makkah, and he wrote it down for me in his own handwriting. Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Kayyisān al-Naḥwī informed us in Baghdad. (Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, both saying: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Abī ʿImrān al-Jawnī, from a man, from Abū Hurayrah, that a man complained to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) about the hardness of his heart. He said: ""If you wish for your heart to become soft, feed the poor and stroke the head of the orphan."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
7096,"(7095) And Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb informed us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Wāsiʿ, that Abū al-Dardāʾ (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Salmān that a man had complained to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) about the hardness of his heart. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""If you wish for your heart to become soft, stroke the head of the orphan and feed him."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
7097,"(7096) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz informed us; Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Makkī informed us; Sufyān narrated from Jaʿfar, from his father, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Prepare food for the family of Jaʿfar, for there has come to them that which occupies them"" — or he said, ""There has come to them that which preoccupies them."" This Jaʿfar is the son of Khālid ibn Sārah, al-Makhzūmī. [Hasan due to supporting narrations. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7098,"(7097) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of that; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us; al-Ḥumaydī informed us; Sufyān informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Khālid ibn Sārah al-Makhzūmī informed me; my father, who was a close companion of ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar, informed me that he heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar say: ""When the death of Jaʿfar was announced, the Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Prepare food for the family of Jaʿfar, for there has come to them a matter that occupies them.'"" [Hasan due to supporting narrations. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7099,"(7098) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed me; al-Layth informed me; ʿUqayl narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that when a member of her household died and the women gathered for that purpose and then dispersed — except for her immediate family and close relatives — she would order a pot of talbīnah to be prepared. It would be cooked and tharīd would be made; then the talbīnah would be poured over it. She would then say: ""Eat from it, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Talbīnah is a restorative for the heart of the sick person and it removes some of the grief.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another route from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7100,"(7099) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī informed us; Isḥāq ibn Yūsuf al-Azraq informed us; Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The soul of the believer remains suspended by his debt until it is discharged on his behalf."" A group narrated it thus from Saʿd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7101,"(7100) And Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ informed us in al-Kūfah; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Duḥaym informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayn al-Qazzāz informed us; al-Faḍl, meaning ibn Dukayn, informed us; Sufyān narrated from Saʿd ibn Ibrāhīm, from ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The soul of the believer remains suspended so long as there is a debt upon him."" Shuʿbah and Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd likewise narrated it from Saʿd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7102,"(7101) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī informed us; Ādam ibn Abī Iyās informed us; Shuʿbah informed us; Qatādah narrated from Muṭarrif ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Shikhkhīr, from his father, who said: When this verse was revealed — {Competition in [worldly] increase diverts you} [al-Takāthur 102:1] — the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The son of Ādam says, 'My wealth! My wealth!' But do you have any of your wealth except what you have consumed and used up, or worn out and made threadbare, or given in charity and thereby preserved?"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar, from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7103,"(7102) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir informed us; ʿAbbās ibn Abī Shamlah narrated from Mūsā ibn Yaʿqūb, from Asīd ibn ʿUbayd, from his father, from Abū Usayd al-Sāʿidī, who said: ""I was the youngest of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and the one who heard the most from him."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Nothing remains for the child of the goodness of the parent except four things: praying over him, supplicating for him, fulfilling his covenant after him, maintaining his ties of kinship, and honouring his friend."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7104,"(7103) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Muʾammal ibn Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsā informed us; al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyib informed us; Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam informed us; Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr informed me; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that a man said to the Prophet (ﷺ): ""My mother has passed away suddenly, and I think that if she had been able to speak, she would have given charity. Will she receive reward if I give charity on her behalf?"" He said: ""Yes."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it through other routes from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7105,"(7104) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Khalīfah al-Faḍl ibn al-Ḥubāb informed us; Musaddad informed us; ʿAbd al-Wārith informed us; Ayyūb narrated from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: ""We gave our pledge of allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he recited to us {That they associate nothing with Allah} [Mumtaḥinah 60:12], and he forbade us from wailing (niyāḥah). A woman withdrew her hand and said, 'So-and-so was kind to me; I wish to reward her.' The Prophet (ﷺ) said nothing to her. She then went away, came back, and gave her pledge of allegiance."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ with this wording from Abī Maʿmar, from ʿAbd al-Wārith. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7106,"(7105) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Tamtām informed us; Musaddad informed us; ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Ayyūb, from Ḥafṣah, from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: ""We gave our pledge of allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: 'Do not associate anything with Allah.' And he forbade us from wailing (niyāḥah). A woman withdrew her hand and said, 'O Messenger of Allah, so-and-so was kind to me, and I wish to reward her.' He said nothing to her, so she left, then returned — meaning he then accepted her pledge of allegiance."" She said: ""None of us fulfilled the pledge except Umm Sulaym, Umm al-ʿAlāʾ, and the daughter of Abī Sabrah — the wife of Muʿādh — or the daughter of Abī Sabrah and the wife of Muʿādh."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad in this manner. It does not contain that he made an exception for her regarding what she intended; rather, it contains that he did not grant her request until she returned and gave her pledge of allegiance. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously.]"
7107,"(7106) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Anbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us; Abū Mu'āwiyah informed us; 'Āṣim reported from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn from Umm 'Aṭiyyah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: When the verse was revealed {When the believing women come to you to pledge to you that they will not associate anything with Allah…} up to His words {and they will not disobey you in what is right}, she said: Among these was wailing (al-niyāḥah). She said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, except for the family of so-and-so, for they used to assist me in the Days of Ignorance, so I must assist them. He said: ""Except for the family of so-and-so.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and others.

Likewise, 'Āṣim ibn Sulaymān al-Aḥwal narrated it from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn, and I do not know whether he preserved what was narrated regarding the permissibility of assisting (in mutual aid), or not. For Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyyānī narrated it, and he is more reliable than him, as we have mentioned.

And Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated it from Ḥafṣah, and he did not mention anything of that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7108,"(7107) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū 'Abdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us; Abū al-Rabī' informed us; Ḥammād informed us; Ayyūb reported from Muḥammad from Umm 'Aṭiyyah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took from us, along with the pledge of allegiance, that we would not wail. Only five women fulfilled it from among us: Umm Sulaym, Umm al-'Alā', the daughter of Abū Sabrah (the wife of Mu'ādh), or the daughter of Abū Sabrah and the wife of Mu'ādh.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥajabī from Ḥammād, and he said in the hadith: ""And two women or another woman."" And Muslim narrated it from Abū al-Rabī'. And in the narration of Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn there is also nothing of what is in the narration of 'Āṣim from Ḥafṣah regarding the exception. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7109,"(7108) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd ibn Aḥmad al-Zawzanī informed us; Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī informed us; 'Abd al-Razzāq reported from—

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfi' informed us; 'Abd al-Razzāq informed us; Ma'mar reported from Thābit from Anas, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) took from the women, when he accepted their pledge of allegiance, that they would not wail. They said: O Messenger of Allah, some women assisted us in the Days of Ignorance; shall we assist them in Islam? The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""There is no assisting (in wailing) in Islam."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasā'.]"
7110,"(7109) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Ma'īn informed us; Ibn 'Uyaynah informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, Hannād ibn al-Sarī, and Isḥāq ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī informed us. Isḥāq said: ""informed us,"" and the other two said: ""narrated to us."" Sufyān reported from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from his father from 'Ubayd ibn 'Umayr, who said: Umm Salamah said: When Abū Salamah died, may Allah be pleased with him, I said: He is a stranger in a land of exile; I shall weep over him with a weeping that will be talked about. She said: When I prepared to weep over him, a woman came wanting to join me. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) met her and said: ""Do you wish to admit Satan into a house from which Allah has expelled him?"" She said: So I refrained from weeping over him. And in the narration of Abū 'Abdillāh: ""I shall weep over him with a weeping that will be spoken of."" She said: While I was in that state, having prepared to weep over him, a woman came wanting to join me from the open ground. He met her and mentioned it, and said: So I refrained from weeping, and I did not weep over him.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm. And this pertains to weeping that is accompanied by lamentation or wailing.

And likewise, what we have narrated previously from 'Ā'ishah regarding the weeping of the women of Ja'far over him, and the prohibition of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7111,"(7110) Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn 'Abdillāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Shāfi'ī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn 'Abd al-Raḥīm Danūqā informed us; 'Affān informed us; Abān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū 'Abdillāh informed us; Abū 'Amr ibn Abī Ja'far informed me; 'Abdullāh ibn Shīrūyah informed us; Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr informed us; Ḥabbān ibn Hilāl informed us; Abān informed us; Yaḥyā informed him that Zayd informed him that Abū Salām informed him that Abū Mālik al-Ash'arī informed him that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""There are four matters from the affairs of the Days of Ignorance that my Ummah will not abandon: boasting about lineages, defaming lineages, seeking rain through the stars, and wailing. And indeed, the wailing woman, if she does not repent before her death, she will be raised on the Day of Resurrection wearing a garment of tar and a coat of mange.""

The wording is that of Ḥabbān's hadith. Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from 'Affān, and from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr from Ḥabbān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasā'.]"
7112,"(7111) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Sa'īd ibn Abī 'Amr both said: Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn 'Abd al-Jabbār narrated to us; Abū Mu'āwiyah reported from al-A'mash from Abū Ṣāliḥ from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There are two things in the people, and they constitute disbelief: wailing and defaming lineage.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Abū Mu'āwiyah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7113,"(7112) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Sa'īd ibn al-A'rābī informed us; Sa'dān ibn Naṣr informed us; Sufyān reported from 'Ubaydullāh ibn Abī Yazīd, who heard Ibn 'Abbās say: Among the traits of the Days of Ignorance are: defaming lineages and wailing. And he forgot the third. Sufyān said: They say it is seeking rain through the stars (al-istisqā' bi-al-anwā').

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from 'Alī ibn al-Madīnī from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7114,"(7113) Abū 'Alī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mūsā informed us; Muḥammad ibn Rabī'ah reported from Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn 'Aṭiyyah from his father from his grandfather from Abū Sa'īd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the wailing woman and the one who listens to her. [Ḍa'īf. Abū Dāwūd.]"
7115,"(7114) The Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān, may Allah have mercy on him, narrated to us by dictation; Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Abū 'Utbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Abū 'Ā'idh, who is 'Ufayr ibn Ma'dān, narrated to us; 'Aṭā' ibn Abī Rabbāḥ narrated that he was with Ibn 'Umar, who said: Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the wailing woman, the one who listens to her, the woman who shaves (her head in mourning), the woman who tears her collarbone area, the tattooed woman, and the woman who has herself tattooed. And he said: Women have no reward in following the funeral procession. [Ḍa'īf. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7116,"(7115) Abū 'Abdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-'Abbās Muḥammad ibn Ya'qūb informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn 'Alī ibn 'Affān al-'Āmirī informed us; 'Abdullāh ibn Numayr reported from al-A'mash from 'Abdullāh ibn Murrah from Masrūq, who said: 'Abdullāh, meaning Ibn Mas'ūd, said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""He is not one of us who strikes the cheeks, tears the garments, and calls with the call of the Days of Ignorance."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7117,"(7116) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Dharr ibn Abī al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Qāsim al-Mudhakkir narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us from Sufyān and Shuʿbah, from al-Aʿmash, from ʿAbdillāh ibn Murrah, from Masrūq, from ʿAbdillāh, from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar hadith.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Bundār, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Sufyān alone, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr, from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī and Muslim]"
7118,"(7117) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Birtī narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbbād narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us from Zubayd, from Ibrāhīm, from Masrūq, who said: ʿAbdillāh narrated to us, he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""He is not one of us who strikes the cheeks, tears the garments, and calls with the call of the Jāhiliyyah."" The wording of both is the same.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
7119,"(7118) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī — may Allah have mercy on him — narrated to us, ʿAbdillāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us, Abū al-ʿUmays narrated to us, he said: I heard Abā Ṣakhrah mention, on the authority of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd and Abī Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā, they both said: Abū Mūsā lost consciousness, and his wife came crying out with a wailing cry — they both said — then he regained consciousness and said: ""Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'I am free of the one who shaves his head, wails, and tears his garments.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd and others, from Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Abī Burdah. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7120,"(7119) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā al-Qanṭarī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir, that al-Qāsim ibn Mukhaymirah narrated to him, he said: Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā narrated to me, he said: Abū Mūsā fell ill and lost consciousness, and his head was in the lap of a woman from his household. One of his women cried out, and he was unable to respond to her. When he regained consciousness, he said: ""I am free of the one from whom the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) declared himself free. Indeed, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) declared himself free of the ṣāliqah, the ḥāliqah, and the shāqqah.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ, and he said: ""And al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā said…"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated before it]"
7121,"(7120) Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Maḥmash al-Faqīh — may Allah have mercy on him — narrated to us by dictation and by reading to him from the original of his book, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Khalīl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, in the year thirty-one and three hundred, ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd al-Nasawī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr, from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh, that Abū Mūsā lost consciousness, and his wife — the daughter of Abī Murrah — wept over him. When he regained consciousness, he said: ""I declare myself free of that from which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) declared himself free — from the one who shaves his head, wails, and tears his garments.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥasan al-Ḥulwānī, from ʿAbd al-Ṣamad. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated before it]"
7122,"(7121) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Ḥumayd ibn al-Aswad narrated to us, al-Ḥajjāj — the governor of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz over al-Rabadhah — narrated to us, he said: Asīd ibn Abī Asīd narrated to me, from a woman among the women who gave the pledge of allegiance (al-mubāyiʿāt), she said: ""Among the conditions the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took from us regarding the good that he enjoined upon us, and that we should not disobey him in it, was that we should not scratch our faces, nor call out 'Wail!' (in the manner of the Jāhiliyyah), nor tear our garments, nor spread our hair."" [Ḥasan — narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7123,"(7122) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us, Ibn Nājiʿah narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us, from Ḥuṣayn, from ʿĀmir, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Bashīr, who said: ʿAbdillāh ibn Rawāḥah — may Allah be pleased with him — lost consciousness, and his sister began to weep over him, saying: ""Woe to my mountain!"" and enumerating his qualities. When he regained consciousness, he said: ""You did not say anything to me except that it was said to me: 'Are you really like that?'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿImrān ibn Maysarah, from Ibn Fuḍayl, and ʿAbthar narrated it from Ḥuṣayn, and he added: ""When he died, she did not weep over him."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7124,"(7123) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Shādhān informed us in Baghdad, Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us, Isrāʾīl narrated to us, from Abī Yaḥyā, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet (ﷺ) forbade that a funeral procession be accompanied by a wailing cry. [Ḍaʿīf — Ibn Mājah]"
7125,"(7124) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from Shaqīq, from Umm Salamah — may Allah be pleased with her — she said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'When you are present at the bedside of the deceased or the sick, say good words, for the angels say āmīn to what you say.'"" When Abū Salamah died, I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what should I say?"" He said: ""Say: 'O Allah, forgive us and him, and grant us in his place a good successor (Allāhumma ighfir lanā wa lahu, wa ʿqibnā minhu ʿuqbatan ṣāliḥah).'"" So I said it, and Allah granted me Muḥammad (ﷺ).

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Abī Muʿāwiyah, from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ — Muslim]"
7126,"(7125) Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: ""There is no Muslim who is afflicted with a calamity and says that which Allah has commanded him to say — 'Innā lillāhi wa innā ilayhi rājiʿūn' (Indeed, to Allah we belong and to Him we shall return) — and 'O Allah, reward me in my affliction and replace for me something better' — except that Allah, the Exalted, will grant him something better.""

She said: When Abū Salamah died, I said: ""Who could be better than Abū Salamah? He was among the first to emigrate to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)!"" Then I said those words, and Allah granted me the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me a message of marriage through Ḥāṭib ibn Abī Baltaʿah. I said: ""I have a daughter, and I am a jealous person."" He said: ""As for her, we will ask Allah to enrich her, and I also pray that Allah remove this jealousy from her.""

(7126) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā al-Ḥīrī narrated to us, Musaddad ibn Quṭn narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Jarīr narrated to us, from Manṣūr, from Mujāhid, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him — who said: ""What excellent recompenses they are, and what excellent exaltations! 'Those who, when afflicted with a calamity, say: ""Indeed, to Allah we belong and to Him we shall return."" Upon them are blessings from their Lord and mercy' — what excellent recompenses they are! 'And it is they who are the guided ones' — what excellent exaltations!"" [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Ḥākim]"
7127,"(7126) Sayyidunā ʿUmar (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: ""What a beautiful comparison and what a noble station: {Those who, when affliction befalls them, say: 'Indeed, to Allāh we belong and to Him we shall return. Upon them are blessings from their Lord and mercy'} — this is the finest comparison — and {it is they who are the rightly guided} — this is the most excellent rank and station."""
7128,"(7127) Abū Ṣāliḥ al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us; Muḥammad, i.e. Ibn Jaʿfar, informed us; Shuʿbah informed us; Thābit al-Bunānī narrated from Anas ibn Mālik (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) passed by a woman who was weeping beside a grave. He said to her: 'Fear Allāh and be patient.' She said: 'Leave me alone, for you have not been afflicted with my affliction' — and she did not recognize him. It was then said to her that he was the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), and a state overcame her like that of death. She came to the door of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) and found no gatekeepers there, so she said: 'O Messenger of Allāh, I did not recognize you.' The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: 'Indeed, patience is at the first blow.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from Shuʿbah. Some of them narrated the hadith with the wording: ""Patience is at the first shock."" [Ṣaḥīḥ — al-Bukhārī]"
7129,"(7128) Abū Ṣāliḥ al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār informed us; Muḥammad, i.e. Ibn Jaʿfar, informed us; Shuʿbah narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī, who said: ""I heard Anas ibn Mālik narrate from the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), who said: 'Patience is at the first shock.'""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ — as above]"
7130,"(7129) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim al-Sayyārī in Marw informed us; Abū al-Muwajjih informed us; ʿAbdān informed us; ʿAbdullāh informed us; ʿĀṣim ibn Sulaymān al-Aḥwal narrated; Abū ʿUthmān narrated; Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated, saying: ""The daughter of the Prophet (ﷺ) sent word: 'My son has passed away; come to us.' So he sent greetings of salām and said: 'Indeed, to Allāh belongs what He has taken, and to Him belongs what He has given, and everything with Him is at a specified term. So be patient and seek reward.' She then sent to him, adjuring him that he must come. So he stood, and with him were Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah and another man. The child was handed to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), and his soul was quivering — I think he said: 'like a waterskin.' His eyes overflowed with tears. Saʿd (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: 'O Messenger of Allāh, what is this?' He said: 'This is mercy, which Allāh has placed in the hearts of His servants. Indeed, Allāh shows mercy to those among His servants who are merciful.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān, and Muslim narrated it through other chains from ʿĀṣim. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7131,"(7130) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us; Abū Dāwūd informed us; Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah, Ḥammād ibn Salamah, and Jaʿfar ibn Sulaymān — all of them — narrated from Thābit, from Anas.

Abū Dāwūd said: ""And a shaykh narrated it to me who heard it from al-Naḍr ibn Anas, and the narrations of some of them overlap with others."" Mālik, the father of Anas, said to his wife Umm Sulaym, who was the mother of Anas: ""Do you see this man"" — meaning the Prophet (ﷺ) — ""prohibiting wine?"" So he (Mālik) departed for Shām and perished there. Then Abū Ṭalḥah came and proposed to Umm Sulaym. He spoke to her about it, and she said: ""O Abū Ṭalḥah, the likes of you are not turned away, but you are a disbelieving man and I am a Muslim woman; it is not permissible for me to marry you."" He said: ""What is your dower then?"" She said: ""What is my dower?"" He said: ""Gold or silver?"" She said: ""I want neither gold nor silver. I want Islam from you."" He said: ""Who can help me with that?"" She said: ""The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) can help you with that."" So Abū Ṭalḥah set out toward the Prophet (ﷺ), while the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) was sitting among his Companions. When he saw him, he said: 'Abū Ṭalḥah has come to you, with the radiance of Islam between his eyes.' He came and informed the Prophet (ﷺ) of what Umm Sulaym had said, and he married her on that basis.

Thābit said: ""We have not heard of any dower greater than this — that she was pleased with Islam as her dower, and so he married her. She was a beautiful woman with large eyes, though she was of small stature. She remained with him until she bore him a son. Abū Ṭalḥah loved the boy intensely. When the boy fell ill, Abū Ṭalḥah was humbled by his illness — or weakened because of it. Abū Ṭalḥah went to the Prophet (ﷺ), and the boy died. Umm Sulaym (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) said: 'Let no one inform Abū Ṭalḥah of his son's death until I myself inform him.' She prepared the boy and laid him out. Abū Ṭalḥah returned from the presence of the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) and entered upon her. He said: 'How is my son?' She said: 'O Abū Ṭalḥah, since he fell ill, he has been more at peace this past hour.' He said: 'All praise is due to Allāh.' She brought him his supper, and he ate from it. Then she rose, perfumed herself, and presented herself to him, and he lay with her. When she knew that he had eaten and been intimate with her, she said: 'O Abū Ṭalḥah, what do you think — if a people lent another people something on loan, and then they asked for it back, would it be right for them to refuse?' He said: 'No.' She said: 'Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, lent your son to you as a loan, then He took him back to Himself. So consider your son a source of reward and be patient.' He became angry and said: 'You left me until I had done what I did, and then you informed me of my son's death!' He went in the morning to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) and informed him. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: 'May Allāh bless you both in your past night.' She conceived from that intercourse.

Umm Sulaym (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) used to travel with the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ), going out when he went out and entering when he entered. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: 'When Umm Sulaym gives birth, bring the boy to me.' She went into labor the night they were near Madīnah. She said: 'O Allāh, I used to enter when Your Prophet entered and go out when Your Prophet went out, and now this matter has come upon me.' She gave birth to a boy — meaning when they arrived in Madīnah. She said to her son Anas: 'Take the boy to the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ).' Anas took the boy and went with him to the Prophet (ﷺ), who was branding camels and sheep. When he looked at the boy, he said to Anas: 'Has the daughter of Milḥān given birth?' He said: 'Yes.' He cast aside what was in his hand and took the boy. He said: 'Bring me some ʿajwah dates.' The Prophet (ﷺ) took the dates and performed taḥnīk on the boy, and the boy began to lick them. He said: 'Look at the love of the Anṣār for dates.' The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) performed taḥnīk for him and named him ʿAbdullāh.""

Thābit said: ""He was counted among the best of the Muslims.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn al-Mughīrah from Thābit, including the story of the death but not the preceding story of the marriage. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Isḥāq ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī Ṭalḥah from Anas in an abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Ṭayālisī]"
7132,"(7131) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us; ʿImrān ibn Mūsā informed us; Abū Kāmil informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar Abū Jaʿfar informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Sahl Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mihrānī informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Tājir informed us; Abū al-Faḍl ʿAyyāsh ibn Muḥammad al-Jawharī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both said: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us; ʿAyyāsh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Jawharī informed us; Dāwūd ibn Rushayd informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""There were two crippled men in Makkah, and they had a son who would carry them in the morning and bring them to the mosque, placing them there. He would then go out and earn for them. When evening came, he would carry them and bring them back. The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) missed him and asked about him. They said: 'He has died.' The Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) said: 'If anyone were to be left for anyone, the son of the two crippled men would be left.' Then the Messenger of Allāh (ﷺ) would frequently say that."" The wording is from the ḥadīth of Dāwūd. [Ḍaʿīf — al-Ṭabarānī]"
7133,"(7132) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd ibn Ibrāhīm al-Asadī al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥamdān al-Jallāb both said: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dīzīl informed us; Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Farwī informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar al-ʿUmarī narrated from his brother ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar from Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Jaḥsh from his father, from Ḥamnah bint Jaḥsh, that it was said to her: ""Your brother has been killed."" She said: ""May Allāh have mercy on him. Indeed, to Allāh we belong and to Him we shall return."" Then it was said to her: ""Your uncle Ḥamzah has been killed."" She said: ""May Allāh have mercy on him. Indeed, to Allāh we belong and to Him we shall return."" Then it was said to her: ""Your husband has been killed."" She said: ""How sorrowful!"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, the husband has a bond with the woman that he has with no one else."" [Ḍaʿīf — Ibn Mājah]"
7134,"(7133) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Zāhid informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh al-ʿAbsī al-Kūfī informed us; Wakīʿ informed us; from al-Aʿmash from Abū Ẓabyān, who said: ""We used to present our muṣḥafs to ʿAlqamah ibn Qays for review. He passed by this verse: {No affliction befalls except by the permission of Allāh, and whoever believes in Allāh, He will guide his heart.} We asked him about it, and he said: 'It refers to the man who is afflicted with a calamity and knows that it is from Allāh, so he is pleased and submits.'"" This has also been narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu). [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by the author]"
7135,"(7134) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad and Mūsā ibn Muḥammad al-Dhuhlī and Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us. He said: ""I read to Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from Saʿid ibn al-Musayyab from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: 'No Muslim has three children die without the Fire touching them, except as a fulfillment of the oath.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī Uways from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ — narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7136,"(7135) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Ādamī in Makkah informed us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar informed us; from al-Zuhrī, who mentioned the same meaning and added: ""They had not reached the age of sin (al-ḥinth).""

Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ and others from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ — as above]"
7137,"(7136) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abū ʿAwānah narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Aṣbahānī from Abū Ṣāliḥ Dhakwān from Abū Saʿīd that a group of women gathered and came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he taught them what Allah had taught him. Then he said: ""There is no woman among you who loses three of her children, but they will be a shield for her from the Fire."" A woman said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about two?"" He said: ""And two.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Kāmil from Abū ʿAwānah.

It was also narrated by Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ from his father from Abū Hurayrah.

And Sharīk narrated it from Ibn al-Aṣbahānī from Abū Ṣāliḥ from Abū Saʿīd and Abū Hurayrah.

And Shuʿbah narrated it from Ibn al-Aṣbahānī from Abū Ṣāliḥ from Abū Saʿīd.

And from Ibn al-Aṣbahānī from Abū Ḥāzim from Abū Hurayrah — Suhayl added in his narration: ""provided she seeks reward for them (fa-taḥtasibahum).""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7138,"(7137) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ from his father from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever loses two or three children who have not yet reached the age of discernment, and he seeks reward for them, they will be a shield for him from the Fire.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7139,"(7138) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Suhayl from his father from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to some women of the Anṣār: ""No one among you loses three of her children and seeks reward for them, but she will enter Paradise."" A woman said: ""Or two, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Or two.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
7140,"(7139) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed me, Abū Yaḥyā ibn Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyā al-Nāqid narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Mahdī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wārith ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb from Anas ibn Mālik who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no Muslim who loses three children who have not yet reached the age of discernment, but Allah will admit him to Paradise by the bounty of His mercy.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7141,"(7140) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us, Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed me, al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Ḥammād al-Maʿnī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated it with the same meaning, except that he said: ""by the bounty of His mercy upon them.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Maʿmar from ʿAbd al-Wārith.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
7142,"(7141) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī, and Abū Saʿīd Masʿūd ibn Muḥammad al-Jurjānī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Maymūnī in al-Raqqah narrated to us, ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us, saying: My father narrated to me from his grandfather Ṭalq ibn Muʿāwiyah from Abū Zurʿah ibn ʿAmr ibn Jarīr from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) who said: A woman came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I have buried three of my children."" He said: ""You have indeed erected a formidable barrier against the Fire.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7143,"(7142) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us, Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated from his father, Abū al-Salīl narrated to us from Abū Ḥassān who said: I said to Abū Hurayrah: ""Two of my sons have died. Will you narrate to me a hadith from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that will comfort our souls regarding our deceased?"" He said: ""Yes. Their young ones are the birds of Paradise. One of them will meet his parents — or his father — and take hold of his hand, just as I am holding the edge of your garment here, and he will not let go until Allah admits him and his father into Paradise.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7144,"(7143) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥātim al-Dārabardī in Marw narrated to us, Abū al-Muthannā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated from al-Taymī, who narrated it with the same meaning, except that he said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Their young ones are the birds of Paradise.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā and others from Muʿtamir, and from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Saʿīd from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
7145,"(7144) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, ʿAwf narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn from Abū Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There are no two Muslims who lose three children who have not yet reached the age of discernment, but Allah will admit them and their parents to Paradise by the bounty of His mercy."" He said: ""They will be at one of the gates of Paradise, and it will be said to them: 'Enter Paradise.' They will say: 'Not until our parents come.' Then it will be said to them: 'You and your parents shall enter Paradise by the bounty of the mercy of Allah.'""

The narrations on this topic are numerous, and what we have mentioned suffices.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7146,"(7145) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash.

(Ḥ) And the Imam Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, my father the Imam narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Thaqafī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ narrated to us, Jarīr narrated from al-Aʿmash from Ibrāhīm al-Taymī from al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What do you consider al-Raqūb among you?"" They said: ""It is one who is not born to him (i.e., one who has no children)."" He said: ""That is not al-Raqūb; rather, al-Raqūb is the man who has not sent forth any of his children (i.e., none of his children have died before him)."" He said: ""What do you consider al-Ṣurʿah among you?"" They said: ""One whom men cannot overcome."" He said: ""It is not that; rather, al-Ṣurʿah is the one who controls himself at the time of anger.""

The wording is that of Jarīr. In the hadith of Abū Muʿāwiyah there is a different order. He said: First he said: ""What do you consider al-Ṣurʿah among you?"" They said: ""One whom men cannot overcome."" He said: ""No; rather, al-Ṣurʿah is the one who controls himself at the time of anger."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""What do you consider al-Raqūb among you?"" We said: ""Al-Raqūb is one who is not born to him."" He said: ""No; rather, al-Raqūb is the one who has not sent forth any of his children.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Abū Muʿāwiyah, and from Qutaybah and ʿUthmān from Jarīr.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7147,"(7146) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from Abū Sinān, who said: I buried my son Sinān, and Abū Ṭalḥah al-Khawlānī was sitting on the edge of the grave. He said: al-Ḍaḥḥāk ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to me from Abū Mūsā, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When Allah takes the child of a servant, He says to His angels: 'What did my servant say?' They reply: 'He praised You and said: *Innā lillāhi wa innā ilayhi rājiʿūn* (To Allah we belong and to Him we shall return).' Allah says: 'Build for My servant a house in Paradise and name it the House of Praise (Bayt al-Ḥamd).'""

[Weak. Reported by al-Ṭayālisī]"
7148,"(7147) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaʿqūb al-Kirmānī — from Muḥammad ibn Abī Yaʿqūb al-Kirmānī — Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — ʿAbd Rabbih ibn Bāriq al-Ḥanafī narrated to me — my grandfather Simāk ibn al-Walīd al-Ḥanafī narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: He heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""Whoever has two children from my Ummah [who predecease him], Allah will admit him into Paradise."" Then ʿĀ'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Even one, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ replied: ""Even one, O blessed one."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""And whoever does not have a child from my Ummah [who predeceases him] — I shall be the predecessor for those who have no predecessor, and they will not be afflicted by the likes of me.""

[Weak. Reported by al-Tirmidhī]"
7149,"(7148) And the Imām Abū al-Ṭayyib Sahl ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us — Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAlī al-Daqqāq narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us — ʿĪsā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Barakī narrated to us — ʿAbd Rabbih ibn Bāriq al-Ḥanafī narrated, mentioning it with the same meaning.

[Weak. Reported by al-Ḥākim]"
7150,"(7149) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkārī in Baghdad informed us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal — from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī — from Usāmah ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Prophet ﷺ was brought the daughter of his daughter, and her soul was rattling as though it were in a water-skin. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""To Allah belongs what He takes, and to Allah belongs what He gives, and everything is for a specified term."" He (Usāmah) said: He ﷺ wept. Then Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said to him: ""Do you weep when you have forbidden weeping?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""It is merely a mercy that Allah has placed in the hearts of His servants, and Allah only shows mercy to those of His servants who are merciful.""

[Authentic. Reported by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from Abū Muʿāwiyah; and al-Bukhārī reported it through other chains from ʿĀṣim al-Aḥwal.]"
7151,"(7150) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said — they narrated: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — Abū al-Naḍr narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn al-Mīghirah narrated from Thābit — from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""A son was born to me this night, and I have named him after my father Ibrāhīm."" Then he handed him to Umm Sayf, a woman of the Qayn tribe in Madinah — she was known as Abū Sayf. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went to visit him, and I went with him. We came to Abū Sayf, who was blowing into his furnace — and the house was filled with smoke. I hastened to walk ahead of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and came to Abū Sayf, saying: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ has come — hold back, hold back!"" So he held back. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came and called for the boy, took him to himself, and said what Allah willed him to say. Anas said: I saw the boy before the Messenger of Allah ﷺ as he was struggling with his soul, and the eyes of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ filled with tears. He ﷺ said: ""The eye weeps and the heart grieves, but we say nothing except what pleases our Lord. By Allah, O Ibrāhīm, we are indeed grieved for you.""

[Authentic. Reported by Muslim in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Hudbah and Shaybān from Sulaymān; and al-Bukhārī reported it from another chain from Thābit. And Mūsā also reported it from Sulaymān.]"
7152,"(7151) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Jābir narrated to us — Shaybān narrated to us — Abū ʿAwānah narrated from Ibn Abī Laylā — from ʬāṭā' — from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, who said: The Prophet ﷺ went out with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf (may Allah be pleased with him) to the palm trees, and there his son Ibrāhīm was expiring. He placed him in his lap, and his eyes overflowed. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf said: ""Do you weep when you forbid people [from weeping]?"" He ﷺ said: ""I have not forbidden weeping; I have only forbidden wailing — two foolish, sinful voices: a voice at a time of amusement, play, and flutes of Shayṭān, and a voice at a time of affliction with face-scratching, garment-rending, and shrieking. And this [weeping] is mercy, and whoever does not show mercy will not be shown mercy. O Ibrāhīm, were it not a true matter and a sincere promise, and were it not that the last of us will join the first of us, we would have grieved for you with a grief more severe than this. And indeed, we are grieved for you — the eye weeps and the heart grieves, but we do not say what angers the Lord.""

[Weak. Reported by al-Tirmidhī]"
7153,"(7152) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn Sawwād narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to us — ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn al-Ḥārith ibn al-Muʿallā al-Anṣārī narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah complained of an ailment, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to visit him, along with ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAwf, Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd. When he entered upon him, he found him in a swoon. He ﷺ said: ""Has he passed away?"" They said: ""No, O Messenger of Allah."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wept. When the people saw the weeping of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, they wept. He ﷺ said: ""Do you not hear? Allah does not punish for the tears of the eye nor for the grief of the heart, but He punishes for this"" — and he pointed to his tongue — ""or shows mercy.""

[Authentic. Reported by al-Bukhārī in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Aṣbagh from Ibn Wahb; and Muslim reported it from ʿAmr ibn Sawwād.]"
7154,"(7153) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Jābir ibn ʿAtīk — from ʿAtīk ibn al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAtīk, who was the grandfather of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdullāh and the mother's father — that he informed him that Jābir ibn ʿAtīk informed him: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to visit ʿAbdullāh ibn Thābit and found him overcome [by death]. He called out to him, but he did not respond. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: *Innā lillāhi wa innā ilayhi rājiʿūn* — ""We have been overcome by you, O Abū al-Rabīʿ."" The women cried out and wept. Ibn ʿAtīk began to silence them. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Leave them, and when it becomes obligatory, no woman should weep."" They said: ""What is the obligation, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ said: ""When he dies.""

[Weak. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
7155,"(7154) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us — Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated to me — al-Zuhrī narrated from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: When the Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned from Uḥud, he heard the women of the Anṣār weeping. He ﷺ said: ""But for Ḥamzah, there would be no one to weep for him."" This reached the women of the Anṣār, and they wept for Ḥamzah. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ then slept. He awoke while they were still weeping. He ﷺ said: ""Woe to you! You have been weeping since this day — let them be silent and let not a single woman weep for any deceased person after this day.""

[Denounced (Munkar). Reported by Ibn Mājah]"
7156,"(7155) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us — ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated to us — Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated from Nāfiʿ — from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ returned on the day of Uḥud and heard the women of Banī ʿAbd al-Ashʿhal weeping over their slain. He ﷺ said: ""But for Ḥamzah, there would be no one to weep for him."" Then the women of the Anṣār came and wept for Ḥamzah in his presence, and he slept. He awoke while they were still weeping. He ﷺ said: ""Woe to you! They have been weeping until now. Send them back, and let them not weep for any deceased person after this day.""

His statement ""and let them not weep for any deceased person after this day"" — if he intended by it a general [prohibition], it would be like his statement in the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAtīk: ""When it becomes obligatory, no woman should weep."" And it is also possible that what is intended thereby is the martyrs of Uḥud, as though he were saying: ""Sufficient for you is what you have wept for them.""

And permission for weeping after death, with the tears of the eye and the grief of the heart, has been reported. Thus the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn ʿAtīk would be interpreted as referring to a recommended [practice], and Allah knows best.

[Denounced (Munkar). Previously referenced]"
7157,"(7156) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl, from Anas ibn Mālik, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) announced the deaths of Jaʿfar, Zayd ibn Ḥārithah, and ʿAbdullāh ibn Rawāḥah. He announced their deaths before the news of them reached him, and his eyes were shedding tears.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb.

And it has been narrated to us from Anas ibn Mālik that he said: ""We attended the funeral of a daughter of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was sitting at the grave, and I saw his eyes shedding tears."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7158,"(7157) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū al-Faḍl al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb both said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us, Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Abū Munayn Yazīd ibn Kaysān narrated from Abū Ḥāzim, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) visited the grave of his mother and wept, and he caused those around him to weep. Then he said: 'I sought permission from my Lord to visit her grave, and He granted me permission. And I sought His permission to seek forgiveness for her, but I was not given permission. So visit the graves, for they remind one of death.'""

Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yazīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7159,"(7158) Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Yazīd narrated it.

Muslim narrated it in some of the copies from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously.]"
7160,"(7159) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from Wahb ibn Kaysān, that Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr informed him, that Salamah al-Azlāq was sitting with Ibn ʿUmar in the marketplace when a funeral procession passed by over which people were weeping. Ibn ʿUmar disapproved of that and rebuked them. Salamah said: ""Do not say that, O Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān! I bear witness that I heard Abū Hurayrah say: 'A funeral was brought before the Prophet (ﷺ) while I was with him, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) was with him, and women were weeping over it. ʿUmar rebuked them and scolded them. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: ""Leave them, O ʿUmar, for the eye is shedding tears, the soul is afflicted, and the wound is fresh.""' They said: 'Did you hear him say this?' He said: 'Yes.' Ibn ʿUmar said: 'Allah and His Messenger know best,' twice."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
7161,"(7160) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Fāris al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, Abū Bishr Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from ʿAlī ibn Zayd, from Yūsuf ibn Mihrān, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The women wept over Ruqayyah (may Allah be pleased with her), so ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) forbade them. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Leave them, O ʿUmar.' Then he said: 'Beware of the howling of Satan, for whatever comes from the eye and the heart is from mercy, and whatever comes from the tongue and the hand is from Satan.'"" He said: ""And Fāṭimah (may Allah be pleased with her) was weeping at the edge of Ruqayyah's grave, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was wiping the tears from her face with his hand, or he said, with his garment.""

And although this is not strong, his (ﷺ) statement in the established ḥadīth from him: ""Allah does not punish for the tears of the eye nor for the grief of the heart, but He punishes for this,"" and he pointed to his tongue, ""or He has mercy,"" indicates its meaning and testifies to its authenticity. And with Allah is the success. [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad.]"
7162,"(7161) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Shaqīq, who said: ""When Khālid ibn al-Walīd died, the women of Banī al-Mughīrah gathered and wept over him. It was said to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him): 'Send to them, for nothing will reach you from them that you would dislike.' ʿUmar said: 'There is no harm upon them shedding their tears over Abū Sulaymān, so long as it is not accompanied by wailing or loud lamentation.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
7163,"(7162) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bushrān and Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār in Baghdād both said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar narrated from Thābit, from Anas, that Fāṭimah (peace be upon her) wept for her father and said: ""O my dear father, how close he is to his Lord! O my dear father, to Jibrīl we announce his death! O my dear father, Jannat al-Firdaws is his abode!""

Ḥammād ibn Zayd added in it from Thābit: ""O my dear father, he answered the call of his Lord.""

And from this chain, al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7164,"(7163) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn ʿUmar, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, the deceased is punished by the wailing over him in his grave.""

Both al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah in this manner. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7165,"(7164) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us, Muḥammad, meaning Ibn Jaʿfar, narrated to us, Shuʿbah said: I heard Qatādah narrating from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from Ibn ʿUmar, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The deceased is punished by what is wailed over him in his grave.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Bashshār, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbdān, from his father, from Shuʿbah. And both also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, from Qatādah, in this manner. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously.]"
7166,"(7165) Abū al-Ḥasan al-ʿAlawī informed us, Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Dūlābī narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Bakr ibn Ḥafṣ, who said: I heard Ibn ʿUmar narrate from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""The deceased is punished by the weeping of the living."" [Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7167,"(7166) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Bashīr narrated to us — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar: that Ḥafṣah wept over ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), so he said: ""Gently, O my daughter! Did you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Indeed, the deceased is tormented by the weeping of his family over him'?""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Muḥammad ibn Bashīr, and he also reported it from the ḥadīth of Abī Ṣāliḥ from Ibn ʿUmar with the same meaning regarding weeping. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced.]"
7168,"(7167) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Abū ʿAmr al-Mustamliyy and Muḥammad ibn Shādhān said — ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn Mushir from Abī Isḥāq al-Shaybānī from Abū Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā from his father, he said: When ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) was stabbed, Ṣuhayb began to cry out: ""Alas, my brother!"" So ʿUmar said to him: ""O Ṣuhayb, did you not know that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: 'Indeed, the deceased is tormented by the weeping of the living'?""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ismāʿīl ibn al-Khalīl from ʿAlī ibn Mushir, and Muslim narrated it from ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced.]"
7169,"(7168) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Thaqafī narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn al-Muthannā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us — ʿAffān ibn Muslim narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Salamah from Thābit from Anas: that when ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) was stabbed, Ḥafṣah wailed over him. He said: ""O Ḥafṣah, have you not heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'The one over whom wailing is done shall be tormented'?"" And Ṣuhayb wailed over him, so ʿUmar said: ""O Ṣuhayb, did you not know that the one over whom wailing is done shall be tormented?""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Muḥammad al-Nāqid from ʿAffān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7170,"(7169) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us — Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭāʾī from ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿah: that one day he went out to the Great Mosque, and al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah was the governor of Kūfah. Al-Mughīrah went out to the mosque, ascended the pulpit, praised Allah and glorified Him, then said: ""What is this wailing in Islam?"" They said: ""A man from the Anṣār — called Qarẓah ibn Kaʿb — has passed away, and wailing has been done over him."" Al-Mughīrah said: ""Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Lying upon me is not like lying upon anyone else. Whoever lies upon me, let him take his seat in the Fire.' And indeed, I heard the Prophet of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever is wailed over shall be tormented by that which is wailed over him.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Nuʿaym in abridged form, and Muslim reported it from another chain from Saʿīd ibn ʿUbayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
7171,"(7170) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr narrated to us — Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Qays al-Asadī from ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿah, he said: The first person over whom wailing was done in Kūfah was Qarẓah ibn Kaʿb, and it is claimed that al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah stood up, praised Allah and glorified Him, then said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whoever lies upon me deliberately, let him take his seat in the Fire.' And I heard him say: 'Whoever is wailed over shall be tormented by that which is wailed over him.'""

Muslim reported it from another chain from Muḥammad ibn Qays. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced.]"
7172,"(7171) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Tamīm ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me — ʿUmar ibn Muḥammad that Sālim narrated to him from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, the deceased is tormented by the weeping of the living.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥarmalah ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim.]"
7173,"(7172) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Zayd from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father: that the statement of Ibn ʿUmar was mentioned to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) — that the one over whom wailing is done is tormented by the weeping of his family over him — so she said: ""May Allah have mercy on Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. He heard something but did not preserve it. Rather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by the funeral of a Jewish man, and his family was weeping over him, so he said: 'They are weeping over him, and he is being tormented.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī al-Rabīʿ from Ḥammād. Abū Usāmah added in his narration from Hishām: ""He is being tormented by his sins or by his transgression, while his family is weeping over him now."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim.]"
7174,"(7173) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāh al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr from his father: that when Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj died, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar said to them: ""Do not weep over him, for indeed the weeping of the living is a torment for the deceased."" And he narrated from ʿAmrah, so I asked ʿĀʾishah about that, and she said: ""May Allah have mercy on him. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said only to a Jewish woman whose family was weeping over her: 'They are weeping over her, and she is being tormented in her grave.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously referenced.]"
7175,"(7174) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others said — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Mālik ibn Anas from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr from his father from ʿAmrah: that she heard ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and it was mentioned to her that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar says: ""Indeed, the deceased is tormented by the weeping of the living."" So ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Indeed, he did not lie, but he erred or forgot. Rather, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ passed by a Jewish woman, and her family was weeping over her, so he said: 'They are weeping over her, and she is being tormented in her grave.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Muslim.]"
7176,"(7175) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Shaybānī narrated to me — my father and Muḥammad ibn Shādhān said — Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd from Mālik ibn Anas, he mentioned it with his chain of narration, except that he said: from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān that she informed him, and he said: and ʿĀʾishah said: ""May Allah forgive Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān,"" and he said: ""Weeping was being done over her.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf — both from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Mālik.]"
7177,"(7176) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdad: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥalīm ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Maymūn al-Ṣāʾigh informed us in Marw: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbdullāh informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn Abī Mulaykah informed me, saying: A daughter of ʿUthmān (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) passed away in Makkah, and we came to attend her funeral. He said: Ibn ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās were present, and I was sitting between them. He said: I was sitting next to one of them, then the other came and sat beside me. ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said to ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān: ""Will you not forbid the women from wailing? For indeed the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The deceased is punished by the weeping of his family.'"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""ʿUmar (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) used to say something to that effect."" Then he narrated and said: ""I departed with ʿUmar from Makkah until we reached al-Baydāʾ, when lo, there was a caravan beneath the shade of a samurah tree. He said: 'Go and see who these riders are.' He said: 'I looked and found it was Ṣuhayb. I informed him.' He said: 'Call him for me.' So I returned to Ṣuhayb and said: 'Set out and join the Commander of the Faithful.' When ʿUthmān was afflicted [i.e., martyred], Ṣuhayb (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā) entered, weeping and saying: 'Alas for my brother! Alas for my companion!' ʿUmar (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: 'O Ṣuhayb, do you weep over me when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed the deceased is punished by some of the weeping of his family over him""?'"" Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""When ʿUmar (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) died, I mentioned that to ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā), and she said: 'May Allah have mercy on ʿUmar. By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never said that Allah punishes the believer by the weeping of his family over him. Rather, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed Allah increases the punishment of the disbeliever by the weeping of his family over him.""'"" She said: ""And ʿĀʾishah said: 'The Qurʾān is sufficient for you: {And no bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another} [al-Anʿām:164].'"" He said: ""And Ibn ʿAbbās said at that time: 'It is Allah alone who causes laughter and weeping.'"" Ibn Abī Mulaykah said: ""By Allah, Ibn ʿUmar said nothing."" The wording is that of the ḥadīth of ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Mubārak, and the ḥadīth of ʿAbd al-Razzāq is to the same meaning.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7178,"(7177) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Walīd narrated to us: Musaddad ibn Quṭn narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated to us: Ayyūb narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Mulaykah, saying: ""I was sitting beside Ibn ʿUmar, and we were waiting for the funeral of Umm Abān bint ʿUthmān."" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth with the meaning of the ḥadīth of Ibn Jurayj, differing from it in some wordings.

Ayyūb said: Ibn Abī Mulaykah narrated to me: al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad narrated to me, saying: ""When the statement of ʿUmar and Ibn ʿUmar reached ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā), she said: 'You are narrating from neither liars nor those who are denied, but the ear sometimes errs.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Dāwūd ibn Rushayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7179,"(7178) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated from al-Shāfiʿī (raḥimahullāh), who said: ""What ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is most likely to have been preserved from him (ﷺ), by the indication of the Book and then the Sunnah. If it is said: 'Where is the indication of the Book?' it is said: In the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {And no bearer of burdens shall bear the burden of another} [al-Anʿām:164], and His words: {And that man shall have nothing but what he strives for} [al-Najm:39], and His words: {Whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil shall see it} [al-Zalzalah:7-8], and His words: {That every soul may be recompensed for what it has earned} [Ṭā Hā:15]. If it is said: 'Where is the indication of the Sunnah?' it is said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to a man: 'This is your son.' He said: 'Yes.' He said: 'Indeed, he shall not be burried with your sin, nor shall you be burried with his sin.' Thus the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made clear, just as Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, made clear, that the sin of every person is upon him alone, as he earned it — not upon another, and not another's upon him.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And ʿAmrah is more knowledgeable in transmission from ʿĀʾishah than Ibn Abī Mulaykah, and her ḥadīth is more likely of the two ḥadīths to be preserved. If the ḥadīth is other than what Ibn Abī Mulaykah narrated from the statement of the Prophet (ﷺ): 'They are weeping over her, and she is being punished in her grave,' then it is clear and needs no interpretation, for she is being punished for disbelief, and they are weeping without knowing what she is in. If the ḥadīth is as Ibn Abī Mulaykah narrated, then it is correct, for upon the disbeliever is a punishment greater than that. If he is punished with less than that, then his punishment is increased by what he has earned. Whatever punishment befalls a disbeliever that is lesser than a greater punishment, and whatever is added to his punishment, it is by his own earning — not by the sin of another in their weeping over him. If it is said: 'He is increased in punishment by the weeping of his family over him,' it is said: 'He is increased by what he has earned through his deeds, and their weeping is a cause — not that he is punished by their weeping over him.' And from what has reached me from Abū Ibrāhīm al-Muzanī, that he said: 'It has reached me that they used to bequeath weeping over them, or wailing, or both, and that is disobedience. So whoever commands it and it is done by his command, it is a sin upon him, just as if he commands an obedience and it is done after him, it is an obedience for him. Just as he is rewarded for that which is a cause of obedience for him, likewise it is permissible that he be punished for that which is a cause of disobedience for him. And by Allah is success.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ chain. Al-Shāfiʿī.]"
7180,"(7179) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: Salm ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Ḥabīb — meaning Ibn Sulaym al-ʿAbsī — narrated to us: Bilāl al-ʿAbsī narrated, saying: ""When there was a funeral among the family of Ḥudhayfah, he would not inform anyone of it, saying: 'I fear that it may be a proclamation of death (naʿy). Indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbid the proclamation of death (naʿy).'""

It is narrated in that regard from Ibn Masʿūd, Ibn ʿUmar, and Abū Saʿīd, and also from ʿAlqamah, Ibn al-Musayyib, al-Rabīʿ ibn Khuthaym, and Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī. And it has reached me from Mālik ibn Anas that he said: ""I do not like shouting at the death of a man at the doors of the mosques. But if one stands at the circles of the mosques and informs the people of his death, there is no harm in that."" And it is narrated from Anas ibn Mālik that the Prophet (ﷺ) announced the deaths of Jaʿfar, Zayd, and Ibn Rawāḥah. And from Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) that the Prophet (ﷺ) announced the death of the Negus. And from him regarding the death of the man who used to sweep the mosque and was buried at night: ""Why did you not inform me?"" And in another narration: ""What prevented you from informing me?"" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7181,"(7180) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Barṭī al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Muslim — meaning Ibn Ibrāhīm — narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Marzūq al-Wāshihī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd — meaning Ibn Rāfiʿ — narrated from his grandmother: that Rāfiʿ ibn Khadīj died after ʿAṣr. Ibn ʿUmar was brought and informed of his death. It was said to him: ""What do you think — should his funeral be taken out now?"" He said: ""One like Rāfiʿ should not be taken out until those around us from the nearby villages have been informed."" So they waited until morning, then they took out his funeral. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7182,"(7181) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us by dictation: Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥusayn Surayj ibn al-Nuʿmān al-Jawharī narrated to us: Fulayḥ ibn Sulaymān narrated from Saʿīd ibn ʿUbayd ibn al-Sabbāq from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""We were ahead of the Prophet (ﷺ). When a deceased person from among us was present, we would inform the Prophet (ﷺ), and he would attend and seek forgiveness for him. When the person passed away, the Prophet (ﷺ) and those with him would depart until he was buried. Sometimes he and those with him would sit until he was buried. Sometimes the delay of that was prolonged for the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ). When we feared the hardship of that upon him, some of the people said to each other: 'Why do we not refrain from informing the Prophet (ﷺ) of anyone until he has passed away? Then when he has passed away, we inform him, and there would be no hardship upon him nor any delay.' So we did that — we would inform him of the deceased after he had died, and he would come and pray over him. Sometimes he would depart, and sometimes he would remain until the deceased was buried. We continued doing that for a time. Then we said: 'Why do we not spare the Prophet (ﷺ) the trouble and carry our funeral to him so that he may pray over it at his house? That would be easier for him.' So we did that, and that has remained the practice to this day."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7183,"(7182) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bushrān informed us in Baghdad: Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Maʿshar narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated from Hishām al-Dastawāʾī from Qatādah from al-Ḥasan from Qays ibn ʿAbbād, who said: ""The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to dislike raising the voice at funerals, at the time of battle, and at the time of remembrance (dhikr)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7184,"(7183) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: al-Aswad ibn Shaybān narrated: ""Al-Ḥasan was present at the funeral of al-Naḍr ibn Anas when Ashʿath ibn Sulaym al-ʿIjlī said: 'O Abū Saʿīd, I would love not to hear any voice at funerals.' He said: 'Indeed, for good there are its people.'""

And it is narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī, Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, and Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that they disliked it being said at a funeral: ""Seek forgiveness for him; may Allah forgive you."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by the author.]"
7185,"(7184) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ṣuhayb narrated: I heard Anas ibn Mālik saying: ""A funeral was brought before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and they spoke well of the deceased."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""It has become obligatory."" Then another funeral was brought, and they spoke ill of the deceased. He said: ""It has become obligatory."" ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said: ""What has become obligatory, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""This one — you spoke well of him, so Paradise has become obligatory for him. And this one — you spoke ill of him, so the Fire has become obligatory for him. You are the witnesses of Allah upon the earth.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUlayyah from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7186,"(7185) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bushrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from Thābit al-Bunānī from Anas (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""A funeral was brought before the Prophet (ﷺ)."" He said: ""Speak of him."" They said: ""He was, as far as we knew, one who loved Allah and His Messenger."" They spoke well of him, and he said: ""It has become obligatory."" Then another funeral was brought before him, and he said: ""Speak of him."" They said: ""What a wretched person he was in the religion of Allah!"" He said: ""It has become obligatory. You are the witnesses of Allah upon the earth."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above.]"
7187,"(7186) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Dāwūd ibn Abī al-Furāt narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Buraydah, from Abū al-Aswad al-Dīlī, who said: I set out for Madīnah when a disease had broken out there. I sat with ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), and a funeral procession passed by them. The deceased was praised with good words, so ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""It has become obligatory."" Then another funeral procession passed by, and the deceased was praised with good words, so ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""It has become obligatory."" Then a third funeral procession passed by, and the deceased was spoken of with ill, so ʿUmar said: ""It has become obligatory."" Abū al-Aswad said: I said, ""What has become obligatory, O Commander of the Faithful?"" He said: ""I said as the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Any Muslim for whom four persons testify to his goodness, Allah will admit him into Paradise.'"" He said: ""We said, 'And three?' He said, 'And three.'"" He said: ""We said, 'And two?' He said, 'And two.'"" He said: ""We did not ask him about one.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: ʿAffān said, and he mentioned it. [Authentic. Preceded before it.]"
7188,"(7187) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmawyah al-ʿAskarī narrated to us in al-Baṣrah, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, al-Aʿmash narrated to us, from Mujāhid, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not revile the dead, for they have already reached that which they sent forth.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās. [Authentic. Al-Bukhārī]"
7189,"(7188) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Qurqūb al-Tammār narrated to us in Hamadhān, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, Abū al-Yamān narrated to us, Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah narrated to us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ḥusayn, who said: Nawfal ibn Musāḥiq narrated to me, from Saʿīd ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not harm a Muslim by reviling a disbeliever."" [Authentic. Al-Ḥākim narrated it.]"
7190,"(7189) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, Abū Kurayb narrated to us, Muʿāwiyah ibn Hishām narrated to us, from ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Mention the virtues of your dead and refrain from their faults.""

The Shaykh (may Allah have mercy on him) said: We have narrated in the ḥadīth of Maʿmar, from Thābit, from Anas, that he said: A funeral procession was brought before the Prophet (ﷺ) and he said, ""Speak well of him,"" so they spoke well of him. Then another was brought and he said, ""Speak well of him,"" so they spoke ill of him. In this there is an indication that they spoke well of one of the two funeral processions and ill of the other, upon the command of the Prophet (ﷺ) to speak well of them both. And in this there is an indication of the permissibility of mentioning a person with what one knows of him when the need arises, such as the question of the judge to the one who testifies, and the like. And it is as though the one of whom they spoke ill was one who made his evil public, so the Prophet (ﷺ) intended to deter the likes of him from their evils and from lengthening their tongues against themselves, so he said what he said. And Allah knows best. [Weak. Abū Dāwūd]"
7191,"(7190) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū al-Yamān narrated to us, Shuʿayb narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, who said: Khārijah ibn Zayd narrated to me, from Umm al-ʿAlāʾ (may Allah be pleased with her), a woman from among their women who had pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who informed him that ʿUthmān ibn Maʿẓūn (may Allah be pleased with him) was allotted their share of residence when the Anṣār drew lots for the residence of the Muhājirūn. Umm al-ʿAlāʾ said: So ʿUthmān ibn Maʿẓūn stayed with us, and we nursed him when he fell ill, until when he passed away and we had placed him in his garments, the Prophet (ﷺ) came to us. I said, ""May Allah's mercy be upon you, Abū al-Sāʾib. My testimony regarding you is that Allah has honored you."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""And what makes you know that Allah has honored him?"" I said, ""I do not know, may my father and mother be your ransom."" The Prophet (ﷺ) said, ""As for ʿUthmān, certainty [i.e., death] has come to him, and I hope good for him. By Allah, I do not know, and I am the Messenger of Allah, what will be done with me."" She said: By Allah, I never declared anyone pure after that, and that grieved me. So I slept and saw for ʿUthmān a flowing spring. I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and informed him, and he said, ""That is his deeds.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān. [Authentic. Preceded before it.]"
7192,"(7191) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, Maʿmar narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from Khārijah ibn Zayd, who said: Umm al-ʿAlāʾ al-Anṣāriyyah used to say… and he mentioned the meaning of this ḥadīth, except that he said, ""What will be done with him."" And Maʿmar added: I heard other than al-Zuhrī say: ""The Muslims disliked what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said about ʿUthmān, until the daughter of the Prophet (ﷺ) passed away, and he said, 'Join our predecessor ʿUthmān ibn Maʿẓūn.'"" [Authentic. Muslim narrated it.]"
7193,"(7192) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Kaysān narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me, Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Kaysān narrated to us, from Abū Ḥāzim, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) visited the grave of his mother and wept, and caused those around him to weep. He said, ""I sought permission from my Lord to seek forgiveness for her, but He did not grant me permission. I sought permission from Him to visit her grave, and He granted me permission. So visit the graves, for they remind you of death.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Authentic. Muslim]"
7194,"(7193) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīb ibn Milḥān narrated to us, ʿAmr — and he is ibn Khālid — narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, from Zubayd, from Muḥārib ibn Dithār, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on a journey and stopped at a place, and we were with him, close to a thousand riders. He stood and prayed two rakʿahs, then turned to us, his eyes flowing with tears. ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) stood before him, offering his father and mother as ransom for him, and said, ""What is the matter with you, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said, ""I sought permission from my Lord to seek forgiveness for my mother, but He did not grant me permission, so I wept for her out of mercy from the Fire. I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them. I had forbidden you from keeping the meat of the sacrificial animals beyond three days, so eat and keep as you wish. I had forbidden you from drinking from certain vessels, so drink from any vessel you wish, but do not drink intoxicants.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, from Zuhayr, without the story of his mother. [Authentic. Aḥmad]"
7195,"(7194) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us, Abū Qutaybah Salm ibn al-Faḍl al-Adamī narrated to us in Makkah, Abū Shuʿayb ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Wāqid al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us, Zuhayr narrated to us, Zubayd ibn al-Ḥārith al-Yāmī narrated to us, and he mentioned it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said: ""So visit them, for their visit will increase you in good."" And Maʿrif ibn Wāṣil narrated it from Muḥārib ibn Dithār, and he said in the ḥadīth: ""So visit them, for in their visit there is a reminder."" [Authentic. Preceded before it.]"
7196,"(7195) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to it, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Yūnus narrated to us, Maʿrif ibn Wāṣil narrated to us, and he mentioned it in abridged form, only regarding the prohibition of visiting graves and the permission therein. [Authentic. Abū Dāwūd narrated it.]"
7197,"(7196) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us. (Ḥ) And he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us. Both said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed us. He said: Usāmah ibn Zayd informed me that Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabān al-Anṣārī informed him that Wāsiʿ ibn Ḥabān narrated to him that Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them, for indeed in them is a lesson. And I had forbidden you from nabīdh (fermented drinks), so prepare nabīdh, but I do not permit anything intoxicating. And I had forbidden you from the meat of sacrificial animals, so eat and store it.""

[Ḥasan. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7198,"(7197) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Ibn Wahb informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me from Ayyūb ibn Hānīʾ from Masrūq ibn al-Ajdāʿ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, I had forbidden you from visiting graves, from eating the meat of sacrificial animals beyond three days, and from nabīdh in vessels. So visit graves, for they induce detachment from this world and remind of the Hereafter. And eat the meat of sacrificial animals and keep as much as you wish. I had only forbidden you from it at a time of scarcity, but Allah has granted abundance to people. Indeed, a vessel does not make anything ḥarām, but every intoxicant is ḥarām.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7199,"(7198) Abū al-Qāsim Zayd ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-ʿAlawī informed us in Kūfah: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayd narrated to us: Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated to us: Ibrāhīm, meaning Ibn Ṭahmān, narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿĀmir and ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us from Anas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade—and he mentioned the meat of sacrificial animals, vessels, and visiting graves—then he mentioned his permission regarding them at length. He said: ""I had forbidden you from visiting graves, then it occurred to me, so visit them, for they soften the heart, cause the eyes to weep, and remind of the So visit, but do not speak ill.""

Yaḥyā ibn al-Ḥārith also narrated it from ʿAmr.

And his statement ""but do not speak ill"" has been narrated in the ḥadīth of Mālik from Rabīʿah ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""And I had forbidden you from visiting graves, so visit them, but do not speak ill."" However, it is mursal, as Rabīʿah did not meet Abū Saʿīd.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7200,"(7199) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us among others. They said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy on him, informed us: Mālik informed us, and he mentioned it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7201,"(7200) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Shādhān informed us in Baghdād: Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Mūsā ibn al-Ḥasan informed us. Both said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr narrated to us: Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated to us from Ḥafṣah from Umm ʿAṭiyyah, who said: ""We were forbidden from following funeral processions, but it was not made obligatory upon us.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from two routes from Hishām.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7202,"(7201) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Ayyūb al-Ṭūsī narrated to us: Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us: Isrāʾīl informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Aswad ibn ʿĀmir narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Salmān from Dīnār Abī ʿUmar from Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet (ﷺ) went out for a funeral and saw women sitting. He said: ""What keeps you seated?"" They said: ""The funeral."" He said: ""Will you carry it among those who carry it?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Will you lower it into the grave among those who lower it?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Will you wash the deceased among those who wash?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""Then return, being sinful and not rewarded."" In the ḥadīh of al-Rūdhbārī, it is ""mawzūrāt"" (burdened with sin).

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7203,"(7202) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr narrated to us from Isrāʾīl, and he mentioned it with its isnād: that the Prophet (ﷺ) passed by women and said: ""What is the matter with you?"" They said: ""We are waiting for the funeral."" He mentioned the ḥadīth, except that he said: ""Will you throw earth among those who throw it?"" They said: ""No."" And washing was not mentioned.

[Ḍaʿīf. Previously referenced.]"
7204,"(7203) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Anas al-Qurashī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: Ḥaywah ibn Sharīḥ narrated to us. He said: Rabīʿah ibn Sayf al-Maʿāfirī narrated to me from Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥublī from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he saw his daughter Fāṭimah, may Allah be pleased with her, and said to her: ""Where have you come from, O Fāṭimah?"" She said: ""I have come from behind the funeral of this man."" He said: ""Did you reach the graveyard with them?"" She said: ""No, and how could I reach it when I have heard from you what I have heard?"" The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""By the One in whose Hand is my soul, had you reached it with them, you would not have seen Paradise until the grandfather of your father saw it.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Its grading has been previously mentioned.]"
7205,"(7204) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥasan ibn Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Shādhān informed us in Baghdād: Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dawrī narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl Abū Salamah narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Abī Salamah narrated to us from his father from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""May Allah curse the frequent visitors of graves.""

[Ḥasan in other than this chain. Narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
7206,"(7205) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ al-Qāḍī informed us in Kūfah: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim ibn Abī Ghurzah informed us: Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Khuthaym from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bahmān from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥassān from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the frequent visitors of graves.

[Ḥasan in other than this chain. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7207,"(7206) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Hammām and ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated from Muḥammad ibn Juḥāda from Abū Ṣāliḥ from Ibn ʿAbbās.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us, he said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us; Shuʿba narrated from Muḥammad ibn Juḥāda, he said: I heard Abū Ṣāliḥ — and he had grown old — from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the women who frequently visit graves, and those who take them as places of worship and light them with lamps.

This is the wording of Shuʿba's ḥadīth, and in the narration of the two of them (Hammām and ʿAbd al-Wārith): ""those who frequently visit graves and those who take them as places of worship and light them with lamps.""

[Ḥasan for something else. Abū Dāwūd reported it.]"
7208,"(7207) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-Muthannā Muʿādh ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Minḥāl al-Ḍarīr narrated to us; Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us; Bisṭām ibn Muslim narrated from Abū al-Tayyāḥ Yazīd ibn Ḥumayd from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Mulayka: that ʿĀ'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) came one day from the graveyard. I said to her: ""O Mother of the Believers, from where have you come?"" She said: ""From the grave of my brother ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr."" I said to her: ""Did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) not forbid visiting graves?"" She said: ""Yes, he forbade it, then he commanded that they be visited.""

Bisṭām ibn Muslim al-Baṣrī is the only one who narrated it, and Allah knows best.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ḥākim reported it.]"
7209,"(7208) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Ḥumayd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥāmid al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Ṭabarān; ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Abū Muṣʿab al-Zuhrī narrated to me; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Fudayk informed me; Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn from his father: that Fāṭima, the daughter of the Prophet (ﷺ), used to visit the grave of her uncle Ḥamza every Friday, and she would pray and weep there. This is what he said.

It has also been narrated from him from Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd from his father from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father, without mentioning ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn from his father in it, and it is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected).

And we have narrated in the established ḥadīth from Anas ibn Mālik: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by a woman at a grave who was weeping, and he said to her: ""Fear Allah and be patient.""

There is no mention in this report that he forbade her from going out to the graveyard, and in this there is corroboration for what we have narrated from ʿĀ'isha (may Allah be pleased with her).

However, the most authentic narration reported explicitly in this regard is the ḥadīth of Umm ʿAṭiyya and the reports that agree with it. Had women refrained from following funeral processions, going out to graveyards, and visiting graves, it would have been more protective of their religion. And with Allah lies success.

[Ḍaʿīf. Al-Ḥākim reported it.]"
7210,"(7209) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to me; Ṣāliḥ ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb al-Muqābirī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me; al-ʿAlā' narrated from his father from Abū Hurayra: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to the graveyard and said: ""Peace be upon you, O people of the abode of a believing people. And indeed, if Allah wills, we shall join you. I wish that we had seen our brothers."" They said: ""Are we not your brothers, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Rather, you are my Companions. My brothers are those who have not yet come."" They said: ""How will you recognize those who have not yet come from your Ummah, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Tell me, if a man has horses with white foreheads and white markings among black horses, would he not recognize his horses?"" They said: ""Yes, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""They shall come with white foreheads and limbs from the traces of wuḍū', and I shall be preceding them at the Ḥawḍ. Beware! Men will be driven away from my Ḥawḍ just as a stray camel is driven away. I shall call out to them: 'Come here!' But it will be said: 'They changed after you.' So I shall say: 'Away! Away!'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb and others.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim reported it.]"
7211,"(7210) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri' informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar al-Madanī narrated from Sharīk ibn Abī Namir from ʿAṭā' ibn Yasār from ʿĀ'isha (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: Whenever it was her night with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he would go out at the end of the night to al-Baqīʿ and say: ""Peace be upon you, O people of the abode of a believing people. What you have been promised will come to you tomorrow, deferred. And indeed, if Allah wills, we shall join you. O Allah, forgive the people of Baqīʿ al-Gharqad.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim reported it.]"
7212,"(7211) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me; my father narrated to us; Ḥajjāj informed us; Ibn Jurayj reported to me, he said: ʿAbdullāh — a man from Quraysh — narrated to me that he heard Muḥammad ibn Qays ibn Makhrama ibn al-Muṭṭalib say one day: ""Shall I not narrate to you about myself and my mother?"" We thought he meant his birth mother. He said: ʿĀ'isha (may Allah be pleased with her) said: ""Shall I not narrate to you about myself and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?"" I said: ""Yes."" Then she mentioned the ḥadīth about his going out to al-Baqīʿ and his return. She said: ""What should I say, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Say: 'Peace be upon the people of the abode from among the believers and the Muslims. May Allah have mercy on those of us who have gone ahead and those who come later. And indeed, if Allah wills, we shall join you.'""

Muslim reported it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Wahb and Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad from Ibn Jurayj.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim reported it.]"
7213,"(7212) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī said: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us, he said: It was recited to Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar while I was listening. He said: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī informed us; Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from ʿAlqama ibn Murthad from Sulaymān ibn Burayda from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to teach them when they entered the graveyards, and one of them would say: ""Peace be upon you, O people of the abode from among the believers and the Muslims. Indeed, if Allah wills, we shall join you. We ask Allah for well-being for us and for you.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb and others from Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Asadī al-Zubayrī. And Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Firyābī narrated it from al-Thawrī and added something to it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7214,"(7213) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated it to us by dictation; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAlqama ibn Murthad from Ibn Burayda from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to teach them when they went out to the graveyards: ""Peace be upon you, O people of the abome from among the believers and the Muslims. And indeed, if Allah wills, we shall join you. You are our predecessors and we are your followers. We ask Allah for well-being for us and for you."" And similarly Shuʿba narrated it from ʿAlqama.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. It was reported previously.]"
7215,"(7214) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq in Naysābūr and Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq in Baghdād said: Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn ʀāṣim informed us; Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salama narrated to us; Qutayba ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz — meaning Ibn Muḥammad — narrated from Suhayl from his father from Abū Hurayra: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""That one of you sit upon a burning coal or fire which burns his clothes until it reaches him is better for him than sitting upon a grave.""

And in the narration of ʿAlī: ""That one of you sit upon a burning coal which burns his clothes until it reaches his skin is better for him than sitting upon a grave."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutayba ibn Saʿīd.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim reported it.]"
7216,"(7215) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazyad al-Bayrūtī narrated to me; my father narrated to us; Ibn Jābir narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ, the son of the daughter of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr, informed us; my grandfather narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salama narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Mūsā al-Anṣārī and Dāwūd ibn Mikhraq al-Fāryābī said: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, he said: I heard ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd ibn Jābir say: Busr ibn ʿUbaydullāh al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to me that he heard Wāthila ibn al-Asqaʿ al-Laythī say: I heard Abū Marthad al-Ghanawī say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Do not sit upon graves, and do not pray facing them.""

The wording of both their ḥadīths is the same, except that in the narration of Ibn Mazyad from Wāthila ibn al-Asqaʿ, he said: Abū Marthad al-Ghanawī narrated to me. Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. And Ibn al-Mubārak narrated it from Ibn Jābir from Busr from Abī Idrīs al-Khawlānī from Wāthila, and it has already been mentioned in the book of Ṣalāh. And we have narrated from Ibn Masʿūd and Ibn ʿUmar regarding the dislike of that and the severity concerning it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim reported it.]"
7217,"(7216) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Sahl — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Nahwi — narrated to us: Abu Qilabah — narrated to us: Abu 'Asim — narrated to us: al-Aswad ibn Shayban — narrated to me: Khalid ibn Sumayr — narrated to me: Bashir ibn Nahik, who said: Bashir, the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — whose name in the Jahiliyyah was Zahm ibn Ma'bad — narrated to me. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""What is your name?"" He said: ""Zahm ibn Ma'bad."" He ﷺ said: ""Rather, you are Bashir."" So his name became Bashir. He said: ""While I was walking alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he said: 'O son of al-Khasasiyyah, you have not risen in the morning except that you are venting your anger against Allah, walking alongside the Messenger of Allah ﷺ.'"" I said: ""I bear no grudge against Allah for anything; every good that has been done to me is from Allah."" Then he ﷺ came upon the graves of the polytheists and said: ""These people have been preceded by abundant good"" — three times. Then he ﷺ came upon the graves of the Muslims and said: ""These people have attained abundant good"" — three times. While he ﷺ was walking, he glanced and saw a man walking among the graves wearing two sandals. He ﷺ said: ""O owner of the two sandals — woe to you! Cast off your two sandals!"" The man looked, and when he recognized the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he removed his sandals and threw them away.

This is a hadith that has been narrated by a group from al-Aswad ibn Shayban, and it is not known except through this chain of narration. And it is established from Anas ibn Malik from the Prophet ﷺ. [Sahih. Abu Dawud narrated it.]"
7218,"(7217) Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Fadl — al-Hasan ibn Ya'qub al-'Adl — narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib — narrated to us: Abd al-Wahhab, meaning ibn Ata' — from Sa'id — from Qatadah — from Anas ibn Malik, that the Prophet of Allah ﷺ said: ""When a servant is placed in his grave and his companions turn away from him, he truly hears the striking of their sandals. Two angels come to him and say: 'What did you use to say about this man?' — meaning Muhammad ﷺ. As for the believer, he says: 'I bear witness that he is the servant of Allah and His Messenger.' It is said to him: 'Look at your seat in the Fire; Allah has replaced it for you with a seat in Paradise.' And he sees them both.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Amr ibn Zurarah from Abd al-Wahhab, and al-Bukhari narrated it from another route from Sa'id ibn Abi 'Urabah. It is possible that the Prophet ﷺ saw filth on his sandals and therefore ordered him to remove them for that reason, and it is also possible that it was for another reason. And Allah knows best. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
7219,"(7218) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us: Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan — narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi — narrated to us: Abd Allah ibn Maslamah.

(And Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr — narrated to us: Abu Dawud — narrated to us: al-Qa'nabi — from Malik — from Ibn Shihab — from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib — from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""May Allah fight the Jews — they took the graves of their prophets as places of prostration."")

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Malik. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
7220,"(7219) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu Muhammad — Ahmad ibn Abd Allah al-Muzani — informed us: Ali ibn Muhammad ibn 'Isa — informed us: Abu al-Yaman — informed me: Shu'ayb — from al-Zuhri, who said: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah informed me that 'A'ishah and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: When the final illness befell the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, he began to throw a garment over his face. When it distressed him, he would remove it from his face, and while he was in that state, he said: ""The curse of Allah be upon the Jews and the Christians — they took the graves of their prophets as places of prostration."" He was warning against the like of what they had done.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu al-Yaman, and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Yunus from al-Zuhri. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
7221,"(7220) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz, Abu Tahir al-Imam, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzakki, and Abu al-'Abbas — Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hammak al-Shadhyakhi, and Abu Sa'id — Muhammad ibn Musa ibn al-Fadl — all said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us: Muhammad ibn Abd Allah ibn Abd al-Hakam — informed us: Anas ibn Iyad — from Hisham ibn 'Urwah — from his father — from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: During the illness of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, some of his wives mentioned a church in the land of Abyssinia called Mariyyah. Umm Salamah and Umm Habibah, may Allah be pleased with them both, had been to the land of Abyssinia, and they mentioned its beauty and its images. The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Those people — when a righteous man was among them, they would build a place of prostration over his grave and then fashion those images within it. Those are the worst of creation in the sight of Allah.""

Al-Bukhari and Muslim both narrated it in their Sahihs from various routes from Hisham ibn 'Urwah. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
7222,"(7221) Abu Zakariyya — Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya, may Allah have mercy on him — narrated to us: Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf al-Hafiz — narrated to us: Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya — narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Yunus — narrated to us: 'Asim ibn Muhammad, meaning ibn Zayd ibn Abd Allah ibn 'Umar — said: I heard my father narrating from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Islam is built upon five: the testimony that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, the establishment of Salah, the giving of Zakah, the Hajj of the House, and the fasting of Ramadan."" [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
7223,"(7222) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me: Abu Bakr al-Warraq — informed us: al-Hasan ibn Sufyan — narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh — narrated to us: my father — narrated to us: 'Asim ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd — from his father, who said: 'Abd Allah said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said — and he mentioned it with the like of it. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Ubayd Allah ibn Mu'adh. [Sahih. Previously mentioned.]"
7224,"(7223) Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hafiz — informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah — Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Musa al-Razi in Bukhara — informed us: Muhammad ibn Ayyub — informed us: Ali al-Madini — narrated to us: Hashim ibn al-Qasim — narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Dinar — from his father — from Abu Salih al-Samman — from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whomever Allah grants wealth and he does not pay its Zakah, his wealth will be represented for him on the Day of Resurrection as a bald serpent with two fangs, which will be placed around his neck on the Day of Resurrection. Then it will seize him by his two jawbones — meaning his two cheeks — and will say: 'I am your wealth, I am your treasure.'"" Then he ﷺ recited this verse: ""Do not think that those who withhold what Allah has given them of His bounty is good for them; rather, it is evil for them. They will be encircled by that which they withheld on the Day of Resurrection.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Ali al-Madini, and Malik narrated it from Abd Allah ibn Dinar as a mawquf narration. And it has been narrated from Ibn Mas'ud from the Prophet ﷺ as a marfu' narration. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari.]"
7225,"(7224) Abu Bakr — Ahmad ibn al-Hasan, Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq, and others — said: Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman — informed us: al-Shafi'i — informed us: Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah — heard Jami' ibn Abi Rashid and 'Abd al-Malik ibn A'yan — both of them heard Abu Wa'il — narrating from 'Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""No man who does not pay the Zakah of his wealth will be represented on the Day of Resurrection except by a bald serpent fleeing from it while it pursues him, until it is placed around his neck."" Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ recited to us: ""They will be encircled by that which they withheld on the Day of Resurrection."" [Sahih. Al-Tirmidhi narrated it.]"
7226,"(7225) Muhammad ibn Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf — informed me: my father, and Yahya ibn Mansur al-Harawi — both said: Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib al-Umawi — narrated to us: 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn al-Mukhtar — narrated to us: Suhayl — from his father — from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""No owner of a treasure who does not pay its Zakah will be spared except that it will be heated in the Fire of Jahannam and made into plates, with which his sides and his forehead will be branded, until Allah judges between His servants on a day the measure of which is fifty thousand years by your reckoning. Then his way will be shown to him — either to Paradise or to the Fire. And no owner of camels who does not pay their Zakah will be spared except that he will be laid out on a flat, level ground, and they will be driven over him — the most driven of them, and none of them will be exempt. Whenever the last of them passes over him, the first of them will be returned to him, until Allah judges between His servants on a day the measure of which is fifty thousand years by your reckoning. Then his way will be shown to him — either to Paradise or to the Fire. And no owner of sheep who does not pay their Zakah will be spared except that they will be laid out on a flat, level ground, and they will trample him with their hooves and gore him with their horns — none of them being hornless or broken-horned. Whenever the last of them passes over him, the first of them will be returned to him, until Allah judges between His servants on a day the measure of which is fifty thousand years by your reckoning. Then his way will be shown to him — either to Paradise or to the Fire.""

Suhayl said: ""I do not know whether he mentioned cattle or not."" They said: ""What about horses, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ said: ""Horses have goodness in their forelocks until the Day of Resurrection"" — or he said: ""Horses are tied in their forelocks with goodness until the Day of Resurrection."" Suhayl said: ""I have some doubt. Horses are three: for one man they are a reward, for another man they are a shield, and upon another man they are a burden. As for those that are a reward for him — a man who takes them for the sake of Allah and prepares them for Him; nothing disappears into their bellies except that Allah writes a reward for him thereby. Even if he pastures them in a meadow, whatever they eat — Allah will write a reward for him thereby. And if he gives them to drink from a river, for every drop that disappears into their bellies he will have a reward — even mentioning reward in their dung and their offspring. And if they gallop up a height or two heights, for every step they take, he will have a reward. As for those that are a shield for him — a man who takes them for dignity and adornment, and does not forget the right of Allah upon their backs and in their bellies, in their hardship and their ease. And as for those upon which there is a burden — that is the one who takes them out of pride, arrogance, and showing off before the people. That is the one upon whom there is a burden.""

They said: ""What about donkeys, O Messenger of Allah?"" He ﷺ said: ""Allah has not revealed to me anything regarding them except this comprehensive, decisive verse: 'Whoever does an atom's weight of good will see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil will see it.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Abi al-Shawarib. And Hafs ibn MaySarah and Hisham ibn Sa'd narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam from Abu Salih, who heard Abu Hurayrah saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""No owner of gold or silver who does not pay its right"" — and he mentioned it, then mentioned camels, then mentioned cattle and sheep. [Sahih. Muslim.]"
7227,"(7226) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: al-Firyābī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from al-Aʿmash, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Murrah, from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: ""The one who retracts his charity is cursed upon the tongue of Muḥammad (ﷺ) on the Day of Resurrection.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Sufyān. And in the narration of Ibn Numayr, it is: ""from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith."" [Weak. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
7228,"(7227) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from ʿĀmir al-ʿUqaylī, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I was shown the first three who will enter Paradise and the first three who will enter the Fire. As for the first three who will enter Paradise: the martyr; a slave who fulfils the right of Allah and is sincere to his master; and a poor, self-restrained man with dependents. As for the first three who will enter the Fire: a tyrannical ruler; a wealthy man who does not give the right of his wealth; and a boastful poor man."" [Weak. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7229,"(7228) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us: Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us: Abū ʿAwanah narrated to us, from al-Suddī, from Abī Ṣāliḥ, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding His (Exalted is He) statement ""al-māʿūn"": He said: ""It is the obligatory zakāh."" This statement has also been narrated to us from Ibn ʿUmar and Anas ibn Mālik, and it is one of the two narrations from Ibn ʿAbbās, and it is the view of Abū al-ʿĀliyah, al-Ḥasan, and Mujāhid. [Ḥasan in light of corroborating narrations. Narrated by al-Ṭabarī.]"
7230,"(7229) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad Dalaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijistānī in Baghdād narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Zayd al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Shabīb narrated to us: My father narrated to us, from Yūnus, from Ibn Shihāb, from Khālid ibn Aslam — and he was the brother of Zayd ibn Aslam — who said: We went out walking with ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. A Bedouin caught up with us and said: ""Are you ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""I have asked about you and was directed to you, so tell me: Does the paternal aunt inherit?"" Ibn ʿUmar said: ""I do not know."" He said: ""You are Ibn ʿUmar and you do not know?"" And he said on another occasion: ""You do not know and we do not know?"" He said: ""Yes, go to the scholars of Madīnah and ask them."" When he turned away, Ibn ʿUmar kissed his hands and said: ""Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān has spoken well — he is asked about what he does not know, so he says: 'I do not know.'"" The Bedouin said: ""Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, says: 'And those who hoard gold and silver…'"" Ibn ʿUmar said: ""Whoever hoards them and does not pay their zakāh, woe to him. This was only before the zakāh was revealed. When it was revealed, Allah made it a means of purifying wealth."" Then he turned to me and said: ""I would not care if I had gold the size of Mount Uḥud, knowing its quantity, paying its zakāh, and using it in obedience to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ in an abbreviated form, and he said: ""And Aḥmad ibn Shabīb narrated…"" and he repeated it in al-Tafsīr from Aḥmad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7231,"(7230) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Any wealth whose zakāh has been paid — even if it were beneath seven earths — is not a hoard (kanz). And any wealth whose zakāh is not paid is a hoard (kanz), even if it were visible on the surface of the earth.""

This is the correct version, as a mawqūf narration. Likewise, a group narrated it from Nāfiʿ, and a group from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar. And it was narrated by Suwayd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz — who is not strong — from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, as a marfūʿ narration traced back to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarī.]"
7232,"(7231) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal narrated to us: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān Aḥmad ibn ʿUthmān al-Nasawī narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us: Suwayd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, mentioning it with the same meaning, as a marfūʿ narration. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7233,"(7232) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, who said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar being asked about the hoard (kanz), and he said: ""It is wealth from which the zakāh is not paid."" This is the correct version, as a mawqūf narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7234,"(7233) Abū Ḥāzim al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-ʿAdl informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān ibn ʀmir narrated to us, selected by Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Rāzī: Hārūn ibn Ziyād al-Miṣṣīṣī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us, from Sufyān, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Any wealth whose zakāh has been paid is not a hoard (kanz), even if it were buried beneath the earth. And any wealth whose zakāh is not paid is a hoard (kanz), even if it were visible.""

This is not preserved (maḥfūẓ). Rather, the well-known narration from Sufyān is from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, as a mawqūf narration. [Munkar.]"
7235,"(7234) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū ʿUtbah Aḥmad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd ibn Kathīr ibn Dīnār narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Muhājir narrated to us: Thābit ibn ʿAjlān narrated to us: ʿAṭāʾ narrated to us, from Umm Salamah: She used to wear gold anklets and asked the Prophet (ﷺ) about that, saying: ""Is it a hoard (kanz)?"" He said: ""When you have paid its zakāh, it is not a hoard (kanz)."" [Weak. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7236,"(7235) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī in Baghdād informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Tarqafī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿlā ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to us: My father narrated to us: Ghaylān — meaning Ibn Jāmiʿ — narrated to us, from ʿUthmān Abī al-Yaqẓān, from Jaʿfar ibn Iyās, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: When the verse ""And those who hoard gold and silver"" was revealed, this weighed heavily upon the Muslims, and they said: ""None of us has the ability to leave wealth for his children that will remain after him."" ʿUmar said: ""I will relieve you of this."" They said: ""So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) set out, accompanied by Thawbān, and they came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: 'O Prophet of Allah, this verse has weighed heavily upon your Companions.' The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, only prescribed the zakāh to purify thereby the remainder of your wealth, and He only prescribed inheritance for wealth that remains after you.'"" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Allāhu Akbar!"" Then he said: ""Shall I not inform you of the best treasure a person can hoard? A righteous woman: when he looks at her, she delights him; when he commands her, she obeys him; and when he is absent, she guards his honour and his wealth."" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7237,"(7236) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah al-Shaybānī narrated to us in Kūfah; Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Zuhrī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿlā ibn al-Ḥārith al-Muḥāribī narrated it with the same chain of narration, and some of the narrators shortened it from Yaḥyā, so they did not mention ʿUthmān, Abū al-Yaqẓān, in its chain. [Weak. Preceded it.]"
7238,"(7237) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥarbī narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Wuhaib narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd ibn Ḥayyān — meaning al-Taymī — narrated from Abū Zurʿah, from Abū Hurayrah: that a Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, direct me to a deed which, if I perform it, I will enter Paradise."" He ﷺ said: ""You worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, you establish the prayer — meaning the obligatory one — you give the obligatory Zakāh, and you fast Ramaḍān."" He said: ""By the One Who sent you with the truth, I will not add anything to this."" When he turned to leave, the Prophet ﷺ said: ""Whoever wishes to look at a man from the people of Paradise, let him look at this one."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm from ʿAffān, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī from ʿAffān.

And the ḥadīth of Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbaydillāh regarding the story of the Bedouin has already passed in the Book of Prayer. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it.]"
7239,"(7238) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muhājir narrated to us; Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj informed me, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""When you have paid the Zakāh of your wealth, you have removed its evil from you.""

Thus did Ibn Wahb narrate it with this chain as a marfūʿ (elevated) narration, and likewise Yūnus ibn ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated it from Ibn Wahb. And ʿĪsā ibn Masrūd narrated it from Ibn Wahb as a statement of Abū al-Zubayr (mawqūf). [Weak. Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
7240,"(7239) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn Nujayd informed us; Abū Muslim informed us; Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj; Abū al-Zubayr informed me that he heard Jābir saying: ""When you have paid the Zakāh of your treasure, its evil has gone."" He narrated it as a mawqūf (stopped) narration, and this is more authentic. It has also been narrated with another chain as a marfūʿ (elevated) narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah narrated it.]"
7241,"(7240) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith, from Darrāj Abū al-Samḥ, from Ibn Ḥujayrah al-Akbar al-Khawlānī, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When you have paid the Zakāh, you have fulfilled what was obligatory upon you. And whoever accumulates ḥarām wealth and then gives it in charity, he shall have no reward for it, and its sin shall remain upon him."" [Weak. Al-Tirmidhī narrated it.]"
7242,"(7241) And among what Abū Dāwūd mentioned in al-Marāsīl, from Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ, from Hushaym, from ʿUdhāfir al-Baṣrī, from al-Ḥasan, from the Prophet ﷺ as a mursal narration: ""Whoever pays the Zakāh of his wealth has fulfilled the right that was upon him, and whoever gives more, it is better for him.""

Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad narrated it to us; Abū al-Ḥusayn informed us; al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated it, and he mentioned it. [Weak. Abū Dāwūd.]"
7243,"(7242) As for the ḥadīth which Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us; Shādhān narrated to us; Sharīk narrated to us, from Abū Ḥamzah, from ʿĀmir, from Fāṭimah bint Qays, that she asked the Prophet ﷺ — or she said: he was asked — about this verse: {And in their wealth is a known right} (al-Maʿārij:24). He ﷺ said: ""In this wealth there is a right other than Zakāh."" And he recited this verse: {Righteousness is not that you turn your faces toward the east or the west, but righteousness is in one who believes in Allah, the Last Day, the angels, the Book, and the prophets; and gives wealth, in spite of love for it, to relatives, orphans, the needy, the traveler, those who ask, and for freeing slaves; and establishes prayer and gives Zakāh} (al-Baqarah:177).

This is a ḥadīth known through Abū Ḥamzah Maymūn al-Aʿwar, a Kūfī, and Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, and those after them among the ḥadīth critics have declared him weak.

And what our colleagues narrate in the taʿlīqāt is: ""In wealth there is no right other than Zakāh."" I do not preserve a chain for it. And what I have narrated regarding its meaning is what I have mentioned previously. And Allah knows best. [Weak. Al-Tirmidhī narrated it.]"
7244,"(7243) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — among the last group they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrānjānī informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ṣaʿṣaʿah al-Māzinī, from his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāq of silver, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five dhawd of camels, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five awsuq of dates.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī.]"
7245,"(7244) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us; Saʿdān ibn Naṣr al-Makhramī narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah ibn Abī Ḥasan al-Māzinī, from his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāq, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five dhawd."" Sufyān said: ""Al-Waqiyyah is forty dirhams.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah, and he mentioned the awsuq along with them. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded it.]"
7246,"(7245) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us — among the last group they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdaws narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah ibn Qanʿab al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us; he said: I read to Mālik ibn Anas, from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā al-Māzinī, from his father, who said: I heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five dhawd, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāq, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five awsuq.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded it.]"
7247,"(7246) Abu ʿAlī — al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī — informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab informed us in Wāsiṭ: Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Sahl ibn ʿUthmān informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī informed me: my father informed me: Thumāmah ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed me: Anas ibn Mālik informed me that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), when he was appointed as khalīfah, dispatched Anas ibn Mālik to al-Baḥrayn and wrote him a letter: ""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is the obligatory ṣadaqah which the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah commanded His Messenger (peace be upon him). Whoever is asked for it among the believers in its proper form, let him give it; and whoever is asked for more than that, let him not give it.

In twenty-four camels and below, sheep [are due]: for every five, one sheep. When they reach twenty-five to thirty-five, there is due a bint makhāḍ (a one-year-old she-camel). If a bint makhāḍ is not available, then a ibn labūn (a two-year-old male camel). When they reach thirty-six to forty-five, there is due a bint labūn (a two-year-old she-camel). When they reach forty-six to sixty, there is due a ḥiqqah (a three-year-old she-camel) ṭarūqat al-jamal (one that has borne a young). When they reach sixty-one to seventy-five, there is due a jadʿah (a four-year-old she-camel). When they reach seventy-six to ninety, there is due two bint labūn. When they reach ninety-one to one hundred and twenty, there is due two ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-jamal. When they exceed one hundred and twenty, then in every forty, a bint labūn, and in every fifty, a ḥiqqah.

Whoever has only four camels, there is nothing due on them unless their owner wills it. When they reach five camels, one sheep is due.

He said: Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a jadʿah but does not possess a jadʿah, though he possesses a ḥiqqah, it shall be accepted from him, and two sheep shall be added if available, or twenty dirhams. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a ḥiqqah but does not possess a ḥiqqah, though he possesses a jadʿah, the jadʿah shall be accepted from him, and the collector shall give him twenty dirhams or two sheep. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a ḥiqqah but possesses only a bint labūn, the bint labūn shall be accepted from him, and two sheep or twenty dirhams shall be given with it. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a bint labūn but does not possess a bint labūn, though he possesses a ḥiqqah, the ḥiqqah shall be accepted from him, and the collector shall give him twenty dirhams or two sheep. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a bint labūn but does not possess a bint labūn, though he possesses a bint makhāḍ, the bint makhāḍ shall be accepted from him, and twenty dirhams or two sheep shall be given with it.

And the ṣadaqah of sheep that graze freely: when they are forty to one hundred and twenty, one sheep is due. When they exceed one hundred and twenty up to two hundred, two sheep are due. When they exceed two hundred up to three hundred, three sheep are due. When the sheep exceed three hundred, then in every hundred, one sheep is due.

An old sheep shall not be taken in ṣadaqah, nor one with a defect, nor a barren one, unless the collector wills it. When a man's freely grazing sheep fall short of forty by one sheep, there is nothing due on them unless their owner wills it.

On silver, one-tenth of a quarter is due. If the wealth amounts to only ninety-one hundred [dirhams], there is nothing due on it unless the owner wills it.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Abū ʿAbd Allāh. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Muthannā al-Anṣārī, separately in two places. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7248,"(7247) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab informed us: Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī said: my father informed me from Thumāmah, from Anas, that Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him), when he was appointed as khalīfah, dispatched him to al-Baḥrayn and wrote him this letter, and sealed it with the seal of the Prophet (peace be upon him). The inscription of the seal consisted of three lines: one line ""Muḥammad,"" one line ""Rasūl,"" and one line ""Allāh.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Anṣārī. Then al-Bukhārī said: And Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal added to me from al-Anṣārī, and he mentioned the story of the seal. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited]"
7249,"(7248) Abū al-Ḥusayn — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad — informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī informed us: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad al-Mu'addib informed us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah said: I took this book from Thumāmah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Anas ibn Mālik, from Anas ibn Mālik, that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to him: ""These are the obligatory ṣadaqah dues which the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, commanded His Messenger. Whoever is asked for them among the Muslims in their proper form, let him give them; and whoever is asked for more than that, let him not give it.

In what is less than twenty-five camels, for every five, one sheep. When they reach twenty-five, a bint makhāḍ is due up to thirty-five. If a bint makhāḍ is not available, then a ibn labūn dhakar (a two-year-old male camel). When they reach thirty-six, a bint labūn is due up to forty-five. When they reach forty-six, a ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-faḥl (a three-year-old she-camel that has borne a young) is due up to sixty. When they reach sixty-one, a jadʿah is due up to seventy-five. When they reach seventy-six, two bint labūn are due up to ninety. When they reach ninety-one, two ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-faḥl are due up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed one hundred and twenty, then in every forty, a bint labūn, and in every fifty, a ḥiqqah.

When the ages of the camels and the ṣadaqah obligations differ: whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a jadʿah but does not possess a jadʿah, though he possesses a ḥiqqah, a ḥiqqah shall be accepted from him, and two sheep shall be added if available to him, or twenty dirhams. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a ḥiqqah but possesses only a jadʿah, it shall be accepted from him, and the collector shall give him twenty dirhams or two sheep. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a ḥiqqah but possesses only a bint labūn, it shall be accepted from him, and two sheep shall be added if available to him, or twenty dirhams. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a bint labūn but possesses only a ḥiqqah, it shall be accepted from him, and the collector shall give him twenty dirhams or two sheep. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a bint labūn but does not possess a bint labūn, though he possesses a bint makhāḍ, it shall be accepted from him, and two sheep shall be added if available, or twenty dirhams. Whoever reaches the ṣadaqah of a bint makhāḍ but possesses only an ibn labūn dhakar, it shall be accepted from him, and nothing shall be with it. Whoever has only four camels, there is nothing due on them unless their owner wills it.

And the ṣadaqah of sheep that graze freely: when they are forty, one sheep is due up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed that, two sheep are due up to two hundred. When they exceed that by one, three sheep are due up to three hundred. When they exceed that by one, then in every complete hundred, one sheep is due.

An old sheep shall not be taken in ṣadaqah, nor one with a defect, nor a barren sheep, unless the collector wills it. Separate flocks shall not be combined, nor shall a combined flock be separated, for fear of the ṣadaqah. And what is taken from two partners, they shall share equally between them.

When a man's freely grazing sheep fall short of forty by one sheep, there is nothing due on them unless their owner wills it.

On silver, one-tenth of a quarter is due. If the wealth amounts to only ninety-one hundred dirhams, there is nothing due on it unless the owner wills it.""

And al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl narrated from Ḥammād ibn Salamah, saying: We took this book from Thumāmah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Anas, who was narrating it from Anas ibn Mālik, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him).

And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān said: al-Shāfiʿī said: The ḥadīth of Anas is a firmly established ḥadīth from the narration of Ḥammād ibn Salamah and others, from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and by it we take.

And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ said regarding the ḥadīth of Ḥammād ibn Salamah and what precedes it: Its chain of narration is ṣaḥīḥ, and all of its narrators are trustworthy (thiqāt). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited]"
7250,"(7249) And Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqri' — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī informed us: Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh al-Mihrajānī informed me: Abū Sahl — Bishr ibn Aḥmad — informed us: Abū Yaʿlā informed us: Abū al-Rabīʿ informed us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd informed us: Ayyūb said: I saw in the possession of Thumāmah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Anas a book which Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq (may Allah be pleased with him) had written to Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with him) when he sent him for the ṣadaqah of al-Baḥrayn. Upon it was the seal of the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""Muḥammad, Rasūl of Allāh."" In it was a statement like this. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited]"
7251,"(7250) Meaning, similar to what Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqri' informed us: al-Ḥasan informed us: Yūsuf informed us: Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf informed me: Bishr ibn Aḥmad informed us: Abū Yaʿlā informed us: Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī informed us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd said: I heard Ayyūb, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sarrāj, and ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrating from Nāfiʿ, that he read the book of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), which stated: ""There is nothing due on less than five camels. When they reach five, one sheep is due up to nine. When there are ten, two sheep are due up to fourteen. When they reach fifteen, three are due up to nineteen. When they reach twenty, four are due up to twenty-four. When they reach twenty-five, a bint makhāḍ is due up to thirty-five. When they exceed that, a bint labūn is due up to forty-five. When they exceed that, a ḥiqqah is due up to sixty. When they exceed that, a jadʿah is due up to seventy-five. When they exceed that, two bint labūn are due up to ninety. When they exceed that, two ḥiqqah are due up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed that, then in every fifty, a ḥiqqah, and in every forty, a bint labūn.

There is nothing due on sheep until they reach forty. When they reach forty, one sheep is due up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed that, two sheep are due up to two hundred. When they exceed two hundred, three are due up to three hundred. When they exceed three hundred, then in every complete hundred, one sheep is due.""

And al-Thawrī narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from ʿUmar.

And Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, saying: This is a copy of the book of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Yaʿlā]"
7252,"(7251) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, along with others, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ informed us: Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah informed us: Nāfiʿ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar informed us, saying: This is the book of ṣadaqah dues, in which [it states]:

""In every twenty-four camels and below, sheep: for every five, one sheep. And in what exceeds that up to thirty-five, a bint makhāḍ. If a bint makhāḍ is not available, then an ibn labūn dhakar. And in what exceeds that up to forty-five, a bint labūn. And in what exceeds that up to sixty, a ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-faḥl. And in what exceeds that up to seventy-five, a jadʿah. And in what exceeds that up to ninety, two bint labūn. And in what exceeds that up to one hundred and twenty, two ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-faḥl. Whatever exceeds that, then in every forty, a bint labūn, and in every fifty, a ḥiqqah.

And in freely grazing sheep: when they are forty up to one hundred and twenty, one sheep. And in what exceeds that up to two hundred, two sheep. And in what exceeds that up to three hundred, three sheep. Whatever exceeds that, then in every hundred, one sheep.

An old sheep shall not be taken in ṣadaqah, nor one with a defect, nor a barren one, unless the collector wills it. Separate flocks shall not be combined, nor shall a combined flock be separated, for fear of the ṣadaqah. And what is taken from two partners, they shall share equally between them.

On silver, one-tenth of a quarter, when the silver of any of them reaches five awāq.""

This is a copy of the book of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), upon which he used to take [the ṣadaqah].

Al-Shāfiʿī said: By all of this we take.

And Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, from the Prophet (peace be upon him). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī]"
7253,"(7252) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Mu'ammal — informed us: al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad al-Shaʿrānī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī informed us: ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām informed us: Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn informed us: al-Zuhrī informed us: Sālim informed us: his father informed us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) wrote the book of ṣadaqah but had not yet sent it to his collectors when he passed away. It was then fastened to his sword. Abū Bakr acted upon it until he passed away. Then ʿUmar acted upon it until he passed away. In it was [the following]:

""In five camels, one sheep; in ten, two sheep; in fifteen, three sheep; in twenty, four sheep; in twenty-five, a bint makhāḍ up to thirty-five. When they exceed by one, a bint labūn up to forty-five. When they exceed by one, a ḥiqqah up to sixty. When they exceed by one, a jadʿah up to seventy-five. When they exceed by one, two bint labūn up to ninety. When they exceed by one, two ḥiqqah up to one hundred and twenty. If the camels are more than that, then in every fifty, a ḥiqqah, and in every forty, a bint labūn.

And in sheep: in every forty, one sheep up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed by one, two sheep up to two hundred. When they exceed two hundred, three sheep up to three hundred. If the sheep are more than that, then in every hundred, one sheep. And there is nothing due on them until they reach one hundred.

A combined flock shall not be separated, nor shall separate flocks be combined, for fear of the ṣadaqah. And what is taken from two partners, they shall share equally. An old sheep shall not be taken in ṣadaqah, nor one with a defect.""

Al-Zuhrī said: When the collector arrives, the sheep shall be divided into thirds: one-third poor, one-third good, and one-third medium. The collector shall take from the medium. Al-Zuhrī did not mention cattle. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other respects. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
7254,"(7253) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-Wāsiṭī informed us: Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn informed us, with its chain of narration and its meaning, saying: ""If a bint makhāḍ is not available, then an ibn labūn."" He did not mention the statement of al-Zuhrī.

Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī said in the book of ʿIlal: I asked Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī about this ḥadīth, and he said: ""I hope it is preserved (maḥfūẓ)."" And Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn is ṣadūq (truthful).

And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad — ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ — said: Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn was in agreement with this narration from Sālim, from his father, regarding the ḥadīth of ṣadaqah, by Sulaymān ibn Kathīr, the brother of Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. Ibn Ṣāʿid informed us from Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī, from Sulaymān, likewise. And a group narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, and stopped it (awqafūh). But Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn and Sulaymān ibn Kathīr raised it to the Prophet (peace be upon him). [Ṣaḥīḥ in other respects. Previously cited]"
7255,"(7254) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqri' — informed us, regarding the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Kathīr: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī informed us: Sulaymān ibn Kathīr informed us: al-Zuhrī informed us: Sālim informed us: his father informed us: from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: ""Sālim read to me a book which the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) had written"" — before Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, took him away — ""regarding the ṣadaqah."" I found in it:

""In five camels, one sheep; in ten, two sheep; in fifteen, three sheep; in twenty, four sheep; in twenty-five, a bint makhāḍ up to thirty-five. If a bint makhāḍ is not available, then an ibn labūn. When they reach thirty-six, a bint labūn up to forty-five. When they reach forty-six, a ḥiqqah up to sixty. When they reach sixty-one, a jadʿah up to seventy-five. When they exceed that, two bint labūn up to ninety. When they exceed that, two ḥiqqah up to one hundred and twenty. When the camels are numerous, then in every fifty, a ḥiqqah, and in every forty, a bint labūn.""

And I found in it: ""In forty sheep, one sheep up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed that, two sheep up to two hundred. When they exceed that, three sheep up to three hundred. Then in every hundred, one sheep.""

And I found in it: ""A combined flock shall not be separated, nor shall separate flocks be combined.""

And I found in it: ""A barren sheep, an old sheep, and one with a defect shall not be taken in ṣadaqah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other respects. Narrated by Ibn Mājah]"
7256,"(7255) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Sulamī — informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Mu'ammal informed us: al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyib informed us: al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī and Abū Naṣr — ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah — both informed us: Abū ʿAmr — Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Maṭar — informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Ṣūfī — informed us: al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah informed us: Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd informed me: al-Zuhrī informed me: Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm informed me: his father informed me: his grandfather informed me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) wrote to the people of Yemen a book containing the obligatory duties, the sunnahs, and the blood-money penalties, and sent it with ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm. It was read to the people of Yemen, and this is its copy:

""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muḥammad, the Prophet, to Shurayḥbīl ibn ʿAbd Kulāl, and Nuʿaym ibn ʿAbd Kulāl, and al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Kulāl — the people of Qayl Dhī Ruʿayn, Muʿāfir, and Ḥamdān. To proceed: Your Messenger has sent me to you, and you have given from the spoils the fifth of Allah, and what Allah has prescribed for the believers of the ʿushr (tenth) on agricultural land. What is watered by the sky, whether it is a grove or a cultivated field, the tenth is due when it reaches five awāq. What is watered by irrigation channels and waterwheels, half the tenth is due when it reaches five awāq.

And in every five freely grazing camels, one sheep, up to twenty-four. When they exceed twenty-four by one, a bint makhāḍ is due. If a bint makhāḍ is not found, then an ibn labūn dhakar, up to thirty-five. When they exceed thirty-five by one, a bint labūn is due up to forty-five. When they exceed forty-five by one, a ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-jamal is due up to sixty. When they exceed sixty by one, a jadʿah is due up to seventy-five. When they exceed seventy-five by one, two bint labūn are due up to ninety. When they exceed ninety by one, two ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-jamal are due up to one hundred and twenty. Whatever exceeds one hundred and twenty, then in every forty, a bint labūn, and in every fifty, a ḥiqqah ṭarūqat al-jamal.

And in every thirty cattle, a tabīʿ (a one-year-old male calf) jadʿ or jadʿah; and in every forty cattle, a cow. And in every forty freely grazing sheep, one sheep up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed one hundred and twenty by one, two sheep are due up to two hundred. When they exceed by one, three are due up to three hundred. When they exceed that, then in every hundred, one sheep.

An old sheep shall not be taken in ṣadaqah, nor an emaciated one, nor one with a defect, nor a barren sheep. Separate flocks shall not be combined, nor shall a combined flock be separated, for fear of the ṣadaqah. And what is taken from two partners, they shall share equally between them.

And in every five awāq of silver, five dirhams. And whatever exceeds that, in every forty dirhams, one dirham. And there is nothing due on less than five awāq. And in every forty dinārs, one dinār.

Know that ṣadaqah is not lawful for Muḥammad or for his household. Rather, it is the zakāh by which they purify themselves, and for the poor believers, and in the cause of Allah. And there is nothing due on a slave, or a cultivated land, or its workers, when they pay their ṣadaqah of the ʿushr. And there is nothing due on a Muslim slave or on his horse.""

Yaḥyā said: ""Excellent."" Then he said: The book also contained: ""The greatest of major sins with Allah on the Day of Resurrection is associating partners with Allah, killing a believing soul without right, fleeing on the day of battle in the cause of Allah, disobedience to parents, accusing a chaste woman, learning magic, consuming usury, and consuming the wealth of an orphan. And the ʿumrah is the lesser pilgrimage. And none shall touch the Qur'ān except one who is pure. And there is no divorce before consummation, and no emancipation until he purchases it. And let none of you pray in a single garment with nothing upon his shoulder. And let none of you wrap himself in a single garment with nothing between his private parts and the sky. And let none of you pray in a single garment with its side exposed. And let none of you pray with his hair bound.""

And the book contained: ""Whoever kills a believer deliberately upon proof, then he is subject to retaliatory punishment (qawd), unless the heirs of the slain are pleased. And for the soul, the blood-money is one hundred camels. And for the nose, if it is cut off entirely, the blood-money is due. And for the tongue, the blood-money is due. And for the two lips, the blood-money is due. And for the two testicles, the blood-money is due. And for the male organ, the blood-money is due. And for the back, the blood-money is due. And for the two eyes, the blood-money is due. And for one leg, half the blood-money. And for a wound to the head (ma'mūmah), one-third of the blood-money. And for a wound to the belly (jā'ifah), one-third of the blood-money. And for a wound that displaces a bone (munqilah), fifteen camels. And for every finger of the hands and feet, ten camels. And for a tooth, five camels. And for a wound to the face (muḍiḥah), five camels. And a man may be killed for [the killing of] a woman. And upon the people of gold [i.e., those whose blood-money is paid in gold], one thousand dinārs.""

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz saying: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal being asked about this ḥadīth of ṣadaqah which Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrates — is it ṣaḥīḥ? He said: ""I hope it is ṣaḥīḥ.""

He said: And I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz saying — and he narrated to us from al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā, from Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah, from Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd, from al-Zuhrī, the ḥadīth of ṣadaqah — he said: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal has narrated this ḥadīth in his Musnad from al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā, from Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah. Abū Aḥmad said: And Ṣadaqah ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated it from Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd, from the Syrians.

As for the ḥadīth of ṣadaqah, it has an origin in part of what Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, but he corrupted its chain of narration. And the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd has a sound chain of narration.

The shaykh said: And al-Khawlānī, Abū Zurʿah al-Rāzī, and Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī, and ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī, and a group of the ḥuffāẓ have praised Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd, and they narrated this ḥadīth which he narrated regarding the ṣadaqah with a connected, sound chain of narration. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Nasā'ī]"
7257,"(7256) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mashshāṭ both narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḍurays narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad al-Farwī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from al-Muthannā ibn Anas, from his father Anas ibn Mālik, from the Prophet ﷺ, similar to a document that was found in the scabbard of the sword of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding Zakāh, up to the section on slaves, and in it was stated: ""Between the two obligatory amounts, there are twenty dirhams or two sheep, each valued at ten dirhams, ten dirhams.""

This is the wording of Abū Naṣr. In the narration of al-Mashshāṭ, it is from al-Muthannā ibn Anas, from his father, from Anas ibn Mālik, and this is more likely, for al-Muthannā is ʿAbd Allāh ibn Anas, who was attributed to his grandfather. This is the narration that al-Shāfiʿī mentioned from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh.

And we have narrated the hadith from the narration of Thumāmah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Anas, from Anas, through authentic chains.

And we have narrated it from Sālim and Nāfiʿ, both connected (mawṣūl) and disconnected (mursal).

And from the narration of ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, connected. All of these strengthen one another, and with Allah is the success. [Weak]"
7258,"(7257) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us, Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Yūnus ibn Yazīd, from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ""This is a copy of the Book of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he wrote regarding Zakāh, and it is in the possession of the family of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb."" Ibn Shihāb said: ""Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar read it to me, and I memorized it thoroughly. It is the document that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz copied from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh when he was appointed over al-Madīnah, and he instructed his workers to act according to it. He wrote with it to al-Walīd ibn ʿAbd al-Malik, and al-Walīd instructed his workers to act according to it. Then the Caliphs continued to command accordingly after him. Then Hishām commanded it, and he sent a copy to every Muslim governor and instructed them to act according to what was in it and not to exceed it.""

This is a document whose explanation is as follows: ""No Zakāh is taken from camels until they reach five. When they reach five, there is one sheep (due) until they reach ten. When they reach ten, there are two sheep until they reach fifteen. When they reach fifteen, there are three sheep until they reach twenty. When they reach twenty, there are four sheep until they reach twenty-five. When they reach twenty-five, they have reached the point of obligation, and the obligation is a bint makhāḍ (a one-year-old she-camel). If a bint makhāḍ is not found, then a ibn labūn (a two-year-old male camel) is accepted until they reach thirty-five. When they reach thirty-six, there is a bint labūn (a two-year-old she-camel) until they reach forty-five. When they reach forty-six, there is a ḥiqqah (a three-year-old she-camel) that has reached the age of being covered by the camel, until they reach sixty. When they reach sixty-one, there is a jadhaʿah (a four-year-old she-camel) until they reach seventy-five. When they reach seventy-six, there are two bint labūn until they reach ninety. When they reach ninety-one, there are two ḥiqqah that have reached the age of being covered by the camel, until they reach one hundred and twenty. When they reach one hundred and twenty-one, there are three bint labūn until they reach one hundred and twenty-nine. When they reach one hundred and thirty, there is one ḥiqqah and two bint labūn until they reach one hundred and thirty-nine. When they reach one hundred and forty, there are two ḥiqqah and one bint labūn until they reach one hundred and forty-nine. When they reach one hundred and fifty, there are three ḥiqqah until they reach one hundred and fifty-nine. When they reach one hundred and sixty, there are four bint labūn until they reach one hundred and sixty-nine. When they reach one hundred and seventy, there is one ḥiqqah and three bint labūn until they reach one hundred and seventy-nine. When they reach one hundred and eighty, there are two ḥiqqah and two bint labūn until they reach one hundred and eighty-nine. When they reach one hundred and ninety, there are three ḥiqqah and one bint labūn until they reach one hundred and ninety-nine. When they reach two hundred, there are either four ḥiqqah or five bint labūn — whichever age group is found among them is taken according to the rates we have written in this document. Then everything regarding camels is taken according to what we have written in this document.

And no Zakāh is taken from sheep until they reach forty. When they reach forty sheep, there is one sheep (due) until they reach one hundred and twenty. When they reach one hundred and twenty-one, there are two sheep until they reach two hundred. When they reach two hundred and one, there are three sheep until they reach three hundred. When they exceed three hundred sheep, there are only three sheep (due) until they reach four hundred. When they reach four hundred sheep, there are four sheep until they reach five hundred. When they reach five hundred sheep, there are five sheep until they reach six hundred. When they reach six hundred sheep, there are six sheep. When they reach seven hundred sheep, there are seven sheep until they reach eight hundred. When they reach eight hundred sheep, there are eight sheep until they reach nine hundred. When they reach nine hundred sheep, there are nine sheep until they reach one thousand. When they reach one thousand sheep, there are ten sheep. Then for every additional hundred, there is one sheep."" [Authentic through other chains. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7259,"(7258) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us, Ḥabīb ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Harim narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Anṣārī — meaning Abū al-Rijāl — narrated, saying: ""When ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz was appointed as Caliph, he sent to al-Madīnah seeking the document of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding Zakāh and the document of ʿUmar. He found with the family of ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm the document of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ to ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm regarding Zakāh, and he found with the family of ʿUmar the document of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding Zakāh. Both were copied for him. Then ʿAmr narrated to me that he requested Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān to copy for him what was in those two documents, and he copied for him. He mentioned the Zakāh of camels from five to two hundred, as had been mentioned in the preceding hadith, and he added, saying: 'When they reach two hundred and ten, there are four bint labūn and one ḥiqqah until they reach two hundred and twenty. When they reach two hundred and twenty, there are three bint labūn and two ḥiqqah until they reach two hundred and thirty. When they reach two hundred and thirty, there are three ḥiqqah and two bint labūn.' Then he mentioned the hadith regarding the obligation of Zakāh, stating that for every additional ten until they reach three hundred, he said: 'When they reach three hundred, there are six ḥiqqah or five bint labūn and two ḥiqqah — whichever of these two age groups the muṣaddiq (Zakāh collector) wishes, he may take. When the camels exceed three hundred, then for every fifty there is a ḥiqqah, and for every forty there is a bint labūn, and nothing is taken from what is less than ten.'"" [Good. Narrated by al-Ṭaḥāwī]"
7260,"(7259) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq and Ḥabīb narrated from ʿAmr ibn Harim that Abū al-Rijāl Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Anṣārī narrated to him:

That when ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz was appointed as Caliph, he sent to al-Madīnah seeking the covenant of the Prophet ﷺ regarding Zakāh. He found with the family of ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm the document of the Prophet ﷺ to ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm regarding Zakāh, and it was found with the family of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) the document of ʿUmar to his workers regarding Zakāh, which was identical to the document of the Prophet ﷺ to ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm. So ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz instructed his workers regarding Zakāh to act according to what was in those two documents. In them, regarding the Zakāh of camels, was the following: ""When they exceed ninety by one, there are two ḥiqqah until they reach one hundred and twenty. When they exceed one hundred and twenty by one, there are three bint labūn until they reach one hundred and twenty-nine. When the camels are more than that, there is nothing in what does not reach ten of them until they reach ten."" [Good. Referred to previously]"
7261,"(7260) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (peace be upon him), regarding twenty-five camels: ""Five,"" meaning sheep. [Weak. Abū Isḥāq is a mudallis]"
7262,"(7261) Abū al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated, and Ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh — meaning Ibn al-Mubārak — narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), similarly. And he added: ""When they exceed one hundred and twenty,"" he said, ""the obligations are returned to their beginning. When the camels become numerous, then for every fifty there is a ḥiqqah."" And this was more beloved to Sufyān than the statement of the people of al-Ḥijāz. [Weak]"
7263,"(7262) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us, Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Sufyān, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Regarding camels, when they exceed one hundred and twenty, the obligations are calculated accordingly."" And from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, similarly.

Abū Yūsuf — meaning Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān — said: ""It reached me from Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd used to narrate a hadith in which he erred from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), saying: 'When the camels exceed one hundred and twenty, the obligation is recalculated.'""

But Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd did not err in this; rather, Ibn al-Mubārak corroborated him. And this is well-known from the narration of Sufyān, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim, from ʿAlī. However, the people of knowledge rejected this from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah because the narration of ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah from ʿAlī (peace be upon him) contradicts the document of the family of ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm and the document of Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both).

The Shaykh said: As for Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn (may Allah have mercy on him), he attributed the error to Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd in that matter. [Weak]"
7264,"(7263) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated, saying: I heard al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad saying: I heard Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn saying: ""Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd used to narrate a hadith in which he erred from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (peace be upon him), saying: 'When the camels exceed one hundred and twenty, the obligation is recalculated.'""

Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn said: ""And Wakīʿ narrated it from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, saying: 'When the camels exceed one hundred and twenty, the obligation is recalculated according to the first calculation.'""

Yaḥyā said: ""This is the more authentic of the two hadiths."" [Authentic]"
7265,"(7264) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī narrated to us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Azhar narrated to us, al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Ghassān al-Ghallābī narrated, saying: ""Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn mentioned that Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated from Sufyān a hadith in which he was unique, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him): 'When the camels exceed one hundred and twenty, the obligation is recalculated according to the first calculation.' He said: 'This is an error.'""

He said: ""And I mentioned to Yaḥyā the hadith of Wakīʿ from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Ibrāhīm, saying: 'When the camels exceed one hundred and twenty, the obligation is recalculated according to the first calculation.' He said: 'This is authentic.'""

The Shaykh said: The statement of Yaḥyā regarding this narration may be understood as him censuring Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān for narrating from Sufyān a hadith in which Sufyān was unique, and which is considered an error among the scholars of hadith, and he used to be cautious about such matters, so he only narrated what was authentic according to him, and Allah knows best.

As for Abū Yūsuf Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān al-Fārisī and other Imams, they attributed the error to ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah and cited as evidence for his error the contradiction it contains to the well-known narrations from the Prophet ﷺ, and then from Abū Bakr and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), regarding Zakāh.

As for al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him), he said in al-Qadīm: ""This was narrated by an unknown person from ʿAlī, and most narrators from that unknown person claim that the one who narrated this from him erred, and that this was not in his hadith"" — referring to his statement about recalculation (al-istiʾnāf).

He cited as evidence for this in another book the narration of those who narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) something contrary to that."
7266,"(7265) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated, saying: al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Sharīk narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (peace be upon him), saying: 'When the camels exceed one hundred and twenty, then for every fifty there is a ḥiqqah, and for every forty there is a bint labūn.'""

He said: ""And ʿAmr ibn al-Haytham and others narrated from Shuʿbah, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim, from ʿAlī, similarly.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And this is what we follow, and it is in accordance with the Sunnah. They — meaning some of the Iraqis — do not follow this, and they contradict what has been narrated from the Prophet ﷺ, Abū Bakr, and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both), and the established narration from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) according to them, in favor of the statement of Ibrāhīm and something that is erroneously attributed to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him)."""
7267,"(7266) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; al-Hasan ibn Ya'qubal-'Adl informed us; Yahya ibn Abi Talib said: 'Abd al-Wahhab — meaning Ibn 'Ata' — was asked about the Zakat of camels, and he informed us from Shu'bah, from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, that 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""For every five camels, one sheep; for ten, two sheep; for fifteen, three sheep; for twenty, four sheep; and for twenty-five, five sheep. When they exceed that, a bint makhad is due up to thirty-five. When they exceed that, a bint labun is due up to forty-five."" He then mentioned the hadith regarding the Zakat of camels up to ninety. He said: ""When they exceed that, two hiqqahs — both capable of bearing the male — are due up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed that, for every fifty a hiqqah is due, and for every forty a bint labun.""

The Sheikh said: Zuhayr ibn Mu'awiyah narrated it from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim and al-Harith, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). [Hasan]"
7268,"(7267) As Abu al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abu Dawud informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad al-Nufayli informed us; Zuhayr informed us; Abu Ishaq informed us from 'Asim ibn Damrah and from al-Harith al-A'war, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him). Zuhayr said: I think it is from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he said: ""Bring a quarter of a tenth…"" Then he mentioned the hadith until he said: ""And regarding camels…"" Then he mentioned their Zakat as al-Zuhri had mentioned. He said: ""For twenty-five, five sheep. When they exceed by one, a bint makhad is due. If there is no bint makhad available, then a ibn labun (male) is given, up to thirty-five."" Then he continued the hadith. He said: ""When they exceed by one — meaning beyond ninety — two hiqqahs, both capable of bearing the camel, are due up to one hundred and twenty. If the camels are more than that, then for every fifty a hiqqah is due."" He mentioned the rest of the hadith. It does not contain what is in the narration of Sufyan from Abu Ishaq regarding the resumption (of the count). And in it, as in many narrations from him, it states: ""For twenty-five, five sheep."" The scholars have unanimously agreed to abandon this statement because the narration of 'Asim ibn Damrah and al-Harith al-A'war from 'Ali (peace be upon him) contradicts the well-known narrations from the Prophet (peace be upon him) and from Abu Bakr and 'Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both) regarding the Zakat payments in this matter.

Similarly, the narration of those who reported the resumption (of the count) contradicts those well-known narrations, in addition to the discrepancies and errors within it, and the criticism of the Imams of hadith scholars regarding it. Therefore, it is obligatory to abandon it and to refer to what is stronger than it. And with Allah lies success. [Hasan. Narrated by Abu Dawud]"
7269,"(7268) As for the narration that Abu Dawud mentioned in his Marasil from Musa ibn Isma'il, he said: Hammad said: I said to Qays ibn Sa'd: ""Fetch me the book of Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm."" He gave me a book in which it was reported that he had taken it from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm, that the Prophet (peace be upon him) had written it for his grandfather. I read it, and in it was mention of what is due from the obligatory Zakat of camels. He narrated the hadith: ""Until they reach one hundred and twenty. When they exceed that, count for every fifty a hiqqah. Whatever remains shall be returned to the first obligatory category of camels. And whatever is less than twenty-five, sheep are due: for every five head, one sheep — there shall be no male among them, nor an old one, nor one with a defect from the flock.""

This is as Abu Bakr al-Sulaymani informed us; Abu al-Husayn al-Fasawi informed us; Abu al-Lu'lu'i informed us; Abu Dawud narrated it. It is munqati' (disconnected) between Abu Bakr ibn Hazm and the Prophet (peace be upon him), and Qays ibn Sa'd took it from a book, not from direct hearing. Similarly, Hammad ibn Salamah took it from a book, not from direct hearing. Although Qays ibn Sa'd and Hammad ibn Salamah are both trustworthy narrators, their narration in this case contradicts the narrations of the Huffaz from the book of 'Amr ibn Hazm and others.

(c) And Hammad ibn Salamah's memory weakened in the latter part of his life.

Therefore, the Huffaz do not use as evidence that in which he contradicts, and they avoid what is uniquely narrated by Qays ibn Sa'd in particular and those similar to him. This hadith combines both issues, in addition to the discontinuity within it. And with Allah lies success. [Da'if. Narrated by al-Tahawi]"
7270,"(7269) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad 'Abd Allah ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz informed us; 'Abd Allah al-Baghawi informed us; Salih ibn Ahmad informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad informed us; 'Ali ibn al-Madini said: Yahya ibn Sa'id — that is, al-Qattan — said: ""Hammad ibn Salamah from Ziyad al-A'lam and Qays ibn Sa'd is not reliable."" Then Yahya said: ""If what Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Qays ibn Sa'd is authentic, then Qays ibn Sa'd is not [a reliable authority] in this. However, the hadith of Hammad ibn Salamah from the shaykhs from Thabit and this category — meaning, he is firm in these."" [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn 'Adi]"
7271,"(7270) Abu Sa'd informed us; Abu Ahmad informed us; Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Hammad informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal informed us. He said: I heard my father say: ""The book of Hammad ibn Salamah from Qays ibn Sa'd was lost, and so he used to narrate to them from memory. This is his story."" [Sahih. Narrated by Ibn 'Adi]"
7272,"(7271) Al-Hakim Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Mu'mmil informed us; al-Fadl ibn Muhammad informed us; Ahmad ibn Hanbal informed us; 'Affan informed us. He said: Hammad ibn Salamah said: ""Hajjaj al-Ahwal borrowed the book of Qays from me. He went to Makkah and said: 'It is lost.'"" [Sahih. Hammad ibn Salamah]"
7273,"(7272) Abu al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr said: Abu Dawud al-Sijistani said: ""I heard it from al-Riyashi and from Abu Hatim and others, and from the book of al-Nadr ibn Shumayl, and from the book of Abu 'Ubayd. Sometimes one of them would mention a word. They said: It is called al-huwar, then al-fasil when it is weaned, then it becomes a bint makhad at one year up to the completion of two years. When it enters the third year, it is a bint labun. When it completes three years, it is a hiqqah up to the completion of four years, because it has become deserving of being ridden and of the male being mounted upon it, and it becomes pregnant. The male is not used for breeding until it reaches maturity (ithna). And the hiqqah is called taruqat al-fahl (one capable of bearing the male) because the male approaches it, up to the completion of four years. When it enters the fifth year, it is a jadha'ah until it completes five years. When it enters the sixth year and sheds its front tooth, it is then a thanni until it completes six years. When it enters the seventh year, the male is called a ruba' and the female a ruba'iyyah up to the completion of the seventh year. When it enters the eighth year and sheds the tooth after the ruba'iyyah tooth, it is a sadis and a sadas up to the completion of the eighth year. When it enters the ninth year and its canine tooth appears, it is a bazil — its canine tooth has emerged, meaning it has appeared — until it enters the tenth year. It is then a mukhlif. After that, it has no specific name, but it is said: bazil of one year, bazil of two years, mukhlif of one year, mukhlif of two years, mukhlif of three years, up to five years. And al-khalifah means a pregnant one.""

Al-Shafi'i (may Allah have mercy on him) mentioned the explanation of the ages of camels in the narration of Harmalah, similar to this, and added: ""The term ibn makhad (meaning the male among them) is so called because it is separated from its mother, and its mother joins the makhad — that is, the pregnant ones — so it is an ibn makhad, even if she is not pregnant. And the term ibn labun is so called because its mother has given birth to another, and she has milk."" [Sahih. Abu Dawud]"
7274,"(7273) Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Bahr ibn Nusr said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Jarir ibn Hazim and another whose name he mentioned informed you, from Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani, from 'Asim ibn Damrah and al-Harith ibn 'Abd Allah, from 'Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), that he said: 'Bring me a quarter of a tenth…'"" Then he mentioned the hadith. At the end of it, Jarir said in the hadith from the Prophet (peace be upon him): ""There is no Zakat on wealth until a full year has passed over it."" [Hasan. Its grading has been given previously]"
7275,"(7274) 'Ali ibn Muhammad ibn Bashran and Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad. They said: Abu 'Amr 'Uthman ibn Ahmad ibn al-Sammak informed us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Dawud informed us; Abu Badr Shuja' ibn al-Walid informed us; Harithah ibn Muhammad informed us from 'Amrah, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her). She said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) say: ""There is no Zakat on wealth until a full year has passed over it."" Similarly, Abu Mu'awiyah, Huraym ibn Sufyan, and Abu Kudaynah narrated it from Harithah, marfu' (raised to the Prophet). And al-Thawri narrated it from Harithah, mawquf (stopped at 'A'ishah).

Harithah is not used as a basis for evidence in his reports. Reliance in this matter is placed upon the authentic narrations from Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah be pleased with him), 'Uthman ibn 'Affan, 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar, and others (may Allah be pleased with them all). [Munkar al-Isnad. Narrated by Ibn Majah]"
7276,"(7275) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan informed us in Baghdad; 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far ibn Durustawayh informed us; Ya'qub ibn Sufyan informed us; Ishaq ibn Ibrahim informed us; 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Salim informed me; al-Zabidi informed me; Yahya ibn Jabir informed me that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Jubayr informed him that his father informed him that 'Abd Allah ibn Mu'awiyah al-Ghadiri informed them that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Three things — whoever does them has tasted the flavor of faith: worshipping Allah alone, for there is no god but Allah; giving the Zakat of his wealth willingly, his soul being at ease with it, offered every year; and not giving the old, the mangy, the defective, or the sick, but rather from the average of your wealth. For Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, has not asked you for the best of it, nor has He commanded you with the worst of it."" And a man said: ""What is the purification of a person's soul, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""He knows that Allah is with him wherever he is."" And another said: ""…nor the defective."" [Sahih. Narrated by Abu Dawud]"
7277,"(7276) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas al-Qasim ibn al-Qasim al-Sayyari narrated to us in Marw; Abu al-Muwajjih narrated to us; 'Abdan informed us; 'Abd Allah informed us; Zakariya ibn Ishaq narrated from Yahya ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Sayfi' from Abu Ma'bad, the freed slave of Ibn 'Abbas, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to Mu'adh ibn Jabal when he sent him to Yemen: ""You will come to a people of the People of the Book. When you reach them, invite them to bear witness that there is no deity worthy of worship except Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. If they obey you in that, then inform them that Allah has obligated upon them five prayers every day and night. If they obey you in that, then inform them that Allah has obligated upon them a charity (sadaqah) that is taken from their wealthy and returned to their poor. If they obey you in that, then beware of the most prized of their possessions, and guard against the supplication of the wronged, for there is no barrier between it and Allah."""
7278,"(7277) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali narrated to us; Waki' narrated from Zakariya ibn Ishaq al-Makki from 'Amr ibn Abi Sufyan al-Jumahi from Muslim ibn Thafnah al-Yashkuri. Al-Hasan said: Ruḥ says: Muslim ibn Shu'bah. He said: Nafi' ibn 'Alqamah appointed my father as overseer of his people's charitable dues and ordered him to collect their zakah. He said: My father sent me among a group of them. I came to an old man — he was called Sa'r ibn Disam — and I said: ""My father has sent me to you — meaning to collect your zakah."" He said: ""Nephew, what sort do you take?"" I said: ""We select carefully, to the point that we measure the udders of the sheep."" He said: ""Nephew, I will narrate to you: I was in one of these mountain passes during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), tending my sheep, when two men came to me on a camel and said: 'We are the messengers of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) to you, that you deliver the zakah of your sheep.' I said: 'What is due upon me in them?' They said: 'A sheep.' So I went to a sheep whose place I knew, full of milk and fat, and I brought it to them. They said: 'This is a shaf'ah (a prized animal), and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) has forbidden us from taking a shaf'ah.' I said: 'Then what do you take?' They said: 'A jad'ah or a thaniyah (a young she-goat).' He said: So I went to a mu'tat (a she-goat that has not yet given birth but has reached the age of giving birth) and brought it to them. They said: 'Hand it over to us.' So they placed it with them on their camel and departed."" Waki' said: ""mahdan"" (colostrum), and the correct word is ""makhadan"" (pregnancy). And the narrator is Muslim ibn Shu'bah — this was stated by Yahya ibn Ma'in and other hadith scholars."
7279,"(7278) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdhan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; al-Harith ibn Abi Usamah narrated to us; Ruh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us; Zakariya ibn Ishaq narrated to us; 'Amr ibn Abi Sufyan narrated to us; Muslim ibn Shu'bah narrated to me. He mentioned the hadith with its meaning and added in it: ""And the shaf'ah is the one that has its young in its womb."""
7280,"(7279) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Ahmad ibn Ja'far al-Qati'i narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; my father narrated from Ibn Ishaq; 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm narrated to me from Yahya ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Sa'd ibn Zurarah from 'Umarah ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) sent me as a zakah collector. I passed by a man who gathered his wealth for me, and I found that nothing was due upon him except a bint makhad (a one-year-old she-camel). I said to him: ""Deliver a bint makhad, for that is your zakah."" He said: ""That is something that has no milk and no use for riding. But here is a large, fat she-camel — take it."" I said: ""I will not take what I have not been commanded to take. And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) is near you. If you wish to come to him and present to him what you presented to me, then do so. If he accepts it from you, I will accept it, and if he rejects it from you, I will reject it."" He said: ""I will do so."" He said: So he went out with me, and he went out with the she-camel that he had offered to me, until we came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said to him: ""O Prophet of Allah, your messenger came to me to take the zakah of my wealth. By Allah, neither the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) nor his messenger ever stood over my wealth before him. I gathered my wealth for him, and he claimed that what is due upon me is a bint makhad — and that is something that has no milk and no use for riding. I offered him a large she-camel for him to take, but he refused it. And here it is — I have brought it to you, O Messenger of Allah. Take it."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: ""That which is due upon you is only what is obligatory. But if you volunteer something better, Allah will reward you for it, and we will accept it from you."" He said: ""Here it is, O Messenger of Allah — I have brought it to you, so take it."" He said: So the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered that it be taken, and he invoked blessings for him in his wealth."
7281,"(7280) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdhan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; Ibn Milhan narrated to us; Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated. (H) And Abu al-Qasim Zayd ibn Abi Hashim al-'Alawi in Kufa informed us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duheym narrated to us; Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Abi al-Hunayd narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us; Layth ibn Sa'd narrated from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from Sa'd ibn Sinan from Anas ibn Malik from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: ""The one who is excessive in collecting charity is like the one who withholds it."" Qutaybah said: Ibn Lahi'ah used to say ""Sinan ibn Sa'd."" The Shaykh Ahmad said: Thus al-Layth says: ""Sa'd ibn Sinan,"" while others say ""Sinan ibn Sa'd."" Al-Bukhari said: The correct one in my view is ""Sinan ibn Sa'd,"" and ""Sa'd ibn Sinan"" is an error; this was stated by Layth ibn Sa'd. He said: And al-Layth once said ""Sinan."""
7282,"(7281) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz narrated to us; 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Salam narrated to us; Harmalah narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed us that Ibn Abi Habib narrated to him from Sinan ibn Sa'd al-Kindi from Anas ibn Malik that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no faith for one who has no trustworthiness, and the one who is excessive in collecting charity is like the one who withholds it."" Thus said Sinan ibn Sa'd, and thus also said Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub, and Abu Salih also narrated it from al-Layth. The Shaykh said: And al-Hasan al-Basri said regarding a man upon whom zakah became obligatory but he did not pay it until his wealth was lost: ""It remains a debt upon him until he discharges it."""
7283,"(7282) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us; Isma'il ibn Muhammad narrated to us; Sa'dan narrated to us; Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated from Ash'ath from al-Hasan, and he mentioned it."
7284,"(7283) Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi and Abu Zakariya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi both said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us. He said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: ""Ibn Lahi'ah and Layth ibn Sa'd informed you from Khalid ibn Yazid from Sa'id ibn Abi Hilal, from one who narrated to him, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), that he said: A man from Banu Tamim came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, when I have delivered it to your messenger, I am absolved of it before Allah and before His Messenger.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Yes, when you have delivered it to my messenger, you are absolved of it, and its reward is for you, and its sin is upon whoever alters it.'"""
7285,"(7284) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us; Abu 'Amr 'Uthman ibn Ahmad ibn al-Sammak narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd Allah ibn Yazid al-Munadi narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd al-Tanafisi narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated. (H) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Ja'far Muhammad ibn 'Ali ibn Duheym al-Shaybani narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Hazim ibn Abi Ghurzah narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us; Waki' narrated to us; al-A'mash narrated from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while he was sitting in the shade of the Ka'bah. When he saw me, he said: ""They are the greatest losers, by the Lord of the Ka'bah!"" He said: I came until I sat down, and I could not remain still, so I stood and said: ""Who are they, may my father and mother be sacrificed for you?"" He said: ""They are those with the most wealth — except one who says with his wealth, 'like this and like this' — four times — and few indeed they are. There is no owner of camels, cattle, or sheep who does not pay their zakah except that they will come on the Day of Resurrection, the largest they have ever been and the fattest, goring him with their horns and trampling him with their hooves. Whenever the last of them passes, the first returns to him, until judgment is passed among the people."" This is the wording of Waki's hadith. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, and al-Bukhari narrated it through another chain from al-A'mash."
7286,"(7285) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Ali al-Hafiz narrated to us; Abu Bakr 'Abd Allah ibn Sulayman narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salih narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to us; 'Amr informed me that Bukayr narrated to him from Abu Salih Dhakwan from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""When a person does not fulfill the right of Allah the Most High regarding the zakah of his camels, they will be spread out for him on a level plain, and they will trample him with their hooves and bite him with their mouths. Whenever the last of them passes over him, the first will return to him, until he sees his final destination — either from Paradise or from the Fire. And the cattle — when the right of Allah the Most High is not fulfilled regarding them — they will be spread out for him on a level plain, and they will trample him with their hooves and gore him with their horns. Whenever the last of them passes over him, the first will return to him, until he sees his final destination — either from Paradise or from the Fire. And the sheep are likewise: they will gore him with their horns and trample him with their hooves. There are among them no sheep with twisted horns or with broken horns, until he sees his final destination — either from Paradise or from the Fire. And horses are of three types: a source of reward, a source of burden, and a source of covering (protection). Whoever keeps them for the sake of chastity and self-sufficiency, they are a covering for him. Whoever keeps them as preparation for jihad in the cause of Allah, they are a source of reward for him. And if he lengthens their reins for one elevation or two, there is reward for him in that. And whoever keeps them out of pride, showing off, and hostility toward the Muslims, they are a source of burden for him."" A questioner said: ""What about donkeys, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Nothing was revealed to me regarding donkeys except this comprehensive, singular verse: 'Whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever does an atom's weight of evil shall see it.'"""
7287,"(7286) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm al-Hāshimī reported to us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz narrated to us by dictation: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Dūrī narrated to us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from Ibrāhīm, and al-Aʿmash from Shaqīq, from Masrūq, and they both said: Muʿādh said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent me to Yemen and commanded me to take from every forty cows a thaniyyah, and from every thirty a tabīʿ or tabīʿah, and from every person who has reached the age of maturity a dīnār or its equivalent in Maʿāfirī cloth."" [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Abū Dāwūd]"
7288,"(7287) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh reported to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us: Maʿmar and al-Thawrī narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Abī Wāʾil, from Masrūq, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, who said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) sent him to Yemen and commanded him to take from every thirty cows a tabīʿ or tabīʿah, and from every forty a musinnah, and from every person who has reached the age of maturity a dīnār or its equivalent in Maʿāfirī cloth."" [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. It was transmitted before this.]"
7289,"(7288) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī reported to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Nufaylī, ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah, and Ibn al-Muthannā said: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us: al-Aʿmash narrated from Ibrāhīm, from Masrūq, from Muʿādh, from the Prophet (ﷺ), with a similar narration."
7290,"(7289) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān reported to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""I asked Nāfiʿ about cows, and he said: 'It has reached me from Muʿādh ibn Jabal that he said: In every thirty (cows) there is a tabīʿ or tabīʿah, and in every forty a cow.'"" [Weak]"
7291,"(7290) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb reported to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān reported to us: al-Shāfiʿī reported to us: Mālik reported — and Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī reported to us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated — from Ḥumayd ibn Qays, from Ṭāwūs al-Yamānī, that Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) took from thirty cows a tabīʿ, and from forty a musinnah. He was brought (cattle) below that (number), and he refused to take anything from them, saying: ""I did not hear anything from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) regarding this until I meet him and ask him."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away before Muʿādh ibn Jabal could arrive. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Mālik transmitted it.]"
7292,"(7291) Abū Zakariyyā reported to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās reported to us: al-Rabīʿ reported to us: al-Shāfiʿī reported to us: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Ṭāwūs, that Muʿādh ibn Jabal was brought the intermediate (numbers) of cows, and he said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) did not command me regarding them with anything."" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Al-Waqṣ is that which has not reached the obligatory number (of the prescribed threshold)."" Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah narrated — though he is not a proof — from al-Ḥakam, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent Muʿādh to Yemen, it was said to him: 'What were you commanded?' He said: 'I was commanded to take from the cows: from every thirty a tabīʿ or tabīʿah, and from every forty a musinnah.'"" [Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī transmitted it.]"
7293,(7292) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ reported to us: Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad al-Daqqāq narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-Munādī narrated to us: Abū Badr Shujaʿ ibn al-Walīd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah narrated to us: al-Ḥakam narrated it. And it has a witness with a better chain than it. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Al-Dāraquṭnī]
7294,"(7293) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh reported it to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Firyābī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us: Baqiyyah narrated to us: al-Masʿūdī narrated from al-Ḥakam, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent Muʿādh to Yemen, he commanded him to take from the cows: from every thirty a tabīʿ or tabīʿah — a jadhaʿ or jadhaʿah — and from every forty a cow, a musinnah. They said: 'What about al-Awqāṣ?' He said: 'He did not command me regarding them with anything. I shall ask the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) when I arrive before him.' When he arrived before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), he asked him about al-Awqāṣ, and he said: 'There is nothing in them.'"" Al-Masʿūdī said: ""Al-Awqāṣ are what is below thirty and between forty and sixty. When they reach sixty, there are two tabīʿahs in them. When they reach seventy, there is a musinnah and a tabīʿ. When they reach eighty, there are two musinnahs. When they reach ninety, there are three tabāyiʿ."" Baqiyyah said: Al-Masʿūdī said: ""Al-Awqāṣ (is written) with a sīn — al-Awqās — so do not take it with a ṣād."" [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Al-Dāraquṭnī]"
7295,"(7294) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Qatādah reported to us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdah narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbadī al-Būshanjī narrated to us: al-Nufaylī Abū Jaʿfar narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah and from al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him). Zuhayr said: ""I think he (ʿAlī) narrated from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: 'Bring a quarter of a tenth.'"" Then he mentioned the hadith in its entirety. In it he said: ""And regarding cows: in every thirty there is a tabīʿ, and in every forty a musinnah, and there is nothing upon working (cattle)."" [Ḥasan in other than this chain. Its grading has been discussed previously.]"
7296,"(7295) Abū Muḥammad Janāḥ ibn Nadhīr ibn Janāḥ reported to us in Kūfah: Abū Jaʿfar ibn Duḥaym narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥāzim reported to us: al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb narrated to us — and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb narrated — from Khuṣayf, from Abī ʿUbaydah, from ʿAbd Allāh, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Regarding cows: in every thirty there is a tabīʿ or tabīʿah — a jadhaʿ or jadhaʿah — and in every forty a musinnah."" Janāḥ did not mention in his narration ""a jadhaʿ or jadhaʿah."" Sharīk narrated it from Khuṣayf, from Abī ʿUbaydah, from his mother, from ʿAbd Allāh. Al-Bukhārī said it. [Ṣaḥīgh in other than this chain. Aḥmad transmitted it.]"
7297,"(7296) And it has already been mentioned in the hadith of Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd from al-Zuhrī from Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm from his father from his grandfather from the Prophet ﷺ: that he wrote to the people of Yemen, and in it he said: ""And in every thirty cattle, a tabīʿ jadhaʿ or a jadhaʿah, and in every forty cattle, a cow (baqarah).""

It was narrated to me by Abū ʿAbd al-Rahmān al-Sulamī, who informed us Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥamdān, who informed us ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, who narrated to us al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā, who narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah from Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīh through other chains]"
7298,"(7297) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Maḥbūbī in Marw narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us, ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā informed us, Sufyān narrated from Dāwūd from al-Shaʿbī, who raised it (marfūʿan), and Ibn Abī ʿAyyāsh from Anas, who raised it (marfūʿan), saying: ""In every forty cattle, a musinnah, and in every thirty, a tabīʿ or a tabīʿah."" [Ṣaḥīh through other chains]"
7299,"(7298) As for the athar which Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad informed us of, Abū al-Ḥusayn al-Fasawī narrated to us, Abū ʿAlī al-Luʾluʾī narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Thawr narrated from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh: ""In every five cattle, one sheep; in ten, two sheep; in fifteen, three sheep; and in twenty, four sheep.""

Al-Zuhrī said: ""When they reach twenty-five, there is one cow up to seventy-five. When they exceed seventy-five, there are two cows up to one hundred and twenty. When they exceed one hundred and twenty, then in every forty there is one cow."" Maʿmar said: al-Zuhrī said: ""And it has reached us that their statement—the Prophet ﷺ said: 'In every thirty cattle, a tabīʿ, and in every forty cattle, a cow'—that this was a concession (takhfīf) for the people of Yemen, and then this came after that.""

This hadith is mawqūf (stopped) and munqaṭiʿ (disrupted). It has also been narrated through another chain from al-Zuhrī as munqaṭiʿ, and no ḥujjah (proof) can be established through a munqaṭiʿ narration. What precedes it is more numerous and more well-known. And Allāh knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
7300,"(7299) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAqīl al-Khuzāʿī informed us, Abū Jaʿfar Muṭayyin narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā al-ʿAnazī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh (meaning al-Anṣārī) narrated to me, my father narrated from Thumāmah, who narrated to me that Anas ibn Mālik said: ""When Abū Bakr (may Allāh be pleased with him) was appointed as khalīfah, he sent him to al-Baḥrayn and wrote for him this document: 'In the name of Allāh, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. This is the obligatory charity (farīḍat al-ṣadaqah) which Allāh has imposed upon the Muslims, which the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ commanded. So whoever is asked for it by the Muslims in its proper form, let him give it; and whoever is asked for more than that, let him not give it.'""

Then he mentioned the hadith regarding the obligation of zakāh on camels and their various ages. Then he said: ""And the ṣadaqah on sheep in their free-grazing state: when they are forty up to one hundred and twenty, there is one sheep. When they exceed one hundred and twenty up to two hundred, there are two sheep. When they exceed two hundred up to three hundred, there are three sheep. When they exceed three hundred, then in every hundred there is one sheep. And no aged (harimah) animal, nor one with a defect (dhāt ʿawār), nor a male goat (tays) shall be taken in ṣadaqah, unless the collector (muṣaddiq) wishes otherwise. And separate flocks shall not be combined, nor shall a combined flock be separated, out of fear of ṣadaqah. And whatever belongs to two partners, they shall make restitution between them equitably. And when a man's free-grazing sheep are fewer than forty by one sheep, there is no ṣadaqah on them, unless their owner wishes (to give voluntarily).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Anṣārī. The rest of the chains of this hadith have already been mentioned, and there has already been mentioned in the book of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ that was in the possession of the family of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allāh be pleased with him) something similar to this and more detailed. In it he said: ""When they are two hundred and one, there are three sheep up to three hundred. When they exceed three hundred by one sheep, there are only three sheep up to four hundred sheep. When they reach four hundred sheep, there are four sheep up to five hundred. When they reach five hundred sheep, there are five sheep."" Then he mentioned it thus in increments of one hundred up to one thousand. He said: ""Then for every additional hundred, one sheep."""
7301,"(7300) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us, Zakariyyā ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Abī Sufyān narrated to me, Muslim ibn Shuʿbah narrated to me, and he mentioned it. However, our shaykh did not firmly establish the name of Saʿr ibn Dayysam. [Ḍaʿīf]"
7302,"(7301) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, Bishr ibn ʿĀṣim narrated from his father that ʿUmar appointed his father, Sufyān ibn ʿAbd Allāh, over al-Ṭāʾif and its surrounding districts. He went out as a zakāh collector and counted the young animals (al-ghadhāʾ) among them but did not take any from them. They said to him: ""If you are counting the young animals against us, then take them from us."" So he withheld (from collecting) until he met ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) and said to him: ""Know that they claim that we wrong them—we count the young animals against them but do not take them from them."" ʿUmar said to him: ""Count the young animals against them, even the sahlah that the shepherd carries on his arm, and say to them: 'I will not take from you the ribā (premium animal), nor the mākhīḍ (pregnant one), nor the dhāt al-darʿ (one giving milk), nor the shātah al-akūlah (the fat sheep), nor the faḥl al-ghanam (the ram of the flock). Take instead the ʿanāq jadhaʿah (a young goat) and the thaniyyah (a two-year-old). That is justice between the nourishment of the wealth and its choicest portion.'""

[Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7303,"(7302) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dīlī from a son of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sufyān al-Thaqafī from his grandfather Sufyān ibn ʿAbd Allāh that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allāh be pleased with him) sent him as a zakāh collector, and he used to count the young animals (sakhl) among the people. They said: ""Do you count the young animals against us and not take anything from them?"" When he came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allāh be pleased with him), he mentioned that to him. ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb said: ""Yes, we count the young animals against them—the sahlah that the shepherd carries—and we do not take them. And we do not take the akūlah (fat one), nor the rubbā (one nursing young), nor the mākhīḍ (pregnant one), nor the faḥl al-ghanam (the ram of the flock). And we take the jadhaʿah (one-year-old) and the thaniyyah (two-year-old). That is justice between the nourishment of the wealth and its choicest portion.""

[Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Mālik]"
7304,"(7303) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿīd and al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān both said: Umayyah ibn Bisṭām narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Zuraīʿ narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣayfī from Abū Maʿbad from Ibn ʿAbbās that when the Prophet ﷺ sent Muʿādh to Yemen, he said: ""You are going to a people of the Book. Let the first thing you call them to be the worship of Allāh (ʿazza wa jall). When they come to know Allāh, inform them that Allāh has obligated upon them five prayers in their day and night. When they do that, inform them that Allāh has obligated upon them a zakāh that is taken from their wealth and returned to their poor. When they obey in that, then take from them, and beware of the choicest possessions of the people.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Umayyah ibn Bisṭām. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7305,"(7304) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abū ʿAwwānah narrated from Hilāl ibn Khabbāb from Maysarah Abī Ṣāliḥ from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, who said: ""I traveled—or he said, someone who traveled with the zakāh collector of the Prophet ﷺ informed me—and in the covenant of the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ was: 'That he shall not take a nursing animal, and that separate flocks shall not be combined, nor shall a combined flock be separated.'""

He used only to come to the watering places when the sheep came to drink, and he would say: ""Pay the ṣadaqah of your wealth."" He (Suwayd) said: ""A man among them set his intention toward a kūmāʾ she-camel."" He said: ""I said: 'O Abū Ṣāliḥ, what is al-kūmāʾ?' He said: 'One of great hump.'"" He said: ""He refused to accept it."" He said: ""(The man) said: 'I would like you to take the best of my camels.'"" He said: ""He refused to accept it."" He said: ""Then he marked for him another one below that, and he refused to accept it. Then he marked for him another one below that, and he accepted it and said: 'I am taking it, and I fear that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ may find fault with me, saying: ""You went to a man and selected the best of his camels for him.""'""

[Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7306,"(7305) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us, Saʿīd (meaning ibn Manṣūr) narrated to us, Hushaym informed us, Hilāl ibn Khabbāb narrated from Maysarah Abī Ṣāliḥ from Suwayd ibn Ghafalah, who said: ""The zakāh collector of the Prophet ﷺ came to us. I came to him and sat with him, and I heard him say: 'In my covenant is that I shall not take a nursing animal, and that a combined flock shall not be separated, nor shall separate flocks be combined.'""

A man came to him with a kūmāʾ she-camel and said: ""Take it,"" but he refused. [Ḥasan. Preceded above]"
7307,"(7306) And Abū al-Ḥusayn narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated to us — Sharīk narrated from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Zurʿah — from Abī Laylā al-Kindī — from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, who said: I took the hand of the Prophet's (ﷺ) collector and brought him a large she-camel. He said: ""What shade of sky would shelter me, and what land would bear me, if I were to take the best of a man's wealth and then come to him with a she-camel from the camels?"" — and he accepted it. [Ḥasan in light of other narrations. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7308,"(7307) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Abū Bakr al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us — Ḥumwayh narrated to us — Abū al-Walīd narrated from Sharīk — from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Zurʿah — from Abī Laylā al-Kindī — from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, who said: The Prophet's (ﷺ) collector came to me, so I took his hand and he took mine, and I read in his instruction: ""That what is separate should not be combined, and what is combined should not be separated, out of fear of ṣadaqah."" He said: Then a man came with a large, bulky she-camel, but he refused to take it. Then he came with another, smaller than it, and he refused to take it. Then he came with yet another, smaller than that, and he refused to take it. Then he said: ""What land would bear me, and what shade of sky would shelter me, if I were to come to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) having taken the best of a Muslim's camels?""

And it has passed in the ḥadīth of Ubayy ibn Kaʿb when he went out as a collector.

And in it is an indication of the permissibility of taking it when its owner volunteers it. [Ḥasan. Previously referenced.]"
7309,"(7308) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Usāmah narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us — Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim said: I saw a man in the place of Ayyūb wearing a woolen cloak. And in the narration of al-Ḥārith, he said: I saw in the gathering of Ayyūb a Bedouin wearing a woolen cloak. When he saw the people conversing, he said: My master, Qurrah ibn Duʿmūṣ, narrated to me. He said: I came to Madīnah, and there the Prophet (ﷺ) was sitting with his Companions around him. I wanted to draw near to him, but I could not. I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, forgive the young Numayrī slave."" He said: ""May Allah forgive you."" He said: And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent al-Ḍaḥḥāk as a collector. He said: He came with large camels, and the Prophet (ﷺ) said to him: ""Did you come to Hilāl ibn ʿĀmir and Numayr ibn ʿĀmir and ʿĀmir ibn Rabīʿah and take the best of their wealth?"" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I heard you mentioning the military expedition, so I wanted to bring you camels upon which you may ride and upon which your Companions may carry supplies."" He said: ""By Allah, what you left behind is more beloved to me than what you have brought. Go and return it to them, and take their ṣadaqah from the margins of their wealth."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ḥārith.]"
7310,"(7309) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us — Ibn Bukayr narrated to us — Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, that he said: Two men from Ashjaʿ informed me that Muḥammad ibn Maslamah al-Anṣārī used to come to them as a collector and say to the owner of the wealth: ""Bring forth the ṣadaqah of your wealth."" He would not lead to him any sheep in which there was a surplus of its due right, except that he would accept it.

Mālik said: The established Sunnah among us is that one should not be restrictive toward people regarding their zakāh, and that one should accept from them whatever they offer as ṣadaqah from their wealth.

The Shaykh said: If what they offer contains a surplus of the due right, as he narrated in the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Maslamah. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7311,"(7310) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us — Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us — Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us — Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to us — Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent a man as a collector and said: ""Do not take anything from the choicest of people's wealth. Take the elderly (shārif) and the young (bakar) and those with defects."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7312,"(7311) And Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us — he said: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārīzī narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us — from Abū ʿUbayd, who says: ""Do not take the choicest of their wealth. Take the elderly (shārif), which is the aged and decrepit she-camel, and the young (bakar), which is the small male camel."" And it was in the early Islam, before the people were taken to the prescribed laws.

The Shaykh said: The ḥadīth is mursal. And it is conceivable in our view that one may take the males, the young, and the defective ones, if all of the livestock is of that nature. [Ṣaḥīh.]"
7313,"(7312) And it has been narrated to us from al-Thawrī — from al-Aʿmash — from al-Ḥakam, who said: When the collector reaches the sheep, he divides them into two parts. Then the owner of the sheep takes the better of the two parts, and the collector of ṣadaqah takes from the other part. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7314,"(7313) And it has been narrated to us from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar — from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, that he said: He divides them into three portions: one-third is the best, one-third is the middle, and one-third is the lesser. The collector leaves the best and takes from the middle.

And Abū Bakr al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us — Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us — Sufyān al-Jawharī narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us — Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from both of them with both of them together.

And al-Shāfiʿī mentioned in the older transmission these two opinions without naming their proponents.

And it has been narrated to us from al-Zuhrī with a similar statement to that of al-Qāsim.

And it has been narrated to us from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: The owner of the sheep chooses one-third, then they choose from the remaining two-thirds. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7315,"(7314) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — both of them said: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Numayr narrated to us — from ʿUbaydullāh, meaning Ibn ʿUmar — from Bishr ibn ʿĀṣim — from his father — from his grandfather, who said: ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, appointed me over the ṣadaqah of my people. I counted the young (al-buhm) among them, and they objected to that and said: ""If you count them among the sheep, then take your ṣadaqah from them."" He said: So we counted them among them. Then I met ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: ""My people have objected to me that I count the young among them, and they said: 'If you consider them part of the sheep, then take your ṣadaqah from them.'"" ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Count the young among them, O Sufyān, even if the shepherd comes carrying them in your hand. And say to your people: 'We leave for them the pregnant (al-mākhīḍ), the suckling (al-rubayʿ), the meat-bearing sheep (shāṭ al-laḥm), and the ram of the flock (faḥl al-ghamam). And we take the jadhaʿ and the thaniyyah — and that is a middle course between us and you regarding the wealth.'"" [Ṣaḥīh. Meaning of its attribution.]"
7316,"(7315) And ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishrān in Baghdād narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Ḥunaynī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣalt narrated to us — Abū Kudaynah narrated from Ḥārithah — from ʿAmrah — from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no zakāh on wealth until a full year (ḥawl) has passed over it."" [Munkar in its isnād.]"
7317,"(7316) And it was narrated from ath-Thawrī, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""If you possess wealth from which you derive benefit, there is no zakāh due upon it until a full year passes over it."" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Isḥāq]"
7318,"(7317) And from Ḥārithah ibn Abī ar-Rijāl, from ʿAmrah, from ʿĀʾishah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā), who said: ""There is no zakāh on wealth that is benefited from until a full year passes over it."" Abū Bakr al-Aṣbahānī informed us of both, Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us — Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us — Sufyān narrated, and he mentioned both of them together. [Ḍaʿīf]"
7319,"(7318) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us — al-Rabīʿ informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Mālik informed us — from Ibn ʿUqbah, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, who said: ""Abū Bakr (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) used not to take zakāh from wealth until a full year had passed over it."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Mālik]"
7320,"(7319) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿAlī ibn Ḥamshādh informed us — Yazīd ibn al-Haytham informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth informed us — al-Ashjaʿī informed us — Sufyān narrated from Ayyūb al-Sakhtiyānī, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Whoever acquires wealth, let him not pay its zakāh until a full year passes over it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7321,"(7320) And Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh — informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī informed us — Muḥammad ibn al-Walīd al-Busrī informed us — ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, that Ibn ʿUmar said: ""There is no zakāh on wealth until a full year passes over it with its owner."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik]"
7322,"(7321) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥammād informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-ʿAnbarī informed us — Muʿtamir narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he said: ""When a man acquires wealth, the zakāh on it does not become due until a full year passes over it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7323,"(7322) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Burshān informed us — Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us — Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""There is no zakāh on wealth until a full year passes over it.""

This is the correct version, it is mawqūf (stopping at the Companion).

And it was narrated by Baqiyyah from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, as marfūʿ (attributed to the Prophet), but it is not correct. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its men are thiqāt (trustworthy)]"
7324,"(7323) And it was narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: ""There is no zakāh on wealth that is benefited from until a full year passes over it."" Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shabīb narrated to me — Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad al-Jārī narrated to me — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam narrated, and he mentioned it.

And ʿAbd al-Raḥmān is ḍaʿīf (weak), he cannot be used as evidence. [Munkar. Narrated by Dāraquṭnī]"
7325,"(7324) As corroboration, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Muzanī informed us — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá informed us — Abū al-Yamān informed us — Shuʿayb informed me — from al-Zuhrī, who informed me: that ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtban ibn Masʿūd informed him that Abū Hurayrah (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) said: ""When the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) passed away, and Abū Bakr assumed the caliphate after him, and the Arabs apostatized — whoever of them apostatized — ʿUmar said: 'O Abū Bakr, how can you fight the people when the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: ""I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: 'There is no god but Allah.' Whoever says: 'There is no god but Allah,' has protected from me his life and his wealth, except by its right, and his reckoning is with Allah""?' Abū Bakr (raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu) said: 'I will most certainly fight whoever separates between the ṣalāh and the zakāh, for the zakāh is the right of the wealth. By Allah, if they withhold from me a young she-goat (ʿanāq) which they used to give to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam), I will fight them for withholding it.' ʿUmar said: 'By Allah, it was not but that I saw that Allah had opened the heart of Abū Bakr to fighting, and I knew that it was the truth.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān, and he said: And al-Layth narrated to me — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Khālid, meaning Ibn Musāfir, from Ibn Shihāb, meaning with that. Al-Bukhārī said in another place in the book: Ibn Bukayr and ʿAbd Allāh narrated to me from al-Layth, meaning from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb: ""ʿanāq (a young she-goat).""

The Shaykh said: And Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd differed with them both, from al-Layth, from ʿUqayl, and said: ""ʿiqāl (a rope/cord)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7326,"(7325) Abū ʿAlī ar-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd informed us — al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from al-Zuhrī, and he mentioned the hadith and said: ""By Allah, if they withhold from me an ʿiqāl."" Abū Dāwūd said: And Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah, Maʿmar, and al-Zubaydī narrated from al-Zuhrī in this hadith: ""If they withhold from me an ʿanāq."" And Rabbāḥ ibn Zayd narrated from Maʿmar, from al-Zuhrī: ""ʿiqāl."" The Shaykh said: And in another narration from Rabbāḥ: ""ʿanāq.""

Abū Dāwūd said: And Ibn Wahb narrated from Yūnus, from al-Zuhrī, who said: ""ʿiqāl."" And ʿAnbasah narrated from Yūnus, from al-Zuhrī in this hadith, who said: ""ʿanāq.""

Abū Dāwūd said: Abū ʿUbaydah Maʿmar ibn al-Muthannā said: ""Al-ʿiqāl is the ṣadaqah of one year, and al-ʿiqālān is the ṣadaqah of two years.""

The Shaykh said: And the ʿanāq is not conceivable to be taken except in what we have mentioned, and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned]"
7327,"(7326) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from Ayyūb: A shaykh from Banī Sadūs — called Daisam — narrated from Bashīr ibn al-Khaṣāṣiyyah, and the Prophet ﷺ had named him Bashīr. He said: We came to him and said: ""Indeed, the collectors of ṣadaqah act aggressively toward us, so we conceal from them the amount by which they exceed against us."" He said: ""No. Rather, gather it (your livestock), and when they take it, instruct them to invoke blessings upon you."" Then he recited: {And pray over them} [Ṣaʿīf (Weak). Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]."
7328,"(7327) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī and Yaḥyā ibn Mūsā said: ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from Maʿmar, with the same chain and meaning, except that he said: We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, indeed the collectors of ṣadaqah…"" And it was narrated by Ḥammād ibn Zayd from Ayyūb, but he did not raise it (to the Prophet ﷺ). [Ṣaʿīf (Weak). Preceded earlier]."
7329,"(7328) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī informed us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Maʿmar narrated from Bahz ibn Ḥakīm ibn Muʿāwiyah, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""For every forty camels that graze freely, a bint labūn (a two-year-old she-camel) is due. Whoever gives it seeking reward shall have his reward. And whoever conceals it, We shall take it along with half his camels — a firm obligation from among the firm obligations of your Lord — which is not permissible for Muḥammad or the family of Muḥammad.""

A group narrated it similarly from Bahz ibn Ḥakīm. And most of them said: ""A firm obligation from among the firm obligations of our Lord.""

Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, and he said: al-Shāfiʿī said: The scholars of ḥadīth do not affirm that ṣadaqah is taken along with half the camels of one who withholds (his zakāh). Had it been established, we would have ruled by it.

The Shaykh said: This is a ḥadīth that Abū Dāwūd extracted in the Book of Sunan. As for al-Bukhārī and Muslim, may Allah have mercy on them, they did not extract it, in accordance with their practice that when a Ṣaḥābī or Tābiʿī has only one narrator, they do not include his ḥadīth in the two Ṣaḥīḥs. And Muʿāwiyah ibn Ḥaydah al-Qushayrī — they did not consider a trustworthy narration established from him other than that of his son, so they did not include his ḥadīth in the Ṣaḥīḥ. And Allah knows best.

The imposition of a penalty (gharāmah) upon one who stole was in the early period of Islam, then it was abrogated.

Al-Shāfiʿī cited as evidence for its abrogation the ḥadīth of al-Barāʾ ibn ʿĀzib regarding what his she-camel destroyed, and he did not transmit from the Prophet ﷺ in that story that he doubled the penalty; rather, he transmitted only his ruling of liability (ḍamān). So it is possible that this (ruling) was from that (earlier period). And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]."
7330,"(7329) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed it to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Nufaylī narrated to us: ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām narrated from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn, from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, who said: Anas narrated to him that Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote for him: ""This is the obligation of ṣadaqah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined upon the Muslims"" — and he mentioned the ḥadīth. And in it: ""Separate (herds) shall not be combined, nor shall combined (herds) be separated, out of fear of ṣadaqah. And whatever belongs to two partners, they shall settle between them on the basis of equality.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī. Al-Bukhārī said in the heading: And it is narrated from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet ﷺ, something similar to it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its extraction preceded]."
7331,"(7330) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Sharīk narrated from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Zurʿah, from Abī Laylā al-Kindī, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, who said: The zakāh-collector of the Prophet ﷺ came to us. I took his hand and read in his document: ""Separate (herds) shall not be combined, nor shall combined (herds) be separated, out of fear of ṣadaqah."" [Ḥasan for other than it. Its meaning was extracted]."
7332,"(7331) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdah informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Būshanjī narrated to me: al-Nufaylī Abū Jaʿfar narrated to us: Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah and from al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him — Zuhayr said: I think he attributed it to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ — and he mentioned the ḥadīth regarding the zakāh of silver, sheep, and camels, until he said: ""When they exceed by one — meaning beyond ninety — then there are two ḥiqqah (three-year-old she-camels) that have reached the age of bearing a stallion's offspring, up to one hundred and twenty. If the camels are more than that, then in every fifty there is a ḥiqqah, and in every forty there is a bint labūn. And combined (herds) shall not be separated, nor shall separate (herds) be combined, out of fear of ṣadaqah."" [Ḥasan. Its meaning was extracted]."
7333,"(7332) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Bazzāz narrated to us: Sharīk narrated from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Zurʿah, from Abī Laylā al-Kindī, from Suwayd ibn Ghaflah, who said: The zakāh-collector of the Prophet ﷺ came to us. I took his hand and read in his document: ""Separate (herds) shall not be combined, nor shall combined (herds) be separated, out of fear of ṣadaqah."" [Ḥasan for other than it. Its meaning was extracted]."
7334,"(7333) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Abū al-Aswad narrated to us: Ibn Lahīʿah narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, who said: I heard al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd say: I accompanied Saʿd ibn Abī Wāqqāṣ for a period of time, and I did not hear him narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except one ḥadīth, in which he said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Combined (herds) shall not be separated, nor shall separate (herds) be combined, in (the matter of) ṣadaqah. And the two partners (khalīṭān) are those who share a stud, a shepherd, and a watering place."" [Munkar. Al-Dāraquṭnī]."
7335,"(7334) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and others informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Sarī ibn Yaḥyā informed us: Qabīṣah narrated from Sufyān, from ʿUbaydullāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Whatever belongs to two partners, they shall settle between them on the basis of equality."" Sufyān said: I asked ʿUbaydullāh, ""What does he mean by al-khalīṭayn (the two partners)?"" He said: ""When they share one enclosure (murrāḥ), one shepherd, and one bucket (dalw)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]."
7336,"(7335) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us: al-Ḥajjāj ibn al-Minhāl informed us: Ḥammād narrated from Ḥumayd, who said: al-Ḥasan came to Makkah, and they asked him about forty sheep owned by two men. He said: ""In them (the zakāh due) is one sheep."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]."
7337,"(7336) Abū Bakr al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī informed us — Abū al-Azhar informed us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Ibn Jurayj informed us, he said: I asked ʿAṭāʾ about a mixed flock of forty sheep. He said: Upon them is one sheep (as ṣadaqah). I said: What if one partner has thirty-nine and the other has one sheep? He said: Upon both of them is one sheep."
7338,"(7337) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed among the last of them — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Baḥr ibn Nāṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī informed us — he said: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sālim, Mālik, Sufyān al-Thawrī, and Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed you that ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā al-Māzinī informed them from his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awqīyah of silver, no ṣadaqah on less than five awsuq of dates, and no ṣadaqah on less than five camels.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah.

Imām al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, said: The statement of the Prophet ﷺ indicates that five camels, five awqīyah of silver, and five awsuq of dates — if any one of them belongs to a free Muslim, then ṣadaqah is due on the wealth itself, not on the owner's (entire) wealth, for if the owner were to be in need of it, no ṣadaqah would be due upon him."
7339,"(7338) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed among the last of them — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — ʿAbd al-Majīd informed us from Ibn Jurayj, from Yūsuf ibn Māhik, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Seek (growth) in the wealth of the orphan, or the wealths of the orphans, lest the ṣadaqah consume or exhaust it.""

This is a mursal (disconnected) ḥadīth, except that al-Shāfiʿī, may Allah have mercy upon him, corroborated it by drawing evidence from the first ḥadīth and from what was narrated from the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, regarding that.

It has also been narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, as marfūʿ (attributed to the Prophet)."
7340,"(7339) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us — Abū ʿĀmir al-Dimashqī informed us — al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed me — al-Muthannā ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ informed me from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Beware! Whoever is the guardian of an orphan who has wealth, let him trade with it and not leave it for the zakāh to consume it.""

It was also narrated from Mandhal ibn ʿAlī, from Abū Isḥāq al-Shaybānī, from ʿAmr, with the same meaning. And al-Muthannā and Mandhal are not strong (in ḥadīth)."
7341,"(7340) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Fārisī informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us — ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us — Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim informed us from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Seek (growth) with the wealths of the orphans, lest the ṣadaqah consume it.""

This is a sound isnād, and there are corroborating narrations from ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him."
7342,"(7341) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us — Shuʿbah informed us from Ḥumayd ibn Hilāl, who said: I heard Abā Miḥjan — or Ibn Miḥjan — and he was a servant of ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ, who said: ʿUthmān ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: ""How much is your land worth? For I have the wealth of an orphan, and the zakāh is about to exhaust it."" He said: So he handed it over to him.

Thus it is in this narration. And Muʿāwiyah ibn Qurrah narrated it from al-Ḥakam ibn Abī al-ʿĀṣ from ʿUmar, and both of them preserved it.

And al-Shāfiʿī narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAmr ibn Dīnār and Ibn Sīrīn from ʿUmar, as mursal."
7343,"(7342) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us — Abū Nuʿaym informed us — Sufyān informed us from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from some of the children of Abū Rāfiʿ, who said: ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, used to pay the zakāh on our wealth while we were orphans."
7344,"(7343) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Muḥammad ibn Makhlad informed us — Bishr ibn Maṭar informed us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us — al-Ashʿath informed us from Ḥabīb ibn Abī Thābit, from Ṣalt al-Makkī, from Abū Rāfiʿ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ had granted Abū Rāfiʿ a piece of land. When Abū Rāfiʿ died, ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, sold it for eighty thousand and handed it over to ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him. He used to pay its zakāh. When the children of Abū Rāfiʿ took possession of it, they counted their wealth and found it deficient. They came to ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, and informed him. He said: ""Did you account for its zakāh?"" They said: ""No."" He said: ""So they calculated its zakāh and found it complete."" Then ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Did you think that I would have wealth with me and not pay its zakāh?""

And Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ and Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd narrated it from al-Ashʿath, and they said: from Ibn Abī Rāfiʿ — and that is the correct version."
7345,"(7344) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us — Muḥammad ibn Makhlad informed us — ʿAlī ibn Sahl ibn al-Mughīrah informed us — Muḥammad ibn Saʿīd al-Aṣbahānī informed us — Sharīk informed us from Abī al-Yaqẓān, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, that ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, used to pay the zakāh on the wealth of the children of Abū Rāfiʿ. He said: When he handed it over to them, they found it deficient. They said: ""We found it lacking."" ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""Do you think that wealth could be with me and I would not pay its zakāh?"""
7346,"(7345) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Mālik informed us from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, who said: ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, used to come to me, and I had an orphan brother in her care, and she used to take out the zakāh from our wealth."
7347,"(7346) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāʿif, from Ibn ʿUmar: that he used to pay zakāh on the wealth of the orphan. And this was narrated from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī and Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with them both. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Shāfiʿī]"
7348,"(7347) As for what ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Maʿmar ibn Sulaymān narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishr, from Layth ibn Abī Sulaym, from Mujāhid, from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: ""Whoever is entrusted with the wealth of an orphan, let him count the years for him, and when he returns his wealth to him, let him inform him of the zakāh due upon it. If he wishes, he may pay the zakāh, and if he wishes, he may leave it."" And Ibn ʿUlayyah and others narrated the same from Layth.

And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī said in a debate that took place between him and those who opposed him, and his response regarding this narration — although you claim that this is not established from Ibn Masʿūd on two grounds: one of them being that it is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected), and that the one who narrated it is not a ḥāfiẓ (precise memorizer).

The Shaykh said: The reason for its disconnection is that Mujāhid did not meet Ibn Masʿūd, and the narrator who is not a ḥāfiẓ is Layth ibn Abī Sulaym, and the scholars of hadith have deemed him weak. And it was narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās, except that Ibn Luḥayʿah is the one who uniquely reports it with this chain, and Ibn Luḥayʿah is not used as a proof. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf]"
7349,"(7348) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Ibn Numayr narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāʿif, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""There is no zakāh on the wealth of a slave until he is freed.""

This is the wording of the hadith of Ibn Numayr. And in the narration of Abū Muʿāwiyah: ""There is no zakāh on the wealth of a mamlūk (slave).""

And this was narrated from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, may Allah be pleased with him. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7350,"(7349) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhtawayh al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah and Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Whoever purchases a slave, his wealth belongs to the one who sold him, unless the purchaser stipulates otherwise."" Muslim reported it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī reported it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
7351,"(7350) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us: Shaybān and Jarīr narrated from Manṣūr, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nāʿif, from a man, who said: I asked ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, is there zakāh upon the slave?"" He said: ""No."" I said: ""Then upon whom is it?"" He said: ""Upon his owner.""

And it is narrated from Ibn Sīrīn, from Jābir al-Ḥaddāʾ, who said: I asked Ibn ʿUmar: ""Is there zakāh on the wealth of the slave?"" He said: ""In the wealth of every Muslim there is zakāh: five out of every two hundred, and whatever exceeds that is according to calculation."""
7352,"(7351) That is from what Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated from al-ʿUmarī, from Nāʿif, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""There is no zakāh on the wealth of the slave, nor on the mukātab (slave contracted for emancipation)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Reported by al-Razzāq]"
7353,"(7352) And from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us from Ibn Jurayj, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: ""There is no zakāh on the wealth of the mukātab, nor the slave, until he is freed.""

And that was narrated regarding the mukātab from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bazīʿ from Ibn Jurayj, raised to the Prophet (ﷺ), but it is weak, and the correct version is mawqūf (stopped at the Companion).

And it is the saying of Masrūq, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, ʿAṭāʾ, and Makḥūl. [Ḥasan. Reported by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7354,"(7353) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Zāhid narrated to us: Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) passed away, wealth came to Abū Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, from the direction of Ibn al-Ḍaḥramī.

Abū Bakr said: ""Whoever has a debt owed to him by the Prophet (ﷺ), or to whom the Prophet (ﷺ) made a promise, let him come to us."" Jābir said: I said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) promised me that he would give me such and such,"" and he spread his hands three times — I think he said: ""Take"" — so I scooped, and it was five hundred. Jābir said: ""He counted in my hand five hundred, then five hundred."" He said: ""And others added to him in the hadith that he said to Jābir: 'There is no ṣadaqah (zakāh) upon you for it until a full year passes over it.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī]"
7355,"(7354) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muazzakī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿUqbah, the mawlā of al-Zubayr: that he asked al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad about a mukātab of his whom he had contracted for emancipation for a great sum of wealth — ""Is there zakāh due upon him for it?"" Al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad said: ""Indeed, Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with him, would not take zakāh from any wealth until a full year had passed over it."" Al-Qāsim said: ""And when Abū Bakr would give the people their stipends, he would ask a man: 'Do you have any wealth upon which zakāh has become obligatory?' If he said: 'Yes,' he would take from his stipend the zakāh of that wealth. And if he said: 'No,' he would hand over his stipend to him and take nothing from it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Mālik]"
7356,"(7355) With this chain of narration, he said: Mālik narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us among others, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik narrated from ʿUmar ibn Ḥusayn, from ʿĀʾishah bint Qudāmah, from her father, who said: ""When I would come to ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, may Allah be pleased with him, to receive my stipend from him, he would ask me: 'Do you have any wealth in which zakāh has become obligatory?' If I said: 'Yes,' he would take from my stipend the zakāh of that wealth. And if I said: 'No,' he would give me my stipend.""

This is the wording of the hadith of al-Shāfiʿī. And in the narration of Ibn Bukayr, with the same meaning, except that he said: ""And if I said: 'No,' he would hand over my stipend to me and take nothing from it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Mālik]"
7357,"(7356) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, among others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik reported from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Zakāh is not due upon wealth until a full year has passed over it."" [Sound. Its meaning has been extracted.]"
7358,"(7357) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik reported from Ibn Shihāb, who said: ""The first person to collect Zakāh from the stipends (al-aʿṭiyāt) was Muʿāwiyah.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""And the stipends (al-ʿaṭāʾ) constitute a benefit, and there is no Zakāh upon them until a full year has passed over them."" [Sound. Mālik extracted it.]"
7359,"(7358) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-Qaṭīʿī informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to me: my father narrated to me: ʿAlī ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to me: Warqāʾ narrated from Abū al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent ʿUmar to collect the ṣadaqah.""

And he mentioned the hadith. Both [al-Bukhārī and Muslim] extracted it in the Ṣaḥīḥ.

And it is established from Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ appointed a man over the ṣadaqāt of Banī Sulaym, called Ibn al-Lutbiyyah. When he came, he settled his account."" And there are many narrations on this matter. [Sound. Muslim extracted it.]"
7360,"(7359) Muḥammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd reported from Ibn Shihāb that Abū Bakr and ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with them both, did not take the ṣadaqah in multiples, but rather they would send agents to collect it during times of drought and plenty, and during fatness and leanness, because the taking of it every year from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was a Sunnah.

And he narrated it in the older version from Ibrāhīm, adding: ""And they did not assign it to its people, nor did they delay its collection beyond every year."" [Sound. Al-Shāfiʿī extracted it.]"
7361,"(7360) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us: Abū al-Azhar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿd informed us: my father narrated to me from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb narrated to me from his father from his grandfather, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ addressed the people on the day of the Conquest [of Makkah]."" And he mentioned the hadith, and in it he said: ""There shall be no driving [of livestock from afar] (jalab), no scattering [to distant pastures] (janab), and their ṣadaqāt shall not be taken except in their own dwellings."" [Sound. Abū Dāwūd]"
7362,"(7361) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Ibrāhīm said: I heard my father say, regarding the words ""There shall be no driving (jalab) and no scattering (janab)"": ""It means that the livestock should be given as ṣadaqah in their own places and not be driven to the collector (al-muṣaddiq)."" And al-janab, according to this method, also means that their owners should not drive them to one side, and that a man should not be at the farthest location of the people of ṣadaqah so that they are driven to him; rather, it should be taken in its place."" [Sound. Abū Dāwūd extracted it.]"
7363,"(7362) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Ibn al-Mubārak reported from Usāmah ibn Zayd from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The ṣadaqāt of the Muslims shall be taken at their watering places or at their courtyards."" Abū Dāwūd was uncertain. [Sound. Al-Ṭayālisī extracted it.]"
7364,"(7363) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdād: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Ḥunayn informed us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad al-Azdī informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ al-Miṣrī informed us: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr reported from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr from ʿAmrah from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The ṣadaqāt of the people of the countryside shall be taken at their watering places in their courtyards."" This is the wording of the hadith of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṣāliḥ.

And in the narration of ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz: ""The ṣadaqāt of the Muslims shall be taken from their wealth at their watering places and their courtyards.""

And he said: from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm. [Sound. Al-Ṭabarānī extracted it in al-Awsaṭ.]"
7365,"(7364) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, among others, who said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik reported from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār from Abū Rāfiʿ that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ borrowed a young camel from a man. Then some camels from the camels of ṣadaqah came to him, and he commanded me to repay it to him. Muslim extracted it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Ibn Wahb from Mālik. [Sound. Muslim extracted it.]"
7366,"(7365) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād: Abū Jaʿfar — Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Razzāz — informed us: Aḥmad ibn Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb informed us: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Zakariyyā reported from al-Ḥajjāj ibn Dīnār from al-Ḥakam ibn ʤtaybah from Ḥujayyah ibn ʿAdī from ʿAlī that al-ʿAbbās, may Allah be pleased with him, asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ about paying his ṣadaqah before it became due, and he gave him permission to do so. [Sound due to corroborating narrations. Abū Dāwūd.]"
7367,"(7366) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us — Abū Dāwūd informed us — Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr mentioned it, and said: Abū Dāwūd said: This ḥadīth was transmitted by Hushaym from Manṣūr ibn Zādhān from al-Ḥakam from al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim from the Prophet ﷺ, and the ḥadīth of Hushaym is more authentic.

The Shaykh said: This is a ḥadīth in which there is a dispute regarding al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtaybah. Ismāʿīl ibn Zakariyyāʾ transmitted it from Ḥajjāj from al-Ḥakam in this manner, but Isrāʾīl contradicted him from Ḥajjāj, saying: from al-Ḥakam from Ḥujr al-ʿAdawī from ʿAlī. And he contradicted him in his wording, saying: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to ʿUmar: ""Verily, we have already taken from al-ʿAbbās the zakāh of the year — the first year.""

And Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allah — al-ʿArzamī — transmitted it from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās, in the story of ʿUmar and al-ʿAbbād, may Allah be pleased with them both.

And al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmārah transmitted it from al-Ḥakam from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah from Ṭalḥah. And Hushaym transmitted it from Manṣūr ibn Zādhān from al-Ḥakam from al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim from the Prophet ﷺ, in a mursal form, that he said to ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, in this story: ""Verily, we have taken in advance the ṣadaqah of the wealth of al-ʿAbbās for this year of ours — the first year."" And this is the most authentic of these transmissions. And it has been transmitted from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, from another route, as a marfūʿ ḥadīth. [Ḥasan on account of other chains; it was presented earlier.]"
7368,"(7367) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us — Abū ʿAlī al-Raffāʾ informed us — Muḥammad ibn Yūnus al-Kudaymī informed us — Wahb ibn Jarīr informed us.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us — ʿĪsā ibn Muḥammad informed us — Wahb ibn Jarīr informed us — my father said: I heard al-Aʿmash narrating from ʿAmr ibn Murrah from Abū al-Bakhtarī from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him. So he mentioned a story regarding the Messenger of Allah's ﷺ sending of ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, as a collector, and al-ʿAbbās's withholding of his ṣadaqah, and that he mentioned to the Prophet ﷺ what al-ʿAbbās had done. He ﷺ said: ""Do you not know, O ʿUmar, that the paternal uncle of a man is like his father? We were in need, so we borrowed from al-ʿAbbās the ṣadaqah of two years.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of al-Qaṭṭān.

In the transmission of Ibn Qatādah: that the Prophet ﷺ took in advance from al-ʿAbbās the ṣadaqah of one year or the ṣadaqah of two years. And in this there is an irsāl (broken chain) between Abū al-Bakhtarī and ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him.

And this meaning has been recorded in the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah from a sound route from him. [Ḥasan on account of other chains; al-Tirmidhī narrated it.]"
7369,"(7368) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar al-ʿAṭīʿī informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me — my father informed me — ʿAlī ibn Ḥafṣ informed us — Warqāʾ informed us — from Abū al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, to collect the ṣadaqah. It was said: Ibn Jamīl, Khālid ibn al-Walīd, and al-ʿAbbās, the paternal uncle of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, have withheld. So the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Ibn Jamīl resents nothing except that he was poor and Allah enriched him. As for Khālid, you wrong him, for he has kept his armour and prepared it in the cause of Allah. As for al-ʿAbbās, it is upon me, and the like of it along with it."" Then he said: ""O ʿUmar, did you not know that the paternal uncle of a man is like his father?""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from ʿAlī ibn Ḥafṣ with this wording, except that he said: ""and he prepared it"" (wa aʿtadahā). And Shābah transmitted it from Warqāʾ. And Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah transmitted it from Abū al-Zinād and said in the ḥadīth: ""It is upon him as ṣadaqah, and the like of it along with it.""

And from the ḥadīth of Shuʿayb, al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ. Then he said: Ibn Abī al-Zinād followed it up from his father, and Ibn Isḥāq said from Abū al-Zinād: ""It is upon him, and the like of it along with it.""

The Shaykh said: Just as Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq transmitted it, Abū Uways al-Madanī transmitted it from Abū al-Zinād, and thus it is in our view from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī al-Zinād from his father. And they understood it to mean that the Prophet ﷺ had deferred the ṣadaqah for two years out of need for al-ʿAbbās. And what Warqāʾ transmitted — that he had borrowed from him the ṣadaqah of two years — contains evidence for the permissibility of advancing the ṣadaqah.

As for what Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah transmitted, it is far-fetched to be the preserved version, because al-ʿAbbās was a man of the ṣalīb of Banī Hāshim, for whom ṣadaqah is forbidden. So how could the Messenger of Allah ﷺ make what was obligatory upon him of two years' ṣadaqah into ṣadaqah upon him?

And Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah transmitted it from Abū al-Zinād and said in the ḥadīth: ""It is for him, and the like of it along with it.""

And it may be said that ""for him"" (lahū) carries the meaning of ""upon him"" (ʿalayhī), so his transmission is carried to accord with the other transmissions. And what may be intended by his saying ""upon him"" is: upon the Prophet ﷺ, so as to agree with the transmission of Warqāʾ. And the transmission of Warqāʾ is more deserving of authenticity because of its agreement with the preceding explicit transmissions regarding the borrowing and advancing [of the ṣadaqah]. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī narrated it.]"
7370,"(7369) Abū Zakariyyāʾ ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Mālik informed us from Nāfiʿ: that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to send the ṣadaqah of al-fiṭr to the one at whose place it was collected, two or three days before al-Fiṭr. [Ṣaḥīḥ; Mālik narrated it.]"
7371,"(7370) Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Abī al-ʿAbbās al-Zawzanī informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rūḥ al-Madāʾinī and Muḥammad ibn Rumḥ al-Bazzāz both said: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Taymī informed us — he heard ʿAlqamah ibn Waqqāṣ saying: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb saying from the minbar: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""Verily, deeds are but by intention, and verily, each person shall have only what he intended. So whose migration was to Allah and His Messenger, his migration was to Allah and His Messenger. And whose migration was for the world he may attain, or for a woman he may marry, his migration was to that for which he migrated.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Yazīd ibn Hārūn and others, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from various routes from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ; al-Bukhārī.]"
7372,"(7371) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī and Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ both informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me — Sulaymān ibn Bilāl informed me — Sharīk ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Namir — from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār — from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent him to Yemen and said: ""Take grain from the grain, sheep from the sheep, camels from the camels, and cattle from the cattle."" [Ḍaʿīf; Abū Dāwūd narrated it.]"
7373,"(7372) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us — from Ibrāhīm ibn Maysarah — from Ṭāwūs, who said: Muʿādh — meaning Ibn Jabal — said in Yemen: ""Bring me a khamees or a labees, I will take it from you in place of the ṣadaqah, for that is easier for you and better for the Muhājirūn in Madīnah."" Thus said Ibrāhīm ibn Maysarah.

But ʿAmr ibn Dīnār contradicted him from Ṭāwūs, saying: Muʿādh said in Yemen: ""Bring me some garments, I will take them from you in place of the wheat and barley."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7374,"(7373) Abū Saʿīd informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī informed us — Yaḥyā ibn Ādam ibn Sulaymān informed us — Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us — from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, and he mentioned it. Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī said in what Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us from him: The ḥadīth of Ṭāwūs from Muʿādh is mursal, so there is no proof in it. And some of them said: ""From the jizyah in place of the ṣadaqah.""

The Shaykh said: This is what is most fitting regarding Muʿādh and most resembling what the Prophet ﷺ commanded him to do — taking the genus in the ṣadaqāt, and taking a dīnār or its equivalent of Maʿāfir garments in Yemen as jizyah. And that the ṣadaqāt should be returned to their poor, not transferred to the Muhājirūn in Madīnah, most of whom are people of war booty, not people of ṣadaqah. And Allah knows best."
7375,"(7374) As for what Mujālid transmitted from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim from al-Ṣunābī al-Aḥmasī, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ saw a mature she-camel among the camels of the ṣadaqah and became angry and said: ""May Allah fight the owner of this she-camel."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I acquired it in exchange for two camels from the peripheral camels of the ṣadaqah."" He said: ""Then very well, in that case.""

And this, as Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, is that Abū al-Walīd informed them — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān informed us — from al-Mujālid, and he mentioned it.

Abū ʿĪsā said: I asked al-Bukhārī about it, and he said: Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid transmitted this ḥadīth from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim: that the Prophet ﷺ saw among the camels of the ṣadaqah — mursal. And he declared Mujālid weak. [Ḍaʿīf; Ibn Abī Shaybah narrated it.]"
7376,"(7375) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us mursal — Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us — ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us — Abū ʿUbayd informed us — Hushaym informed us — from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khālid — from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim — from the Prophet ﷺ: that he saw among the camels of the ṣadaqah a tall she-camel, so he asked about it. The collector said: ""I took it in exchange for some camels."" So he remained silent. [Ḍaʿīf; it was presented earlier.]"
7377,"(7376) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Ṣamad al-Dimashqī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah narrated to me: Abū Ṣakhrah, the companion of al-ʿAbāʾ, narrated from Abū Saʿīd al-Maqburī, who said: I came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) with two hundred dirhams. I said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, this is the zakāh of my wealth."" He said: ""Have you been set free, O Kaysān?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then take it yourself and distribute it."" [Hasan. Narrated by Ibn al-Jaʿd.]"
7378,"(7377) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl narrated to us in Baghdād: ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Mukram narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, who informed me: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd informed me that Abū Hurayrah informed him: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) passed away and Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) was appointed as his successor, and those among the Arabs who disbelieved did so, ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Abū Bakr, how can you fight the people when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'I have been commanded to fight the people until they say: There is no deity but Allah. Whoever says: There is no deity but Allah has protected his life and his wealth from me, except by its right, and his reckoning is with Allah'?"" Abū Bakr said: ""By Allah, I shall surely fight whoever separates between the ṣalāh and the zakāh, for the zakāh is the right of wealth. By Allah, if they withhold from me a young she-camel that they used to give to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), I shall fight them for withholding it."" ʿUmar said: ""By Allah, it was not but that I saw Allah had opened the heart of Abū Bakr to fighting, and I knew that he was upon the truth."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah from al-Layth, and both said: ""ʿIqālan"" (a young she-camel).

And the ḥadīth of Bahz ibn Ḥakīm from his father from his grandfather from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): ""Whoever gives it seeking reward, for him is its reward, and whoever withholds it, then indeed We shall take it."" Its mention has already passed. [Ṣaḥīh. Meaning of its extraction.]"
7379,"(7378) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me in al-Fawāʾid: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Abū Usāmah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Ismāʿīl al-Sulamī narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hilāl al-ʿAbsī, from Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: Some Bedouins came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""Those who collect the ṣadaqah come to us and act unjustly towards us."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Please them."" They repeated this to him three times, and each time he said: ""Please them."" Jarīr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""After that, no collector of ṣadaqah came to me but that he departed pleased."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq from Abū Usāmah, and he narrated it through other chains from Muḥammad ibn Abī Ismāʿīl in its full length. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7380,"(7379) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Saʿdī narrated to us: Bishr ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Abū al-Ghuṣn narrated from Ṣakhr ibn Isḥāq, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Jābir ibn ʿAtīk, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There will come to you riders in a state of anger. When they come to you, welcome them and let them have what they seek. If they act justly, it is for their own souls, and if they act unjustly, then the burden is upon them. Please them, for the completeness of your zakāh lies in their satisfaction, and let them supplicate for you."" Abū Dāwūd narrated it, and he said: Abū al-Ghuṣn is Thābit ibn Qays ibn Ghuṣn.

The Sheikh said: This ḥadīth differs in its chain of narration from Abū al-Ghuṣn. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7381,"(7380) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Ṣādiq Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Ṣaydalānī both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū Bakr Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us: Yūnus ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to me: Hunayd, the freed slave of al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah, who was in charge of his wealth in al-Ṭāʾif, narrated: al-Mughīrah ibn Shuʿbah said: ""What do you do with the ṣadaqah of my wealth?"" He said: ""Part of it I give to the sulṭān, and part of it I distribute as charity."" He said: ""What do they do with it?"" He said: ""They purchase garments with it, marry women with it, and acquire lands with it."" He said: ""Then return it to them, for the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us to return it to them, and their reckoning is upon them."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7382,"(7381) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī narrated to us in Naysābūr, and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us in Baghdād, both saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Muʿādh ibn Muʿādh narrated to us: Ibn ʿAwn narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Pay the ṣadaqah of your wealth to those whom Allah has placed in authority over your affairs. Whoever acts righteously, it is for his own soul, and whoever sins, the burden is upon him."" [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7383,"(7382) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us. And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj narrated to us: Muṭayyin narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ṭarīf narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from ʿĀṣim, from Abū ʿUthmān, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When the collector of ṣadaqah comes to you, give him your ṣadaqah. If he acts unjustly towards you, turn your back to him and do not curse him. Say: 'O Allah, I seek Your reward for what he has taken from me.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
7384,"(7383) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb said: Saʿīd, meaning Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, was asked about the zakāh. He informed us from Qatādah, from Qazʿah, the freed slave of Ziyād, that Ibn ʿUmar said: ""Pay it to them, even if they drink wine with it"" — meaning the amīrs. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7385,"(7384) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh ibn al-Munādī narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Ḥasan ibn Saʿd al-Juhanī narrated to us, saying: I asked Zayd ibn Aslam about the zakāh. He said: ""Have you heard from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""He used to pay it to them"" — meaning the sulṭān — ""during the fitnah, and they would use it to feed their animals."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7386,"(7385) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us in it: Abū ʿAmr ibn Najayd narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Būshanjī narrated to us: Umayyah ibn Bisṭām narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Zurayʿ narrated to us: Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim narrated from Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ, from his father, that he came to Saʿd ibn Abī Waqqāṣ and said: ""I have some wealth that has come into my possession, and I wish to pay its zakāh. I know of a proper place for it, but these people do with it what you have seen."" He said: ""Pay it to them."" He said: ""I asked Abū Saʿīd the same thing and he said: 'Pay it to them.'"" He said: ""I asked Ibn ʿUmar the same thing and he said: 'Pay it to them.'""

And we have narrated similarly in this regard from Abū Hurayrah, Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with them all). [Hasan. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7387,"(7386) Abu Bakr — Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Isbahani al-Hafiz — informed us; Abu Nasr al-Iraqi — informed us; Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari — narrated to us; Ali ibn al-Hasan — narrated to us; Abdullah ibn al-Walid — narrated to us; Sufyan — from Sulayman al-Shaybani — from Abu Nasr — from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: A man asked Ibn 'Umar about the zakah of his wealth, so he said: ""Distribute it to them."" Sa'id ibn Jubayr said to him: ""A man from the people of Sham came to Bishr ibn Marwan and asked him, and he said: 'I passed by a perfumer woman in the market; had I had something, I would have given it to her.' So he said: 'O Ghadban, give her five hundred dirhams from the zakah.'"" Ibn 'Umar said: ""They have confused the matter for us; may Allah confuse the matter for them."" [Da'if]"
7388,"(7387) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq — informed us; Abu al-'Abbas — Muhammad ibn Ya'qub — informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman — informed us; al-Shafi'i — informed us; Anas ibn Iyyad — from Usamah ibn Zayd al-Laythi, that he asked Salim ibn 'Abdullah about zakah, and he said: ""You distribute it."" He said: ""Did not Ibn 'Umar say: 'Hand it over to the ruler'?"" He said: ""Yes, but I do not think that you should hand it over to the ruler."" [Hasan. Al-Shafi'i]"
7389,"(7388) Abu 'Amr al-Adib — informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili — informed us; al-Hasan, i.e., ibn Sufyan — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Khallad and Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna — both said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Ansari — narrated to us; my father — narrated to me; Thumamah — that Anas narrated to him: that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to him this book, and wrote: ""This is the obligatory charity (sadaqah) that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah commanded His Messenger to do."" Then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: ""And the zakah of sheep applies to those that are pasturing (sa'imah)."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from al-Ansari. And we narrated in the hadith of Hammad ibn Salamah from Thumamah in this hadith something similar to that. And we narrated it from Ibn 'Umar from a copy of the book of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""And in pasturing sheep, when they reach forty up to one hundred and twenty, one sheep is due."" [Sahih. Meaning of its extraction]"
7390,"(7389) Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Hafiz — narrated to us; Abu Zakariyya al-'Anbari — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'Abdi — narrated to us; Abu Salih — al-Hakim ibn Musa al-Qantari — narrated to us; Yahya ibn Hamzah — from Sulayman ibn Dawud — from al-Zuhri — from Abu Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn Hazm — from his father — from his grandfather — from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him: that he wrote to the people of Yemen a book, and he mentioned the hadith, and in it: ""And for every five pasturing camels, one sheep is due, until they reach twenty-four; and for every forty pasturing sheep, one sheep is due, until they reach one hundred and twenty. If they exceed by one, then two sheep are due."" [Da'if. Meaning of its extraction]"
7391,"(7390) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz — informed us; Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih — narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Mukram — narrated to us; Yazid ibn Harun — (H) and Ahmad ibn Salman al-Faqih — informed us, he said: it was recited to Muhammad ibn Maslamah al-Wasiti while I was listening; Yazid ibn Harun — narrated to us; Bahz ibn Hakim ibn Mu'awiyah al-Qushayri — from his father — from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, say: ""In every forty pasturing camels, one bint labun is due. Camels shall not be separated from their account. Whoever gives it seeking reward, he shall have his reward. And whoever withholds it, then we shall take it along with half of his camels, as a firm decree from among the firm decrees of our Lord. Nothing from it is permissible for the family of Muhammad."" [Hasan. Meaning of its extraction]"
7392,"(7391) Abu Sa'id al-Malini — informed us; Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi — narrated to us; Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Sufi — narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Musa al-Marwazi — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Hamza al-Raqqi — from Ghalib al-Qattan — from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb — from his father — from his grandfather — from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""There is no sadaqah on working camels."" Thus said Ghalib al-Qattan. And it has been narrated regarding cattle from Ibn 'Abbas in a marfu' narration, and from Mu'adh ibn Jabal in a mawquf narration, and in both of their chains there is weakness. And the most well-known narration in it is as a musnad and mawquf. [Munkar. Al-Daraqutni]"
7393,"(7392) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad — informed us; Abu 'Amr — 'Uthman ibn Ahmad ibn al-Sammak — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Dawud — narrated to us; Abu Badr — narrated to us; Zuhayr — that Abu Ishaq narrated to them — from 'Asim ibn Damrah — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""There is nothing due on working cattle."" [Munkar. Preceded before it]"
7394,"(7393) Abu al-Husayn — informed us; Abu 'Amr — narrated to us; Muhammad — narrated to us; Abu Badr — narrated to us; Zuhayr — narrated to us; Abu Ishaq — from al-Harith — from 'Ali — from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Nothing is due on working cattle."" Abu Badr Shuja' ibn al-Walid raised it from Zuhayr without doubt. And al-Nufayli narrated it from Zuhayr with doubt, and he said: Zuhayr said: ""I think it is from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him."" And others narrated it from Abu Ishaq as a mawquf narration. [Munkar. Preceded before it]"
7395,"(7394) Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl — informed us; Abu 'Amr ibn al-Sammak — narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn Abi Dawud — narrated to us; Abu Badr — narrated to us; Ali ibn Salih — narrated to us; Abu Ishaq — from 'Asim ibn Damrah — from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""Nothing is due on working cattle used for ploughing."" [Da'if]"
7396,"(7395) Abu al-Hasan — al-'Ala' ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Sa'id al-Mihrajani — informed us; Bishr ibn Ahmad — narrated to us; Hamzah ibn Muhammad al-Katib — narrated to us; Nu'aym ibn Hammad — narrated to us; Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash — from Abu Ishaq — from 'Asim ibn Damrah — from 'Ali, that he said: ""There is no sadaqah on working camels, nor on working cattle."" [Da'if. Preceded before it]"
7397,"(7396) Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Zakariyyā informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl informed us: my grandfather Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Zakariyyā ibn Yaḥyya ibn Abān narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us: Yaḥyya ibn Ayyūb informed us that Khālid ibn Yazīd narrated to him that Abū al-Zubayr narrated to him that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh say: ""There is no zakāh upon the animal used for ploughing the land.""

It is also narrated from Yaḥyya ibn Saʿīd, from Abū al-Zubayr, with the same meaning. And it is narrated from Ziyād ibn Saʿd, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, as a marfūʿ narration, but in its chain there is weakness, and the correct version is mawqūf. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
7398,"(7397) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Aṣbahānī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd al-Khāliq narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Rushdīn narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿUfayr narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated from Yaḥyya ibn Saʿīd, from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: ""Nothing is taken in zakāh from the cows that are used for ploughing.""

Khālid ibn Yazīd followed him in narrating it from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir, thus as a mawqūf narration, and it is a ṣaḥīḥ chain. This is the view of Mujāhid, Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, and Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī.

Al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī said: ""There is no ṣadaqah upon working cows when they are in a city (miṣr)."" [Very weak. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
7399,"(7398) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik ibn Anas informed us. And Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us — both of them from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥaraḍī informed us: Abū Muḥammad Dalaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijzī narrated to us, dictating: Mūsā ibn Abī Khuzaymah narrated to us: Yaḥyya ibn Yaḥyya said: I read before Mālik, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from ʿIrāk ibn Mālik, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah upon a Muslim regarding his slave or his horse.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyya ibn Yaḥyya, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī.]"
7400,"(7399) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdād informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Yaḥyya ibn Saʿīd al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Khuthaym ibn ʿIrāk narrated to me: my father narrated to me, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""There is no ṣadaqah upon a Muslim regarding his slave or his horse.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, from Yaḥyya al-Qaṭṭān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī.]"
7401,"(7400) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī informed me: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated from Khuthaym ibn ʿIrāk. He said: I heard my father say: I heard Abū Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah upon a Muslim regarding his slave or his horse.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah.

And Bukayr ibn al-Ashajj narrated it from ʿIrāk ibn Mālik. He said: I heard Abū Hurayrah, with a similar narration, regarding the slave.

Thus, the hearing (samāʿ) of ʿIrāk ibn Mālik from Abū Hurayrah is established and authentic, without any doubt. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its grading has preceded above.]"
7402,"(7401) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us: Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyya narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Khālid ibn Ḥāzim informed us: Yaḥyya ibn Abī Zāʾidah informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ṣāʿid narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Dāwūd narrated to us: Yazīd ibn Khālid ibn Mawhab narrated to us: Yaḥyya ibn Zakariyyā ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah upon horses and slaves, except that upon slaves there is the ṣadaqah of Fiṭr.""

The wording of both is the same. Thus it was narrated with this chain from ʿUbaydullāh. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
7403,"(7402) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā and Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyya ibn Fayyāḍ both said: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh narrated from a man, from Makḥūl, from ʿIrāk ibn Mālik, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""There is no zakāh upon horses and slaves, except the zakāh of Fiṭr upon slaves.""

This is the more authentic version. His ḥadīth from Abū al-Zinād is not preserved (maḥfūẓ). And Makḥūl did not hear it directly from ʿIrāk; rather, he narrated it from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from ʿIrāk. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its grading has preceded above.]"
7404,"(7403) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Sahl ibn Baḥr narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyya ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us: Sufyān narrated from Ayyūb ibn Mūsā, from Makḥūl, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, from ʿIrāk ibn Mālik, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""There is no ṣadaqah upon a Muslim regarding his slave or his horse.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its grading has preceded above.]"
7405,"(7404) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad narrated to us: Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated to me: Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated to me: Makḥūl narrated from ʿIrāk, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""There is no ṣadaqah upon a Muslim regarding his slave or his horse."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its grading has preceded above.]"
7406,"(7405) And with his chain, Usāmah narrated from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), a similar narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its grading has preceded above.]"
7407,"(7406) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥurḍī informed us; Abū Muḥammad Dalaj ibn Aḥmad ibn Dalaj al-Sijzī narrated to us by dictation; Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Zayd al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; from Abū ʿAwānah, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I have exempted you from the ṣadaqah (obligatory charity) of horses and slaves. So bring the ṣadaqah of silver: from every forty dirhams, one dirham; and there is nothing due upon one hundred and ninety; but when it reaches two hundred, five dirhams are due therein.""

Al-Aʿmash also narrated it from Abū Isḥāq as Abū ʿAwānah narrated it, and it was narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Abū Isḥāq likewise, in abbreviated form. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Ibn Mājah]"
7408,"(7407) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah and Sufyān al-Thawrī informed you from Abū Isḥāq al-Hamdānī, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he said: 'I have exempted you from (the ṣadaqah of) horses and slaves.'""

Al-Thawrī said in the hadith: ""So pay the zakāh of (your) wealth.""

And a group narrated it thus, and the hadith from Abū Isḥāq is from both of them together, from ʿAlī. [Ḥasan. Previously mentioned before it]"
7409,"(7408) And we have narrated in the ḥadīth of Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, from his father, from his grandfather, from the Prophet (ﷺ), in the letter he wrote to the people of Yemen: ""And indeed there is nothing due upon a Muslim slave nor upon his horse.""

Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed it to us; Abū Zakariyyā al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdullāh al-ʿAbadī narrated to us; al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Its meaning has been extracted]"
7410,"(7409) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Mutawakkil narrated to us; Baqiyyah narrated to us; Abū Muʿādh al-Anṣārī narrated to me, from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""I have exempted you from the ṣadaqah of al-jabḥah, al-kusʿah, and al-nukhah.""

Baqiyyah said: Al-jabḥah is horses; al-kusʿah is mules and donkeys; and al-nukhah are those (slaves) raised in the houses.

Thus narrated Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd from Abū Muʿādh, and he is Sulaymān ibn Arqaṁ, who is matrūk al-ḥadīth (abandoned in hadith), and no proof is established by him. There has been disagreement regarding its isnād; it was said thus, and it was said from him, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah. [Ḍaʿīf]"
7411,"(7410) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed it to us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us; Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us; Saʿīd ibn ʿUfayr narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh ibn Yazīd, Abū ʿAmr, narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Arqaṁ, from al-Ḥasan, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Samurah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah in al-kusʿah, al-jabḥah, and al-nukhah."" Abū ʿAmr explained it: al-kusʿah is the donkey, al-jabḥah is the horse, and al-nukhah is the slave.

And Kathīr ibn Ziyād, Abū Sahl, narrated it from al-Ḥasan, from the Prophet (ﷺ), as a mursal hadith. Abū Dāwūd extracted it in al-Marāsīl. [Ḍaʿīf]"
7412,"(7411) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, saying: Abū ʿUbayd said regarding the hadith of the Prophet (ﷺ): ""There is no ṣadaqah in al-jabḥah, nor in al-kusʿah, nor in al-nukhah.""

It was narrated from Ibn Abī Maryam, from Ḥammād ibn Zayd, from Kathīr ibn Ziyād al-Khurāsānī, who raised it (to the Prophet), and from other than Ḥammād, from Juwaybir al-Ḍaḥḥāk, who raised it.

Abū ʿUbayd said: Al-jabḥah is horses; al-nukhah is slaves; and al-kusʿah is donkeys.

Al-Kisāʾī and others said the same regarding al-jabḥah and al-kusʿah.

Al-Kisāʾī said: It is al-nukhah with a raised nūn, and he and others explained it in his session as working cattle.

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū ʿUbayd]"
7413,"(7412) Abū ʿUbayd said: Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us, from Ibn al-Darāwardī al-Madanī, from Abū Ḥazrah al-Qāṣṣ — Yaʿqūb ibn Mujāhid — from Sāriyah al-Khuljī, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Pay your ṣadaqāt, for Allah has relieved you of al-jabḥah, al-sajjah, and al-bajjah.""

He explained that they were idols that they used to worship in the Jāhiliyyah.

Abū ʿUbayd said: This contradicts what is in the first hadith, and the explanation (tafsīr) is in the hadith, and Allah knows best which of the two is preserved.

The Shaykh said: The chains of this hadith are weak, and in the authentic hadiths before it there is sufficiency, and with Allah is the success (tawfīq).

[Ḍaʿīf. See al-Gharīb]"
7414,"(7413) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār: that the people of Shām said to Abū ʿUbaydah ibn al-Jarrāḥ (may Allah be pleased with him): ""Take ṣadaqah from our horses and our slaves."" But he refused. Then he wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), and he also refused. So they spoke to him again, and he wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, and ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb wrote to him: ""If they wish, then take it from them and return it to them, and provide for their slaves.""

Mālik said: Meaning, return it to their poor. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Mālik]"
7415,"(7414) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Ṣaydalānī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us; Sufyān narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from Ḥārithah ibn Muḍarrib, who said: Some people from the people of Shām came to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""We have acquired wealth — horses and slaves — and we would like there to be in it zakāh and purification."" He said: ""What my two companions (the Prophet ﷺ and Abū Bakr) did before me, I shall do."" So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) consulted ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) among a group of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) (may Allah be pleased with them). ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""It is good, provided it is not a tax (jizyah) taken from them on a regular basis."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
7416,"(7415) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, who said: I asked Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab about the ṣadaqah of al-barādhīn (horses), and he said: ""Is there any ṣadaqah in horses?"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik]"
7417,"(7416) `Umar ibn `Abd al-`Aziz ibn Qatadah informed us that Abū `Amr ibn Nujayd informed us that Muhammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-`Abadī narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated to us that Mālik narrated from `Abdullāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn `Amr ibn Hazm that he said: A letter came from `Umar ibn `Abd al-`Aziz, may Allah be pleased with him, to my father while he was at Minā: ""Do not take zakah from horses or from honey.""

[Sahih. Reported by Mālik.]"
7418,"(7417) Through this same chain of narration, Mālik narrated from `Abdullāh ibn Dīnār who said: I asked Sa`īd ibn al-Musayyab about the zakh of al-barādhīn (Turkish horses). He said: ""Is there any zakah on horses?""

[Sahih. In meaning, as previously stated.]"
7419,"(7418) Muhammad ibn `Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Ṭāhir Muhammad ibn `Abdullāh al-Juwaynī informed us that Abū Bakr Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn Rajā' ibn al-Sindī narrated to us that Suwayd ibn Sa`īd narrated to us that Ḥafs ibn Mayṣarah narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam that Abū Ṣāliḥ Dhakwān informed him that he heard Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, say:

The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""There is no possessor of gold or silver who does not pay its due right…"" Then he mentioned the hadith regarding the threat concerning withholding its due right, and the due right of camels, cattle, and sheep. And regarding camels, he mentioned: ""And among their rights is milking them on the day they come to water."" Then he said: It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about horses?"" He said: ""Horses are of three kinds: for one man they are a burden (of sin), for another they are a reward, and for another they are a shield. As for the one for whom they are a burden: a man who keeps them out of showing off and pride and in opposition to the people of Islam — they are a burden for him. As for the one for whom they are a shield: a man who keeps them for the sake of Allah, then does not forget the right of Allah regarding their backs and their necks — they are a shield for him. As for the one for whom they are a reward: a man who keeps them for the sake of Allah for the people of Islam in a meadow or pasture. Whatever that meadow or pasture provides for them to eat, he will have recorded for him a number of good deeds equal to what they ate. And he will have recorded for him a number of good deeds equal to the number of their droppings and urine. And if they break their tether and gallop up one elevation or two, Allah will record for him a number of good deeds equal to the number of their tracks and their droppings. And if their owner passes by a river and they drink from it, without him intending to water them, Allah will record for him a number of good deeds equal to what they drank.""

It was said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what about donkeys?"" He said: ""Allah has not revealed to me anything regarding donkeys except this comprehensive, all-encompassing verse: {Whoever has done an atom's weight of good shall see it, and whoever has done an atom's weight of evil shall see it}.""

Muslim reported it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Suwayd ibn Sa`īd.

And Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ narrated from his father, and he said in the hadith: ""And he does not forget the right of Allah regarding their backs and their bellies, in times of hardship and ease."" And this does not indicate the obligation of zakah.

[Sahih. Reported by Muslim.]"
7420,"(7419) Abū al-Ḥasan `Aḥmad ibn `Abdān informed us that my father narrated to me that Abū `Abdullāh Muhammad ibn Mūsā al-Iṣṭakhrī narrated to us that Ismā`īl ibn Yaḥyā ibn Baḥr al-Azdi narrated to us that al-Layth ibn Ḥammād al-Iṣṭakhrī narrated to us that Abū Yūsuf narrated from Ghūrak ibn al-Ḥiṣrim, Abū `Abdullāh, from Ja`far ibn Muhammad from his father from Jābir who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Regarding grazing horses, for every horse there is one dīnār.""

Ghūrak is alone in narrating this.

And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us, and he said: `Alī ibn `Umar al-Ḥāfiẓ said: Ghūrak is alone in narrating this from Ja`far, and he is very weak, and those below him are among the weak narrators.

[Munkar. Reported by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7421,"(7420) Abū Naṣr `Umar ibn `Abd al-`Aziz ibn `Umar ibn Qatādah informed us that Abū `Amr Ismā`īl ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī informed us that Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn `Abdullāh narrated to us that Abū `Āṣim informed us that Ibn Jurayj informed me that `Amrad informed me that Ḥayy ibn Ya`lā informed him that he heard Ya`lā say: `Abd al-Raḥmān ibn Umayyah, the brother of Ya`lā, purchased a mare from a man for one hundred qalūṣ (a unit of currency). Then something became apparent to him, so the seller regretted it and came to `Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: ""Ya`lā and his brother have taken my horse by force."" So `Umar wrote to Ya`lā ibn Umayyah: ""Come to me."" So he came to him and informed him. He said: ""Horses have reached this value among you?"" He said: ""I did not know that any horse had reached this before."" `Umar said: ""We take one sheep from every forty sheep, and we take nothing from horses. So take one dīnār from every horse."" So he imposed one dīnār upon each horse.

And we have narrated in the chapter before it that which indicates that `Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, only commanded this when its owners desired it. If this narration is authentic, it would be interpreted in a similar manner so that the narrations may agree and not contradict one another. And the hadith of `Irāk from Abū Hurayrah is the most authentic of what has been narrated regarding this, and it conclusively negates zakah on them. And Allah knows best.

[Ḍa`īf. Reported by `Abd al-Razzāq.]"
7422,"(7421) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, Abū `Abd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī, Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn `Alī al-Fāmī, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris, among others, said: Abū al-`Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya`qūb narrated to us that Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us. He said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: ""Abdullāh ibn `Umar, Yaḥyā ibn `Abdullāh ibn Sālim, Mālik, Sufyān al-Thawrī, and Sufyān ibn `Uyaynah informed you that `Amr ibn Yaḥyā al-Māzinī narrated to them from his father from Abū Sa`īd al-Khudrī that the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: 'There is no zakah on less than five awqiyyah of silver, and there is no zakah on less than five wasq of dates, and there is no zakah on less than five dhawd (head) of camels.'""

[Sahih. Reported by al-Bukhārī.]"
7423,"(7422) He said: And Baḥr narrated to us. He said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: ""`Iyāḍ ibn `Abdullāh al-Qurashī informed you that Abū al-Zubayr narrated from Jābir ibn `Abdullāh from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, the same (hadith).""

[Sahih. Muslim.]

Al-Bukhārī reported it from the hadith of Mālik, and Muslim reported it from the hadith of Sufyān ibn `Uyaynah. And Muslim narrated it from Hārūn ibn Ma`rūf and Hārūn al-Aylī from Ibn Wahb from `Iyāḍ, and he mentioned the narration of Jābir."
7424,"(7423) Abū al-Ḥasan Muhammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-`Alawī informed us by dictation and recitation. Abū Naṣr Muhammad ibn Ḥamdawayh ibn Sahl al-Muṭawwi`ī narrated to us that `Abdullāh ibn Ḥammād al-Āmulī narrated to us that Nu`aym ibn Ḥammād, Abū `Abdullāh al-Fāriḍ al-Marwazī, narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Thawr narrated from Ma`mar from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, Ayyūb, Qatādah, and Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr from the two sons of Jābir from Jābir — all of them mentioned from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""There is no zakah on less than five wasq, and there is no zakah on less than five awqiyyah, and there is no zakah on less than five dhawd (head).""

[Sahih. Previously stated before it.]"
7425,"(7424) Abū `Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Sa`īd ibn Abī `Amr said: Abū al-`Abbās Muhammad ibn Ya`qūb narrated to us that al-Ḥasan ibn `Alī ibn `Affān narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us that `Abd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb narrated from Layth from Nāfi` from `Abdullāh ibn `Umar who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""There is no zakah on less than five wasq.""

[Sahih. For other than him.]"
7426,"(7425) Muhammad ibn `Abdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Zakariyyā al-`Anbarī narrated to us that Abū `Abdullāh al-`Abadī narrated to us that al-Ḥakam ibn Mūsā narrated to us that Yaḥyā ibn Ḥamzah narrated from Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd from al-Zuhrī from Abū Bakr ibn Muhammad ibn `Amr ibn Hazm from his father from his grandfather from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, that he wrote to the people of Yemen a document in which he mentioned: ""That which is watered by the sky, or is saḥīḥ (rain-fed) or ba`l (irrigated by rainfall on elevated land) — in it is the `ushr (one-tenth) when it reaches five wasq. And that which is watered by ropes and buckets — in it is half of the `ushr when it reaches five wasq.""

[Sahih for other than him. In meaning, its reporting.]"
7427,"(7426) Muhammad ibn Mūsā ibn al-Faḍl informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Yūnus, from al-Zuhrī, who said: I heard Abā Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf narrating in the gathering of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib: ""The established Sunnah has been that no ṣadaqah is taken from dates until their estimated yield reaches five awsuq."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarī]"
7428,"(7427) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Ayyūb ibn Muḥammad al-Raqqī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, Idrīs ibn Yazīd al-Awdī narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, from Abī al-Bakhtarī al-Ṭāʾī, from Abī Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who attributed it to the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""There is no zakāh on less than five awsuq. And one wasq is sixty sealed (measures).""

Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd also narrated it from Idrīs, and he said in the hadith: ""And one wasq is sixty ṣāʿ."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its meaning has been referenced]"
7429,"(7428) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Wakīʿ narrated from Sharīk, from Layth, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""One wasq is sixty ṣāʿ."" Yaḥyā said: ""I asked Sharīk about it, but he did not retain it."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7430,"(7429) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā informed me, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Yaʿqūb ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ, from Qatādah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: ""One wasq is sixty ṣāʿ."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7431,"(7430) And through this chain of narration from Yaʿqūb ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ, from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: ""Zakāh is due on five awsuq, and that is three hundred ṣāʿ."" He said: ""And one wasq is sixty ṣāʿ.""

And it has been narrated to us from al-Ḥasan, al-Shaʿbī, al-Nakhaʿī, and others. The discussion regarding the exact measure of the ṣāʿ will come at the end of this book, in shāʾ Allāh taʿālā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7432,"(7431) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʢUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Faḍl narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ, from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from ʿAttāb ibn Asīd: ""The Prophet ﷺ used to send someone to estimate their grapevines and their fruits for them.""

The wording is from the hadith of Ibrāhīm. And in the narration of al-Shāfiʿī: ""He used to send someone to estimate the people's grapevines and their fruits."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī]"
7433,"(7432) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, Yaʿqūb ibn Ḥumayd ibn Kāsib narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated from Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Dīnār.

(Ḥ) And Ibn Kāsib narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Rajāʾ narrated from ʿAbbād ibn Isḥāq — both of them — from al-Zuhrī, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, from ʿAttāb ibn Asīd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Grapes are to be estimated just as dates are estimated, and their zakāh is to be taken as raisins, just as the zakāh of dates is taken as dried dates."" [Ḍaʿīf. Referenced previously]"
7434,"(7433) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated it with its chain of narration and its meaning. [Ḍaʿīf. Referenced previously]"
7435,"(7434) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh al-Mihrajānī informed us, Bishr ibn Aḥmad informed us, Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Naṣr al-Ḥaddāʾ narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us, Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, al-Zuhrī informed me, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded ʿAttāb ibn Asīd that grapes be estimated just as dates are estimated, and that their zakāh be paid as raisins, just as the zakāh of dates is paid as dried dates."" He said: ""That is the Sunnah of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ regarding dates and grapes."" [Ḍaʿīf. Referenced previously]"
7436,"(7435) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān, known as Abī al-Shaykh al-Aṣbahānī, informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manīʿ informed us, Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us, Yūnus narrated, who said: I heard al-Zuhrī say: I heard Abā Umāmah ibn Sahl narrating to us in the gathering of Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, who said: ""The established Sunnah has been that zakāh is not taken from dates or grapes until their estimated yield reaches five awsuq."" Al-Zuhrī said: ""And I do not know of any fruit being estimated except dates and grapes."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its meaning has been referenced]"
7437,"(7436) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr al-Ḥarashī narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Sahl Muḥammad ibn Naṣrwayh ibn Aḥmad al-Marwazī informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ṭaghāmijī narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Tirmidhī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us, in Makkah, in the year one hundred and twenty-five: Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā, from ʿAbbās ibn Sahl al-Sāʿidī, from Abī Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) during the expedition of Tabūk. We came to Wādī al-Qurā at a garden belonging to a woman. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Estimate it."" So we estimated it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) estimated it at ten wasqs. And he said: ""Count it until we return to you, if Allah wills."" We proceeded until we arrived at Tabūk. Then he mentioned the hadith and said: We then turned back until we came to Wādī al-Qurā, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) asked the woman about her garden: ""How much did its fruit amount to?"" She said: ""It amounted to ten wasqs.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Sulaymān and Wuhayb, from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7438,"(7437) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to the Jews of Khaybar when he conquered Khaybar: ""I confirm you upon what Allah has confirmed you upon, on the condition that the dates are shared between us and you."" He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would send ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah (may Allah be pleased with him), and he would estimate the harvest for them. Then he would say: ""If you wish, it is yours, and if you wish, it is mine."" And they would take it.

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its chain. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7439,"(7438) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) would send ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah, and he would estimate between him and the Jews. He said: They gathered for him jewellery from the jewellery of their women and said: ""This is for you; lighten our burden and be lenient in the division."" ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O assembly of Jews, by Allah, you are among the most hated of Allah's creation to me, but that does not compel me to be unjust to you. As for what you have offered of bribery, it is unlawful (suḥt), and we do not consume it."" They said: ""By this the heavens and the earth stand.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its chain. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7440,"(7439) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū Yaʿlā narrated to us: Abū Khaythamah narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Sābiq narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān narrated to us, from Abī al-Zubayr, from Jābir, who said: Allah granted the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) the Banū al-Naḍīr as booty. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) confirmed them upon what they had been upon and made it shared between him and them. He then sent ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah, and he estimated it for them. Then he said to them: ""O assembly of Jews, you are the most hated of people to me. You killed the prophets of Allah and lied against Allah. Yet my hatred for you does not compel me to be unjust to you. I have estimated for you twenty thousand wasqs of dates. If you wish, it is yours, and if you refuse, it is mine."" They said: ""By this the heavens and the earth stand."" They said: ""We have taken it, so depart from us.""

[Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7441,"(7440) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn narrated to us: Ḥajjāj narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, who said: I was informed from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she said, mentioning the affair of Khaybar: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would send ʿAbd Allāh ibn Rawāḥah to the Jews, and he would estimate the palm trees when they ripened before they were eaten. Then he would give the Jews the choice: they could take it according to that estimate, or they could hand it over to them according to that estimate, so that the zakāh could be calculated before the fruits were eaten and dispersed.

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7442,"(7441) Maʿmar narrated, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Ṭāwūs, from Abī Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) would say to the estimators (khurrāṣ): ""Do not estimate the ʿarāyā (gifts of dates).""

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
7443,"(7442) And Saʿīd ibn Sālim al-Qaddāḥ informed us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Fuṭayr al-Anṣārī, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not estimate the ʿarāyā, nor did Abū Bakr, nor ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both).

The Shaykh said: Both of these are mursal. A connected (mawṣūl) ḥadīth has been narrated regarding it.

[Ḍaʿīf.]"
7444,"(7443) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Wahb ibn Jarīr narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, in the Kitāb al-Sunan: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Khubayb ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Masʿūd, who said: Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah came to our gathering and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us, saying: ""When you estimate, take and leave one-third. And if you do not leave one-third, then leave one-quarter.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7445,"(7444) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muʿāwiyah al-Naysābūrī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Muslim ibn Wārah informed me: ʿĀṣim ibn Yazīd al-ʿUmarī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Mughīth al-Jurashī narrated to us, from al-Ṣalt ibn Zayd al-Mazanī, who heard him narrating from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) appointed him over the estimation (kharṣ) and said: ""Secure for us half and leave for them half, for they steal and do not maintain ties of kinship."" Muḥammad said: I narrated this ḥadīth to ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, and he said: ""It has been established with us that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Secure for us two-thirds and leave for them one-third.'""

The Shaykh said: This isnad is unknown (majhūl). It has also been narrated regarding it from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him).

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym.]"
7446,"(7445) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī, the grandson of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī, informed us: My grandfather informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr Kashmard narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us: Sulaymān, that is Ibn Bilāl, narrated to us: Yaḥyā, that is Ibn Saʿīd, narrated to us, from Bushayr ibn Yasār, that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb used to send Abū Ḥathmah as an estimator to estimate the palm trees. He would instruct him: When you find the people in their garden estimating it, leave for them what they will eat, and do not include it in the estimate.

(T) And al-Shāfiʿī mentioned it in al-Qadīm from a man from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd. And Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated it from Yaḥyā as a connected (mawṣūl) ḥadīth.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7447,"(7446) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us; Abū al-Muthannā informed us; Musaddad informed us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Bushayr ibn Yasār, from Sahl ibn Abī Ḥathmah: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) sent him to estimate the dates and said: ""When you come to a land, estimate it, and leave for them the amount they will eat.""
Al-Awzāʿī mentioned it from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) as a mursal narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ḥākim]"
7448,"(7447) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed him; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us; Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; Abū ʿĀmir informed us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed us; Abū ʿAmr, meaning al-Awzāʿī, narrated:
that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Be lenient with the people in estimation, for indeed among them are the ʿariyyah, the waṭiyyah, and the uklah."" Al-Walīd said: I said to Abū ʿAmr, ""What is the ʿariyyah?"" He said: ""A date palm, two, or three that a man grants to a man from among the people of need."" I said: ""And what is the uklah?"" He said: ""The owners of the wealth eat from it fresh dates, so that is not estimated and is deducted from its estimation."" I said: ""And what is the waṭiyyah?"" He said: ""Those who visit them and come to them.""
The Sheikh said: This wording narrated by al-Awzāʿī from ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding leniency has also been narrated by Makḥūl from the Prophet (peace be upon him) as a mursal narration."
7449,"(7448) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed him; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us; Baḥr ibn Naṣr informed us; it was read to Ibn Wahb: Muslim ibn Khālid and al-Qāsim ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed you from Ḥarām ibn ʿUthmān, from Abī ʿAtīq, from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh that the Prophet (peace be upon him) said: ""Exercise caution for the people of wealth regarding the waṭiyyah (guests), the ʿāmilah (workers), the nawāʾib (calamities), and what is obligatory in dates of the right (zakāh)."" [Very weak. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
7450,"(7449) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us; Sharīḥ ibn ʿAqīl informed us; Abū Marwān al-ʿUthmānī informed us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated from Ḥarām ibn ʿUthmān, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān and Muḥammad, the two sons of Jābir, from their father, and he mentioned it as marfūʿ.
It has also been narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him) in a mursal hadith: ""There is no ṣadaqah (zakāh) on the ʿarāyā."" [Very weak. Previously mentioned]"
7451,"(7450) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us; Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ informed us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Ibn Jurayj informed us; ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmārah informed me from his father Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmārah, who said: I heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī say: I heard the Prophet (peace be upon him) say, and the Prophet (peace be upon him) gestured with his hand with five fingers: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāq, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five awsuq, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five dhawd (camels).""
And it was additionally narrated from the Prophet (peace be upon him) in this hadith: ""And there is no ṣadaqah on the ʿarāyā,"" from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān.
The Sheikh said: Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān narrates the hadith of the awāq, the awsuq, and the adhwād from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmārah from Abū Saʿīd, so it is possible that this addition was part of the hadith along with it. And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
7452,"(7451) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Abū al-Qāsim Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Lakhmī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us; Abū Ḥudhayfah informed us; Sufyān narrated from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā, from Abī Burdah, from Abū Mūsā and Muʿādh ibn Jabal: that the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) sent them to Yemen and commanded them to teach the people the matters of their religion. And he said: ""Do not take ṣadaqah (zakāh) except from these four categories: barley, wheat, raisins, and dates."" [Ḥasan for other than it. Al-Dāraquṭnī]"
7453,"(7452) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us; al-Ashjaʿī narrated from Sufyān ibn Saʿīd, from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā, from Abī Burdah, from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī and Muʿādh (may Allah be pleased with them both): that when they were sent to Yemen, they did not take ṣadaqah (zakāh) except from wheat, barley, dates, and raisins."
7454,"(7453) Abū ʿAbd Allāh, Abū Bakr, and Abū Saʿīd informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Ḥasan informed us; Yaḥyā informed us; Wakīʿ informed us; Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā narrated from Abī Burdah, from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī: that when he came to Yemen, he did not take ṣadaqah (zakāh) except from wheat, barley, dates, and raisins. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7455,"(7454) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥumayd al-Ruʾāsī narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Nujayḥ al-Saʿdī al-Madanī, from Bishr ibn ʿĀṣim and ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aws: that Sufyān ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Thaqafī wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him), and he was his governor over al-Ṭāʾif, and he wrote to him: ""Before me are gardens containing vineyards, and among them are peaches and pomegranates that yield a produce many times greater than the vineyards."" He wrote to him seeking his counsel regarding the ʿushr (tithe). So ʿUmar wrote to him: ""There is no ʿushr upon them, for they are from the ʿiḍāh (thorny trees), and there is no ʿushr upon them.""
This is the view of Mujāhid, al-Ḥasan, al-Nakhaʿī, and ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, and it has been narrated from the seven jurists among the Tābiʿīn of the people of Madīnah. [Weak. Al-Albānī commented on it]"
7456,"(7455) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us: that he asked Ibn Shihāb about olives, and he said: ""There is ʿushr (tithe) on them."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik]"
7457,"(7456) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, Abū ʿAmr — who is al-Awzāʿī — informed me that Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī said: ""The established practice (sunnah) regarding the zakāh of olives is that it is taken from the one who presses his olives at the time he presses them. For that which is irrigated by the sky and the rivers, the ʿushr (one-tenth) is due; and for that which is irrigated by means of the water-drawer (al-nāḍiḥ), half of the ʿushr is due.""

He said: And al-Walīd narrated to me that ʿUthmān ibn ʬāʾiʾ narrated from his father, ʬāʾ al-Khurāsānī, that when ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb arrived at al-Jābiyyah, the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) brought to his attention that they differed regarding the ʿushr of olives. ʿUmar said: ""In it is the ʿushr, when its fruit reaches five awsuq — he pressed it and took the ʿushr of its oil.""

The hadith of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) in this chapter is munqaṭiʿ (discontinuous), and its narrator is not strong. The most authentic narration in this regard is the statement of Ibn Shihāb al-Zuhrī. And the hadith of Muʿādh ibn Jabal and Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī is more elevated and more deserving of being acted upon, and Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7458,There is no hadith under this chapter.
7459,"(7457) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us, Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad ibn Yarḥam al-Ṭūsī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Abī Salamah narrated from Ṣadaqah ibn ʿAbd Allāh from Mūsā ibn Yasār from Nāʿif from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet (peace be upon him), who said: ""In every ten azqāq (skins) of honey, one ziq (skin) is due.""

Ṣadaqah ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Samīn is the only one who narrated it in this manner, and he is weak (ḍaʿīf). Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal and Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn and others declared him weak.

Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī said: ""I asked Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī about this hadith, and he said: It is a mursal hadith from Nāʿif from the Prophet (peace be upon him)."""
7460,"(7458) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak — informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated from Sulaymān ibn Mūsā from Abū Sayyārah al-Mutʿī, who said: ""I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, I have bees (honey).' He said: 'Pay the ʿushr (one-tenth).' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, protect for me its mountain.' So he protected it for me.""

This is the most authentic narration regarding the obligation of the ʿushr in honey, though it is munqaṭiʿ (discontinuous).

Abū ʿĪsā al-Tirmidhī said: ""I asked Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī about this, and he said: 'This is a mursal hadith, and Sulaymān ibn Mūsā did not meet any of the Companions of the Prophet (peace be upon him). And there is nothing authentic regarding the zakāh of honey.'""

Al-Bukhārī said: ""ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥarrar is matrūk al-ḥadīth (abandoned in hadith), meaning thereby the weakness of his narration from al-Zuhrī from Abī Salamah from Abī Hurayrah, marfūʿ, regarding honey."" [Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Mājah]"
7461,"(7459) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Isḥāq — Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim al-Zāhid — narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbbād al-Dabarī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥarrar from al-Zuhrī from Abī Salamah from Abī Hurayrah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) wrote to the people of Yemen that the ʿushr (one-tenth) should be taken from honey."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
7462,"(7460) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Abī Shuʿayb al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Aʿyan narrated from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Miṣrī from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather, who said: ""Hilāl, one of the Banī Mutʿan, came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) with the ʿushr (tithe) of his bees' honey and asked him to protect for him a valley called Salabah. So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) protected that valley for him. When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) became caliph, Sufyān ibn Wahb wrote to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) asking him about that. So ʿUmar wrote: 'If he pays you what he used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) from the ʿushr of his bees' honey, then protect Salabah for him. Otherwise, it is merely the fly of the rain — whoever wishes may eat from it.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Abū Dāwūd]"
7463,"(7461) Abū ʿAlī informed us, Muḥammad narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah al-Ḍabbī narrated to us, al-Mughīrah — whose lineage is Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith al-Makhzūmī — narrated to me, my father narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather: that Shabābah, a clan from Fahm, and he mentioned something similar to it, and he said: ""From every ten qirb (skins), one qirbah (skin)."" And Sufyān ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Thaqafī said: ""He used to protect for them two valleys."" He added: ""So they paid to him what they used to pay to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), and he protected their two valleys for them.""

It was also narrated by Usāmah ibn Zayd from ʿAmr, similarly. [Ḍaʿīf. Previously mentioned]"
7464,"(7462) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhubāb from his father from Saʿd ibn Abī Dhubāb, who said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and accepted Islam. Then I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, grant my people, upon their acceptance of Islam, their wealth.' So the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) did so and appointed me as an agent over them. Then Abū Bakr appointed me, and then ʿUmar appointed me."" He said: ""And Saʿd was from the people of al-Sarāh."" He said: ""So I spoke to my people about honey and said to them: 'Give its zakāh, for there is no good in a fruit that is not purified (by zakāh).' They said: 'How much?' I said: 'The ʿushr (one-tenth).' So I took the ʿushr from them and came to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him of what had transpired. So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) took it, sold it, and placed its price in the charitable funds of the Muslims."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad]"
7465,"(7463) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbbād al-Makkī narrated to us, Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ — Abū Ḍamrah — narrated it similarly.

It was also narrated by ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Isḥāq from Ibn Abī Dhubāb from his father from his grandfather regarding honey. And it was narrated by al-Ṣalt ibn Muḥammad from Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ from al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Dhubāb from Munīr — who is Ibn ʿAbd Allāh — from his father from Saʿd.

And similarly, it was narrated by Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsā from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhubāb from Munīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh from his father from Saʿd. [Ḍaʿīf. Previously mentioned]"
7466,"(7464) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Nājiyah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsā al-Zuhrī narrated to us, al-Ḥārith ibn Abī Dhubāb narrated from Munīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh from his father from Saʿd ibn Abī Dhubāb. Ibn Nājiyah said to us: ""Thus he said,"" and he mentioned the hadith with its meaning.

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us, he said: ""I heard Ibn Ḥammād say: Al-Bukhārī said: 'ʿAbd Allāh, the father of Munīr, from Saʿd ibn Abī Dhubāb — his hadith is not authentic (lam yaṣiḥḥ).'""

Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Isfarāyinī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Barāʾ informed us, he said: ""ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī said regarding this hadith: 'This Munīr — we do not know him except through this hadith.'"" [Ḍaʿīf. Previously mentioned]"
7467,"(7465) Abū Saʿīd — Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Khaṭīb — informed us, Abū Baḥr al-Barbahārī narrated to us, Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Mayṣarah narrated from Ṭāwūs, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), that he was brought a calf of a cow and honey. I think he said: Muʿādh (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Regarding both of these, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not command me to take anything."" [Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī]"
7468,"(7466) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Mālik narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr, who said: A letter came from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz to my father while he was at Minā, stating: ""Do not take zakāh from horses or honey.""

Al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said: Saʿd ibn Abī Dhubāb narrates that which indicates that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not command him to take zakāh from honey. Rather, it is something concerning which it was considered that zakāh should be due, and then it was imposed upon the people as an additional obligation.

Al-Zaʿfarānī said: Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Shāfiʿī said: The hadith stating that there is a tenth (ʿushr) in honey is weak, and the hadith stating that the tenth should not be taken from it is also weak, except from ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz. My preference is that nothing be taken from it, because the Sunnah and the established narrations are confirmed regarding what is taken from it, and there are no confirmed narrations regarding it [i.e., honey], so it is as though it is exempt. [Weak. Reported by Mālik]"
7469,"(7467) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ḥusayn ibn Zayd narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""There is no zakāh on honey."" Yaḥyā said: al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ was asked about honey, and he did not see anything [i.e., any zakāh] in it. [Weak]"
7470,"(7468) It is narrated from Muʿādh that he did not take anything from honey. Abū Bakr al-Ardistānī informed us, Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī narrated to us, Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Walīd narrated from Sufyān, from Ibrāhīm ibn Mayṣarah, from Ṭāwūs, who said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) sent Muʿādh to Yemen,"" and he mentioned something similar to it. [Weak]"
7471,"(7469) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah, from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah, from Abū Saʿīd, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), who said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on grain or dates unless it reaches five awsuq.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd ibn Ḥumayd, from Yaḥyā ibn Ādam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its meaning was referenced previously]"
7472,"(7470) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Aḥmad ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad narrated to us, my father narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, Sufyān narrated it with his chain of narration, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on grain or dates until it reaches five awsuq, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five camels, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāq [of silver].""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī."
7473,"(7471) Abū Saʿd al-Zāhid (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us, Abū ʿAlī Ḥāmid ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Harawī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr al-Ḍabbī narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Muslim, i.e., al-Ṭāʾifī, narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh and Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who both said: The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on crops, nor on grapes, nor on dates, unless it reaches five awsuq, and that is one hundred faraq."" [Ḥasan. Reported by Dāra Quṭnī]"
7474,"(7472) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāniʾ narrated to us, al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Muslim narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""There is no zakāh upon a Muslim man for his grapes or his crops if they are less than five awsuq."" [Ḥasan. Referenced before it]"
7475,"(7473) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ibn Abī Zāʾidah narrated from Dāwūd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ, who said: ""Wheat and barley should not be combined together, nor should dates and raisins, in the matter of ṣadaqah, when each one of them does not reach five awsuq."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7476,"(7474) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Sinān narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān, from Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah, who said: ""We have the written document of Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him) from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that he only took ṣadaqah from wheat, barley, raisins, and dates."" [Ḥasan for other than it. Reported by Aḥmad]"
7477,"(7475) It was narrated by 'Abdullah ibn al-Walid al-'Adani from Sufyan, who added in it: Hajjaj sent Musa ibn al-Mughirah to collect zakah on greens and crops. He intended to take zakah from fresh vegetables and produce. Musa ibn Talhah said: ""We have a written document from Mu'adh from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, in which he commanded him to take zakah only from wheat, barley, dates, and raisins."" He then wrote to Hajjaj about this, and Hajjaj replied: ""He has spoken the truth.""

It was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Isbahani, who was informed by Abu Nasr al-'Iraqi, who narrated from Sufyan ibn Muhammad al-Jawhari, who narrated from 'Ali ibn al-Hasan, who narrated from 'Abdullah, and he mentioned it. [Hasan due to supporting narrations; it was preceded earlier.]"
7478,"(7476) It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, who narrated from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan, who narrated from Yahya ibn Adam, who narrated from 'Abdus-Salam ibn Harb, from 'Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, who said: Musa ibn al-Mughirah intended to take zakah from the vegetables of the land of Musa ibn Talhah. Musa ibn Talhah said to him: ""There is no zakah on vegetables."" He attributed this to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. They wrote about this to Hajjaj, and Hajjaj wrote back: ""Musa ibn Talhah is more knowledgeable than Musa ibn al-Mughirah."" [Da'if]"
7479,"(7477) It was narrated to us by Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz, who was informed by 'Abdur-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi in Hamadhan, who narrated from 'Umair ibn Mirdas, who narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Nafi' al-Sa'igh, who narrated to me from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah, from his uncle Musa ibn Talhah, from Mu'adh ibn Jabal, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""On that which is watered by the sky, by rainfall, and by natural flooding, the 'ushr (one-tenth) is due; and on that which is watered by irrigation, half of the 'ushr is due."" This applies only to dates, wheat, and grains.

As for cucumbers, watermelons, pomegranates, and fresh herbage (al-qadb), the Messenger of Allah ﷺ exempted them. [Sahih due to supporting narrations; it was narrated by al-Daraqutni.]"
7480,"(7478) It was narrated by Yahya ibn al-Mughirah from Ibn Nafi', who said: ""And al-qadb (fresh herbage) and vegetables are exempt — the Messenger of Allah ﷺ pardoned them."" It was narrated to us by Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih, who informed us that 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us, who said: 'Ali ibn Ahmad al-Azraq narrated to us in Egypt, who said: Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Naffah narrated to us, who said: Yahya ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, and he mentioned it with this addition. [Sahih due to supporting narrations; it was preceded earlier.]"
7481,"(7479) It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub, who narrated from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan, who narrated from Yahya ibn Adam, who narrated from Ghiyath al-Jazari, from Khusaif, from Mujahid, who said: ""There was no sadaqah (zakah) collected during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except on five things: wheat, barley, dates, raisins, and dhurah (maize)."" [Da'if]"
7482,"(7480) It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas, who narrated from al-Hasan, who narrated from Yahya ibn Adam, who narrated from Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah, from 'Amr ibn 'Ubayd, from al-Hasan, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not make zakah obligatory except on ten things: camels, cattle, sheep, gold, silver, wheat, barley, dates, and raisins.""

Ibn 'Uyaynah said: ""I think he also said: 'and dhurah (maize).'"" [Very Da'if]"
7483,"(7481) It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas, who narrated from al-Hasan, who narrated from 'Ubaydullah ibn Musa, from Sufyan, from 'Amr, from al-Hasan, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not make sadaqah (zakah) obligatory except on ten things."" He then enumerated them and mentioned sult (a type of barley) among them, but did not mention dhurah (maize). [Very Da'if; it was preceded earlier.]"
7484,"(7482) It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas, who narrated from al-Hasan, who narrated from Yahya ibn Adam, who narrated from Abu Bakr ibn 'Ayyash, from al-Ajla', from al-Sha'bi, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ wrote to the people of Yemen: 'Sadaqah (zakah) is only due on wheat, barley, dates, and raisins.'""

All of these hadiths are mursal (incomplete chains), except that they come through different routes, some corroborating others. Alongside them is the narration of Abu Burdah from Abu Musa, which has already passed in the chapter on date palms, as well as the statements of some of the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them. [Da'if]"
7485,"(7483) It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas al-Asam, who narrated from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan, who narrated from Yahya ibn Adam, who narrated from Hafs ibn Ghiyath, from Layth, from Mujahid, from 'Umar, who said: ""There is no sadaqah (zakah) on vegetables.""

A group narrated it from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym.

It has also been narrated through another chain as a mawsul (complete chain) from 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, in the chapter on date palms. [Da'if; it was narrated by Abu 'Ubayd.]"
7486,"(7484) It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas al-Asam, who narrated from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, who narrated from Yahya ibn Adam, who narrated from Qays ibn al-Rabi', from Abu Ishaq, from 'Asim ibn Damrah, from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""There is no sadaqah (zakah) on vegetables and produce."" Al-Ajla' corroborated it from Abu Ishaq.

It has also been narrated through another chain from 'Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, as a marfu' (elevated) narration traced back to the Prophet ﷺ.

It has also been narrated from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, in what she mentioned, that the Sunnah was established that there is no zakah on what the earth produces of vegetables.

It was narrated to us by Abu Sa'id, who narrated from Abu al-'Abbas, who narrated from al-Hasan ibn 'Ali, who narrated from Yahya ibn Adam, who narrated from Ibn al-Mubarak, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Ata', who said: ""There is no sadaqah (zakah) except on date palms, grapes, and grain. There is nothing due on any vegetables thereafter, and all fruits are subject to sadaqah."" [Da'if; it was narrated by Ibn Shaybah.]"
7487,"(7485) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed me. ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us. Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us. Ibn Wahb narrated to us. Yūnus informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, that he prescribed: for that which is irrigated by the sky and the springs, or which is ʿathrī (rain-fed), the ʿushr (one-tenth); and for that which is irrigated by manual drawing of water (naḍḥ), half of the ʿushr.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7488,"(7486) Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī narrated it from Ibn Wahb with this isnād. He said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""For that which is irrigated by the sky, the rivers, and the springs, or which is baʿl (naturally watered), the ʿushr; and for that which is irrigated by the sāwānī (water-drawing animals) or naḍḥ, half of the ʿushr.""

Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Hārūn ibn Saʿīd ibn al-Haytham al-Aylī narrated to us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Referred to previously.]"
7489,"(7487) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār informed us. Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us. Abū al-Ashʿath narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj. Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah informed me, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he used to say: the ṣadaqah (obligatory charity) of fruits and crops — that which consists of dates, grapes, or crops of wheat, barley, or salt (a type of grain) — if irrigated by a river, by a spring, or is ʿathrī (rain-fed, watered by rain), then in it is the ʿushr: one out of every ten. And that which is irrigated by naḍḥ (manual water-drawing) has half the ʿushr: one out of every twenty. And the Prophet ﷺ wrote to the people of Yemen — to al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Kulāl and those with him from Maʿāfir and Hamdān — regarding the believers: in the ṣadaqah of fruits (or he said, of property), the ʿushr is due on what the spring irrigates, and what the sky irrigates; and on what is irrigated by the bucket (gharb), half the ʿushr.

The Shaykh said: Thus I found it connected with the hadith. And his phrase ""regarding the believers"" indicates that it is not taken from the people of the dhimmah (non-Muslim subjects). And Allah knows best. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
7490,"(7488) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was read to Ibn Wahb: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed you that Abū al-Zubayr narrated to him that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh mention that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""For that which is irrigated by the rivers and the clouds, the ʿushūr (tenths); and for that which is irrigated by the sāniyah (water-drawing animal), half the ʿushr.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Saʿīd and others, from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7491,"(7489) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us. His father informed him: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us. Ibn Bukayr narrated to us. Mālik narrated to us, from the trustworthy one in his opinion, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār and from Busr ibn Saʿīd, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""For that which is irrigated by the sky and the springs, or is baʿl (naturally watered), the ʿushr; and for that which is irrigated by naḍḥ (manual water-drawing), half the ʿushr.""

Al-Shāfiʿī narrated it in al-Qadīm from Mālik, and he said in al-Jadīd: ""It has reached me that this hadith is connected from the hadith of Ibn Abī Dhubāb from the Prophet ﷺ, and I know of no one who contradicts it.""

He meant by that al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhubāb, for he narrates it from Sulaymān ibn Yasār and Busr ibn Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, as a connected (mawṣūl) narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mālik.]"
7492,"(7490) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī informed me. Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥarbī narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī say: Mālik ibn Anas abandoned the narration from Ibn Abī Dhubāb; there is no mention of him in his books, and he narrated nothing from him.

He said: and ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīl al-Ashjaʿī narrated to us. Al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Dhubāb narrated to us, from Sulaymān ibn Yasār and Busr ibn Saʿīd, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""For that which is irrigated by the sky, the ʿushr; and for that which is irrigated by naḍḥ (manual water-drawing), half the ʿushr.""

ʿĀṣim said: Mālik narrated to us, saying: ""I was informed from Sulaymān ibn Yasār and Busr ibn Saʿīd,"" and he omitted Ibn Abī Dhubāb due to the objectionable narrations (munkarāt) in his narration.

The Shaykh said: this hadith is self-sufficient without the narration of Ibn Abī Dhubāb, for we have narrated it through two sound chains from Ibn ʿUmar from the Prophet ﷺ, and through a sound chain from Jābir from the Prophet ﷺ. It is the position of the majority; they did not differ concerning it. And the hadith of ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm has been previously mentioned. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
7493,"(7491) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us. Abū Bakr ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us, from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd, from Abū Wāʾil, from Masrūq, from Muʿādh ibn Jabal, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ sent me to Yemen and commanded me to take from that which is irrigated by the sky, and that which is irrigated by baʿl (natural water), the ʿushr; and that which is irrigated by the dawālī (water-wheels/buckets), half the ʿushr.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Nasāʾī.]"
7494,"(7492) Abū Saʿīd informed us alone. Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us. Al-Ḥasan narrated to us. Yaḥyā narrated to us. ʿAmmār ibn Ruzayq narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""For that which is irrigated by the sky, or irrigated by fatḥ (open channels), the ʿushr; and that which is irrigated by the dalw (bucket), half the ʿushr.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7495,"(7493) He said: and Yaḥyā narrated to us. Isrāʾīl ibn Yūnus narrated to us, from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""That which is irrigated by the sky: one out of every ten; and that which is irrigated by the gharb (bucket): one out of every twenty.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Referred to previously.]"
7496,"(7494) Abū Saʿīd informed us. Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us. Al-Ḥasan narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us. Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ prescribed for that which is irrigated by the sky, or irrigated by the sayl (flood), the ghayl (stream), or baʿl (natural water), the ʿushr; and that which is irrigated by the nawāḍiḥ (water-drawing animals), half the ʿushr.

Ḥātim said: ""And al-ghayl is that which is irrigated by fatḥ (open channels); and al-baʿl is the ʿidhī (rain-fed land) which is watered by rainwater.""

Yaḥyā ibn Ādam said: ""And I asked Abū Iyās, meaning al-Asadī, and he said: 'Al-baʿl, al-ʿathrī, and al-ʿidhī are that which is irrigated by the water of the sky.'""

Yaḥyā said: ""Al-ʿathrī is that which is cultivated specifically for the clouds (i.e., rain), and is not irrigated except by water from rain — that is al-ʿathrī. Al-baʿl is that of the grapevines whose roots have gone deep into the earth to the water, so they do not need irrigation for five or six years; it is possible to abandon watering — that is al-baʿl. Al-sayl is the water of the valley when it flows. As for al-ghayl: it is a sayl less than the abundant sayl; when a small amount flows with clear water, that is al-ghayl. And al-ʿidhī is rainwater.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7497,"(7495) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ibn Mubārak narrated from Ibn Jurayj from ʿAṭāʾ: that he was asked concerning land that is irrigated by flowing water (al-sayḥ) and then irrigated by waterwheels (al-dawālī), or irrigated by waterwheels and then by flowing water — upon which of the two is zakāh assessed? He said: ""Upon the one by which it is irrigated more."" Yaḥyā ibn Ādam said: ""Zakāh is assessed according to its share (proportion)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7498,"(7496) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī narrated to us, Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar from Ibn Ṭāwūs from his father: that food would be taken out for him from his land, and he would give its ṣadaqah (zakāh), then he would hold it for a year or two without paying its zakāh, while he intended to sell it. Abū al-Rabīʿ said: ""Then I heard it myself afterwards from Ibn al-Mubārak."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Ibn Abī Shaybah narrated it.]"
7499,"(7497) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿAmr ibn Maymūn ibn Mihrān who said: I asked ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz about a Muslim who possesses land subject to kharāj (land tax) and is asked about zakāh, so he says, ""I am liable for kharāj."" He said: ""The kharāj is upon the land, and the zakāh is upon the produce."" He said: ""I asked him another time and he said the same."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū ʿUbayd narrated it.]"
7500,"(7498) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, Ibn Mubārak narrated from Yūnus who said: I asked al-Zuhrī about the zakāh of land upon which jizyah (poll tax) is levied. He said: ""The Muslims have continuously, from the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and after him, dealt with land and leased it, and they have paid zakāh from what it produced. We consider this land to be along those lines."" The discussion concerning the Sawād (agricultural region) of Iraq belongs in the book of jizyah. As for the hadith which… [Ṣaḥīḥ. This chain is sound up to al-Zuhrī.]"
7501,"(7499) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī narrated to us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā al-Sarakhsī narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn ʿAnbasah narrated to us, Abū Ḥanīfah narrated from Ḥammād from Ibrāhīm from ʿAlqamah from ʿAbd Allāh who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Kharāj and ʿushr (tithe) shall not be combined upon a Muslim."" This hadith is false (bāṭil); its being connected (ṣal) and elevated (rafʿ) is baseless, and Yaḥyā ibn ʿAnbasah is accused of fabrication (waḍʿ). Abū Saʿd said: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī said: Abū Ḥanīfah only narrated it from Ḥammād from Ibrāhīm as his own statement. Yaḥyā ibn ʿAnbasah narrated it from Abū Ḥanīfah and connected it to the Prophet ﷺ. Yaḥyā ibn ʿAnbasah is well-known in his weakness due to his narrations from trustworthy narrators containing fabricated reports. [Mawḍūʿ. Ibn ʿAdī narrated it in al-Kāmil.]"
7502,"(7500) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū al-Qāsim ʿAlī ibn al-Muʾammal narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, al-Nufaylī narrated to us, Mūsā ibn Aʿyan narrated from Layth from ʿAlqamah ibn Murthad from Sulaymān ibn Buraydah from his father from the Prophet ﷺ that he said regarding the People of the Dhimmah: ""They retain what they possess of their wealth, their slaves, their homes, their land, and their livestock; nothing is due upon them concerning it except ṣadaqah (zakāh)."" [Ḍaʿīf. Aḥmad narrated it.]"
7503,"(7501) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated from al-Ḥajjāj from al-Ḥakam from Miqsam from Ibn ʿAbbās regarding the words of the Most High: ""And give its due (ḥaqqahu) on the day of its harvest"" (al-Anʿām: 141). He said: ""The ʿushr (tithe) and half the ʿushr."" [Ḍaʿīf. al-Ṭabarī narrated it.]"
7504,"(7502) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us, Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, al-Sājī narrated to us, Ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Ṣamad narrated from Yazīd ibn Dirham from Anas regarding: ""And give its due on the day of its harvest."" He said: ""Zakāh."" Both narrations are mawqūf (stopped at the Companion) and are not strong. [Ḍaʿīf. al-Ṭabarī.]"
7505,"(7503) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ibn Mubārak narrated from Maʿmar from Ibn Ṭāwūs from his father regarding the words of the Most High: ""And give its due on the day of its harvest."" He said: ""Zakāh."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭabarī.]"
7506,"(7504) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, Ibn Mubārak narrated from Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān from Ḥayyān al-Aʿraj from Jābir ibn Zayd regarding the words of the Most High: ""And give its due on the day of its harvest."" He said: ""The obligatory zakāh (al-zakāh al-mafrūḍah)."" Something similar is narrated from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, from Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥanafiyyah, and from Mālik ibn Anas. A group of the Tābiʿūn held the view that what is intended by it is other than the obligatory zakāh, and it is narrated from Ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭabarī narrated it in his Tafsīr.]"
7507,"(7505) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us, saying: Ḥafṣ and ʿAbd al-Raḥīm narrated from Ashʿath ibn Sawwār from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, and from Nāʿim from Ibn ʿUmar, regarding the words of the Most High: *""And give its due on the day of its harvest""* — he said: ""They used to give something to those who asked them, apart from the obligatory alms (ṣadaqah)."" Except that Ḥafṣ did not say ""apart from the obligatory alms."" [Weak]"
7508,"(7506) Abū ʿAbd Allāh, Abū Bakr, and Abū Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥīm narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik from ʿAṭāʾ, regarding the words of the Most High: *""And give its due on the day of its harvest""* — he said: ""Whoever is present and asks you on that day, you should give him handfuls, and it is not the obligatory zakāh."""
7509,"(7507) Abū Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ from Mujāhid, regarding the words of the Most High: *""And give its due on the day of its harvest""* — he said: ""At the time of sowing, handfuls are given from it"" — and he gestured with the tips of his fingers as though handing something with them. ""And at the time of the harvest (ṣirām), handfuls are given"" — and he gestured with his palm as though grasping with it. He said: ""He gives a handful, and then leaves them to follow the traces of the harvest."" A group held the view that this was abrogated by the obligatory zakāh. [Sound. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7510,"(7508) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr, and Abū Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: Isrāʾīl narrated from Mughīrah from Ibrāhīm, regarding the words of the Most High: *""And give its due on the day of its harvest""* — he said: ""The verse of zakāh abrogated it."" [Weak]"
7511,"(7509) He said: And Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: Sharīk narrated from Sālim from Saʿīd — meaning Ibn Jubayr — regarding the words of the Most High: *""And give its due on the day of its harvest""* — he said: ""It was [revealed] before the zakāh. When the zakāh was revealed, it abrogated it."" He said: ""So he gives from it a bundle (ḍighth).""

It is also narrated from al-Suddī that it was a Meccan verse abrogated by the zakāh. [Weak]"
7512,"(7510) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us, saying: Abū Manṣūr al-Naḍarawī narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Najdah narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated from Abī Isḥāq from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Whoever pays the zakāh of his wealth, there is no sin upon him if he does not give voluntary charity.""

All other narrations on this meaning have been cited at the beginning of the Book of Zakāh. And with Allah lies success. [Weak. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7513,"(7511) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Qāsim Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Mūsawī narrated to us in Makkah, saying: Abū Ḥātim Muḥammad ibn Idrīs al-Ḥanẓalī narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Yaḥyā al-Marʾī narrated to us, saying: Shuʿbah narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad from his father from his grandfather: that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade harvesting dates by night and reaping crops by night. Jaʿfar said: ""I believe it was on account of the poor.""

Wuhayb ibn Khālid also narrated it from Jaʿfar. [Sound. Narrated by al-Ḥārith.]"
7514,"(7512) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us, saying: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Salamah narrated to us, saying: Wahb ibn Kaysān narrated from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr al-Laythī from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him): that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""While a man was passing through a desert, he heard a thunderclap in a cloud, and he heard in it a voice saying: 'Water the garden of so-and-so by name.' That cloud came to a barren land and poured out all the water it contained. Then it came to the mouth of a watercourse and flowed into a channel, filling it with water. The man followed the cloud until it reached a man standing in his garden, watering it. He said: 'O servant of Allah, what is your name?' He said: 'Why do you ask?' He said: 'I heard in this cloud, whose water is this: ""Water the garden of so-and-so by your name."" So what do you do with it when you harvest it?' He said: 'Now that you have said that, I divide it into thirds: I keep one third for myself and my family, I return one third into it, and I give one third to the poor, the beggars, and the wayfarer.'""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah al-Ḍabbī from Abū Dāwūd. [Sound. Muslim.]"
7515,"(7513) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān narrated to us, saying: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, saying: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us, saying: ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah ibn Abī Ḥasan narrated from his father from Abū Saʿīd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāqī (of silver), and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five camels."" Sufyān said: ""And one awqiyyah is forty dirhams."" [Sound. Its meaning has been mentioned repeatedly.]"
7516,"(7514) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, saying: Bishr ibn Mūsā narrated to us, saying: al-Ḥumaydī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated to us, saying: ""I asked ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah ibn Abī Ḥasan al-Māzinī, and he narrated to me from his father, mentioning it with its meaning."" He added: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awsuq."" And he did not mention the statement of Sufyān.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Sufyān. And Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī, Ibn Jurayj, Mālik ibn Anas, Sufyān al-Thawrī, Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj, and others narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Yaḥyā. And Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, ʿUmarah ibn Ghaziyyah, and others narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah. [Previously mentioned.]"
7517,"(7515) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah informed us, Abū ʿAmr ibn Nujayd informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ṣaʿṣaʿah al-Māzinī, from his father, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāqī of silver, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five dhawd of camels, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five awsq of dates.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it.]"
7518,"(7516) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥanẓalī narrated to us. He said: I said to Abū Usāmah: ""Did al-Walīd ibn Kathīr al-Makhzūmī narrate to you from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Ṣaʿṣaʿah, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah, from ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him) that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'There is no ṣadaqah on less than five awsq of dates, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five awāqī of silver, and there is no ṣadaqah on less than five dhawd of camels'?"" Abū Usāhid confirmed it and said: ""Yes.""

Abū Ṣāliḥ informed us, my grandfather informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us. He said: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā said: ""These chains are preserved from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, and the hadith became narrated from him through three narrators from Abū Saʿīd: from his father, Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmarah, and ʿAbbād ibn Tamīm."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it.]"
7519,"(7517) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ṣaqr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Ḥamzah al-Zubayrī narrated to us, al-Dāwardī narrated to us, Yazīd ibn al-Hād narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, who said: ""I asked ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ): 'What was the ṣadāq of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)?' She said: 'His ṣadāq for his wives was twelve awuqiyyah and a nashsh.' She said: 'Do you know what a nashsh is?' I said: 'No.' She said: 'It is half an awuqiyyah.' That amounts to five hundred dirhams. This was the ṣadāq of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) for his wives.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm and Ibn Abī ʿUmar from al-Dāwardī. In it is evidence that an awuqiyyah is forty dirhams and that five awāqī are two hundred dirhams. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7520,"(7518) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn Ziyād narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, ʿAmr ibn Dīnār narrated to us. He said: I heard Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no ṣadaqah on silver until it reaches two hundred dirhams."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
7521,"(7519) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar ibn Aḥmad ibn Shawdhab narrated to us, Shuʿayb ibn Ayyūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī narrated to us, my father ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muthannā narrated to us, Thumāmah ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Anas narrated to us that Anas narrated to him that Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote this document when he sent him to al-Baḥrayn:

""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. These are the obligatory ṣadaqāt which Allah has imposed upon the Muslims, which Allah has commanded His Messenger (ﷺ) to collect. So whoever is asked for it by the Muslims in its proper manner, let him give it; and whoever is asked for more than that, let him not give it."" He said: And he mentioned the hadith in its entirety, and in it is: ""And on silver is a quarter of a tenth (rubʿ al-ʿushr). If it is only ninety-one and one hundred, there is no ṣadaqah on it unless its owner wishes to give.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Anṣārī. [Ṣaḥīḥ in meaning.]"
7522,"(7520) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Musaddad narrated to us, Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""I have exempted you from the ṣadaqah on horses and slaves. Bring the ṣadaqah on silver: one dirham for every forty dirhams. And there is nothing on ninety-one and one hundred. When it reaches two hundred, then there are five dirhams due on it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Meaning extracted.]"
7523,"(7521) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdah informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us, al-Nufaylī Abū Jaʿfar narrated to us, Zuhayr ibn Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, Abū Isḥāq narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah and from al-Ḥārith al-Aʿwar, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him). Zuhayr said: I think he attributed it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: ""Bring a quarter of a tenth: one dirham for every forty dirhams. But there is nothing upon you until it reaches two hundred dirhams. When it is two hundred dirhams, then there are five dirhams due on it. And whatever exceeds that is upon this same calculation.""

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from al-Nufaylī. [Ṣaḥīḥ through other chains. Meaning extracted.]"
7524,"(7522) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq narrated to us, Maʿmar narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Whatever exceeds two hundred is upon the same calculation."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7525,"(7523) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Raffāʾ al-Baghdādī informed us, Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways and ʿĪsā ibn Mīnāʾ (Qālūn) both said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī al-Zinād narrated to us that his father said: ""Among the jurists of ours whose opinion is followed whom I met were Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, Khārijah ibn Zayd ibn Thābit, ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUtbah, and Sulaymān ibn Yasār, among other eminent elders besides them. They would sometimes differ on a matter, and we would adopt the view of the majority of them or the one with the most sound opinion."" He mentioned several rulings and said: ""And they used to say: 'There is no ṣadaqah on grain or seed crops until the estimated yield of the dates or the measured quantity of the grain reaches five awsq by the ṣāʿ of the Prophet (ﷺ).' And they did not consider there to be zakāh on any of the fruits except grapes, when their estimated yield reached five awsq by the ṣāʿ of the Prophet (ﷺ). And they considered there to be ṣadaqah on every surplus amount of gold, silver, dates, grain, and grapes—even if it exceeded by a mudd or more or less. And they did not consider there to be ṣadaqah on any surplus of livestock—camels, cattle, or sheep.""

And it has been narrated to us from Ibrāhīm al-Nakhaʿī that he said: ""Whatever exceeds""—meaning upon two hundred—""is upon the same calculation."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭaḥāwī.]"
7526,"(7524) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Bukayr narrated from Ibn Isḥāq, who said: al-Minhāl ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated to me from Ḥabīb ibn Najīḥ from ʿUbādah ibn Nusayy from Muʿādh ibn Jabal that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded him when he sent him to Yemen that he should not take anything from the broken coins. When the silver reached two hundred dirhams, five dirhams were to be taken from it. And he should not take anything from any excess until it reached forty dirhams, at which point one dirham was to be taken from it.

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us. He said: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ said after this hadith: ""al-Minhāl ibn al-Jarrāḥ is abandoned in hadith (matrūl al-ḥadīth). He is Abū al-ʿAṭūf, and his name is al-Jarrāḥ ibn al-Minhāl. Ibn Isḥāq used to reverse his name when narrating from him. And ʿUbādah ibn Nusayy did not hear from Muʿādh.""

The Sheikh said: ""Had this been sound, we would have acted upon it and not contradicted it, except that its chain of narration is very weak. And Allah knows best."" [Very weak. Al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
7527,"(7525) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us, saying: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me, saying: Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, saying: Sulaymān ibn Kathīr narrated to us, saying: al-Zuhrī narrated from Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl ibn Ḥunayf, from his father, that the Prophet ﷺ prohibited two types of dates: the *juʿrūr* and the *ḥubayq*. There were people who would deliberately select the worst of their fruits and give them in charity, so they were prohibited from [giving] those two types of dates [in zakāh]. Then the verse was revealed: **""And do not aim toward the defective therefrom, spending [from that]""** (al-Baqarah, 2:267).

Abū al-Walīd narrated it with a complete chain, and Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm and Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated it as a *mursal* report from Sulaymān ibn Kathīr. [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7528,"(7526) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Nuṣayr al-Khuldī narrated to us, saying: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us, saying: Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām narrated from Sufyān ibn Ḥusayn, from al-Zuhrī, from Abī Umāmah ibn Sahl, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ ordered [the giving of] charity, so a man from the people of that lowland came with bundles of dates — Sufyān said, meaning *al-shīṣ* [inferior, unripe dates] — and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""Who brought this?""** No one would bring anything without it being attributed to the one who brought it. Then the verse was revealed: **""And do not aim toward the defective therefrom, spending [from that]""** (al-Baqarah, 2:267). He said: And the Messenger of Allah ﷺ prohibited the *juʿrūr* and the *ḥubayq* from being taken in charity. Al-Zuhrī said: They are two types of dates from Madīnah.

And Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥafṣah similarly narrated it from al-Zuhrī. [Weak. Preceded earlier.]"
7529,"(7527) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Saʿdī and Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Anas al-Qurashī both narrated to us, saying: Abū ʿĀṣim al-Nabīl narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me, saying: Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī ʿArīb narrated from Kathīr ibn Murrah, from ʿAwf ibn Mālik, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ went out with a staff, and lo, there were clusters of dates hanging, among which was a cluster of *ḥashaf* [inferior, dried-out dates]. He poked that cluster with his staff and said: **""No harm would befall the owner of this if he gave charity from the better of it. Indeed, the owner of this will eat *ḥashaf* on the Day of Resurrection.""** Then he said: **""By Allah, you will leave it humbled for forty years for the *ʿawāfī*.""** Then he said: **""Do you know what the *ʿawāfī* are?""** They said: ""Allah and His Messenger know best."" He said: **""The birds and the wild beasts.""**

[Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7530,"(7528) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Ibrāhīm ibn Marzūq narrated to us, saying: Abū Ḥudhayfah narrated from Sufyān, from al-Suddī, from Abī Mālik, from al-Barāʾ, who said: The Anṣār used to give the inferior dates in zakāh, so the verse was revealed: **""O you who believe, spend of the good things which you have earned and of that which We have brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim toward the defective therefrom, spending [from that]. And you would not take it [yourselves] unless you closed your eyes to it""** (al-Baqarah, 2:267).

He said: The *dūn* [inferior] is the *khabīth* [vile]. If a person owes you something and gives you something less, he has diminished some of your right. If you accept it, that is *al-iḍmāḍ* [closing the eyes, i.e., overlooking the shortfall]."
7531,"(7529) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us among others, who all said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, saying: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: Sufyān narrated from Dāwūd ibn Abī Hind, from al-Shaʿbī, from Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""When the collector of zakāh comes to you, let him not depart from you except in a state of satisfaction.""**

Al-Shāfiʿī said: Meaning — and Allah knows best — that you give it willingly and do not turn him away, nor give him from your wealth what is not obligatory upon them. By this we instruct them and we instruct the collector.

And what al-Shāfiʿī (may Allah have mercy on him) said is plausible, were it not for the additional wording found in the narration of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hilāl al-ʿAbsī."
7532,"(7530) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: Abū Kāmil narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād narrated to us, saying:

(Ḥ) And ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān narrated to us — and this is the wording of Abū Kāmil — from Muḥammad ibn Abī Ismāʿīl, saying: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hilāl al-ʿAbsī narrated to us, from Jarīr ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: Some people — meaning from among the Bedouin — came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""Some collectors of zakāh come to us and oppress us."" He said: **""Satisfy your collectors.""** They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, even if they oppress us?"" He said: **""Satisfy your collectors.""** ʿUthmān added: **""Even if you are oppressed.""** And Abū Kāmil said in his hadith: Jarīr said: ""No collector departed from me after I heard this from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ except that he was pleased with me.""

Muslim narrated it in the *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Abū Kāmil and from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah from ʿAbd al-Raḥīm. [Sound. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7533,"(7531) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Sarrāj narrated to us, saying: Muṭayyin narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Ṭarīf narrated to us, saying: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from ʿĀṣim, from Abī ʿUthmān, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""When the collector of zakāh comes to you, give him your charity. If he transgresses against you, turn your back to him and do not curse him. Rather say: 'O Allah, I seek Your reward for what has been taken from me.'""**

In this [hadith] there is an indication that he considered it praiseworthy to exercise patience in the face of their transgression. Similarly, in the hadith of Jābir ibn ʿAtīk from the Prophet ﷺ: **""Leave them and what they seek. If they act justly, it is for themselves, and if they oppress, it is upon themselves.""**

This has already been discussed in the chapter on the choice of giving charity to the governor.

Many narrations have been reported from the Prophet ﷺ regarding patience in the face of the oppression of governors. This is understood to mean that he commanded patience when one knows that no aid will reach him and that the one who governs him cannot be restrained. However, if it is possible to repel [the oppression] or one hopes for aid…

[Sound. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
7534,"(7532) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh narrated to us, saying: Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān narrated to us, saying: ʿAmr ibn Khālid al-Ḥarrānī narrated to us, saying: ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr al-Raqqī narrated from Zayd ibn Abī Unaysah, from al-Qāsim ibn ʿAwf al-Shaybānī, from ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn, who said: Umm Salamah narrated to us that the Prophet ﷺ was in her house while some of his Companions were with him conversing, when a man came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah ﷺ, how much is the zakāh on such-and-such [an amount] of dates?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Such-and-such."" The man said: ""So-and-so has transgressed against me and taken from me such-and-such, adding an extra ṣāʿ."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""Then what will it be when one comes to you who transgresses even more severely than this transgression?""** The people discussed the matter at length, until one of them said: ""O Messenger of Allah, if a man is absent from you with his camels, his livestock, and his crops, and he pays the zakāh of his wealth, and then his right is transgressed against — what should he do while he is absent from you?"" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""Whoever pays the zakāh of his wealth with a contented soul, seeking by it the Countenance of Allah and the Hereafter, and does not conceal any of his wealth, and establishes the Ṣalāh, and then his right is transgressed against — let him take his weapon and fight. If he is killed, he is a martyr.""**

[Weak. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7535,"(7533) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me, saying: Abā Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Juwaynī narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Rajāʾ narrated to us, saying: Suwayd ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, saying: Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, that Abū Ṣāliḥ Dhakwān informed him that he heard Abā Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: **""There is no owner of gold or silver who does not pay its right except that, on the Day of Resurrection, plates of fire will be prepared for him. They will be heated in the Fire of Hell, and his side, his forehead, and his back will be branded with them. Whenever they cool down, they will be returned to him, on a day the extent of which is fifty thousand years, until judgment is passed among the servants, and he will see his path — either to Paradise or to the Fire.""**

Muslim narrated it in the *Ṣaḥīḥ* from Suwayd ibn Saʿīd, and similarly Hishām ibn Saʿ narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam. [Sound. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7536,"(7534) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī all narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, saying: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated, saying: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim informed you —

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us, saying: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us, saying: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, saying: Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-Mahrī narrated to us, saying: Ibn Wahb informed us, saying: Jarīr ibn Ḥāzim informed me — and he named another narrator — from Abī Isḥāq, from ʿĀṣim ibn Ḍamrah and al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet ﷺ, that he said: **""Bring me a quarter of a tenth: from every forty dirhams, one dirham. Nothing is due upon you until you possess two hundred dirhams. When you possess two hundred dirhams and a year has passed over them, there are five dirhams [due]. Nothing is due upon you until you possess twenty dīnārs. When you possess twenty dīnārs and a year has passed over them, there is half a dīnār [due]. Whatever exceeds that is according to that calculation.""** He said: ""And I do not know whether ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said 'according to that calculation' or raised it to the Prophet ﷺ, except that Jarīr said in the hadith from the Prophet ﷺ: **""There is no zakāh on wealth until a year passes over it.""**

The wording is that of Baḥr ibn Naṣr, and he added in his chain, from Ibn Wahb, from al-Ḥārith ibn Nabhān, from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿUmarah, from Abī Isḥāq.

[Sound through other chains. Its meaning has been narrated extensively.]"
7537,"(7535) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrānī al-ʿAdl informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Qāsim, from his father, that ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, used to look after the orphaned daughters of her brother who were under her care. They had jewellery, yet she did not pay zakāh on it.

In the narration of al-Shāfiʿī, he said, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that she used to do so."
7538,"(7536) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us. (Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-ʿAdl informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Mālik narrated, from Nāfiʿ, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) used to adorn his daughters and female slaves with gold jewellery, and he did not pay zakāh on it.

In the narration of al-Shāfiʿī, he said, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he used to do so, and he said: ""Then he did not pay zakāh on it."""
7539,"(7537) Nāfiʿ reported that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar said: ""There is no zakāh on jewellery."""
7540,"(7538) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Usāmah ibn Zayd narrated, from Nāfiʿ, who said: Ibn ʿUmar used to adorn his daughters with jewellery worth four hundred dīnārs, yet he did not pay its zakāh."
7541,"(7539) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, who said: I heard a man asking Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh about jewellery: ""Is there zakāh on it?"" Jābir said: ""No."" The man said: ""Even if it reaches a thousand dīnārs?"" Jābir replied: ""That is even more so."""
7542,"(7540) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Saʿīd narrated, from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik, regarding jewellery, he said: ""If it is lent out and worn, then its zakāh is paid once."""
7543,"(7541) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Abī Rajāʾ narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated to us: Sharīk narrated, from ʿAlī ibn Sulaym, who said: I asked Anas ibn Mālik about jewellery, and he said: ""There is no zakāh on it."""
7544,"(7542) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed us: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar informed us: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Abī Rajāʾ narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated to us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated, from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, that she used to adorn her daughters with gold jewellery and did not pay its zakāh, amounting to approximately fifty thousand."
7545,"(7543) Māsāwir al-Warrāq narrated from Shuʿayb, who said: ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to Abū Mūsā: ""Command the Muslim women under your authority to give the zakāh of their jewellery.""

This is from what Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ permitted me to narrate from him, from Abū al-Walīd, narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Abū Bakr narrated to us: Wakīʿ and ʿAbd al-Raḥīm narrated, from Māsāwir, and he mentioned it. This is a mursal narration; Shuʿayb ibn Yasār did not meet ʿUmar."
7546,"(7544) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān ibn Fāris narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī narrated, and he said: Zakariyyā said to me: Abū Usāmah narrated to us: Māsāwir al-Warrāq narrated to me: Shuʿayb ibn Yasār informed me that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote: ""Let jewellery be subjected to zakāh."" Al-Bukhārī said: ""It is mursal."""
7547,"(7545) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Fārisī narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; al-Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim informed from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from ʿUrwah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""There is no harm in wearing jewellery so long as its zakāh is given.""

And from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather: that he used to write to his treasurer Sālim: ""Let him discharge the zakāh of his daughters' jewellery every year."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Dāraquṭnī.]"
7548,"(7546) Abū Bakr al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī informed us; Sufyān ibn Muḥammad narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ḥammād, from Ibrāhīm, from ʿAlqamah: that the wife of ʿAbd Allāh asked him about some jewellery she had. He said: ""If it reaches two hundred dirhams, then zakāh is due upon it."" She said: ""Shall I spend it on a nephew of mine who is under my care?"" He said: ""Yes.""

And this has been narrated as marfūʿ (elevated) to the Prophet ﷺ, but it is not established. [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7549,"(7547) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥamdān al-Jallāb in Hamadhān informed us; Abū Ḥātim al-Rāzī narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Ṭāriq narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Jaʿfar narrated: that Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed him from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Shaddād ibn al-Hād, who said: We visited ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet ﷺ, and she said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ came to me and saw in my hand silver rings (sikhāb). He said: 'What is this, O ʿĀʾishah?' I said: 'I made them to adorn myself for you, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'Do you give their zakāh?' I said: 'No' — or 'whatever Allah wills of that.' He said: 'They will be sufficient for you as a cause of [entering] the Fire.'"" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7550,"(7548) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Hārūn Abū Nashīṭ narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn al-Rabīʿ ibn Ṭāriq narrated it with the same meaning, except that he said: ""Muḥammad ibn ʿAṭāʾ informed him,"" and in the report he said: ""silver rings (fatakhāt) made of silver."" ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar said: ""This Muḥammad ibn ʿAṭāʾ is unknown (majhūl)."" The Shaykh said: ""He is Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿAṭāʾ, and he is well-known (maʿrūf)."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7551,"(7549) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Abū Kāmil and Ḥumayd ibn Masʿadah, both narrated: that Khālid ibn al-Ḥārith narrated to them; Ḥusayn narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather: that a woman came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with her daughter, and in her daughter's hand were two thick gold bangles. He said to her: ""Do you give the zakāh of these?"" She said: ""No."" He said: ""Would it please you that Allah adorn you on the Day of Resurrection with two bangles of Fire?"" He (the narrator) said: ""So she snatched them and cast them before the Prophet ﷺ and said: 'They are for Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and for His Messenger.'"" This is narrated uniquely by ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather. [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
7552,"(7550) The ḥadīth of Thābit ibn ʿAjlān has already been mentioned, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I used to wear gold necklaces (awḍāḥ). I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, is this a treasure (kanz)?' He said: 'Whatever reaches the point that its zakāh is given and it is purified — it is not a treasure.'"" Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us of it; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsá narrated to us; ʿAttāb narrated from Thābit, and he mentioned it. This is narrated uniquely by Thābit ibn ʿAjlān, and Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7553,"(7551) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Sarī ibn Yaḥyá narrated to us; Abū Ghassān narrated to us; Kāmil ibn al-ʿAlāʾ narrated from Ḥabīb, meaning Ibn Abī Thābit, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The zakāh of jewellery is lending it (as ʿāriyah)."" [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7554,"(7552) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Saʿīd ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us; Qatādah narrated from Saʿīd, that is, Ibn al-Musayyib, regarding the zakāh of jewellery. He said: ""It should be lent out and worn.""

And it is narrated from al-Shaʿbī in one of the two narrations from him. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7555,There is no ḥadīth under this chapter.
7556,"(7553) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Asīd ibn Abī Asīd al-Barrād, from Nāfiʿ ibn ʿAyyāsh, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever would love for his beloved to be given a bracelet of fire, let him give him a bracelet of gold; and whoever would love for his beloved to be given a necklace of fire, let him give him a necklace of gold; and whoever would love for his beloved to be given a bangle of fire, let him give him a bangle of gold. But take to silver, and play with it as you wish."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7557,"(7554) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us, from Sufyān, from Manṣūr, from Ribʿī, from his wife, from the sister of Ḥudhayfah, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) addressed us and said: 'O assembly of women, is there not silver with which you may adorn yourselves? Verily, there is no woman among you who adorns herself with gold and displays it except that she will be punished with it.'"" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7558,"(7555) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad ibn al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us, from Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Abī Kathīr, who said: Maḥmūd ibn ʿAmr narrated to us that Asmāʾ bint Yazīd narrated to him that she heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Any woman who hangs a necklace of gold, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, will hang for her the like of it from the Fire on the Day of Resurrection. And any woman who places an earring of gold in her ear, Allah will place in her ear the like of it on the Day of Resurrection.'"" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7559,"(7556) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Hishām narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abī Salām, from Abī Asmāʾ, from Thawbān, who said: ""The daughter of Hubayrah came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and she had on her hand fatakh of gold — that is, large rings. The Prophet (ﷺ) began striking her hand, so she came to Fāṭimah complaining about that."" Thawbān said: ""The Prophet (ﷺ) entered upon Fāṭimah while I with him, and she had taken from her neck a chain of gold. She said: 'Abū al-Ḥasan gifted this to me.' The chain was in her hand. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: 'Would it please you that the people say: Fāṭimah, daughter of Muḥammad, has in her hand a chain of fire?'"" He then left and did not sit. Fāṭimah went to the chain, sold it, and with its price purchased a slave and freed him. This reached the Prophet (ﷺ), and he said: 'All praise is due to Allah who saved Fāṭimah from the Fire.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī]"
7560,"(7557) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Tamtām Muḥammad ibn Ghālib narrated to us: Mūsā narrated to us: Hammām narrated to us, from Yaḥyā, from Zayd Abī Salām, that his grandfather narrated to him that Abī Asmāʾ narrated to him that Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), said, and he mentioned its meaning. These narrations and what has been reported to their effect indicate the prohibition of adorning with gold. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited]"
7561,"(7558) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Saʿīd ibn Abī Hind, from Abū Mūsā al-Ashʿarī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Silk and gold are forbidden upon the males of my Ummah and permissible for their females."" We have narrated it from the ḥadīth of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī]"
7562,"(7559) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Ibn Nufayl narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, who said: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbbād narrated to me, from his father ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""A piece of jewellery came to the Prophet (ﷺ) from the Negus, which he had gifted to him. Among it was a gold ring with an Abyssinian stone."" She said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took it with a stick, keeping himself somewhat distant from it, or with some of his fingers. Then he called Umāmah bint Abī al-ʿāṣ, the daughter of his daughter Zaynab, and said: 'Adorn yourself with this, O young girl.'"" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7563,"(7560) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Muzakkī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, saying: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Ḥātim ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿUmarah, from Zaynab bint Nubayṭ, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) adorned her mother and her maternal aunt. Their father, Abū Umāmah Asʿad ibn Zurārah, had bequeathed them to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so he adorned them with earrings of gold ingot in which there were pearls. Zaynab said: ""I came upon the jewellery, or some of it."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by al-Ḥākim]"
7564,"(7561) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Abū ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsā and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn ʿUmarah.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to me: Muḥammad ibn ʿUmarah narrated to me, from Zaynab bint Nubayṭ, from her mother, who said: ""I was in the care of the Prophet (ﷺ), I and my two sisters, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to adorn us with gold and pearls.""

The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr. In the narration of Abū ʿUbayd: ""He used to adorn us."" Ibn Jaʿfar said: ""Earrings of gold and pearls."" And Ṣafwān said: ""He used to adorn us with gold ingot and pearls.""

Abū ʿUbayd said: Abū ʿAmr said: ""The singular of riʿāth is raʿthah or raʿʿah, and it is the earring (qurt).""

These narrations and what has been reported to their effect indicate the permissibility of adorning with gold for women. We have drawn evidence from the attainment of consensus (ijmāʿ) upon its permissibility for them, indicating the abrogation of the narrations that indicate its prohibition specifically for them. And Allah knows best. [Ḥasan]"
7565,"(7562) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn al-ʿAbbās al-Rāzī narrated to us: Sahl ibn ʿUthmān narrated to us: ʿUqbah ibn Khālid narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet (ﷺ): ""A ring of gold was brought to him, and he placed it on his right hand with its stone facing his palm. The people then took to wearing rings of gold. When he saw that, he removed it and said: 'I shall never wear it again.' He then had a ring made of silver (wariq).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sahl ibn ʿUthmān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7566,"(7563) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū al-Muthannā narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated to us, from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from ʿAbd Allāh, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took a ring of gold and placed its stone facing his palm. The people then took to wearing such rings, so he cast it away and took a ring of silver (wariq) or silver (fiḍḍah).

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad, and Muslim narrated it from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited]"
7567,"(7564) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us that Isma'il ibn Qutaybah informed us that Yahya ibn Yahya informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ had a ring made of silver which was upon his hand, then it was upon the hand of Abu Bakr after him, then upon the hand of 'Umar, then upon the hand of 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, until it fell from him into the well of Aris. Its inscription was ""Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya, and al-Bukhari narrated it from Muhammad ibn Salam from 'Abdullah ibn Numayr.

And it has been related to us from 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abu Rawwad from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar that the Prophet ﷺ used to wear his ring on his left hand. And it has been related to us from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar from Nafi' that Ibn 'Umar used to wear his ring on his left hand. It is therefore possible that what was placed on his right hand was the one he made of gold and then discarded, and what he placed on his left hand was the one he made of silver, thereby reconciling the two narrations. [Sahih. Al-Bukhari reported it.]"
7568,"(7565) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq both said: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us that Isma'il ibn Abi Uways narrated to us that Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated from Yunus from Ibn Shihab from Anas that the Prophet ﷺ wore a silver ring on his right hand, its gemstone being Ethiopian (habashi), and he used to position its gemstone toward the palm of his hand.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Zuhayr ibn Harb from Isma'il. [Sahih. Muslim reported it.]"
7569,"(7566) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us that Ahmad ibn Sahl al-Bukhari informed us that Salih ibn Muhammad al-Hafiz narrated to us that 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and 'Abbad ibn Musa both said: Talhah ibn Yahya narrated to us from Yunus from al-Zuhri from Anas, who said: The Prophet ﷺ took a silver ring for his right hand, in which was an Ethiopian gemstone, and he used to position its gemstone toward the palm of his hand.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from 'Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah and 'Abbad ibn Musa; thus stated al-Zuhri. [Sahih. Previously mentioned.]"
7570,"(7567) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz narrated to us that Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq informed us that Abu al-Muthanna narrated to us that Abu al-Walid narrated to us that Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Thabit from Anas, who said: It is as though I am looking at the white gleam of the ring of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he gestured with his left hand.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abi Bakr ibn Khallad from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi from Hammad, and in the hadith he said: ""The ring of the Prophet ﷺ was on this,"" pointing to the middle finger of his left hand. [Sahih. Muslim reported it.]"
7571,"(7568) Abu al-Husayn ibn al-Fadd al-Qattan informed us that Abu Sahl ibn Ziyad al-Qattan narrated to us that Sa'id ibn 'Uthran al-Ahwazi narrated to us that Abu Bakr ibn Khallad narrated to us that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated to us that Hammad ibn Salamah narrated from Thabit from Anas, who said: The ring of the Prophet ﷺ was on this, and he pointed to his left middle finger.

The Sheikh said: It is likely that this is more sound than the narration of al-Zuhri from Anas regarding the ring he made of silver, for al-Zuhri narrated from Anas that he saw upon the hand of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ a ring of silver on a single day, after which the people fashioned silver rings and wore them, so the Messenger of Allah ﷺ discarded his ring and the people discarded their rings. It is likely that the mention of silver in this account was a slip of the tongue on the part of al-Zuhri, and it was therefore attributed to error, for what he discarded was his gold ring, after which he took a silver ring.

The narration of Ibn 'Umar indicates that what he placed on his right hand was his gold ring, which he then discarded. It is likely that the error in the narration of Yunus from al-Zuhri from Anas occurred in this regard, so that Anas ibn Malik only mentioned the right hand in connection with what was made of gold, as 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar clarified, and al-Zuhri's tongue slipped to the mention of silver, and the error occurred in the narration of those who transmitted from al-Zuhri regarding the mention of the right hand with silver. And Allah knows best.

Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father, which indicates the correctness of this reconciliation: that what he placed on his right hand was his gold ring, and what he placed on his left hand was his silver ring. And Allah knows best. [Sahih. Muslim.]"
7572,"(7569) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us that Ibn Wahb narrated to us that Sulayman ibn Bilal narrated from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ wore a gold ring on his right hand, on his middle finger, until he returned to his home and cast it away, and he never wore it again. Then he wore a silver ring and placed it on his left hand.

And that Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, 'Ali ibn Abi Talib, al-Hasan, and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them, used to wear their rings on their left hands.

Ja'far ibn Muhammad said: Upon the ring of al-Hasan and al-Husayn was the remembrance of Allah, and upon the ring of my father was ""Al-'izzatu lillahi jami'an"" (All dignity belongs entirely to Allah). [Sahih. Reported by the author.]"
7573,"(7570) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Adanan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us that al-Asfati, meaning 'Abbas ibn al-Fadl, narrated to us that Sahl ibn Bakkar narrated to us that Jarir ibn Hazim narrated from Qatadah from Anas, who said: The pommel of the sword of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was of silver.

Jarir ibn Hazim is alone in narrating this from Qatadah from Anas. And the hadith is defective (ma'lul) due to what has been stated. [Munkar al-isnad. Abu Dawud reported it.]"
7574,"(7571) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us that Mu'adh ibn Hisham narrated to us that my father narrated from Qatadah from Sa'id ibn Abi al-Hasan, who said: The pommel of the sword of the Prophet ﷺ was of silver. Qatadah said: I do not know anyone who followed him upon that.

The Sheikh said: And this is a mursal narration, and it is the preserved one (al-mahfuz).

And it has been narrated through another chain, connected, from Anas. [Da'if. Abu Dawud.]"
7575,"(7572) Abu Sa'd al-Malini informed us that Abu Ahmad ibn 'Adi al-Hafiz narrated to us that 'Abdan narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Ma'mar narrated to us that Yahya ibn Kathir, meaning Abu Ghassan al-'Anbari, narrated to us that 'Uthman ibn Sa'd al-Katib narrated from Anas that the pommel of the sword of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was of silver.

And Abu Dawud narrated it from Muhammad ibn Bashshar from Yahya ibn Kathir. And Allah knows best. [Da'if. Abu Dawud.]"
7576,"(7573) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Abu 'Utbah narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Hamir narrated to us that Abu al-Hakam narrated to us that Marzuq al-Sayqal said: I polished the sword of the Prophet ﷺ, Dhu al-Faqar, and it had a pommel of silver, a silver bakkah (fitting) in its middle, and silver rings (hilaq) on its scabbard (qayd).

[Da'if. Ibn 'Asakir.]"
7577,"(7574) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated from ʿUthmān ibn Mūsā from Nāfiʿ that Ibn ʿUmar girded himself with the sword of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) on the day of the killing of ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him), and it was adorned. He (Nāfiʿ) said: I asked: How much was its adornment? He replied: Four hundred (dirhams). [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Nuʿaym.]"
7578,"(7575) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Juwayriyyah ibn Asmāʾ narrated from Nāfiʿ, who said: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar was killed on the day of Ṣiffīn, so Muʿāwiyah purchased his sword and sent it to ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar. Juwayriyyah said: I asked Nāfiʿ: Was it the sword of ʿUmar that it had been? He said: Yes. I asked: How much was its adornment? He said: They found in its scabbard forty dirhams. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
7579,"(7576) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to me: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: Abū Hammām al-Sakūnī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Mushhir narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father, who said: The sword of al-Zubayr (may Allah be pleased with him) was adorned with silver. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Farwah ibn Abī al-Maghrāʾ from ʿAlī ibn Mushhir, and he added: Hishām said: And the sword of ʿUrwah was adorned with silver. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī. Narrated by ʿAbd al-Barr.]"
7580,"(7577) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us in Baghdad, saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us: Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: al-Masʿūdī narrated to us, saying: I saw in the house of al-Qāsim, meaning Ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, a sword whose pommel was of silver. I said: Whose sword is this? He said: The sword of ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd. [Ḥasan.]"
7581,"(7578) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us: Dāwūd ibn Rushayd narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, saying: I asked Mālik about adorning the muṣḥafs with silver, so he brought out a muṣḥaf to us and said: My father narrated to me from my grandfather that they compiled the Qurʾān in the time of ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with him) and that they adorned the muṣḥafs in this manner or something similar to it. [Ṣaḥīḥ.]"
7582,"(7579) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn ʿUthmān al-Tanūkhī narrated to us: Bishr ibn Bakr narrated to us: al-Awzāʿī narrated to us: Sulaymān ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, saying: I heard Abā Umāmah say: By Allah, a people achieved conquests whose swords' adornment was not of gold and silver; rather, their swords' adornment was of al-ʿalābiyy, al-anūk, and iron. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Mubārak from al-Awzāʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7583,"(7580) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Muʿallā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us: Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ziyād narrated to us, saying: I saw a man asking Abā Umāmah: What is your view regarding the adorning of swords — is it from the treasures (kanz)? Abā Umāmah said: Yes. Then he said: Indeed, I have only narrated to you what I have heard. [Ṣaḥīḥ. al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7584,"(7581) Abū ʿAbdillāh Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Sūsī narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Baḥr narrated to us: al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated from al-Awzāʿī from Ḥassān ibn ʿAṭiyyah from Zayd ibn Arṭāh from Jubayr ibn Nufayr from Abū Dharr, who said: A person will be branded with his treasure, even with the scabbard of his sword. Thus he mentioned it as a mawqūf narration. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7585,"(7582) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Mujīb al-ʿĀbid narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Saʿīd al-ʿAbdī narrated to us by dictation. (Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz narrated to us: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd al-Bazzāz al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us: al-Nufaylī narrated to us: Miskīn ibn Bukayr narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid al-Thaqafī from Abī al-Mujīb from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that Abū Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) looked at Abū Hurayrah while he had a sword adorned with silver, and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Whoever leaves behind silver or gold will be branded with it on the Day of Resurrection."" He (the narrator) said: So he cast it aside. Thus said Miskīn. [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Aḥmad.]"
7586,"(7583) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī narrated to us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī narrated to us: Abū ʿAwn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us: ʿAbdān narrated to me: My father narrated to me from Shuʿbah from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid al-Thaqafī, who said: I heard Abā Mujīb say: The scabbard of the sword of Abū Hurayrah was of silver, so Abū Dharr forbade him from it and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Whoever leaves behind gold or silver will be branded with it."" Thus said ʿUthmān ibn Jabalah from Shuʿbah. And Ibn Abī ʿAdī narrated it from Shuʿbah from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. And Abū Dāwūd said from Shuʿbah from ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Fulān or Fulān ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. And Muʿādh said from Shuʿbah from Ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid. Al-Bukhārī said regarding it: There is a consideration (naẓar) in it. [Ḥasan li-ghayrihi. Preceded before it.]"
7587,"(7584) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī informed us: Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd al-Faḥḥām informed us: Ḥajjāj ibn Muḥammad informed us: Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, from al-Naḍr ibn Anas, from Bashīr ibn Nahīk, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) that the Prophet (ﷺ) prohibited the wearing of a gold ring.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Ghundar, from Shuʿbah. The ḥadīth of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, al-Barāʾ ibn ʀĀzib, and al-Miqdād ibn Maʿdī Karib (may Allah be pleased with them) has preceded in this regard. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7588,"(7585) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk informed us: Ṣafwān narrated: al-Walīd narrated: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated from ʿUmar ibn Yaʿlā al-Ṭāʾifī al-Thaqafī, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) while I had a gold ring on my finger. He said: ""Have you paid its zakāh?"" I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, does this have zakāh?"" He said: ""Yes, it is a great ember.""

Al-Walīd said: I said to Sufyān: ""How does one pay the zakāh of a ring when its value is only a mithqāl or thereabouts?"" He said: ""You add it to what you own in terms of items upon which zakāh is obligatory by weight, then you pay its zakāh.""

A group narrated it similarly from al-Walīd ibn Muslim. It was also narrated by al-Ashjaʿī from al-Thawrī. [Very weak (Ḍaʿīf Jiddan). Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7589,"(7586) As Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sakhawayh narrated to me: Yazīd ibn al-Haytham informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī al-Layth informed us: al-Ashjaʿī informed us: Sufyān ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿUmar ibn Yaʿlā ibn Murrah al-Thaqafī, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) wearing a large gold ring. The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Do you purify this (i.e., pay its zakāh)?"" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is the zakāh of this?"" When the man turned to leave, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""It is a great ember."" [Very weak (Ḍaʿīf Jiddan). Preceded above.]"
7590,"(7587) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us.

(Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb said:) And Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Thaqafī informed us: al-Walīd ibn Shujaʿ informed us. Both said: ʿAlī ibn Mushhir narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Zayd ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, the one who drinks from a silver vessel is as though he is dragging the fire of Jahannam into his belly.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and al-Walīd ibn Shujaʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7591,"(7588) As ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yazīd and Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā both said: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Sufyān informed us: Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and al-Walīd ibn Shujaʿ both said: ʿAlī ibn Mushhir narrated it, except that he said: ""Indeed, the one who eats or drinks from a vessel of gold or silver…""

Muslim (may Allah have mercy on him) said: In the ḥadīth of none of them — meaning the ḥadīth of the group who narrated it from Nāfiʿ, and then the group who narrated it from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar — is there any mention of eating or gold, except in the ḥadīth of Ibn Mushhir. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7592,"(7589) And ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh and Abū Aḥmad both said: Ibrāhīm informed us: Muslim informed us: Abū Maʿn al-Raqāshī informed us: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from ʿUthmān — meaning Ibn Murrah — ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us from his maternal aunt, Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever drinks from a vessel of gold or silver is only dragging a fire from Jahannam into his belly.""

In this narration there is mention of gold but not of eating.

We have also narrated the mention of eating in the ḥadīth of Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān, and then in the ḥadīth of ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib and Anas ibn Mālik (may Allah be pleased with them) in the Book of Purification. And upon Allah is the reliance. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above.]"
7593,"(7590) ʿUmar ibn Abī ʿUmar al-Kalāʿī al-Dimashqī narrated from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no zakāh on stone (jewels).""

Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Zayd ibn ʿAbd Allāh in Ḥimṣ informed us: Kathīr ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Baqiyyah narrated from ʿUmar al-Kalāʿī, and he mentioned it.

It was also narrated by ʿUthmān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Waqqāṣī from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, raised to the Prophet (marfūʿ). And Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd Allāh al-ʿArzamī narrated it from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb, from his father, from his grandfather, as a stopped narration (mawqūf).

All the narrators of this ḥadīth from ʿAmr are weak, and Allah knows best. (Munkar. Narrated by Ibn ʿAdī.)"
7594,"(7591) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan informed us: Abū ʿĀmir informed us: al-Walīd informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿUthmān narrated from al-Ḥakam, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""There is no zakāh on jewels."" This narration is disconnected (munqaṭiʿ) and stopped (mawqūf). [Very weak (Ḍaʿīf Jiddan).]"
7595,"(7592) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Aṣamm informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us: Sharīk narrated from Sālim, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, who said: ""There is no zakāh on stone (jewels), except that which is held for trade — neither on jewels, nor rubies, nor pearls, nor anything else — except gold and silver.""

We have narrated a similar statement from ʿAṭāʾ, Sulaymān ibn Yasār, ʿIkrimah, al-Zuhrī, al-Nakhaʿī, and Makḥūl."
7596,"(7593) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār, from Udhaynah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""There is no zakāh on ambergris; it is merely something cast forth by the sea."" (Weak. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.)"
7597,"(7594) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, al-Ḥumaydī, Ibn Qaʿnab, and Saʿīd — all of them said: Sufyān narrated it, mentioning it. However, he said: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās say: ""Ambergris is not rikāz (buried treasure); rather, it is something that the sea has cast forth.""

It was also narrated by Ibn Jurayj from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār. [Weak. Previously discussed.]"
7598,"(7595) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Aḥmad ibn Shaybān informed us, Sufyān — (ḥ) and Abū Zakariyyā informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān narrated from Ibn Ṭāwūs, from his father, from Ibn ʿAbbās (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), that he was asked about ambergris. He said: ""If there is anything [obligatory] in it, then it is the khums (one-fifth).""

This is the wording of the hadith of al-Shāfiʿī. In the narration of Ibn Shaybān, it is said: ""Ibn ʿAbbās was asked: 'Is there zakāh on ambergris?'"" Then he mentioned the rest.

Thus, Ibn ʿAbbās made the ruling conditional in this narration, while he categorically stated that there is no zakāh on it in the first narration. The categorical statement is more appropriate, and Allāh knows best. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah.]"
7599,"(7596) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ādam informed us, Warqāʾ narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, regarding the words of the Most High: {Spend from the good things which you have earned} — he said: ""That refers to trade."" {And from that which We have brought forth for you from the earth} — he said: ""That refers to date-palms."" [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Ibn ʿAddī.]"
7600,"(7597) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, Abū Dāwūd informed us, Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd ibn Sufyān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ḥassān informed us, Sulaymān ibn Mūsā Abū Dāwūd informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Saʿd ibn Samurah ibn Jundub informed me, Khubayb ibn Sulaymān narrated to me from his father Sulaymān ibn Samurah, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: ""To proceed — the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command us to give ṣadaqah from that which we prepare for sale."" [Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7601,"(7598) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Qutaybah Salm ibn al-Faḍl al-Adamī in Makkah informed us, Mūsā ibn Hārūn informed us, Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas, from Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān, from Abū Dharr (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Camels have their ṣadaqah, sheep have their ṣadaqah, and cloth (al-bazz) has its ṣadaqah."" [Weak. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7602,"(7599) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Hishām ibn ʿAlī informed us, Ibn Rajāʾ informed us, Saʿīd — that is, Ibn Salamah ibn Abī al-Ḥusām — informed me, Mūsā narrated from ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas, from Mālik ibn al-Ḥadathān, from Abū Dharr (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Camels have their ṣadaqah, sheep have their ṣadaqah, and cloth (al-bazz) has its ṣadaqah. And whoever hoists up dinars or dirhams, or gold or silver, without intending to pay a debt therewith or to spend it in the cause of Allah, it is a treasure (kanz) with which he will be branded on the Day of Resurrection."" The mention of cattle (al-baqar) is missing from this narration. [Weak. Previously discussed.]"
7603,"(7600) It was also narrated by Dalaj ibn Aḥmad from Hishām ibn ʿAlī al-Sadūsī, and he mentioned in it: ""And cattle have their ṣadaqah."" Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us of that; Dalaj ibn Aḥmad al-Sijzī in Baghdād informed me, Hishām ibn ʿAlī al-Sadūsī informed us, and he mentioned it. [Weak. Previously discussed.]"
7604,"(7601) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī narrated it from Dalaj ibn Aḥmad and said: ""I wrote it from the ancient original manuscript, and in al-bazz it is muqayyad (restricted/conditional)."" Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us of that; Abū al-Ḥasan informed us, Dalaj ibn Aḥmad narrated from the original copy of his book, and he mentioned it. [Weak. Previously discussed.]"
7605,"(7602) Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr informed us, Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah, ʿImrān ibn Abī Anas narrated to me, from Mālik ibn Aws ibn al-Ḥadathān, who said: ""While I was sitting with ʿUthmān, Abū Dharr came to him."" Then he mentioned the hadith and said: ""They said: 'O Abū Dharr, narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' He said: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Camels have their ṣadaqah, sheep have their ṣadaqah, cattle have their ṣadaqah, and cloth (al-bazz) has its ṣadaqah.""'"" He said it with the letter zāy. [Weak. Previously discussed.]"
7606,"(7603) Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us, Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us, Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd informed us — (ḥ) and Abū Zakariyyā and others said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, Sufyān informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Salamah, from Abī ʿAmr ibn Ḥimās, that his father said: ""I passed by ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) while I was carrying a hide (adamah) on my neck. ʿUmar said: 'Will you not pay your zakāh, O Ḥimās?' I said: 'O Amīr al-Muʾminīn, I have nothing besides this which is on my back and a container of al-qaraẓ (tanning agent).' He said: 'That is wealth — put it down.' So I placed it before him. He assessed it, and it was found that zakāh was due upon it, so he took the zakāh from it.""

This is the wording of the hadith of Sufyān. The hadith of Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn is abridged. He said: ""Ḥimās used to sell hides and water-skins (al-jirāb). ʿUmar (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) said to him: 'Pay the zakāh of your wealth.' He said: 'My wealth is only water-skins and hides.' He said: 'Assess it and pay its zakāh.'"" [Weak. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
7607,"(7604) Abū Zakariyyā and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Sufyān narrated to us: Ibn ʿAjlān narrated from Abū al-Zinād from Abū ʿAmr ibn Ḥimās from his father, the same [hadith]. He [al-Shāfiʿī] said this after his first narration from Sufyān.

[Weak. Referred to previously.]"
7608,"(7605) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatāda informed us from his book: Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbda narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us: ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""There is no zakāh on goods (ʿurūḍ) except that which is intended for trade.""

The Shaykh said: This is the position of the majority of the scholars. As for what is narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""There is no zakāh on goods (ʿarḍ)"" — al-Shāfiʿī stated in al-Qadīm that the chain of this hadith from Ibn ʿAbbās is weak. Following the hadith of Ibn ʿUmar due to its soundness is preferable to me, and exercising caution in matters of zakāh is more beloved to me. And Allah knows best.

The Shaykh said: Ibn al-Mundhir narrated from ʿĀʾisha and Ibn ʿAbbās the same as what we narrated from Ibn ʿUmar, and no contradiction from them was reported from anyone. It is possible that the meaning of his statement — if it is sound — ""There is no zakāh on goods"" is when trade is not intended by it.

[Sound. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shayba.]"
7609,"(7606) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us from Ibn Shihāb from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd that ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: ""This is the month of your zakāh. So whoever has a debt upon him, let him pay his debt so that your assets become free, and then you may pay the zakāh from them.""

[Sound. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7610,"(7607) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī informed me: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us: Abū al-Yamān informed us: Shuʿayb informed us from al-Zuhrī, who said: al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd informed me that he heard ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him) delivering a sermon from the minbar of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), saying: ""This is the month of your zakāh."" Al-Sāʾib did not name the month for me, and I did not ask him about it. He said: ʿUthmān then said: ""So whoever among you has a debt upon him, let him settle his debt so that your assets become free, and then pay the zakāh from them.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Yamān.

[Sound. Referred to previously.]"
7611,"(7608) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us: Abū ʿAwāna narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Iyās from ʿAmr ibn Ḥarim from Jābir ibn Zayd from Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn ʿUmar regarding a man who borrows money and spends it on his produce and his family. Ibn ʿUmar said: ""He should begin by repaying what he borrowed, and then pay zakāh on what remains."" And Ibn ʿAbbās said: ""He should repay what he spent on the produce, and then pay zakāh on what remains.""

[Sound. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shayba.]"
7612,"(7609) Abū Saʿīd narrated to us alone: Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us: al-Ḥasan narrated to us: Yaḥyā narrated from Ibn al-Mubārak from Ibn Jurayj from Abū al-Zubayr from Ṭāwūs, who said: ""There is no ṣadaqah [zakāh] upon it.""

[Ḥasan. Al-Razzāq.]"
7613,"(7610) Through his chain of narration: Yaḥyā narrated to us: Wakīʿ narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAbd al-Malik, who said: I said to ʿAṭāʾ: ""Land that I cultivate?"" He said: ""Pay your expenses and give zakāh on what remains.""

[Sound.]"
7614,"(7611) Through his chain of narration: Yaḥyā narrated to us: Mindil, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth, and ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Sulaymān narrated from Layth from Ṭāwūs, who said: ""There is no zakāh upon a man on his wealth if he has a debt that encompasses his wealth.""

[Weak.]"
7615,"(7612) He said: Yaḥyā narrated to us: Ibn al-Mubārak narrated from Hishām from al-Ḥasan, the same [ruling].

[Weak.]"
7616,"(7613) He said: Yaḥyā narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Mughīra from Fuḍayl from Ibrāhīm, who said: ""Whatever debt is upon you, its zakāh is upon its owner [i.e., the creditor].""

[Weak.]"
7617,"(7614) Abū Aḥmad al-ʿAdl informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Mālik narrated from Yazīd ibn Khuṣayfah, that he asked Sulaymān ibn Yasār: ""A man has wealth upon which there is a debt equal to it — is there any Zakāh due upon it?"" He said: ""No."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Mālik]"
7618,"(7615) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, Ibn al-Mubārak reported from Yūnus, who said: ""I asked al-Zuhrī about a man who borrows against his garden and his cultivated land, the produce of which is not known — what is the extent of what his land yields?"" He said: ""We do not know of any Sunnah whereby the crop or fruit of a man upon whom there is a debt is left un-Zakāted. Rather, he pays Zakāh even if he owes a debt. However, as for a man who has gold and silver upon which there is a debt, he does not pay Zakāh until the debt is repaid."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7619,"(7616) He said: And Ibn al-Mubārak narrated to us from Ṭalḥah ibn al-Naḍr, who said: ""I heard Ibn Sīrīn saying: 'They used to not count fruits as part of a debt.' Ibn Sīrīn said: 'It is appropriate for a pledged asset to be counted as part of a debt.'""

The Sheikh said: ""This is the old position of al-Shāfiʿī, in which he differentiated between apparent (ẓāhir) wealth and hidden (bāṭin) wealth."" [Ḥasan]"
7620,"(7617) Abū Saʿīd informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Yaḥyā narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Salām reported from Misʿar, from al-Ḥakam, that Ibrāhīm said: ""He pays Zakāh on his wealth even if there is a debt upon him equal to it."" He (al-Ḥakam) said: ""I spoke to him about it until he retracted from that."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7621,"(7618) He said: And Yaḥyā narrated to us, Abū Bakr al-Nahshalī narrated to us, from Ḥammād ibn Abī Sulaymān, that he said: ""A man pays Zakāh on his wealth even if there is a debt upon him equal to it, because he eats from it and marries from it.""

The Sheikh said: ""The apparent texts (al-ẓawāhir) that have come regarding the obligation of Zakāh on wealth testify to the correctness of this statement, and it is the later position of al-Shāfiʿī. He used to say that the ḥadīth of ʿUthmān resembles it — and Allah knows best — that he may have only commanded the repayment of the debt before the Zakāh becomes due on the wealth. And his statement: 'This is the month of your Zakāh' — it is possible that he meant: 'This is the month after the passing of which your Zakāh becomes due,' just as it is said: 'The month of Dhū al-Ḥijjah,' whereas the Ḥajj occurs only after several days of it have passed.""

Abū Saʿīd informed us with this chain, Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us, al-Rabīʿ informed us, and he said: al-Shāfiʿī said, and he mentioned it. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7622,"(7619) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us, Ibn Lahīʿah reported from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from al-Sāʾib ibn Yazīd, from ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Pay Zakāh — meaning on the debt — when it is in the hands of the wealthy."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
7623,"(7620) He said: And al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us from al-Layth ibn Saʿd, that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbbās and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar both said: ""Whoever borrows wealth, its Zakāh is due upon him every year, provided it is in a reliable (profitable) venture."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
7624,"(7621) And it has been narrated to us from Thawr ibn Zayd, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that he was asked about the Zakāh of absent wealth. He said: ""Pay Zakāh on the absent wealth just as you pay it on the present wealth."" The man said to him: ""Then the wealth will be destroyed!"" He said: ""The destruction of wealth is better than the destruction of the dīn (religion).""

This was reported to me by Abū ʿAbdillāh from Abī al-Walīd from ʿAbdillāh from Abī ʿAbdillāh from ʿUbaydullāh ibn Saʿd from his uncle from his father from Ibn Isḥāq from Thawr. [Ḍaʿīf. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7625,"(7622) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from ʿUbaydullāh, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he used to borrow the wealth of orphans under his care, because he considered it more secure for them than leaving it idle. And he used to pay its Zakāh from their wealth.

And it has been narrated to us from ʿAlī and ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with them both) a statement similar to this, and also from al-Ḥasan, Ṭāwūs, Mujāhid, al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, al-Zuhrī, and al-Nakhaʿī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by ʿAbd al-Razzāq]"
7626,"(7623) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Kārizī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz informed us, and he said: Abū ʿUbayd said regarding the ḥadīth of ʿAlī about a man who has a doubtful debt (al-dayn al-ẓannūn): ""He pays its Zakāh for past years when he receives it, provided he is truthful.""

It was narrated to us by Yazīd ibn Hārūn from Hishām, from Ibn Sīrīn, from ʿAbīdah, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him).

And Abū ʿUbayd said: His statement ""al-ẓannūn"" refers to a debt whose holder does not know whether the debtor will repay it or not — as though it is one that is not hoped for. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Mentioned by Abū ʿUbayd]"
7627,"(7624) Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Aṣbahānī — informed us, Abū Naṣr al-ʿIrāqī — informed us, Sufyān ibn Muḥammad — narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan — narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh al-ʿAdanī — narrated to us, Sufyān, from Mūsā ibn ʿUbaydah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""Pay zakāh on what is in your possession, and whatever debt is owed by a trustworthy person is equivalent to what is in your possession, but whatever debt is doubtful, there is no zakāh on it until you receive it."" [Weak]"
7628,"(7625) Abū al-Qāsim — ʿAbd al-Khāliq ibn ʿAlī al-Mu'adhdhin — informed us, Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Khanb — informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl — narrated to us, Ayyūb ibn Sulaymān — narrated to me, Abū Bakr ibn Abī Uways, from Sulaymān ibn Bilāl, from Yūnus, from Ibn Shihāb, from Ḥumayd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd al-Qārī, who was in charge of the bayt al-māl of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him), said: ""The people used to collect zakāh from debts. When the stipends were distributed, the officials would withhold their debts, and whatever remained in their hands, their zakāh was taken before they received it. Thereafter, the people incurred debts that were lost, and they would not take zakāh from the debt except what had materialized from it. However, when they received the debt, they would pay zakāh for the years that had passed."" [Sound. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7629,"(7626) Abū Aḥmad al-ʿAdl — informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar — narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm — narrated to us, Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us, Mālik, from Ayyūb ibn Abī Tamīmah al-Sakhtiyānī, that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote regarding wealth that some governors had seized unjustly, ordering that it be returned to its people and that its zakāh be collected for the years that had passed. Then he followed this with another letter stating that only one year's zakāh should be taken from it, because it was ḍimār (absent wealth not expected to be recovered). Abū ʿUbayd said: ḍimār refers to that which is not hoped for. [Weak. Mālik reported it]"
7630,"(7627) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith — informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān — narrated to us, Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan — narrated to us, Abū ʿĀmir — narrated to us, al-Walīd ibn Muslim — narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn al-Aswad, that he heard ʿAṭāʾ say: ""There is no zakāh on a debt owed to you, even if it is fully established."" Ibn al-Mundhir also narrated this from Ibn ʿUmar, ʿĀʾishah, then ʿIkrimah, and ʿAṭāʾ. [Good]"
7631,"(7628) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us, al-Rabīʿ — informed us, al-Shāfiʿī — a scholar from the people of Makkah narrated to me, saying: ""I heard Ṭāwūs, while I was standing at his head, asking about selling ṣadaqah before it is received. Ṭāwūs said: 'By the Lord of this House, it is not permissible to sell it before it is received, nor after it is received.'"" Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""Because the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded that it be taken from their wealthy and returned to their poor — the poor of the people of the shares — and it should be returned in its exact form, and its price should not be returned."" The scholar said: ""The reports that have come regarding the obligations of ṣadaqāt are evidence on this matter."" [Weak. Al-Shāfiʿī reported it]"
7632,"(7629) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh — informed us, Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān — informed us, Ibn Abī ʿĀṣim — narrated to us, Ibn Kāsib — narrated to us, ʿĪsā ibn al-Ḍarimī ibn Kulthum ibn ʿAlqamah ibn Nājiyah ibn al-Ḥārith, from his father, from his grandfather Kulthum, from his father, who said: ""They asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) to purchase from them the remainder of their ṣadaqāt that was still owed. He said: 'We do not sell anything from the ṣadaqāt until we receive it.'"" This chain is not strong. It was also narrated from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh through two chains — one from Abū Saʿīd, disconnected, and one from Abū Hurayrah, connected and elevated — regarding the prohibition of that. Ismāʿīl is not used as a proof, and Allah knows best. [Weak. Al-Ṭabarānī reported it]"
7633,"(7630) Al-Sharīf Abū al-Fatḥ al-Faqīh — informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Shurayḥ — informed us, Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī — narrated to us, ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd — narrated to me, Muḥammad ibn Rāshid, from Makḥūl, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Do not sell the ṣadaqāt until they are branded and secured."" Abū Dāwūd reported it in al-Marāsīl, then he said: ""This is also narrated as the statement of Makḥūl."" [Denounced. Ibn Abī Shaybah reported it]"
7634,"(7631) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh — informed us, Asad ibn Mūsā — narrated to us, al-Ḥumaydī — narrated to us, Sufyān, who said: ""I heard Mālik ibn Anas asking Zayd ibn Aslam, and Zayd said: 'I heard my father say: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I provided a horse for the cause of Allah, and I saw it being sold. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) whether I should buy it. He said: 'Do not buy it, and do not return to your ṣadaqah.'""'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī. [Sound. Al-Bukhārī reported it]"
7635,"(7632) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — narrated to us, Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Manṣūr — narrated to us, Hārūn ibn Yūsuf — narrated to us, Ibn Abī ʿUmar — narrated to us, Sufyān, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, who said: ""ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) provided a horse for the cause of Allah, and he saw some of its offspring being sold. He wished to buy it, so he asked the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) about it. He said: 'Do not buy it, and do not return to your ṣadaqah.'"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Sound. Reported previously]"
7636,"(7633) Abū al-Ḥasan — ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān — informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār — narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Qāḍī and Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh, who both said: narrated to us, al-Qaʿnabī. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us, and the wording is his — narrated to me, Abū al-Ḥasan — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs — narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī — narrated to us, al-Qaʿnabī, from what he read to Mālik, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, who said: ""I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) say: 'I provided a noble horse for the cause of Allah, and the one who had it neglected it. I wanted to buy it from him, thinking he would sell it cheaply. I asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) about that. He said: ""Do not buy it, even if he gives you it for a single dirham."" For the one who returns to his ṣadaqah is like the dog that returns to its vomit.'"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and al-Bukhārī reported it from a group from Mālik. [Sound. Al-Bukhārī reported it]"
7637,"(7634) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us — al-Layth narrated to me — ʿUqayl narrated from Ibn Shihāb — from Sālim ibn ʿAbdullāh: that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar used to narrate that ʿUmar had given a horse in charity (ṣadaqah) in the path of Allah, and later he found it being sold, so he wished to purchase it. He then came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and sought his counsel regarding that. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said to him: ""Do not take back your charity."" It was because of this that Ibn ʿUmar would refrain from purchasing anything he had given in charity or conferred as a benefit, except that he would give it again as ṣadaqah."
7638,"(7635) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bishr narrated to us — Marwān ibn Muʿāwiya narrated to me — ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAṭāʾ al-Madanī narrated to me — ʿAbdullāh ibn Burayda al-Aslamī narrated from his father, who said: I was with the Prophet ﷺ when a woman came to him and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I had given a slave-girl in charity on behalf of my mother, then my mother died and the slave-girl remained."" He said: ""Your reward has been secured, and she has returned to you through inheritance."" She said: ""She died with a month's fasting still due upon her."" He said: ""Fast on behalf of your mother."" She said: ""She also never performed Ḥajj."" He said: ""Perform Ḥajj on behalf of your mother."""
7639,"(7636) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us — al-Shāfiʿī informed us — Mālik narrated from Rabīʿa ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from more than one of their scholars: that the Prophet ﷺ granted (aqṭaʿa) Bilāl ibn al-Ḥārith al-Muzanī the mines of al-Qabaliyya, which are in the direction of al-Furʿ. Those mines are such that nothing is taken from them [by way of tax] except the zakāh, up to this day.

Al-Shāfiʿī said: This is not among the matters that the scholars of hadith would affirm as established. Even if it were established, there would be no narration from the Prophet ﷺ in it except his act of granting (iqṭāʿ). As for the zakāh on mines without the khums, it is not reported from the Prophet ﷺ.

The Shaykh said: It is as al-Shāfiʿī said regarding the narration of Mālik, and it has also been narrated from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Dārawardī from Rabīʿa with a connected chain."
7640,"(7637) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us — al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyib narrated to us — Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us — ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated from Rabīʿa ibn Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān — from al-Ḥārith ibn Bilāl ibn al-Ḥārith — from his father: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took the ṣadaqah from the mines of al-Qabaliyya, and that he granted (aqṭaʿa) Bilāl ibn al-Ḥārith the ʿAqīq in its entirety. When ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābah — may Allah be pleased with him — became caliph, he said to Bilāl: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ only granted it to you so that you would work it."" So ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābah — may Allah be pleased with him — distributed the ʿAqīq among the people."
7641,"(7638) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us — al-Walīd narrated to us — Saʿīd narrated from Qatāda: that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz — may Allah be pleased with him — treated the mine (maʿdin) as equivalent to buried treasure (rikāz), taking the khums from it. Then he followed this with another decree in which he established the zakāh.

And it has been narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Bakr: that ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz took five dirhams from every two hundred dirhams [extracted] from the mines.

And from Abū al-Zinād, who said: ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz established a quarter of the ʿushr (tenth) on mines, unless it was a buried treasure (rikza), in which case the khums applied."
7642,"(7639) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us — Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn al-Ṣaqr narrated to us — Dāwūd ibn ʿAmr narrated to us — Ḥibbān ibn ʿAlī narrated to us — ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd narrated to me — from his father — from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Al-rikāz is the gold that grows in the earth."""
7643,"(7640) Abū Yūsuf narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd — from his father — from his grandfather — from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""In al-rikāz there is the khums."" It was asked: ""And what is al-rikāz, O Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Gold and silver that Allah created in the earth on the day it was created.""

Abū Saʿd al-Zāhid narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās ibn Mīkāl narrated to us — Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Faqīh in Fāris narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — Bishr ibn al-Walīd al-Kindī narrated to us — Abū Yūsuf narrated, and he mentioned it.

ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd al-Maqburī is alone in narrating this, and he is extremely weak (ḍaʿīf jiddan). Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal, Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn, and a group of the imams of hadith criticized him.

Al-Shāfiʿī said in the narration of Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Shāfiʿī al-Baghdādī from him: Abū Salamah, Saʿīd, Ibn Sīrīn, Muḥammad ibn Ziyād, and others have narrated from Abū Hurayrah his hadith from the Prophet ﷺ: ""In al-rikāz there is the khums."" Not one of them mentioned anything of what al-Maqburī mentioned in his hadith, and that which was narrated [with the additional wording] was only from a weak narrator — it was only ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿīd al-Maqburī who narrated it, and ʿAbdullāh was avoided by the people [due to his weakness]. The report of a man whose hadith the people have avoided should not be used as evidence."
7644,"(7641) Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us — they both said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us — Ibn Wahb informed me — ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith and Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated to me — from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb — from his father — from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ: that a man from Muzayna came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what do you say about the guarded mountain pasture?"" He said: ""It and the like of it [are subject to the same ruling]. The penalty (nakāl) [applies] — there is no amputation for anything of the livestock except what a thief has taken and whose value reaches the price of a shield (mijann), in which case the hand is amputated. And whatever does not reach the value of a shield incurs a penalty of twice its value and disciplinary lashes (jaladāt nakāl).""

He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, then what do you say about hanging fruit?"" He said: ""It and the like of it [are subject to the same ruling], along with the penalty. There is no amputation for anything of the hanging fruit except what has been stored in the granary (jarīn). Whatever is taken from the granary and reaches the value of a shield — in that there is amputation. And whatever does not reach the value of a shield incurs a penalty of twice its value and disciplinary lashes.""

He said: ""Then what do you say about what is found on a desolate road or in an uninhabited village?"" He said: ""Announce it for a year. If its claimant comes, hand it over to him; otherwise, it is your affair. But if its seeker ever comes on some day, then give it to him. Whatever is found on a road other than a desolate one, or in a village other than an uninhabited one — in that, and in al-rikāz, there is the khums.""

He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, then what do you say about a lost sheep?"" He said: ""It is food to be eaten — for you, or for your brother, or for the wolf. Restrain your brother's lost animal for him.""

He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, then what do you say about a lost camel?"" He said: ""What concern is it of yours? It has its water-skin and its shoes [to sustain it], and the wolf is not feared for it. It eats the pasture and drinks the water. Leave it until its owner comes.""

Whoever holds the first view responds to this by stating that this report pertains to what is found of the wealth of the Jāhiliyya, visible upon the earth, on a road that is not desolate and in a village that is not uninhabited. In such a case, and in al-rikāz, there is the khums, and this has nothing to do with the mine.

Al-Shāfiʿī mentioned, in the narration of al-Zaʿfarānī from him, their reliance on the first hadith, then said: ""It is, according to the scholars of hadith, weak."" He then mentioned their reliance on the hadith of Hishām ibn Saʿd from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb — this hadith — and then said: ""If the hadith of ʿAmr is to be used as evidence, then what is used as evidence against it [i.e., the counter-argument] is not based on a [valid] ruling. And if the hadith of ʿAmr is not to be used as evidence, then to use something other than evidence as evidence is ignorance."" He then mentioned their disagreement with the hadith regarding the penalty (gharāma), and regarding fresh dates (rutab) when stored in the granary, and regarding the picked-up object (luqṭa). Then said: ""The hadith of ʿAmr contradicts what he narrated in more than one ruling, and yet they derive from it only a single matter — that is merely a conjecture in the hadith. If it were evidence in one matter, then let him hold by it in what he has omitted.""

The Shaykh said: His statement ""that is merely a conjecture in the hadith"" is an indication of what we have mentioned — that it does not pertain to the mine. Rather, it pertains to what falls under the meaning of al-rikāz from the wealth of the Jāhiliyya. And Allah knows best."
7645,"(7642) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us — Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us — al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to us — Ḥafṣ ibn Ghaylān narrated from Maḥkūl: that ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābah — may Allah be pleased with him — treated the mine (maʿdin) as equivalent to buried treasure (rikāz), in which there is the khums.

And this is disconnected (munqaṭiʿ), for Maḥkūl did not reach the era of ʿUmar — may Allah be pleased with him."
7646,"(7643) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us — Ḥammād narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq — from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatāda — from Maḥmūd ibn Labīd — from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Anṣārī, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ when a man came with an egg-sized piece of gold and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I found this in a mine. Take it as ṣadaqah, for I own nothing else."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ turned away from him. Then he came from his right side and said the same thing, and he turned away from him. Then he came from his left side and he turned away from him. Then he came from behind him, and the Messenger of Allah ﷺ took it and threw it at him — had it struck him, it would have hurt him or wounded him. Then the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""One of you comes with what he owns and says: 'This is ṣadaqah,' then he sits and begs from the people. The best charity is that which comes from a state of self-sufficiency (ẓahr ghināʾ).""

This hadith allows the possibility that he ﷺ only refrained from taking the obligatory portion from it because it fell below the niṣāb, and it allows other possibilities as well. The hadiths regarding the niṣāb of gold and silver have already been discussed."
7647,"(7644) It is narrated mawṣūlan: Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh — informed us Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān al-Aṣbahānī — narrated to us Ibn Abī Ḥātim — narrated to us Abū Zurʿah — narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ — narrated to us ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ al-Madanī — narrated to us Ibn Abī Dhiʾb from al-Maqburī from Abū Hurayrah: that a man came to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ with five awqiyyah and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I obtained this from a mine, so take the zakāh from it."" He ﷺ said, ""There is nothing [due] in it,"" and he returned it to him.

And it is possible that this and the ḥadīth of Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh are narrations about a single incident, except that Jābir did not mention the quantity, and that was mentioned in the ḥadīth of Abū Hurayrah. And the two aḥādīth are agreed upon that he ﷺ did not see anything [due] in it at that time. And Allah knows best. [Weak]"
7648,"(7645) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — informed us Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭūsī — narrated to us ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn Munīb — narrated to us Sufyān from al-Zuhrī from Abū Salamah and Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab, both of whom heard it from Abū Hurayrah, who reported from the Prophet ﷺ that he said: ""The wound of the animal (al-ʿajmāʾ) is without liability (jubār), the well is without liability, the mine is without liability, and in the buried treasure (al-rikāz) is the fifth (al-khums).""

[Sound. Al-Bukhārī narrated it.]"
7649,"(7646) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us al-Ḥasan ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAlī — narrated to us Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā — informed us Sufyān, and he mentioned it in a similar manner.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and others. [Sound. Previously mentioned.]"
7650,"(7647) Abū Aḥmad — ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan — informed us Abū Bakr — Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī — narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī — narrated to us Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us Mālik from Ibn Shihāb from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab and Abū Salamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The wound of the animal is without liability, the well is without liability, the mine is without liability, and in the buried treasure is the fifth.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Mālik. [Sound. Previously mentioned.]"
7651,"(7648) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī and others — informed us Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — informed us al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān — informed us al-Shāfiʿī — informed us Sufyān from Dāwūd ibn Shābūr and Yaʿqūb ibn ʿAṭāʾ from ʿAmr ibn Shuʿayb from his father from his grandfather that the Prophet ﷺ said regarding a treasure (kanz) that a man found in an abandoned ruin of the Jāhiliyyah: ""If you find it in an inhabited village or a well-trodden path, then announce it. But if you find it in an abandoned ruin of the Jāhiliyyah or in an uninhabited village, then in it and in the buried treasure is the fifth.""

[Good. Al-Shāfiʿī narrated it.]"
7652,"(7649) Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī — informed us Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī — narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Khawwāṣ — narrated to us Bakkār ibn Qutaybah — narrated to us Abū ʿĀmir al-ʿAqadī — narrated to us Zuhayr ibn Muḥammad from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam from his father from Anas ibn Mālik, who informed him, saying: We arrived with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and one of our companions entered a ruin to relieve himself. He went to take a brick from it, and it collapsed upon him with gold (tibr). He took it and brought it to the Prophet ﷺ, who said, ""Weigh it."" So he weighed it, and it was two hundred dirhams. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""This is buried treasure (rikāz), and in it is the fifth.""

ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Zayd ibn Aslam is weak. [Weak. Aḥmad narrated it.]"
7653,"(7650) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī — informed us Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar — narrated to us Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm — narrated to us Ibn Bukayr — narrated to us Mālik: that he heard some of the people of knowledge say regarding buried treasure (al-rikāz): ""It is only the burial of the Jāhiliyyah — that which is not sought with wealth and for which no great effort is required. As for that which is sought with wealth or for which great effort is required, and one sometimes obtains it and sometimes misses it, then it is not buried treasure.""

(T) And Abū Dāwūd narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb from ʿAbbād ibn al-ʿAwwām from Hishām from al-Ḥasan, who said: ""Buried treasure (al-rikāz) is the ancient treasure (al-kanz al-ʿādī)."" And that fell out of my book. [Sound. Al-Muwaṭṭaʾ.]"
7654,"(7651) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī — informed us Muḥammad ibn Bakr — narrated to us Abū Dāwūd — narrated to us Jaʿfar ibn Musāfir — narrated to us Ibn Abī Fudayk — narrated to us al-Zamʿī from his aunt Quraybah bint ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb from her mother Karīmah bint al-Miqdād from Ḍubāʿah bint al-Zubayr ibn ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib ibn Hāshim, who informed her, saying: Al-Miqdād went to relieve himself in Baqīʿ al-Khabkhabah, when a jerboa emerged from a hole with a dinar. It continued to bring out one dinar after another until it had brought out seventeen dinars. Then it brought out a red piece of cloth in which there was a dinar, making eighteen dinars in total. He went with them to the Prophet ﷺ, informed him, and said, ""Take its charity (ṣadaqah)."" The Prophet ﷺ said to him, ""Did you descend into the hole?"" He said, ""No."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""May Allah bless you in it.""

[Weak. Abū Dāwūd narrated it.]"
7655,"(7652) Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī — informed us Abū Ḥāmid ibn al-Sharqī — narrated to us Abū al-Azhar — narrated to us Wahb ibn Jarīr — narrated to us my father, who said: I heard Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrate from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah from Bujayr ibn Abī Bujayr, who said: I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ say: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say when we went out with him to al-Ṭāʾif and passed by a grave. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said, ""This is the grave of Abū Fulān. He used to seek protection through this sanctuary, and when he left it, the punishment that befell his people struck him in this place, and he was buried in it. The sign of that is that a branch of gold was buried with him. If you exhume him, you will find it with him."" So the people rushed to it and extracted the branch from it.

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Sunan from Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn from Wahb ibn Jarīr, and he said: ""The grave of Abū Righāl."" [Weak. Abū Dāwūd narrated it.]"
7656,"(7653) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān — informed us Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān — narrated to us Abū Yaʿqūb — Isḥāq ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Maymūn al-Ḥarbī — narrated to us al-Riyāḥī, meaning ʿUmar ibn al-Wahhāb — narrated to us Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ — narrated to us Rūḥ ibn al-Qāsim from Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah from Bujayr ibn Abī Bujayr from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr, may Allah be pleased with him: that they were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a journey or expedition and passed by a grave. He ﷺ said, ""This is the grave of Abū Righāl. He was from the people of Thamūd. When Allah destroyed his people with what He destroyed them, he was protected by virtue of his being in the sanctuary. But when he left and reached this place or this spot, he died, and he was buried with a branch of gold."" So we rushed to it and extracted it. [Weak. Previously mentioned.]"
7657,"(7654) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bashrān informed us; Abū ʿAmr ibn al-Sammāk informed us; Ḥanbal ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Abū ʿAwānah narrated from Simāk, from Jarīr ibn Rabbāḥ, from his father, that they came upon a grave in al-Madāʾin and found therein a man upon whom were garments woven with gold, and they found wealth with him. They brought it to ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir (may Allah be pleased with him), and he wrote concerning it to ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him). So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to him: ""Give it to them and do not take it from them.""

The Sheikh said: This is if they found it in dead land (mawāt) which they own, and the khums is due upon it, and it has not reached the niṣāb, or it has been delegated to them so that they may extract it. And Allah knows best."
7658,"(7655) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khāld narrated to us, from al-Shaʿbī, who said: A man came to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: ""I found one thousand and five hundred dirhams in a ruin in the Sawād."" ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I shall indeed pass a clear judgment concerning it. If you found it in a village whose kharāj is paid by another village, then it belongs to the people of that village. And if you found it in a village whose kharāj is not paid by any other village, then four-fifths are yours and the khums is for us."" Then he said: ""And the khums is also for you.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: They have narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) through a connected chain of narration that he said: ""Four-fifths are yours, and distribute the khums among your poor relatives."" And this hadith is more akin to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), and Allah knows best.

The Sheikh said: It is as he said, for Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr al-Makkī narrated in his book from Ibn ʿUyaynah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishr al-Khathʿamī, from a man from his people called Ibn Ḥummah, who said: ""A jar fell upon me from an old monastery in Kūfah containing four thousand dirhams, so I took it to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him)."" He said: ""Divide it into five parts."" So I divided it, and ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) took one-fifth from it and gave me four-fifths. When I turned to leave, he called me and said: ""Are there any poor and needy among your neighbors?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Take it and distribute it among them."""
7659,"(7656) The noble Abū al-Fatḥ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Saqaṭī informed us in Makkah; Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUmar ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from ʿAbdullāh ibn Bishr al-Khathʿamī, from a man from his people, that a man had a jar fall upon him from a monastery in Kūfah, and he brought it to ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him). He said: ""Divide it into fifths."" Then he said: ""Take four-fifths from it and leave one."" Then he said: ""Are there any poor or needy in your neighborhood?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Take it and distribute it among them."" [Weak. Narrated by Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr.]"
7660,"(7657) The jurist Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Aḥmad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us in Ṭabarān; Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed us; ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us; Abū al-Walīd narrated to us; Shuʿbah said: ʿAmr ibn Murrah informed me.

(Ḥ) And Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us by dictation; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūj al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us; Abū ʿUmar narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, who said: When the Prophet (ﷺ) was brought the ṣadaqah of a people, he would say: ""O Allah, send Your blessings upon the family of so-and-so."" And my father brought his ṣadaqah, so he said: ""O Allah, send Your blessings upon the family of Abī Awfā.""

The wording is that of the hadith of Abū ʿUmar. And in the narration of Abū al-Walīd, he said: from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Awfā, who was among the Companions of the Tree (may Allah be pleased with them), that the Prophet (ﷺ), when a people brought him their ṣadaqah, would say: ""O Allah, send Your blessings upon them."" Then he mentioned the rest thereafter.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū ʿUmar Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmar and others, and Muslim narrated it through various routes from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7661,"(7658) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Yūnus al-Ḍabbī informed us. And Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Tamīm al-Qanṭarī informed us in Baghdād; Abū Qilābah informed us. They said: Abū ʿĀṣim narrated to us, from Sufyān, from ʿĀṣim ibn Kulayb, from his father, from Wāʾil ibn Ḥujr, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he sent a man, and the man sent to him a weaned young she-camel. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The collector of Allah and the collector of His Messenger came to him, and he sent a weaned young she-camel. O Allah, do not bless him or his camels."" This reached the man, so he sent to him a she-camel of great beauty and excellence. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Such-and-such has been reached by what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said, so he sent a she-camel of great beauty. O Allah, bless him and his camels."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
7662,"(7659) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Milḥān informed us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us; Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated to me, from ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿbad ibn ʿAbbās, from ʿĀṣim ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī, from Qays ibn Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah al-Anṣārī (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent him as a collector, and his father said: ""Do not go out until you take a covenant from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" When he wished to depart, he came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to him: ""O Qays, do not come on the Day of Resurrection with a camel upon your back that is bellowing, or a cow that is lowing, or a sheep that is bleating. And do not be like Abū Righāl."" Saʿd (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""O Messenger of Allah, and what is Abū Righāl?"" He said: ""A collector whom a righteous man sent, and he found a man in Ṭāʾif with a flock of sheep close to a hundred, except for one sheep, and a small boy who had no mother. The milk of that sheep was his sustenance. The owner of the sheep said: 'Who are you?' He said: 'I am the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' He welcomed him and said: 'These are my sheep; take whichever you wish.' He looked at the milking ewe and said: 'This one.' The man said: 'This boy, as you see, has no food or drink other than it.' He said: 'If you love milk, then I love it.' He said: 'Take two sheep in its place.' He kept increasing and offering until he offered five sheep in its place, but he refused. When he saw that, he took his bow and shot him, killing him, and said: 'It is not fitting that anyone should bring this news to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) before me.' So the owner of the sheep came to the righteous Prophet (ﷺ) and informed him. The righteous one said: 'O Allah, curse Abū Righāl! O Allah, curse Abū Righāl!'"" Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said: ""O Messenger of Allah, excuse Qays from collecting."" [Weak. Narrated by Ibn Khurayj.]"
7663,"(7660) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, from al-Qāsim ibn Muḥammad, from ʿĀʾishah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), that she said: ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) was passed by with sheep from the ṣadaqah, and he saw among them a pregnant ewe with an enormous udder. ʿUmar said: ""What is this sheep?"" They said: ""A sheep from the ṣadaqah."" ʿUmar said: ""Its owners did not give it willingly. Do not tempt the people. Do not take the choicest livestock of the Muslims; turn away from such food."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7664,"(7661) And Abū Aḥmad informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Jaʿfar informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; Mālik narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us; al-Rabīʿ informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Mālik narrated from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd, from Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Ḥabbān, that he said: Two men from Ashjaʿ informed me that Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-Anṣārī (may Allah be pleased with him) used to come to them as a collector and say to the owner of wealth: ""Bring out the ṣadaqah of your wealth to me."" He would not lead to him a sheep in which there was the fulfillment of his due except that he accepted it. [Weak. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7665,"(7662) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khāld informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh informed us, and Abū al-Walīd informed me; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Khāld narrated from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, from ʿAdī ibn ʿAmīrah al-Kindī, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: ""Whomever we appoint over any of our work and he conceals from us so much as a needle or more, it is ghulūl (embezzlement) which he will bring with him on the Day of Resurrection."" He said: A black man from the Anṣār stood up before him—it is as though I am looking at him—and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, accept my resignation from your work."" He said: ""What is the matter with you?"" He said: ""I heard you say such and such."" He said: ""And I say it now: Whomever we appoint over any work, let him bring the little and the much of it. Whatever he is commanded, he takes, and whatever he is forbidden, he abstains.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7666,"(7663) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-ʿAbbās al-Rāzī informed us; Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ibn Ṭāwūs, from his father, from ʿUbādah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent him to collect the ṣadaqah and said: ""O Abū al-Walīd, beware—do not come on the Day of Resurrection carrying a camel that is bellowing, or a cow that is lowing, or a sheep that is bleating."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, will that indeed happen?"" He said: ""Yes, by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, it will indeed be so, except those whom Allah has mercy upon."" He said: ""Then, by the One Who sent you with the truth, I shall never work over anything ever again""—or he said: ""over two."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than its chain. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
7667,"(7664) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us — and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān in Baghdād informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us — both saying: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from Abū Ḥumayd al-Sāʿidī that the Prophet ﷺ appointed a man from al-Azd over the ṣadaqah, known as Ibn al-Lutbiyyah. When he came, he said to the Prophet ﷺ: ""This is for you, and this was given to me as a gift."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ stood upon the pulpit, praised Allah, and extolled Him, saying: ""What is the matter with a worker whom we appoint over some of our affairs, and he comes saying: 'This is for you, and this was given to me as a gift'? Why did he not sit in his father's house or his mother's house and see whether anything would be given to him as a gift or not? By the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Muḥammad, none of you brings anything from it except that he will come with it on the Day of Resurrection, carrying it upon his neck — if it is a camel, it will be braying; or a cow, it will be lowing; or a sheep, it will be bleating."" Then he raised his hands until I could see the whiteness of his armpits, and he said: ""O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed? O Allah, have I conveyed?""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn ʿAbdullāh, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others, all from Ibn ʿUyaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7668,"(7665) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Walīd informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us; Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from Abū Ḥumayd, who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say…"" and he mentioned the hadith.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded above.]"
7669,"(7666) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣafwān al-Jumaḥī narrated from —

And Abū Saʿd al-Mālinī informed us; Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us; Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ṣūfī narrated to us; Surayj ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣafwān ibn Umayyah narrated to us; Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated from his father, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Ṣadaqah does not mix with any wealth except that it destroys it."" And in the narration of al-Shāfiʿī: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Ṣadaqah does not mix with any wealth except that it destroys it.""

Abū Aḥmad said: ""I do not know of anyone other than him who narrated it from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ḥumaydī.]"
7670,"(7667) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Yūsuf ibn Isḥāq ibn Yaʿqūb al-Sūsī narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Ādam narrated to us; Abū Ḥammād al-Ḥanafī narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he used to say: ""This verse was revealed — {Indeed, he succeeds who purifies himself and mentions the name of his Lord and prays} (al-Aʿlā:14–15) — concerning the zakāh of Ramaḍān."" [Very weak.]"
7671,"(7668) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Yāsīn narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Musayyibī narrated to us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Nāfiʿ narrated from Kathīr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Muzanī, from his father, from his grandfather, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was asked about His statement: {Indeed, he succeeds who purifies himself and mentions the name of his Lord and prays} (al-Aʿlā:14–15). He said: ""It is the zakāh of al-fiṭr."" [Very weak. Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
7672,"(7669) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās, who is al-Aṣamm, informed us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Ḥammād narrated to me; a shaykh from Banī Saʿd narrated from Abū al-ʿĀliyah, regarding {Indeed, he succeeds who purifies himself} (al-Aʿlā:14), he said: ""He gives the ṣadaqah of al-fiṭr, then he prays.""

And it has been narrated to us from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib and Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn and others among the Tābiʿīn (may Allah be pleased with them all). [Weak. Narrated by ʿAbd ibn Ḥamīd.]"
7673,"(7670) Abū Dharr Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Ḥusayn ibn Abī al-Qāsim al-Mudhakkir informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us; Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Saʿdī informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; ʿUbaydullāh narrated from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ enjoined the zakāh of al-fiṭr: a ṣāʿ of dates or a ṣāʿ of barley, upon every free person and slave, young and old.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]

As for what has been narrated from Qays ibn Saʿd concerning that:"
7674,"(7671) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated it to us; Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Baṣrī informed us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from al-Qāsim ibn Mukhaymirah, from Abū ʿAmmār, who said: ""We asked Qays ibn Saʿd about the ṣadaqah of al-fiṭr, and he said: 'The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us with it before the zakāh was revealed. When the zakāh was revealed, he neither commanded us nor forbade us, and we continued to do it.'""

The Shaykh said: ""This does not indicate the abrogation of its obligation, because the revelation of one obligation does not necessitate the abrogation of another. The people of knowledge have unanimously agreed upon the obligation of the zakāh of al-fiṭr, even though they differed regarding its designation as a 'farḍ' (obligation). It is not permissible to abandon it. And success is through Allah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī in al-Kabīr.]"
7675,"(7672) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; al-Qaʿnabī narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ, from Abū Saʿīd, who said: ""We used to bring out, when the Messenger of Allah ﷺ was among us, the zakāh of al-fiṭr for every young and old, free or enslaved person: a ṣāʿ of food, or a ṣāʿ of aqīṭ (dried curd), or a ṣāʿ of barley, or a ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of raisins.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7676,"(7673) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad al-Kaʿbī informed us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Junayd informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us; Ibn Wahb informed us; Makhrah ibn Bukayr informed me from his father, from ʿIrāk ibn Mālik, who said: ""I heard Abū Hurayrah narrate from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that he said: 'There is no ṣadaqah upon a slave except the ṣadaqah of al-fiṭr.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir and others from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7677,"(7674) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Abd al-Khaliq narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Rishdin narrated to us; Ibn Abi Maryam narrated to us; Nafi' ibn Yazid narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah narrated to us from 'Irak ibn Malik from Abu Hurayrah from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that he said: ""There is no obligatory charity upon a man regarding his horse or his slave, except the Zakat al-Fitr.""

Muhammad ibn Sahl ibn 'Askar narrated it from Ibn Abi Maryam and said in the hadith: ""There is no charity upon a Muslim regarding his slave or his horse, except the Zakat al-Fitr."" [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
7678,"(7675) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz and Abu 'Uthman Sa'id ibn Muhammad ibn Muhammad ibn 'Abdan and Abu Muhammad ibn Abi Hamid al-Muqri' informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn 'Ali ibn 'Affan narrated to us; Muhammad ibn 'Ubayd narrated to us from 'Ubayd Allah from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obligated Zakat al-Fitr as a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley, upon every person, whether elderly or young, free or enslaved. Thus they said: ""upon every young person."" [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
7679,"(7676) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us, saying: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih narrated to us; Abu al-Muthanna narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Yahya narrated to us from 'Ubayd Allah from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet (ﷺ) that he obligated Zakat al-Fitr as a sa' of barley or dates, upon the young and the elderly, the free and the enslaved.

Thus I found it in my book: ""upon the young."" And thus al-'Abbas al-Narisi reported it from Yahya ibn Sa'id al-Qattan. And al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Musaddad from Yahya, and said: ""'ala (upon)."" And Muslim narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah from Abu Usamah and 'Abd Allah ibn Numayr from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar, and said in the hadith: ""'ala (upon)."" [Sahih. This is the wording of al-Bukhari.]"
7680,"(7677) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us by dictation; Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri narrated to us in Makkah; Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'igh narrated to us; Qabisah ibn 'Uqbah narrated to us; Sufyan al-Thawri narrated to us from 'Ubayd Allah ibn 'Umar and Yahya ibn Sa'id from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded Zakat al-Fitr upon every young and elderly person, free or enslaved, a sa' of barley or a sa' of dates. Then the people equated it with two mudds of wheat.

And thus 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid al-'Adani narrated it from al-Thawri from 'Ubayd Allah alone, saying: ""upon every young and elderly person, free or enslaved."" [Sahih. This is the wording of 'Abd al-Razzaq.]"
7681,"(7678) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ibrahim ibn Marzuq al-Basri narrated to us in Egypt; 'Arim narrated to us; Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyub narrated to us from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obligated Zakat al-Fitr — or he said, ""the Zakat of Ramadan"" — upon the male and the female, the free and the enslaved, a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley. Then the people equated it with half a sa' of wheat. He said: Ibn 'Umar used to give dates, but when the people of Madinah ran short of dates, he gave barley. And Ibn 'Umar used to give it on behalf of the young and the elderly, even to the extent that he would give it on behalf of Banu Nafi'. [Sahih. This is the wording of al-Bukhari.]"
7682,"(7679) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us; Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me; Abu Ya'la narrated to us; Abu al-Rabi' narrated to us; Hammad narrated it with its chain, similar to it, except that he said: ""Zakat al-Fitr"" — without doubt — and he said: ""of dates"" generally. And he added, saying: 'Abd Allah used to give it when those who collected it sat, and they would sit one or two days before the Fitr. Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Arim. And Yazid ibn Zurai' narrated it from Ayyub, saying: ""upon the free and the enslaved, the male and the female.""

And with its meaning, Malik ibn Anas and al-Dahhak ibn 'Uthman narrated it from Nafi'. [Sahih. Previously cited.]"
7683,(7680) Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr narrated to us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik informed us from Nafi' from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar that he used to pay Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of his slaves who were in Wadi al-Qura and Khaybar. [Sahih. Reported by Malik.]
7684,"(7681) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us; Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Madini narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Musayyibi narrated to us; Anas ibn 'Iyad narrated to us from Musa ibn 'Uqbah from Nafi', who said: 'Abd Allah used to pay Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of every slave he owned, whether in his land or elsewhere, and on behalf of every person he supported, whether young or elderly, and on behalf of his wife's slaves. [Sahih. Reported by 'Abd al-Razzaq.]"
7685,"(7682) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzaki and others informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Ibrahim ibn Muhammad informed us from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obligated Zakat al-Fitr upon the free and the enslaved, the male and the female, among those whom you employ. [Da'if. Reported by al-Shafi'i.]"
7686,"(7683) Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated it from Ja'far ibn Muhammad from his father from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obligated upon every young or elderly person, free or enslaved, among those whom you employ, a sa' of barley or a sa' of dates or a sa' of raisins, for every person.

And it is in what Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz permitted me to narrate from him, from Abu al-Walid: Makki ibn 'Abdan narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Yahya narrated to us; al-Nufayli narrated to us; Hatim ibn Isma'il narrated it — and it is mursal.

And that was narrated from 'Ali ibn Musa al-Rida from his father from his grandfather from his forefathers from the Prophet (ﷺ). [Da'if.]"
7687,"(7684) And Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Sulami informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar informed us; 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Marwazi narrated to us; al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Rabi' narrated to us; 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated from al-Thawri, from 'Abd al-A'la, from Abi 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Ali (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Whoever's maintenance is your responsibility, give on his behalf half a sa' of wheat or a sa' of dates."" And this is mawquf. 'Abd al-A'la is not strong, except that when he is joined by the one before him, they are strong together in what they agree upon. [Weak. Narrated by al-Daraqutni.]"
7688,"(7685) And Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Isbahani informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Sa'id al-Hamadhani narrated to us; al-Qasim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn 'Amir ibn Zurarah narrated to us; 'Umair ibn 'Ammar al-Hamadhani narrated to us; al-Abyad ibn al-Agharr narrated to us; al-Dahhak ibn 'Uthman narrated to me; from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded the Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of the young and the old, the free and the enslaved, those whom you maintain."" Its chain is not strong, and Allah knows best. [Weak. Narrated by al-Daraqutni.]"
7689,"(7686) Abu Muhammad 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Balawayh al-Muzakk informed us; Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan narrated to us; Qatn ibn Ibrahim narrated to us; Hafs ibn 'Abd Allah narrated to us; Ibrahim, meaning Ibn Tahman, narrated to us; from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar: ""He used to pay Zakat al-Fitr on behalf of every slave he owned in his land and outside his land, and on behalf of every person he maintained, whether young or old, and on behalf of his wife's slaves. He had a mukatab (slave contracted for emancipation) in Madinah, and he would not pay on his behalf."" And al-Thawri narrated it from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, from Nafi', who said: ""Ibn 'Umar had two mukatab slaves, and he would not give Zakat on their behalf on the day of Eid."" [Sound. Its meaning has been previously referenced.]"
7690,"(7687) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us among the last group. They said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us; al-Shafi'i narrated to us; Malik informed us. (H) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad and Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Salam both narrated to us; they said: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us; he said: I read to Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr in Ramadan upon the people: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley, upon every free person or slave, male or female, among the Muslims."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf from Malik, and Muslim narrated it from Yahya ibn Yahya and others. [Sound. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7691,"(7688) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us. (H) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ishaq al-Thaqafi narrated to us; Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Jahdam narrated to us; Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated from 'Umar ibn Nafi', from his father, from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley, upon the free and the enslaved, the male and the female, the young and the old, among the Muslims. And the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded that Zakat al-Fitr be paid before the people go out for the prayer."" Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Muhammad ibn al-Sakan. [Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7692,"(7689) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us; Abu 'Utbah Ahmad ibn al-Faraj al-Hijazi narrated in Hims; Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated to me; al-Dahhak narrated. (H) And Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir al-'Anbari informed us; my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to us; Ibn Abi Fudayk informed us; al-Dahhak narrated from Nafi', from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr in Ramadan upon every soul among the Muslims, free or enslaved, man or woman, young or old: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley."" The wording of both their hadiths is the same, except that in the hadith of Abu 'Utbah it is ""upon every soul among the Muslims."" Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Muhammad ibn Rafi'. [Sound. Its meaning from before.]"
7693,"(7690) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abd informed us; Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar narrated to us; 'Ubayd ibn Sharik narrated to us; Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to me; Kathir ibn Farqad narrated to me, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), that he said: ""Indeed, Zakat al-Fitr is upon every enslaved and free person among the Muslims: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley."" [Sound. Its meaning from before.]"
7694,"(7691) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us; Ja'far ibn Muhammad ibn Nusayr al-Khuldi narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn al-Hajjaj ibn Rishdin al-Fihri narrated in Egypt; Yahya ibn Bukayr narrated, and he mentioned it by his chain, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Zakat al-Fitr is an obligation upon every free and enslaved Muslim, male or female among the Muslims: a sa' of dates or a sa' of barley."" [Sound. Its meaning as previously stated.]"
7695,"(7692) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Mihran al-Isma'ili narrated to us; my father narrated to us; Mahmud ibn Khalid al-Dimashqi narrated to us; Marwan ibn Muhammad al-Dimashqi narrated to us; Yazid ibn Muslim al-Khawlani narrated to us — and he was a trustworthy shaykh, and 'Abd Allah ibn Wahb used to narrate from him — Sayyar ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Sadafi narrated from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) obligated Zakat al-Fitr as a purification of the fast from idle talk and obscenity, and as food for the poor. Whoever pays it before the prayer, it is an accepted zakah, and whoever pays it after the prayer, it is a charity among charities."" Thus said our shaykh. [Hasan. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
7696,"(7693) And the sound narration is what Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us; Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abu Dawud narrated to us; Mahmud ibn Khalid al-Dimashqi and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Samarqandi both narrated to us; they said: Marwan ibn Muhammad narrated to us. 'Abd Allah said: Abu Yazid al-Khawlani narrated to us — and he was a trustworthy shaykh, and Ibn Wahb used to narrate from him. And thus al-'Abbas ibn al-Walid al-Khallal narrated it from Marwan, and Abu Ahmad al-Hafiz mentioned it in al-Kuna but did not know his name. [Hasan. It preceded before it.]"
7697,"(7694) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: it was recited to Ibn Wahb: ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar and Mālik ibn Anas informed you from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obligated upon the people the Zakāh of Fiṭr in Ramadhān: a ṣāʿ of dates or a ṣāʿ of barley, upon every free person or slave, male or female, among the Muslims.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Meaning from before.]"
7698,"(7695) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us that Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us that Abū al-Nuʿmān narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from al-Nuʿmān ibn Rāshid from al-Zuhrī from Ibn Abī Ṣuʿayr from his father, who said: the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said regarding the Ṣadaqah of Fiṭr: ""Discharge a ṣāʿ of wheat or grain upon every male or female, young or old, free or enslaved. As for the wealthy, Allah will purify him, and as for the poor, He will return to him more than he gave.""

Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAtkī narrated it from Ḥammād, and he said in the hadith: ""young or old, wealthy or poor."" However, he said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Thaʿlabah or Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Ṣuʿayr.

And Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated from Ḥammād from Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Ṣuʿayr from his father, and he mentioned in its text: ""the wealthy and the poor."" He also said in its text: ""Discharge a ṣāʿ of wheat,"" or he said: ""grain, for every person.""
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7699,"(7696) Aḥmad ibn Abī al-ʿAbbās al-Zawzanī informed us that Sulaymān ibn Aḥmad al-Ṭabarānī narrated to us that Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Dabarī narrated from ʿAbd al-Razzāq from Maʿmar from al-Zuhrī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""The Zakāh of Fiṭr was upon every free person and slave, male and female, young and old, poor and wealthy: a ṣāʿ of dates or half a ṣāʿ of wheat.""

Maʿmar said: ""And it reached me that al-Zuhrī used to raise it to the Prophet (ﷺ).""

It is narrated from ʿAṭāʾ that he said: ""The one who receives from the Zakāh of Fiṭr pays on his own behalf."" Similarly from al-Ḥasan. And Abū al-ʿĀliyah and al-Shaʿbī held this view.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7700,"(7697) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us that Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us that Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obligated the Ṣadaqah of Ramadhān: a ṣāʿ of dates or a ṣāʿ of barley, upon the male and the female, the free and the enslaved."" Then the people equated it with half a ṣāʿ of wheat. Ibn ʿUmar used to give dates, but one year the people of Madīnah found dates scarce, so he gave barley. Ibn ʿUmar used to give it when those who collected it sat down, and they would sit one or two days before Fiṭr. He used to give it on behalf of the young and the old among his household, even giving it on behalf of his grandchildren, i.e., the children of Nāfiʿ.

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Nuʿmān from Ḥammād, and Muslim narrated it in abridged form from the hadith of Yazīd ibn Zuraiʿ from Ayyūb.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Wording of al-Bukhārī.]"
7701,"(7698) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us that al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us that Mālik narrated.

(Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām and Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, saying: I recited to Mālik from Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Saʿd ibn Abī Sarḥ, who said: he heard Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī say: ""We used to bring out the Zakāh of Fiṭr: a ṣāʿ of food, or a ṣāʿ of barley, or a ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of aqiṭ (dried curd), or a ṣāʿ of raisins.""

The wording is that of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. In the narration of al-Shāfiʿī: ""a ṣāʿ of food, a ṣāʿ of barley"" — he did not mention the word ""or"" in this place, but mentioned it after that.

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf from Mālik, following the wording of the hadith of Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam and added in the hadith: ""We used to give the Zakāh of Fiṭr in the time of the Prophet (ﷺ),"" and he mentioned it.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7702,"(7699) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us that Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ narrated to us that Qabīṣah ibn ʿUqbah narrated to us that Sufyān narrated it with its chain and its meaning, along with this addition, without mentioning raisins. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Yazīd al-ʿAdanī from al-Thawrī in its entirety, and from Qabīṣah from al-Thawrī in abridged form.

Dāwūd ibn Qays narrated from ʿIyāḍ from Abū Saʿīd, who said: ""We used to bring out the Zakāh of Fiṭr when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was among us.""

Ismāʿīl ibn Umayyah narrated from ʿIyāḍ and also mentioned this addition, except that he limited himself to mentioning only some of these types. Thus the raising of the hadith to the Prophet (ﷺ) is established by this. Had it not been permissible for them, they would not have been bringing it out from these types without him informing them of that. And Allah knows best.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it.]"
7703,"(7700) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Bakr ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥamdān al-Ṣayrafī narrated to us that ʿAbd al-Ṣamad ibn al-Faḍl al-Balkhī narrated to us that Makkī ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us that ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Abī Rawwād narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: ""The people used to bring out the Ṣadaqah of Fiṭr in the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): a ṣāʿ of barley, or a ṣāʿ of dates, or of sult (hulled barley), or of raisins.""
[Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7704,"(7701) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Razzāz in Baghdād, from the original of his book, narrated to us that Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Sammāk narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā al-Birtī al-Qāḍī narrated to us that al-Qaʿnabī narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us that Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abū Dāwūd narrated to us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah narrated to us that Dāwūd, i.e., ibn Qays, narrated from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbdillāh from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, who said: ""We used to bring out, when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was among us, the Zakāh of Fiṭr upon every young and old, free or enslaved: a ṣāʿ of food, a ṣāʿ of aqiṭ, or a ṣāʿ of barley, or a ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of raisins. We continued to bring it out until Muʿāwiyah came as a pilgrim or an ʿumrah pilgrim, and he addressed the people from the minbar. Among what he said to the people was: 'I consider that two mudd of samrāʾ (Syrian wheat) equal a ṣāʿ of dates.' So the people adopted that.""

Abū Saʿīd said: ""As for me, I will continue to bring it out as I always used to bring it out, for as long as I live.""

In the narration of al-Razzāz: ""a ṣāʿ of food or a ṣāʿ of aqiṭ.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī, without the word ""or"" in this place. And both of them narrated it from the hadith of Zayd ibn Aslam from ʿIyāḍ, and in it is the word ""or.""
[Ṣaḥīḥ. This wording is Muslim's. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7705,"(7702) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ṣaydalānī al-ʿAdl narrated by dictation that al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Bajalī narrated to us that Abū ʿAbdillāh Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us that ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us that al-Qāḍī al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl al-Maḥāmilī and ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Aḥmad al-Daqqāq narrated to us, saying: Yaʿqūb al-Dawraqī narrated to us that Ibn ʿUlayyah narrated from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq that ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām narrated to me from ʿIyāḍ ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Abī Sarḥ, who said: Abū Saʿīd said — and the Ṣadaqah of Ramadhān was mentioned in his presence — ""I will not bring out except what I used to bring out in the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): a ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of wheat, or a ṣāʿ of barley, or a ṣāʿ of aqiṭ."" A man from the people said: ""Or two mudd of wheat?"" He said: ""That is the valuation of Muʿāwiyah. I do not accept it, nor do I act upon it.""

Likewise Isḥāq al-Ḥanẓalī narrated it from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah.
[Ṣaḥīḥ. Preceded before it.]"
7706,"(7703) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh narrated to us that Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Khazzāz narrated to us that Ismāʿīl ibn Ibrāhīm al-Tarjamānī narrated to us that Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Jumaḥī narrated to us that ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) obligated the Zakāh of Fiṭr: a ṣāʿ of dates or a ṣāʿ of wheat, upon every free person or slave, male or female, among the Muslims.

Thus said Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Jumaḥī, and the mention of wheat (burr) in it is not preserved.
[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ḥākim.]"
7707,"(7704) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUzayz narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿAnbarī informed me; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us; Muḥammad ibn ʿUzayz al-Aylī narrated to us; Salamah ibn Rūḥ narrated from ʿUqayl ibn Khālid from ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUtbah ibn Masʿūd from Abū Isḥāq al-Hamdānī from al-Ḥārith: that he heard ʿAlī ibn Abī Ṭālib, may Allah be pleased with him, commanding the Zakāt al-Fiṭr, saying: ""It is a ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of barley, or a ṣāʿ of wheat, or of sulṭ, or of raisins.""

This is the ḥadīth of Abū Bakr, and Abū ʿAbd Allāh did not mention ʿUtbah ibn ʿAbd Allāh in his isnād. It has been narrated as marfūʿ (elevated to the Prophet), but the mawqūf (stopped at the Companion) version is more authentic. [Weak. Al-Ḥākim narrated it.]"
7708,"(7705) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, who said: I heard Abū Rajāʾ saying: I heard Ibn ʿAbbās delivering a sermon upon the minbar, saying: ""Regarding the Zakāt al-Fiṭr, it is a ṣāʿ of food.""

This is the authentic version, and it is mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). [Sound. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7709,"(7706) And Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fārisī informed us, both saying: Abū ʿAmr ibn Maṭar narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb from Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""Discharge a ṣāʿ of food"" — meaning in the Fiṭr (Zakāt).

[Denounced in its chain of narration. Abū Nuʿaym narrated it.]"
7710,"(7707) Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us in Baghdād; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us; Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Bashshār narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Abū Isḥāq, who said: Ibn al-Zubayr wrote to us regarding the verse: ""{Evil is the name of disobedience after faith}"" — that the Zakāt al-Fiṭr is a ṣāʿ, a ṣāʿ. [Sound. Ibn Abī Shaybah narrated it.]"
7711,"(7708) Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Saʿīd ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Rāzī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb narrated to us; Muslim narrated to us; Hishām narrated to us; Qatādah narrated from al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī regarding the Zakāt of Ramadan: ""Upon the one who fasted, a ṣāʿ of dates or a ṣāʿ of wheat."" [Sound. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7712,"(7709) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from al-Nuʿmān ibn Rāshid from al-Zuhrī from Thaʿlabah ibn Abī Ṣuʿayr from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""A ṣāʿ of wheat or grain is due upon every two — whether young or old, free or slave, male or female. As for your wealthy, Allah will purify him, and as for your poor, He will return to him more than what he gave.""

[Weak. See its context.]"
7713,"(7710) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Musaddad and Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAtakī narrated — and he mentioned the ḥadīth. In the narration of Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd, it is: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Thaʿlabah or Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Ṣuʿayr from his father. Sulaymān added in his ḥadīth: ""whether rich or poor.""

It has also been narrated from Bakr ibn Wāʾil al-Kūfī from al-Zuhrī from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Thaʿlabah ibn Ṣuʿayr from his father, and it is also said from him: Thaʿlabah ibn ʿAbd Allāh or ʿAbd Allāh ibn Thaʿlabah from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, as a mursal (disconnected) narration.

It is also said from him in that: ""upon every head.""

Similarly, in the ḥadīth of al-Nuʿmān ibn Rāshid, and it is said: ""specifically in wheat, upon every two."" And Allah knows best.

Ibn Jurayj narrated it, saying: al-Zuhrī said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Thaʿlabah said: ""The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, delivered a sermon and mentioned it."" And he said: ""In wheat, between two (persons).""

Maʿmar contradicted them, narrating it from al-Zuhrī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Hurmuz from Abū Hurayrah, mawqūf (stopped) upon him. Then he said: ""It has reached me that al-Zuhrī used to raise it (to the Prophet)."" Aḥmad said, and Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Dhuhalī said in the book of ʿIlal (defects): ""It is only ʿAbd Allāh ibn Thaʿlabah, and it is: 'upon every head or every person.' This is the narration of Bakr ibn Wāʾil. No one besides him established this ḥadīth; he got the isnād and the text correct."" And it was narrated from Abū Salamah from Hammām from Bakr ibn Wāʾil. [Weak. See what precedes it.]"
7714,"(7711) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us; Abū al-Azhar narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Shuraḥbīl narrated to us; Ibn Jurayj narrated to us; Ayyūb ibn Mūsā informed me that Nāfiʿ informed him from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, commanded ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm regarding the Zakāt al-Fiṭr: half a ṣāʿ of wheat or a ṣāʿ of dates.

This is not authentic. How can this be authentic when the narration of the group from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar is that the equivalence of a ṣāʿ to two mudd of wheat was established after the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him? [Denounced. Al-Dāraquṭnī narrated it.]"
7715,"(7712) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Sahl ibn Yūsuf narrated to us; Ḥumayd al-Ṭawīl narrated from al-Ḥasan, who said: Ibn ʿAbbās delivered a sermon to us in Baṣrah at the end of Ramadan and said: ""Discharge the Zakāt of your fast."" It was as though the people did not know. He said: ""Whoever is here from the people of Madinah, teach your brothers, for they do not know. The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, obligated this charity upon every young and old, male and female, free and slave: a ṣāʿ of dates, or a ṣāʿ of barley, or half a ṣāʿ of wheat."" When ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, arrived and saw the low price of barley, he said: ""If you were to make it a ṣāʿ of everything, that would be better."" Al-Ḥasan said: ""He considered it obligatory upon the one who fasted."" He said: ""He delivered a sermon to us.""

Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā narrated it from Sahl ibn Yūsuf, and he said: ""He delivered a sermon"" — and this is more authentic. [Weak. Abū Dāwūd narrated it.]"
7716,"(7713) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Isfarāyinī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Barāʾ narrated to us, saying: I heard ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Madīnī, and he was asked about the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, regarding the Zakāt al-Fiṭr. He said: ""It is a Baṣran ḥadīth, and its chain is mursal (disconnected).""

He said: ʿAlī also said: ""Al-Ḥasan did not hear from Ibn ʿAbbās, and he never saw him at all. He was in Madinah during the time when Ibn ʿAbbās was in Baṣrah.""

He said: ʿAlī also said to me regarding the ḥadīth of al-Ḥasan: ""Ibn ʿAbbās delivered a sermon to us in Baṣrah"" — it is only like the statement of Thābit: ""ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn came to us,"" and like the statement of Mujāhid: ""ʿAlī went out to us,"" and like the statement of al-Ḥasan: ""Surāqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum narrated to them."" Al-Ḥasan did not hear from Ibn ʿAbbās. The Shaykh Aḥmad said: ""The ḥadīth of al-Ḥasan from Ibn ʿAbbās is mursal (disconnected).""

And we have narrated from Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī that he heard from Ibn ʿAbbās in this sermon regarding the Zakāt al-Fiṭr: ""A ṣāʿ of food."" [Sound.]"
7717,"(7714) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Abū al-Ashʿath narrated to us; al-Thaqafī narrated from Hishām, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""We were commanded to give the Ṣadaqat Ramaḍān on behalf of the young and the old, the free and the enslaved — a ṣāʿ of food. Whoever brought wheat, it was accepted from him; whoever brought barley, it was accepted from him; whoever brought raisins, it was accepted from him; whoever brought sawīq (parched barley meal), it was accepted from him."" He said: ""And I think he said: 'Whoever brought flour, it was accepted from him, and whoever brought sawīq, it was accepted from him.'"" This is also a mursal narration. (j) Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn did not hear anything from Ibn ʿAbbās, except that it corresponds with the connected ḥadīth of Abū Rajāʾ al-ʿUṭāridī from Ibn ʿAbbās; thus it is more likely to be ṣaḥīḥ. Whatever the narrator doubted and for which there is no corroborating witness, no consideration is given to it. And Allāh knows best. [Munkar. Narrated by Dāraquṭnī]"
7718,"(7715) Abū Isḥāq Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Urmuwī informed us; Shāfiʿ ibn Muḥammad informed us; Abū Jaʿfar al-Ṭaḥāwī narrated to us; al-Muzanī narrated to us; al-Shāfiʿī narrated from Yaḥyī ibn Ḥassān, from al-Layth ibn Saʿd, from ʿUqayl ibn Khālid and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Khālid ibn Musāfir, from Ibn Shihāb, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) enjoined Zakāt al-Fiṭr as two mudd of wheat. Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""The ḥadīth of 'two mudd' is an error (khaṭaʾ)."" The Shaykh said: ""It is as he said, for the established reports indicate that the adjustment to two mudd occurred after the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."" We have received narrations regarding the permissibility of half a ṣāʿ of wheat in Zakāt al-Fiṭr from Abū Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, ʿUthmān ibn ʿAffān, ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd, Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh, Abū Hurayrah, and in one of the two narrations from ʿAlī and Ibn ʿAbbān (may Allah be pleased with them). Ibn al-Mundhir said: ""This is not established from Abū Bakr and ʿUthmān (may Allah be pleased with them)."" The Shaykh said: ""It is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected) from Abū Bakr and mawṣūl (connected) from ʿUthmān. And Allāh knows best."" There are narrations from the Prophet (ﷺ) regarding a full ṣāʿ of wheat, and there are narrations regarding half a ṣāʿ, but none of these are ṣaḥīḥ. I have explained the defect of each one in al-Khilāfiyyāt. We have received in the ḥadīth of Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī and in the established ḥadīth from Ibn ʿUmar that the adjustment of two mudd of wheat — which is half a ṣāʿ — to a ṣāʿ of barley occurred after the Prophet (ﷺ). And in Allah is the granting of success."
7719,"(7716) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥāmid al-Tirmidhī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥibāl narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr, from his mother Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr (may Allah be pleased with her), that she narrated to him: ""They used to give Zakāt al-Fiṭr during the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) by the mudd by which the people of the household measured, or by the ṣāʿ by which they measured — the people of Madīnah all did that."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah]"
7720,"(7717) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Qabīṣah narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ḥanẓalah, from Ṭāwūs, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The scale (mīzān) is the scale of the people of Makkah, and the measure (mikyāl) is the measure of the people of Madīnah."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7721,"(7718) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym and Aḥmad ibn Sahl said: Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us; Sufyān narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, Ayyūb, Ḥumayd, and ʿAbd al-Karīm, from Mujāhid, from Ibn Abī Laylā, from Kaʿb ibn ʿUjrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed by him at Ḥudaybiyyah, before he entered Makkah, while he was in iḥrām and was kindling a fire under his cooking pot, and lice were falling on his face. He said: ""Are these vermin of yours harming you?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then shave your head and give a farq of food to six poor persons."" A farq is three ṣāʿ: ""Or fast three days, or offer a sacrifice (nasīkah)."" Ibn Abī Najīḥ said: ""Or slaughter a sheep."" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn Abī Najīḥ, Ayyūb, Sayf ibn Sulaymān, and others, from Mujāhid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7722,"(7719) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, from a book read to him and compared with its original; Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd al-Sijistānī narrated to us, saying: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say: ""A farq is sixteen raṭl."" And I heard him say: ""The ṣāḥ of Ibn Abī Dhiʾb is five raṭl and a third."" He said: ""Whoever said eight raṭl, that is not preserved (maḥfūẓ)."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7723,"(7720) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Aḥmad al-Ḥīrī narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Abū Aḥmad Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us, saying: I heard my father say: ""Abū Yūsuf asked Mālik in the presence of the Amīr al-Muʾminīn about the ṣāʿ — how many raṭl is it?"" He said: ""The Sunnah with us is that the ṣāʿ is not measured in raṭl."" So he silenced him with that. Abū Aḥmad said: ""I heard al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Walīd say: Abū Yūsuf said: 'I came to Madīnah and gathered the sons of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and requested their ṣāʿ measures. Each one narrated to me from his father, from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): ""This is his ṣāʿ."" I measured them and found them equal. So I abandoned the statement of Abū Ḥanīfah and returned to this.'"" [Ḥasan for something else]"
7724,"(7721) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me; ʿAbdullāh ibn Saʿd al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdullāh narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Walīd narrated to us, saying: ""Abū Yūsuf came to us from Hajj. We visited him and he said: 'I wish to open for you a door of knowledge that has preoccupied me. I left and came to Madīnah and asked about the ṣāʿ. They said: ""Our ṣāʿ is this — the ṣāʿ of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""' I said to them: 'What is your proof for that?' They said: 'We will bring you our proof.' When morning came, about fifty shaykhs from among the sons of the Muhājirūn and the Anṣār came to me, each man having a ṣāʿ under his cloak. Each one of them narrated from his father or his household: ""This is the ṣāʿ of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).""' I looked and found them all equal. I measured it and found it to be five raṭl and a third, with a slight deficiency. I saw a strong matter, so I abandoned the statement of Abū Ḥanīfah regarding the ṣāʿ and adopted the statement of the people of Madīnah."" Al-Ḥusayn said: ""I performed Hajj that year and met Mālik ibn Anas, so I asked him about the ṣāʿ. He said: 'Our ṣāʿ is this — the ṣāʿ of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' I said: 'How many raṭl is it?' He said: 'The measure is not weighed in raṭl — it is this.'"" Al-Ḥusayn said: ""I met ʿAbdullāh ibn Zayd ibn Aslam, and he said: 'My father narrated to me from my grandfather that this is the ṣāʿ of ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him).'"" [Ḥasan. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7725,"(7722) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me; Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Khusrūjirdī narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us, saying: I heard Muḥammad ibn Saʿd al-Jallāb say: ""I asked Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways in Madīnah about the ṣāʿ of the Prophet (ﷺ). He brought out to me an old, worn ṣāʿ and said: 'This is the very ṣāʿ of the Prophet (ﷺ).' I measured it and it was five raṭl and a third."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
7726,"(7723) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: ""I read in the handwriting of Abū ʿAmr al-Mustamlī: I heard Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā, meaning al-Dhuhlī, say: 'I borrowed the ṣāʿ of Mālik ibn Anas from Ismāʿīl ibn Abī Uways and found written upon it: ""The ṣāʿ of Mālik ibn Anas, measured against the ṣāʿ of the Prophet (ﷺ).""' And I think I measured it with lentils and found it to be five raṭl and a third."""
7727,"(7724) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us: al-Khasib ibn Nasaah narrated to us, from 'Abdillah ibn Ja'far al-Madini, from al-'Ala' ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: They said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him): ""O Messenger of Allah, our sa' is the smallest of sa's and our mudd is the smallest of mudds."" The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O Allah, bless us in our sa' and our mudd, in our little and our much, and grant us along with the blessing two blessings. O Allah, indeed Ibrahim is Your servant and Your intimate friend, and he supplicated to You for the people of Makkah, and I am Your servant and Your Messenger; I supplicate to You for the people of Madinah the same as Ibrahim supplicated to You for the people of Makkah.""

That which was narrated by Salih ibn Musa al-Talhah, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, from 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her): The established practice of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) for the ghusl of janabah was one sa', and for wudu' two rittls. And one sa' equals eight rittls. However, Salih is alone in narrating this, and he is weak in hadith, as Yahya ibn Ma'in and others among the scholars of hadith have stated. Similarly, what was narrated from Jarir ibn Yazid from Anas ibn Malik, and what was narrated from Ibn Abi Layla from 'Abd al-Karim from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform wudu' with two rittls and perform ghusl with one sa' — which is eight rittls — both of their chains of narration are weak.

The authentic narration from Anas ibn Malik is that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform wudu' with one mudd and perform ghusl with one sa' up to five mudds. Then Asma' bint Abi Bakr reported that they used to give the zakah of fitr with the sa' by which they measured their food.

This indicates that the sa' of zakah and the sa' of food are distinct from the sa' of ghusl. Furthermore, 'A'ishah (may Allah be pleased with her) reported that she and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) used to perform ghusl from a vessel the size of a farq. And we have already established that a farq equals three sa's. If a sa' is five and one-third rittls, then the amount of water each of them would use for ghusl is eight rittls, which is one and a half sa'. The amount of water used for ghusl would vary according to the manner of use. Therefore, there is no justification for abandoning the authentic hadiths regarding the measure of the sa' designated for the zakah of fitr in light of narrations such as this. And success is through Allah. [Hasan]"
7728,"(7725) Abu 'Ali al-Rawdhbari informed us: Muhammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Hamid ibn Yahya narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us. (H) And he said: Musaddad narrated to us: Yahya narrated, from Ibn 'Ajlan, who heard 'Iyad say: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Khudri say: ""I will never give anything but a sa'. Indeed, we used to give, during the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a sa' of dates, or barley, or dried yogurt (aqit), or raisins.""

This is the hadith of Yahya. Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah added therein: ""or a sa' of flour.""

Hamid said: They rejected this from him, so Sufyan abandoned it.

Abu Dawud said: This addition is an error from Ibn 'Uyaynah.

The Shaykh said: A group narrated it from Ibn 'Ajlan, among them Hatim ibn Isma'il. In this manner, Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih, as did Yahya al-Qattan, Abu Khalid al-Ahmad, Hammad ibn Mas'adah, and others. None of them mentioned ""flour"" other than Sufyan, and it was rejected from him, so he abandoned it.

It was narrated from Muhammad ibn Sirin from Ibn 'Abbas, as a mursal narration, and as a mawquf narration based on conjecture, and it is not established. It was narrated through weak routes that are not worth mentioning. [Shadhdh. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
7729,"(7726) Muhammad ibn 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Ali al-Warraq, whose nickname was Hamdan, narrated to us: Dawud ibn Shabib narrated to us: Yahya ibn 'Abbad narrated to us — and he was among the best of people — Ibn Jurayj narrated to us, from 'Ata', from Ibn 'Abbas, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered a crier in the valley of Makkah to proclaim: ""Indeed, the zakah of fitr is an obligatory right upon every Muslim — young or old, male or female, free or slave, city-dweller or bedouin — a sa' of barley or dates.""

Muhammad ibn Makhlad narrated it from Hamdan and added therein: ""two mudds of wheat.""

Al-Kudaymi also narrated it from Dawud ibn Shabib. This is a hadith in which Yahya ibn 'Abbad is alone in narrating from Ibn Jurayj in this manner. Others narrated it from Ibn Jurayj from 'Ata' as his own statement regarding the two mudds.

And from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, it is marfu' to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) in the rest of its wording. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Hakim.]"
7730,"(7727) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Farisi narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Talib narrated to us: 'Abd al-Wahhab ibn 'Ata' informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed us. He said: 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb said: ""It reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) ordered a crier to cry out upon every Muslim."" He mentioned it. He said: And Ibn Jurayj narrated to us; he said: 'Ata' said: ""Two mudds of wheat, or a sa' of dates, or barley. The free person and the slave are equal in this.""

And 'Abd al-Razzaq narrated it from Ibn Jurayj from 'Amr, as a munqati' narration. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Daraqutni.]"
7731,"(7728) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu al-Husayn Ahmad ibn 'Uthman al-Adami in Baghdad informed us: Abu Qalabah 'Abd al-Malik ibn Muhammad al-Raqashi narrated to us: Malik ibn 'Abd al-Wahid narrated to us: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from 'Ali ibn Salih, from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The zakah of fitr is upon the city-dweller and the bedouin.""

Ibrahim ibn Mahdi narrated it from al-Mu'tamir and narrated the hadith in its entirety. And Salim ibn Nuh narrated it from Ibn Jurayj, from 'Amr, from his father, from his grandfather, as a marfu' narration, except that he did not mention ""the city-dweller and the bedouin.""

Abu 'Isa al-Tirmidhi said: I asked Muhammad, meaning al-Bukhari, about this hadith, and he said: ""Ibn Jurayj did not hear from 'Amr ibn Shu'ayb."" [Da'if. Narrated by al-'Uqayli.]"
7732,"(7729) Abu Zakariyya Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us: Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf al-Shaybani informed us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us: Qabisa, i.e., ibn 'Uqbah, narrated to us: Sufyan narrated, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from 'Iyad ibn 'Abdillah ibn Abi Sarh, from Abu Sa'id, who said: ""We used to give the zakah of fitr in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) as a sa' of food, or a sa' of barley, or a sa' of dried yogurt (aqit)."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7733,"(7730) It was narrated to us by 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan: Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid informed us: Tamtam narrated to us: Abu Hudaifah narrated to us: Sufyan narrated it with his chain of narration, except that he said: ""We used to give, during the era of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a sa' of food, a sa' of dates, a sa' of barley, a sa' of raisins, a sa' of dried yogurt (aqit). Then when Mu'awiyah came and the rice of Samarra was cultivated, the people considered it equivalent to two mudds of wheat.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from the hadith of Sufyan al-Thawri, as was mentioned previously. [Sahih. Previously mentioned.]"
7734,"(7731) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi both informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Bahr ibn Nasr narrated to us. He said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb, and he said: Kathir ibn 'Abdillah ibn 'Amr al-Muzani wrote to me, informing from Rubayh ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: ""Some men from among the bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, we are people of wealth. Will you exempt us from the zakah of fitr?' He said: 'No. Give it on behalf of the young and the old, the male and the female, the free and the slave — a sa' of dates, or a sa' of raisins, or a sa' of barley, or a sa' of dried yogurt (aqit).'"" [Munkar.]"
7735,"(7732) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz and Abu Sa'id ibn Abi 'Amr both informed us: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us: Hushaym narrated, from Abu Hurrah, who said: ""Al-Hasan was asked about the bedouin giving the zakah of fitr. He said: 'A sa' of milk.'"" [Da'if.]"
7736,"(7733) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran al-'Adl in Baghdad informed us: Abu al-Hasan Ahmad ibn Ishaq al-Taybi informed us: Abu 'Ali Bishr ibn Musa al-Asadi informed us: al-Muqri' narrated to us: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us: Ziyad ibn Nu'aym al-Hadrami narrated to me, saying: I heard Ziyad ibn al-Harith al-Suda'i, a companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), narrating. He said: ""I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him"" — and he mentioned the hadith until he said: ""Then another man came and said: 'O Prophet of Allah, give me something.' The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: 'Whoever asks the people while being self-sufficient, it is a headache in the head and an illness in the stomach.' The supplicant said: 'Then give me from charity.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him: 'Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, was not pleased to leave the matter of charity to the judgment of any prophet or anyone else; rather, He Himself decreed it and divided it into eight categories. If you are among those categories, I will give you — or he said: We will give you — your right.'"" [Da'if. Narrated by al-Tabari.]"
7737,"(7734) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū al-Qāsim al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥabīb and others narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram al-Bazzāz narrated to us, ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar narrated to us, Ibn ʿAwn informed us from Ḥafṣah bint Sīrīn from Umm al-Rāʾiḥ bint Ṣulayʿ from Salmān ibn ʀmir al-Ḍabbī that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, your charity given to a poor person is one act of charity, and when given to a kinsman, it is two: an act of charity and an act of maintaining the ties of kinship."" [Ṣaḥīgh for other than it. It was narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
7738,"(7735) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-ʿUṭāridī narrated to us, Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us from Hishām from Ḥafṣah from al-Rabāb from Salmān ibn ʀmir al-Ḍabbī, who raised it (as a marfūʿ narration), saying: ""Charity given to a poor person is one act of charity, and when given to a kinsman, it is two: an act of charity and an act of maintaining the ties of kinship."" [Ṣaḥīgh for other than it. Previously mentioned.]"
7739,"(7736) Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, al-Shāfiʿī informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Muʾammal said: I heard Ibn Abī Mulaykah, while a man was saying to him: ""Indeed, ʿAṭāʾ has instructed me to place the Zakāt al-Fiṭr in the mosque.""

Ibn Abī Mulaykah said: ""The uncultured one has given you a fatwā without his own reasoning. Distribute it, for Ibn Hishām used to give it to his guards and to whomever he wished.""

Al-Shāfiʿī narrated it with his chain from Sālim ibn ʿAbdillāh. Its mention has already passed at the end of the chapter on intention (niyyah) in the giving of charity, and we have narrated it from a group. [Weak. It was narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
7740,"(7737) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, he said: It was recited to Ibn Wahb: Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah informed you from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, Abū Khaythamah narrated to us from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded that Zakāt al-Fiṭr be given before the people go out for the prayer.

The wording is from the ḥadīth of Abū Khaythamah. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās from Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7741,"(7738) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī informed us, my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us, Ibn Abī Fudayk informed us, al-Ḍaḥḥāk narrated from Nāfiʿ from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded that Zakāt al-Fiṭr be given before the people go out for the prayer. And ʿAbdullāh used to give it one or two days before that.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, without the mention of ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar's practice of giving it early. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
7742,"(7739) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us, al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Qāḍī narrated to us, Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Abū Maʿshar narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) commanded us to give Zakāt al-Fiṭr on behalf of every young and old, free and enslaved person—a ṣāʿ of dates or barley. He said: They were also given raisins and dried yogurt, and they accepted these from them. And we were commanded to give it before we went out to the prayer. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) instructed them to distribute it among them and said: ""Suffice them from going about (begging) on this day.""

This Abū Maʿshar is Najīḥ al-Sindī al-Madanī; others are more reliable than him. And there is a ḥadīth of Ibn ʿAbbās in this chapter, the mention of which has already passed. [Weak. It was narrated by Saʿīd ibn Manṣūr.]"
7743,"(7740) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl al-Bazzāz narrated to us, Yaḥyā ibn al-Rabīʿ narrated to us, Sufyān narrated from Jaʿfar ibn Burqān, who said: A letter of ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz (may Allah be pleased with him) came to us: ""Give charity before the prayer. {Indeed, he has succeeded who purifies himself and mentions the name of his Lord and prays.} And say as your father said: {Our Lord, we have wronged ourselves, and if You do not forgive us and have mercy upon us, we will surely be among the losers.} And say as Nūḥ said: {And if You do not forgive me and have mercy upon me, I will be among the losers.} And say as Ibrāhīm said: {And the One who I hope will forgive my sin on the Day of Judgment.} And say as Mūsā said: {My Lord, I have wronged myself, so forgive me, and He forgave him. Indeed, He is the Forgiving, the Merciful.} And say as Dhū al-Nūn said: {There is no deity except You; exalted are You. Indeed, I have been of the wrongdoers.}""

And I think he wrote: ""Whoever does not have anything with which to give charity, let him fast""—meaning after the ʿĪd. And Allah knows best. [Weak. It was narrated by ʿAbd al-Razzāq.]"
7744,"(7741) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥbūb narrated to us in Marw, Abū ʿUthmān Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān narrated to us, al-Naḍr ibn Shumayl informed us, Shuʿbah ibn al-Ḥajjāj informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak narrated to us—and the wording is his—ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Aḥmad ibn Fāris narrated to us, Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us, Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAwn ibn Abī Ḥuṣayfah, who said: I heard al-Mundhir ibn Jarīr narrating from his father Jarīr ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: We were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in the forenoon when a group of people came—barefoot, naked, wearing torn cloaks (nimār), upon them were woolen garments (ʿabāʾ), or he said: carrying swords—most of them from Muḍar, rather all of them were from Muḍar. I saw the face of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) change upon seeing their state of destitution. Then he went inside, then he came out and commanded Bilāl, who called the iqāmah. He prayed the Ẓuhr prayer, then he addressed the people and said: {O mankind, fear your Lord, who created you from one soul} to the end of the verse. Then he said: {O you who have believed, fear Allah. And let every soul look to what it has put forth for tomorrow.} to the end of the verse. A man gave charity from his dīnār, from his dirham, from his garment, from a ṣāʿ of his wheat, from a ṣāʿ of his dates—until he said: Even if it be half a date.

He said: Then a man from the Anṣār came with a bundle that his hand could scarcely carry—rather, it had exceeded his capacity—and he handed it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). The people followed one another in giving charity, and I saw two heaps of food and clothing before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and the face of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) began to shine as if it were a piece of gold, and he said: ""Whoever establishes a good practice in Islam shall have its reward and the reward of those who act upon it after him, without that diminishing anything from their rewards. And whoever establishes an evil practice in Islam shall bear its sin and the sin of those who act upon it after him, without that diminishing anything from their sins.""

The wording is from the ḥadīth of Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī, and the ḥadīth of al-Naḍr carries the same meaning, except that al-Naḍr did not mention ""upon them were woolen garments (ʿabāʾ).""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn al-Muthannā from Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar from Shuʿbah, and he said: ""Wearing torn cloaks (nimār) or woolen garments (ʿabāʾ), carrying swords."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated by Muslim.]"
7745,"(7742) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū ʿAmr ibn Abī Jaʿfar narrated to us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī al-Shawārib narrated to us, Abū ʿAwwānah narrated from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿUmayr from al-Mundhir ibn Jarīr from his father, who said: I was sitting with the Prophet (ﷺ) when a group of people came—wearing torn cloaks (nimār), carrying swords, and they had no lower garments (izār) nor anything else besides them. Most of them were from Muḍar, rather all of them were from Muḍar. When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw their state of hardship, nakedness, and hunger, his face changed. Then he stood and entered his house, then he went to the mosque and prayed the Ẓuhr prayer. Then he ascended his pulpit—a small pulpit—and praised Allah and extolled Him, then he said: ""To proceed: Indeed, Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, has revealed in His Book: {O mankind, fear your Lord, who created you from one soul} to His words: {an ever-watchful Overseer.} {Fear Allah, and let every soul look to what it has put forth for tomorrow} to His words: {the successful ones.} Give charity before you are unable to give charity. Give charity before a barrier is placed between you and charity."" A man gave charity from his dīnār, from his dirham, from his wheat, from his barley. And none of you should belittle anything of charity, even if it be half a date.

Then a man from the Anṣār stood with a bundle in his hand and handed it to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) while he was upon his pulpit. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took it, and joy was visible on the face of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: ""Whoever establishes a good practice and it is acted upon shall have its reward and the like of the reward of those who act upon it, without that diminishing anything from their rewards. And whoever establishes an evil practice and it is acted upon shall bear its sin and the like of the sin of those who act upon it, without that diminishing anything from their sins.""

Then the people stood and dispersed—some with dīnārs, some with dirhams, some with this, and some with that. He said: Then it was gathered and he distributed it among them.

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī al-Shawārib and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
7746,"(7743) The shaykh Abū al-Fatḥ Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us—I read to him in Baghdad—Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣāʾigh narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated from Abī Isḥāq, who said: ""Fear Allah and do good, for I heard ʿAbdullāh ibn Maʿqil say: I heard ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: {Guard yourselves from the Fire, even if it be with half a date.}""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb from Shuʿbah, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Abī Isḥāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. It was narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7747,"(7744) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdad: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī al-Razzāz reported to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir reported to us: Abū Usāmah reported to us: al-Aʿmash reported from Khaythamah ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is not one of you except that his Lord will speak to him, with no veil between them and no interpreter. He will look to his right and will see nothing except something he had sent forth; he will look to his left and will see nothing except something he had sent forth; and he will look before him and will see nothing except the Fire. So protect yourselves from the Fire, even if only with half a date.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yūsuf ibn Mūsā from Abū Usāmah, and Muslim narrated it from another route from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7748,"(7745) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār reported to us: Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh reported to us: Abū al-Walīd and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb reported to us — and the wording is that of Abū al-Walīd — both saying: Shuʿbah reported from ʿAmr ibn Murrah from Khaythamah from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim al-Ṭāʾī that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned the Fire and sought refuge from it and turned his face away. He mentioned the Fire again and sought refuge from it and turned his face away. Shuʿbah said: ""This happened at least twice, without doubt."" Then he said: ""Protect yourselves from the Fire, even if only with half a date. And if you cannot find that, then with a good word.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Walīd and Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7749,"(7746) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqrīʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris informed us, all saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb reported to us: al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī reported to us: Abū al-Naḍr reported to us: Warqāʾ reported from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dīnār from Saʿīd ibn Yasār from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever gives in charity the equivalent of a date from pure earnings — and nothing ascends to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, except that which is pure — Allah accepts it with His Right Hand and nurtures it for its owner, just as one of you nurtures his foal, until it becomes like Mount Uḥud.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ, saying: ""And Warqāʾ said, from Ibn Dīnār,"" and he mentioned it. Muslim narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Maqburī from Saʿīd ibn Yasār, and both of them narrated it from the ḥadīth of Abū Ṣāliḥ from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7750,"(7747) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb reported to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Salām reported to us: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā reported to us: al-Layth ibn Saʿd reported to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us: My grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr reported to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah reported to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd reported to us: al-Layth reported from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd from his father from Abū Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to say: ""O women of the Muslims! Let no woman belittle what she gives to her neighbor, even if it be only the trotter of a sheep.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā and Qutaybah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf from al-Layth. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7751,"(7748) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar reported to us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb reported to us: Abū Dāwūd reported to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṣāliḥ, the son of the daughter of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī, informed us: My grandfather reported to us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah reported to us: Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr informed us: Abū Dāwūd reported to us: Shuʿbah reported from al-Aʿmash, who said: I heard Abū Wāʾil narrating from Abū Masʿūd, who said: We used to carry burdens, and a man would give a great charity, and it would be said: ""This one is showing off."" And a man would give half a ṣāʿ, and it would be said: ""Allah is indeed free of need of this."" Then the verse was revealed: {Those who criticize the voluntary charitable giving of the believers in charity…} up to {a painful punishment.} The wording is that of the ḥadīth of Abū Ṣāliḥ. In the narration of Abū Bakr, he said: from Abū Masʿūd al-Badrī, who said: We used to carry burdens, and a man would come with a great charity.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from two other routes from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7752,"(7749) Abū Aḥmad al-Mihrajānī informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī reported to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī reported to us: Ibn Bukayr reported to us: Mālik reported from Zayd ibn Aslam from Muḥammad ibn Bijayd al-Anṣārī, then al-Ḥārithī, from his grandmother Ḥawwāʾ, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Respond to the beggar, even if it is with a burnt hoof."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7753,"(7750) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār reported to us: Ibn Milḥān reported to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr reported to us: al-Layth reported from Saʿīd ibn Abī Saʿīd al-Maqburī from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, one of the Banī Ḥārithah, that his grandmother — who was Umm Bijayd, and she was among those who gave bayʿah to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — reported to him. She said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a poor person stands at my door and I find nothing to give him."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to her: ""If you find nothing to give him except a burnt hoof, then hand it to him.""

Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān said, from al-Layth, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Bijayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7754,"(7751) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbd Allāh reported to us: Ḥarmalah ibn ʿImrān reported that he heard Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrating that Abū al-Khayr reported to him that he heard ʿUqbah ibn ʿĀmir saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: ""Every person will be in the shade of his charity until judgment is rendered among the people"" — or he said, ""until judgment is passed among the people.""

Yazīd said: ""Abū al-Khayr would not let a day pass without giving some charity, even if only a small cake or an onion."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7755,"(7752) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, all saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam reported to us: Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ reported from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām ibn Khuwaylid, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying: ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand. Let each of you begin with those whom you support. The best charity is that which is given from surplus wealth. Whoever seeks chastity, Allah will make him chaste. Whoever seeks self-sufficiency, Allah will make him self-sufficient."" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7756,"(7753) Abū ʿAmr al-Adīb informed us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī informed us: Ibn Yāsīn reported to me: Muḥammad ibn Sufyān ibn Abī al-Zarad al-Ubullī reported to us: Ḥibbān reported to us: Wahbīb reported from Hishām from his father from Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — and he mentioned something similar to it, except that he said: ""And whoever seeks self-sufficiency, Allah will make him self-sufficient."" He did not mention the word regarding seeking chastity (al-istiʿfāf). [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7757,"(7754) He said: And Wahb narrated to us, Hishām ibn ʿUrwah narrated to us from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, a hadith similar to this hadith of Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl from Wahb, with both chains of narration, and he mentioned the word al-istiʿfāf (seeking abstinence). Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah from Ḥakīm, and from the hadith of Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim from Abū Hurayrah, with additions and omissions. [Ṣaḥīh. Previously cited.]"
7758,"(7755) Abū Ṣāliḥ, the son of the daughter of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr the Qāḍī, informed us; my grandfather informed us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī narrated to us; al-Layth narrated to us from Abū al-Zubayr from Jābir, that he said: A man from Banī ʿUdhrah set free a slave of his upon his death (as a mudabbir). This reached the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, so he said: ""Do you have any wealth other than him?"" He said: ""No."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Who will purchase him from me?"" So Nuʿaym ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-ʿAdawī purchased him for eight hundred dirhams. He brought them to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who handed them over to him (the man), and then said: ""Begin with yourself and give charity from it. If anything remains, then for your family. If anything remains beyond your family, then for your relatives. If anything remains beyond your relatives, then thus and thus"" — meaning, ""in front of you, to your right, and to your left."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7759,"(7756) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed me; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Ādam narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated to me; ʿAdiyy ibn Thābit informed me; he said: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd al-Anṣārī narrating from Abū Masʿūd al-Anṣārī. I said: ""From the Prophet ﷺ?"" He said: ""From the Prophet ﷺ,"" who said: ""Indeed, when a Muslim spends on his family while hoping for reward from it, it is counted as charity for him."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it from various routes from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7760,"(7757) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us, dictating; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb, ʿĀrim, Abū al-Rabīʿ, Muḥammad ibn ʿUbayd, Musaddad, and Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; they said: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us; Ayyūb narrated to us from Abū Qilābah from Abū Asmāʾ from Thawbān, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The best dinar a man spends is a dinar he spends on his dependents; a dinar a man spends on his mount in the cause of Allah; and a dinar a man spends on his companions in the cause of Allah."" Abū Qilābah said: ""He began with the dependents. So what man is greater in reward than a man who spends on young dependents, through whom Allah sustains them and through whom He benefits them?"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7761,"(7758) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥumayd narrated to me; ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamarī narrated to me. (Ḥ) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; al-ʿAbbās al-Asfaṭī narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn al-Mundhir narrated to us; Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ narrated to us; Ibn Abī Ḥumayd narrated to us from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Umayyah al-Ḍamarī from his father: that ʿUmar passed by him while he was bargaining for a cloak (mirṭ). He said: ""What is this?"" He said: ""I wish to purchase it and give it in charity."" So he purchased it and gave it to his family, and said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whatever you give them is charity.'"" So ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Who will testify with you?"" So he came to ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) and stood behind the door. She said: ""Who is it?"" He said: ""ʿAmr."" She said: ""What has brought you?"" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Whatever you give them is charity?'"" She said: ""Yes."" The wording is that of the hadith of Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ, and the hadith of Abū Dāwūd is more complete. Ibn Abī Ḥumayd is Ḥammād ibn Abī Ḥumayd, and he is also called Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥumayd. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī.]"
7762,"(7759) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān narrated to us; Ibn Numayr narrated to us from al-Aʿmash from Shaqīq from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith from Zaynab, the wife of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ commanded us to give charity, and said: ""Give charity, O company of women, even from your jewellery."" She said: ""I was the one who provided for ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd and the orphans in his care, and ʿAbd Allāh was of limited means. So I said to ʿAbd Allāh: 'Go to the Prophet ﷺ and ask him whether that (your spending) will suffice for me, or whether I should direct it (as charity) on your behalf.' He said: 'No, rather go yourself and ask him.'"" She said: ""So I went to him and sat down. I found at the door a woman from the Anṣār whose need was the same as mine, and reverence for him had been cast upon her. She said: ""Bilāl came out to us, so we said: 'Ask the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, but do not tell him who we are.' So he asked him: 'Two women who provide for their husbands and have orphans in their care — will that suffice them regarding charity?' He said: 'Who are they?' He said: 'Zaynab and a woman from the Anṣār.' He said: 'Which of the Zaynabs?' He said: 'The wife of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd and a woman from the Anṣār.' He said: 'Yes, for them both there are two rewards: the reward of kinship and the reward of charity.'"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the hadith of Abū al-Aḥwaṣ from al-Aʿmash in its full length, and al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from the hadith of Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7763,"(7760) Abū ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us; Anas ibn ʿIyāḍ informed us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father, who informed him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to him from Rayṭah bint ʿAbd Allāh, the wife of ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd and mother of his children, who was a skilled woman, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd had no wealth, and she used to spend on him and his children from the earnings of her craft. She said: ""By Allah, you and your children have kept me from giving charity, so I am unable to give charity along with you."" He said: ""I would not like you to do that without there being any reward for you in it."" So she asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he was present, and she said: ""O Messenger of Allah, I am a woman of skill, I sell from it, and I have nothing, nor do my children, nor my husband. They have occupied me, so I cannot give charity. Is there any reward for me in that?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""For you in that is the reward of what you spend on them, so spend on them."" [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
7764,"(7761) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar informed us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from Zaynab bint Abī Salamah from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the children of Abī Salamah are in my care, and they have nothing except what I spend on them, and I am not going to leave them like this and like that. Is there any reward for me if I spend on them?"" The Prophet ﷺ said: ""Spend on them, for you have the reward of what you spend on them."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from another route from Hishām. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7765,"(7762) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh the Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Muhājir narrated to us; Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī narrated to us; Ibn Wahb narrated to me; ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed me from Kurayb from Maymūnah bint al-Ḥārith, that she freed a slave-girl during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ. She mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, who said: ""Had you given her to your maternal uncles, it would have been greater in reward for you."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hārūn ibn Saʿīd, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from another route from ʿAmr. [Ṣaḥīh. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7766,"(7763) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us; Abū Muslim Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; al-Anṣārī narrated to us; Bahz ibn Ḥakīm narrated to us from his father from his grandfather, who said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, who is most deserving of my good companionship?"" He said: ""Your mother."" I said: ""Then who?"" He said: ""Your mother."" I said: ""Then who?"" He said: ""Your mother."" I said: ""Then who?"" He said: ""Then your father, then the nearest and the nearest."" [Ṣaḥīh in other than this chain. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7767,"(7764) Abū al-Ḥasan informed us; Aḥmad informed us; Muḥammad ibn al-Faraj narrated to us; al-Sahmiyy, meaning ʿAbdullāh ibn Bakr, narrated to us; Bahz ibn Ḥakīm narrated from his father, from his grandfather, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""No man shall come to his freedman and ask him for something of surplus that he possesses, and he withhold it from him, except that on the Day of Resurrection he will be called to and a bald serpent will be set upon him, licking at the surplus which he withheld."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7768,"(7765) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us; al-Ḥārith ibn Murrah narrated to us; Kulayb ibn Manfaʿah narrated from his grandfather that he came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, who is most deserving of my good treatment?"" He said: ""Your mother, your father, your sister, your brother, and your freedman who is next to you — a right that is obligatory and a tie of kinship that is to be maintained."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7769,"(7766) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Bakr al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Abū ʿUtbah narrated to us; Baqiyyah ibn al-Walīd narrated from Baḥīr ibn Saʿd, from Khālid ibn Maʿdān, from al-Miqdād ibn Maʿdīkarib, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""Indeed, Allah enjoins you regarding your mothers; then He enjoins you regarding your fathers; then He enjoins you regarding the nearest and the next nearest."" Al-Miqdād said: And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: ""Whatever you feed yourself, your child, your wife, and your servant — it is charity."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7770,"(7767) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar al-Dabbī narrated to us; Musaddad narrated to us; Abū ʿAwanah narrated from Manṣūr, from ʿAlī ibn ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUrfuṭah, from Khidāsh Abī Salamah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""I enjoin a man regarding his mother three times; I enjoin a man regarding his father twice; and I enjoin a man regarding his freedman who is next to him, even if there is harm that afflicts him."" The Shaykh said: The companions of Manṣūr differed regarding the name of the one who narrated from him. It was said thus from him, and it was said from him from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿAlī, and other than that was said. And Allah knows best. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7771,"(7768) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us; Abū al-Ṭāhir narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī al-Faqīh informed me; ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūnus narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Saʿīd narrated to us. Both said: Ibn Wahb narrated to me; Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated from al-Walīd ibn Abī al-Walīd, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Dīnār, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, that a man from the Bedouins met him on the road to Makkah, and ʿAbdullāh greeted him, mounted him upon a donkey that he was riding, and gave him a turban that was upon his head. Ibn Dīnār said: We said to him: ""May Allah set you right — indeed, the Bedouins are pleased with very little."" ʿAbdullāh said: ""Indeed, the father of this man was a close friend of ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭābb (may Allah be pleased with him), and indeed I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'The most righteous form of righteousness is that a child maintain ties of kinship with the loved ones of his father.'"" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Ṭāhir. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7772,"(7769) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qāsim ibn al-Qāsim al-Sayyārī narrated to us in Marw; Abū al-Muwajjih informed us; ʿAbdān informed us; ʿAbdullāh informed us; Yūnus narrated from al-Zuhrī; Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib narrated to me that he heard Abū Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The best charity is that which is given from sufficiency, and begin with those whom you support."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7773,"(7770) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us; Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb narrated to us; Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated, saying: I heard Mūsā ibn Ṭalḥah mention from Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The best charity is that which is given from sufficiency; the upper hand is better than the lower hand; and begin with those whom you support."""
7774,"(7771) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir informed us; my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah al-Dabbī and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī both narrated to us, saying: Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Saʿīd, narrated to us; ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān narrated it with a similar chain, except that he said: He narrated that Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām narrated to him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""The most excellent charity — or the best charity — is from sufficiency."" Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdah and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously mentioned.]"
7775,"(7772) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us; Aḥmad ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Ibn Bukayr narrated to us; al-Layth narrated from Abī al-Zubayr, from Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿdah, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: ""O Messenger of Allah, which charity is most excellent?"" He said: ""The effort of the one who has little, and begin with those whom you support."""
7776,"(7773) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn al-Walīd narrated to us; Ḥajjāj said: Ibn Jurayj said: ʿUthmān ibn Abī Sulaymān narrated to me from ʿAlī al-Azdī, from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Ḥubshiyy, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) was asked: ""Which deeds are most excellent?"" He said: ""Faith in which there is no doubt, jihad in which there is no treachery, and an accepted Ḥajj."" It was said: ""Which prayer is most excellent?"" He said: ""Lengthy standing (in prayer)."" It was said: ""Which charity is most excellent?"" He said: ""The effort of one who has little."" It was said: ""Which migration is most excellent?"" He said: ""The one who migrates from that which Allah has forbidden him."" It was said: ""Which jihad is most excellent?"" He said: ""The one who strives against the polytheists with his wealth and his life."" It was said: ""Which death is most noble?"" He said: ""The one whose blood is shed and whose steed is hamstrung."""
7777,"(7774) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Dīnār al-ʿAdl narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Nuʿaym narrated to us: Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from his father, who said: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) commanded us one day to give charity. That coincided with some wealth I possessed, so I said: Today I shall outdo Abū Bakr, if I can ever surpass him on any day. So I came with half of my wealth. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""What have you left for your family?"" I said: The same amount. Then Abū Bakr came with everything he possessed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""What have you left for your family?"" He said: I have left for them Allah and His Messenger. I said: I shall never outdo you in anything, ever.

Abū Dāwūd narrated it in the Book of Sunan from ʿUthmān ibn Abī Shaybah and others, from Abū Nuʿaym al-Faḍl ibn Dukayn.

[Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7778,"(7775) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq narrated to us: ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from ʿUqayl, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, that ʿAbdullāh ibn Kaʿb, the guide of Kaʿb when he went blind among his sons, said: I heard Kaʿb ibn Mālik narrate his ḥadīth regarding his staying behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) during the expedition of Tabūk. He mentioned the ḥadīth at length. In it I said: O Messenger of Allah, part of my repentance is that I separate from my wealth as charity to Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, and to the Messenger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Retain some of your wealth, for it is better for you."" I said: Then I shall retain my share that is in Khaybar. And he mentioned the ḥadīth.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from al-Layth.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7779,"(7776) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us in Baghdad: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to me: al-Rabīʿ ibn Rūḥ narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ḥarb narrated to us: al-Zubaydī narrated from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥusayn ibn al-Sāʾib ibn Abī Lubābah, that his grandfather narrated to him: that when Abū Lubābah's repentance was accepted by Allah concerning his staying behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), and regarding what had preceded that in matters in which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) had found fault with him — Ḥusayn asserted that Abū Lubābah said when Allah accepted his repentance: O Messenger of Allah, I shall abandon the house of my people in which I committed the sin, and I shall move and reside with you, and I shall separate from my wealth as charity to Allah and to His Messenger. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him — Ḥusayn asserted — : ""One-third shall suffice you.""

Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥafṣah narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥusayn ibn al-Sāʾib ibn Abī Lubābah, from his father, who said: When Allah accepted the repentance of Abū Lubābah, Abū Lubābah said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: … and he mentioned it. And he said: ""One-third shall suffice you.""

[Weak. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7780,"(7777) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Razzāz informed us in Baghdad: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Jahm al-Sammārī informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd informed us: Muḥammad — meaning Ibn Isḥāq ibn Yasār — narrated from ʿĀṣim — meaning Ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah — from Maḥmūd ibn Labīd, from Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a man came with a piece of gold the size of an egg, which he had obtained in one of the military expeditions — or he said, from the mines — and he brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) from his right side and said: O Messenger of Allah, take this from me as charity. By Allah, I have no wealth other than it. So he turned away from him. Then he came with it from his left side and said the same. Then he came with it from in front of him and said the same. He said: ""Bring it here."" And he flung it at him — a fling that, had it struck him, would have hurt him or wounded him. Then he said: ""One of you goes and brings his wealth and gives it in charity, then afterwards sits and begs from people. Indeed, charity is only from surplus wealth. Take what is yours — we have no need of it."" So the man took his wealth and left.

Ḥammād ibn Salamah said from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq in this ḥadīth: ""I obtained this from a mine.""

[Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7781,"(7778) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miqrīʾ al-Mihrājānī informed us therein: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Musaddad informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān, who said: ʿIyāḍ narrated to me from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, that a man entered the mosque while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was on the pulpit on Friday. He called him and commanded him to pray two rakʿahs. Then he entered the second Friday while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) was on the pulpit, and he called him and commanded him to pray two rakʿahs. Then he entered the third Friday and mentioned the same. Then he said: ""Give charity."" So they gave charity, and he gave him two garments from what had been given in charity. Then he said: ""Give charity."" So he cast off one of his two garments. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) rebuked him and disapproved of what he had done. Then he said: ""Look at this one — he entered the mosque in a wretched state. I called him, hoping that you would take notice of him and give charity upon him and clothe him. But you did not do so. So I said: 'Give charity.' So they gave charity, and I gave him two garments from what had been given in charity. Then I said: 'Give charity,' and he cast off one of his garments. Take your garment."" And he rebuked him.

[Ḥasan. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7782,"(7779) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū Bakrah Bakkāru ibn Qutaybah al-Qāḍī in Egypt informed us: Ṣafwān ibn ʿĪsā informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAjlān narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abū Ṣāliḥ, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""A dirham has outstripped a hundred thousand."" They said: O Messenger of Allah, how can a dirham outstrip a hundred thousand? He said: ""A man who had two dirhams took one of them and gave it in charity, while another man who had abundant wealth took a hundred thousand from its profits — meaning — and gave it in charity.""

[Munkar in its chain. Narrated by al-Nasāʾī.]"
7783,"(7780) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī informed us: al-ʿAbbās al-Dūrī informed us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ḥafarī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī informed us: Ibn Abī Maryam informed us: Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us. Both said: Sufyān narrated from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Three men came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him). One of them said: I have a hundred awqiyyahs, and I gave ten awqiyyahs in charity. The second said: I have a hundred dinars, and I gave ten dinars in charity. The third said: I have ten dinars, and I gave one dinar in charity. The Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Each one of you gave charity with a tenth of his wealth. You are all equal in reward.""

[Weak. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7784,"(7781) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāh al-Muzakkī both said: Abū ʿAbdillāh ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym and Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr both said: Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Yūnus narrated to us.

And Abū Ṣāliḥ, the son of the daughter of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī, informed us: my grandfather Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr informed us: Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bashshār al-ʿAbdī informed to us: ʿUmar ibn Yūnus al-Ḥanafī narrated to us: ʿIkrimah ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us: Shaddād ibn ʿAbdillāh narrated — and in the narration of al-Jahḍamī, Shaddād is Abū ʿAmmār, and the rest is the same — who said: I heard Abū Umāmah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""O son of Adam, if you spend the surplus, it is better for you, and if you withhold it, it is worse for you. You shall not be blamed for sufficiency. Begin with those whom you support. The upper hand is better than the lower hand.""

The wording is from the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Bashshār.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Naṣr ibn ʿAlī al-Jahḍamī and Muḥammad ibn Bashshār.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7785,"(7782) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed me: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām and Aḥmad ibn al-Naḍr ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb Abū al-Faḍl both said: Shaybān ibn Abī Shaybah al-Aylī informed us: Abū al-Ashhab narrated from Abū Naḍrah, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: While we were on a journey with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), a man came upon his mount. He began striking it right and left. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Whoever has a surplus of mounts, let him provide for him who has no mount. And whoever has surplus provisions, let him provide for him who has no provisions."" He said: He then mentioned various kinds of wealth until we thought that none of us had any right to a surplus.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Shaybān ibn Abī Shaybah.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7786,"(7783) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed me: Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Jābir informed us: al-Haytham ibn Khārijah and Mahdī ibn Ḥafṣ both said: Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from Muṭʿim ibn al-Miqdām, from Naṣīḥ al-ʿAnsī, from Rakb al-Miṣrī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""Blessed is he who shows humility without being diminished, who humbles himself without being in need, and who spends wealth he gathered without disobedience. Blessed is he who shows mercy to the people of lowliness and need, and who keeps the company of the people of understanding and wisdom. Blessed is he who humbles himself, whose earnings are pure, whose inner self is sound, whose outward conduct is good, and who keeps his evil away from people. Blessed is he who acts upon his knowledge, spends the surplus of his wealth, and withholds the surplus of his speech.""

[Weak. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7787,"(7784) And ʿAlī informed us — Aḥmad informed us — ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us — Ādam narrated to us — Ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated from al-Muṭʿim ibn Miqdām and ʿAnbasah ibn Saʿīd al-Kalāʿī, from Naṣīḥ, from Rakb al-Miṣrī, who mentioned it with a similar meaning, except that he did not mention his statement: ""Blessed is the one who humbles himself and whose earnings are pure."" And he said: ""Blessed is the one whose inner self is sound and whose outward conduct is noble.""
[Weak. Reported by al-Ṭabarānī]"
7788,"(7785) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-Walīd al-Faqīh informed me — al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us — my father narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Malik, meaning Abū Sulaymān, narrated from Abū al-Zubayr, from Jābir, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""There is no owner of camels, or sheep, or cattle who does not fulfill their rights except that he will be made to sit for them on the Day of Resurrection on a flat, level plain. The cloven-hooved will trample them with their hooves, and the horned will gore them with their horns. On that day there will be no camel with a docked tail, nor any with a broken horn."" We said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what are their rights?"" He said: ""Lending their stallion, lending their bucket, giving them as a gift, milking them at the watering place, and carrying them in the cause of Allah. And there is no owner of wealth who does not pay its zakāh except that it will be transformed on the Day of Resurrection into a bold, bald-headed serpent that will follow its owner wherever he goes, fleeing from him. It will be said: 'This is your wealth with which you used to be miserly.' When he sees that there is no escape from it, he will put his hand into its mouth and begin to chew it as a stallion chews.""
Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr.
And Ibn Jurayj narrated it from Abū al-Zubayr with the same meaning. Abū al-Zubayr said: And I heard ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr saying this statement. Then we asked Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh about that, and he said: ""Like the statement of ʿUbayd."" And Abū al-Zubayr said: I heard ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr saying: A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, what is the right of the camels?"" He said: ""Milking them at the watering place, lending their bucket, lending their stallion, giving them as a gift, and carrying them in the cause of Allah.""
[Sahīh. Reported by Muslim]"
7789,"(7786) Abū Ṣāliḥ, the son of the daughter of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr, informed us — my grandfather narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us — Ibn Jurayj informed me — Abū al-Zubayr informed me: that he heard Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh, and he mentioned it.
Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ. And the narration of Abū al-Zubayr from ʿUbayd ibn ʿUmayr from the Prophet ﷺ is munqaṭiʿ (disconnected), while his narration from Jābir ibn ʿAbd Allāh is musnad (connected).
[Sahīh. Previously mentioned]"
7790,"(7787) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū Muḥammad ibn Ziyād informed me — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Yūnus ibn ʿAl-Aʿlá informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me — Hishām ibn Saʿd narrated from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Sammān, from Abū Hurayrah: that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said — and he mentioned the hadith, in which is: ""And no owner of camels who does not give their rights, and among their rights is milking them on the day they come to water — except that they will be gathered for him on the Day of Resurrection, not a single young one missing from them. Then he will be laid out for them on a flat, level plain, and they will trample him with their hooves and bite him with their mouths. Whenever the last of them passes over him, the first of them will return to him, on a day whose extent is fifty thousand years, until judgment is rendered among the people. Then he will see his path — either to Paradise or to Fire…"" — and he mentioned the hadith.
Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Yūnus ibn ʿAl-Aʿlá. And likewise Ḥafṣ ibn Maysarah narrated it from Zayd ibn Aslam. And Suhayl ibn Abī Ṣāliḥ narrated it from his father, and said in the hadith: ""There is no owner of camels who does not pay their zakāh"" — and he did not mention the wording about milking.
And Abū ʿUmar al-Ghuddānī narrated it from Abū Hurayrah with the same meaning regarding one who does not fulfill their rights. He was asked: ""What is the right of the camels, O Abū Hurayrah?"" He said: ""Giving the generous one, granting the abundant one, saddling the back, lending the stallion, and giving the milk to drink.""
[Sahīh. Reported by Muslim]"
7791,"(7788) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Maḥbūbī narrated to us — Saʿīd ibn Masʿūd narrated to us — Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, from Abū ʿUmar al-Ghuddānī, from Abū Hurayrah, and he mentioned it. The wording is different, except what I have transmitted from the wording of Abū Hurayrah.
This narration might give the impression that the explanation of ""the right"" in the narration of Abū Ṣāliḥ is from the words of Abū Hurayrah, as is the case in the narration of Abū ʿUmar al-Ghuddānī from Abū Hurayrah.
And the majority of the scholars have held that the obligation of zakāh abrogated the obligation of these rights other than zakāh, unless another person is compelled to them. The evidence for that has been presented at the beginning of the Book of Zakāh. And there have been narrations encouraging the granting of the manīḥah (gift of milk), and these are interpreted as being recommended (mustaḥabb). And Allah knows best.
[Sahīh. Previously mentioned]"
7792,"(7789) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us — Abū ʿAwānah narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd, from Shaqīq, from ʿAbd Allāh, who said: We used to count al-māʿūn, in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, as lending the bucket and the cooking pot.
[Ḥasan. Reported by Abū Dāwūd]"
7793,"(7790) And likewise Shaybān narrated from ʿĀṣim, except that he did not say ""in the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ,"" and he added: ""the axe and whatever you lend among yourselves.""
Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed him — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us — Ādam ibn Abī Iyās narrated to us — Shaybān narrated from ʿĀṣim ibn Abī al-Najūd, from Abū Wāʾil, from Ibn Masʿūd, and he mentioned it.
[Better. Previously mentioned]"
7794,"(7791) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us — Ādam narrated to us — al-Masʿūdī narrated from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Abū al-Ubaydayn, from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: It is the withholding of the axe, the bucket, the cooking pot, and what people lend among themselves.
And al-Ḥārith ibn Suwayd narrated it from ʿAbd Allāh.
[Ḥasan for other than it]"
7795,"(7792) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us — they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd Allāh informed us — Wakīʿ narrated from al-Aʿmash, from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said regarding {wa yamnaʿūna al-māʿūn}: ""It is the lending of household goods.""
[Ṣaḥīh]"
7796,"(7793) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥasan informed us — Ibrāhīm ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us — Ādam narrated to us — Warqāʾ narrated from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""Al-māʿūn is the household furnishings."" And likewise ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Abī Yazīd narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās. And a group held that it refers to the obligatory zakāh.
[Ṣaḥīh. Previously mentioned]"
7797,"(7794) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū ʿUthmān al-Baṣrī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us — Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us — Sufyān, that is al-Thawrī, (ḥ) and ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us by dictation — ʿAlī ibn ʿĪsā narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us — Ibn Abī ʿUmar narrated to us — Sufyān, that is Ibn ʿUyaynah, both from Ibn Abī Najīḥ, from Mujāhid, from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him), regarding the statement {"
7798,"(7795) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās, that is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us — Ibn Wahb narrated to us — Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Sālim narrated to us — Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah narrated — ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAlī ibn Isḥāq al-Mu'malī narrated — from Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding {"
7799,"(7796) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Abū al-ʿAbbās narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Abū Sulaymān al-Ashqar narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn ʿUthmān al-Qurashī narrated — from Yazīd ibn Dirham, from Anas: ""Al-māʿūn is the zakāh."" [Ḍaʿīf]"
7800,"(7797) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, both saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, that is al-Aṣamm, narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār narrated to us — Wakīʿ narrated — from Saʿīd ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṭāʾī, from ʿAlī ibn Rabīʿah al-Wālibī, who said: ""I asked Ibn ʿUmar about al-māʿūn. He said: 'What do they say about it?' I said: 'They say it is what people give to one another.' He said: 'What they say is nothing. It is wealth whose right is not fulfilled.'"" [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7801,"(7798) Abū ʿAbd Allāh — Isḥāq ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb al-Sūsī — informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-ʿAbbās ibn al-Walīd ibn Mazīd informed us — my father narrated to us. And Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf narrated to us — Abū al-Mughīrah narrated to us — and the ḥadīth is from al-ʿAbbās — both saying: al-Awzāʿī narrated to us — Ḥassān ibn Aṭiyyah said: ""Abū Kabshah al-Salūlī entered the mosque of Damascus, and ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Zakariyyāʾ, Maḥkūl, and Abū Baḥriyyah stood before him among a group of people."" Ḥassān said: ""I was among those who stood before him, and he narrated to us, saying: 'I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ saying: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: ""There are forty good deeds, the highest of which is the milking of a goat. No man performs any of them, hoping for its reward and believing in its promise, except that Allah will thereby admit him into Paradise.""'"" Ḥassān said: ""We then began to count returning the greeting, removing a stone, and the like short of the milking of a goat, and we could not reach fifteen."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī reported it.]"
7802,"(7799) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us — Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — ʿĪsā narrated — from al-Awzāʿī, from Ḥassān ibn Aṭiyyah, from Abī Kabshah al-Salūlī, who said: ""I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr saying: The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: 'There are forty qualities, the highest of which is the milking of a goat. No servant performs any of them, hoping for its reward and believing in its promise, except that Allah, the Almighty and Majestic, will thereby admit him into Paradise.'"" Then he mentioned the statement of Ḥassān with its meaning. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Musaddad. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Previously cited.]"
7803,"(7800) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Anmāṭī narrated to us — Hārūn ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us — Zakariyyāʾ ibn ʿAdī narrated to us — ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿAmr narrated — from Zayd, from ʿAdī ibn Thābit, from Abī Ḥāzim, from Abī Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam): ""He forbade and mentioned certain traits, and said: 'Whoever offers a milking animal, it becomes a ṣadaqah in the morning and a ṣadaqah in the evening — its morning drink and its evening drink.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Khalaf, from Zakariyyāʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim reported it.]"
7804,"(7801) Abū ʿAbd Allāh informed us — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Aḥmad ibn Sahl narrated to us — ʿAbd al-Jabbār ibn al-Aʾlā narrated to us — Sufyān narrated — from Abī al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abī Hurayrah, reaching the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam), who said: ""The best ṣadaqah is the milking animal — except that a Muslim man may give a she-camel to a household to milk, going forth in the morning with a large vessel and returning in the evening with a large vessel — its reward with Allah is immense."" Muslim narrated it with part of its meaning from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7805,"(7802) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyāʾ ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, both saying: Abū al-Ḥusayn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us — ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us — Musaddad narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Dāwūd narrated — from Fuḍayl, that is ibn Ghazwān, from Abī Ḥāzim, from Abī Hurayrah: ""A man came to the Prophet (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam), so he sent word to his wives, and they said: 'We have nothing but water.' The Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) said: 'Who will host this man?' A man from the Anṣār said: 'I will.' He took him to his wife and said: 'Honor the guest of the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam).' She said: 'We have nothing but the food of the children.' He said: 'Prepare your food, extinguish your lamp, and put your children to sleep when they wish for supper.' So she prepared her food, set right her lamp, and put her children to sleep. Then she stood as though setting right her lamp and extinguished it, making them (the guest and her husband) appear as though they were eating, while they both went hungry. When morning came, he went to the Messenger of Allah (ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam) and said: 'Allah laughed last night — or was amazed — at your deed.'"" And he said: ""Allah then revealed: {"
7806,"(7803) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us — Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us — Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us — Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated — from al-Aʿmash, from Nāfiʿ, who said: ""Ibn ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) fell ill and desired grapes when the first grapes arrived. Ṣafiyyah sent a dirham and bought a bunch of grapes for a dirham. A beggar followed the messenger, and when he reached the door, he entered and said: 'A beggar, a beggar.' Ibn ʿUmar said: 'Give it to him.' So they gave it to him. Then she sent another dirham and bought a bunch with it. The beggar followed the messenger again, and when he reached the door and entered, he said: 'A beggar, a beggar.' Ibn ʿUmar said: 'Give it to him.' So they gave it to him. Then Ṣafiyyah sent word to the beggar, saying: 'By Allah, if you return, you will never find any good from me again.' Then she sent another dirham and bought with it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7807,"(7804) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl informed us in Baghdād; Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz informed us; Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir narrated to us; ʿAffān narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥasan al-Hilālī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿArʿarah narrated to us; Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyab and al-Ḥasan, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah, that he came to the Prophet ﷺ and said: ""Which charity is most pleasing to you?"" He said: ""Providing water to drink."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn ʿArʿarah. And in the ḥadīth of ʿAffān: that Saʿd ibn ʿUbādah said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, which charity is best?"" And he added: ""Saʿd had a water-skin (siqāyah) in Madīnah."" He (the narrator) said: ""I said to Qatādah: Who said 'to the family of Saʿd'?"" He said: ""Al-Ḥasan."" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7808,"(7805) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us; Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Abū Badr narrated to us; Abū Khālid — who used to stay among Banī Dalān — narrated from Nubayḥ, from Abū Saʿīd, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Any Muslim who clothes a naked person, Allāh will clothe him from the green silk of Paradise; and any Muslim who feeds a hungry Muslim, Allāh will feed him from the fruits of Paradise; and any Muslim who gives water to a thirsty Muslim, Allāh will give him to drink from the sealed wine (al-Raḥīq al-Makhtūm)."" [Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7809,"(7806) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq narrated to us; ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah narrated from Mālik.

And Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh al-Mihrajānī narrated to us in it; Bishr ibn Aḥmad narrated to us; Dāwūd ibn al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Qutaybah narrated from Mālik, from Sumayy, the freedman of Abū Bakr, from Abū Ṣāliḥ al-Sammān, from Abū Hurayrah, may Allāh be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said: ""While a man was walking on a path, he was overcome by intense thirst. He found a well, so he descended into it and drank. Then he came out and found a dog panting and eating the earth out of thirst. The man said: 'This one has reached the same state of thirst that I had reached.' — And in the narration of Qutaybah: 'the same as I reached' — So he descended into the well, filled his shoe with water, and held it in his mouth until he climbed out, and he gave the dog to drink. Allāh thanked him and forgave him."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allāh, is there reward for us in (kindness to) animals?"" He said: ""In every moist liver (i.e., every living creature) there is reward.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Maslamah al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Authentic.]"
7810,"(7807) ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān informed us in Baghdād; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ramādī narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah, from Suraqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum, that he came to the Prophet ﷺ during his illness and said: ""What do you think about a stray camel that comes to the watering place of my camels — is there reward for me if I give it to drink?"" He said: ""Yes, in the warm liver (i.e., every living creature) there is reward."" [Authentic. Narrated by Ibn Mājah.]"
7811,"(7808) Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Yāsār narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Kaʿb ibn Mālik, from his uncle Suraqah ibn Mālik ibn Juʿshum, who said: ""I asked the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ about a stray camel that comes to my watering place — is there reward for me?"" He said: ""Yes, and every creature with a warm liver (has reward).""

Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Razzāz informed us in Baghdād; Abū Bakr al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn al-Jahm narrated to us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated it.

And Yazīd ibn Hārūn narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, from his uncle. [Authentic.]"
7812,"(7809) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated from Abū Isḥāq; Kudayr al-Ḍabbī informed me that a Bedouin man came to the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ and said: ""Tell me of a deed that will draw me close to His obedience and distance me from the Fire."" He said: ""Will you act upon it?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Speak justice and give the surplus."" He said: ""By Allāh, I am not able to speak justice every hour, and I am not able to give the surplus of my wealth."" He said: ""Then feed food and spread peace (salām)."" He said: ""This too is difficult."" He said: ""Do you have camels?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Then look to a camel from your camels and a water-skin, and go to the people of households who do not drink water except every other day, and give them to drink. Perhaps your camel will not perish and your water-skin will not tear until Paradise becomes obligatory for you."" He said: ""The Bedouin departed saying 'Allāhu Akbar.'"" He said: ""His water-skin did not tear and his camel did not perish until he was killed as a martyr."" [Weak. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
7813,"(7810) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī and Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; they said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; they said: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated from Abū al-Zinād, from al-Aʿraj, from Abū Hurayrah, reaching the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""The example of the one who spends and the miserly one is like two men upon whom are two coats of mail (or two garments) of iron, from their breasts to their collarbones. When the one who spends intends to spend, the armor expands for him, or it passes until it covers his fingers and erases his traces. And when the miserly one intends to spend, it contracts upon him and each ring stays in its place until it takes him by his neck or by his collarbone — and he tries to widen it, but it does not widen. Then he tries to widen it, but it does not widen."" [Authentic. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7814,"(7811) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and others informed us; they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us; al-Shāfiʿī informed us; Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah narrated it with its chain, similar to it. Except that he said: the Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said — and he did not say ""of iron"" — ""and he tries to widen it, and it does not widen, even once."" [Authentic. See the previous.]"
7815,"(7812) He said: And Sufyān ibn ʿUyaynah informed us from Ibn Jurayj, from al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim, from Ṭāwūs, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, similar to it. Except that he said: ""And he tries to widen it, and it does not widen.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr al-Nāqid from Sufyān with both chains, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from another direction from al-Ḥasan ibn Muslim. [Authentic.]"
7816,"(7813) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq narrated to us; Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Ḥabīb al-Farrāʾ informed us; Muḥāḍir narrated to us; Hishām — that is, Ibn ʿUrwah — narrated from Fāṭimah, meaning the daughter of al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ, meaning the daughter of Abī Bakr, may Allāh be pleased with both of them, who said: The Messenger of Allāh ﷺ said to me: ""Spend and scatter (in charity) in this way and in this way and in this way, and do not count, lest Allāh count against you, and do not store up, lest Allāh store up against you.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it from various routes from Hishām. [Authentic. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7817,"(7814) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Muḥammad ibn Abī Ḥāmid al-Muqriʾ, and Abū Ṣādiq ibn Abī al-Fawāris al-ʿAṭṭār informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, al-Ḥajjāj al-Aʿwar narrated, saying: Ibn Jurayj said: Ibn Abī Mulaykah informed me, from ʿAbbād ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Zubayr, that he informed him, from Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr (may Allah be pleased with her), that she came to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: ""O Prophet of Allah, I have nothing except what al-Zubayr brings to me. Is there any sin upon me if I spend from what he brings me?"" He said: ""Spend as much as you are able, and do not hoard, lest Allah make you destitute.""

It was narrated in the two Ṣaḥīḥs. Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm from Ḥajjāj, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Ḥātim and others from Ḥajjāj. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
7818,"(7815) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us, Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, Allah said to me: 'Spend, and I will spend upon you.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7819,"(7816) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū al-Qāsim al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Ḥabīb al-Mufassir from his original, and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-ʿAbbās ibn Muḥammad al-Dūrī narrated to us, Khālid ibn Makhlad narrated to us, Sulaymān ibn Bilāl narrated to us, from Muʿāwiyah ibn Abī Muzarrid, from Saʿīd ibn Yasār, from Abū Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""There is no day in which the servants wake except that two angels descend. One of them says: 'O Allah, give the one who spends a replacement,' and the other says: 'O Allah, give the one who withholds destruction.'""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qāsim ibn Zakariyyā from Khālid ibn Makhlad, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through another chain from Sulaymān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7820,"(7817) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym narrated to us, Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, from al-ʿAlāʾ, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Charity does not decrease wealth, and Allah does not increase a person in pardon except in honour, and no one humbles himself for the sake of Allah except that Allah raises him.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7821,"(7818) Al-Faqīh Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb in Ṭabarān informed us, Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-ʿAbbās ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Bazzāz verbatim informed us, Ibrāhīm ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥarbī narrated to us, ʿAffān narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Murrah: I heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ḥārith narrating from Abī Kathīr that he heard ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr narrating from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: ""Beware of miserliness, for it destroyed those who came before you. It commanded them to sever ties of kinship, so they severed them; it commanded them to be stingy, so they were stingy; and it commanded them to commit immorality, so they committed it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7822,"(7819) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us, Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī informed us, Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār informed us, Saʿdān narrated to us, Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, al-Aʿmash narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""No man gives anything in charity except that it frees him from the jaws of seventy devils."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7823,"(7820) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī (may Allah have mercy on him) informed us, Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawayh al-Muzakkī informed us, Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Maʿmar informed us, from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah narrated to us, saying: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Every joint of every person has a charity due upon it every day the sun rises. He said: Reconciling between two people is a charity. Helping a man with his mount, loading him upon it or lifting his belongings onto it for him, is a charity. A good word is a charity. Every step taken towards the prayer is a charity. And removing a harmful thing from the path is a charity.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it from Isḥāq ibn Naṣr, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ, both from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7824,"(7821) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Maḥmūyah al-ʿAskarī in Baṣrah in the year forty-one and three hundred informed us, Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad al-Qalānisī narrated to us, Ādam narrated to us, Shuʿbah narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Abī Burdah ibn Abī Mūsā narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Upon every Muslim is a charity."" They said: ""What if he does not find anything?"" He said: ""Then let him work with his hands, benefit himself, and give charity."" They said: ""What if he is unable to do so or does not do it?"" He said: ""Then let him assist the one in desperate need."" They said: ""What if he does not do that?"" He said: ""Then let him enjoin good""—or he said, ""the good."" They said: ""What if he does not do that?"" He said: ""Then let him refrain from evil, for that is a charity for him.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ādam ibn Abī Iyās, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Shuʿbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7825,"(7822) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Abū Sahl ibn Ziyād al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us, Abū Tawbah narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn al-Muʾammal ibn al-Ḥasan ibn ʿĪsá informed us, al-Faḍl ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Musayyab informed us, Abū Tawbah al-Rabīʿ ibn Nāfiʿ narrated to us, Muʿāwiyah ibn Salām narrated to us, from his brother Zayd, that he heard Abū Salām saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Farrūkh narrated to me that he heard ʿĀʾishah saying: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, every human being among the children of Adam is created with three hundred and sixty joints. Whoever declares the greatness of Allah, praises Allah, declares the oneness of Allah, glorifies Allah, seeks forgiveness from Allah, removes a stone from the path of the people, or removes a thorn from the path of the people, or enjoins good or forbids evil, to the number of those three hundred and sixty joints—he will have, on that day, distanced himself from the Fire.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī al-Ḥulwānī from Abī Tawbah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7826,"(7823) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ informed us, Mahdī ibn Maymūn narrated to us, Wāṣil, the freedman of Abī ʿUyaynah, narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn ʿUqayl, from Yaḥyā ibn Yaʿmar, from Abī al-Aswad al-Dīlī, from Abī Dharr, that some of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): ""O Messenger of Allah, the people of wealth have taken the reward. They pray as we pray, they fast as we fast, and they give charity from the surplus of their wealth."" He said: ""Has Allah not made for you that which you can give in charity? Indeed, every declaration of glory (tasbīḥ) is a charity, every declaration of Allah's greatness (takbīr) is a charity, every declaration of Allah's oneness (tahlīl) is a charity, every praise of Allah (taḥmīd) is a charity, enjoining good is a charity, and forbidding evil is a charity. And in the intimate relations of one of you there is a charity."" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, does one of us satisfy his desire and still have a reward for it?"" He said: ""Do you not see that if he were to place it in the unlawful, would there be a sin upon him for it?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Likewise, when he places it in the lawful, there is a reward for him.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Muslim.]"
7827,"(7824) Abū Zakariyyā — Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm al-Muzzakī — informed us; Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Salmān al-Faqīh — narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Muḥammad al-Raqqāshī — narrated to us; ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar — narrated to us; Abū ʀĀmir al-Khazzāz — Ṣāliḥ ibn Rustum — from Abī ʿImrān al-Jawnī — from ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ṣāmit — from Abū Dharr that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""O Abū Dharr, do not belittle any act of goodness, even if it be that you meet your brother with a cheerful face, or that you pour from your bucket into the vessel of one who seeks water. And when you cook a pot, increase its broth and ladle some out for your neighbours.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Ghassān from ʿUthmān ibn ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7828,"(7825) Ḥudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) reported that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""Every act of goodness is a charity (ṣadaqah).""

In the narration of Abū Dāwūd, he said: ""Your Prophet (ﷺ) said.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of Ibn al-Munkadir from Jābir. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7829,"(7826) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān — informed us; Abū Jaʿfar — Ḥafṣah ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb — narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Ḥarb — narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUaynah — narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī — informed us; Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī — narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī — narrated to us; Sufyān ibn ʿUaynah — from al-Zuhrī — from Sālim — from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""There is no envy (ḥasad) except in two cases: a man to whom Allah has given the Qur'an and he stands with it during the hours of the night and the day, and a man to whom Allah has given wealth and he spends it during the hours of the night and the day.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah and others from Ibn ʿUaynah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7830,"(7827) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakī — both said: al-Qāḍī Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn Kāmil ibn Khalaf — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Saʿd al-ʿAwfī — narrated to us; Rūḥ — narrated to us; Shuʿbah — from Sulaymān al-Aʿmash, who said: I heard Dhakwān from Abī Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""There is no envy except in two cases: a man whom Allah has taught the Qur'an and he recites it during the hours of the night and the hours of the day, so a neighbour of his hears it and says: 'Would that I had been given the like of what so-and-so has been given, so that I might do as he does.' And a man to whom Allah has given wealth and he destroys it in what is right. So a man says: 'Would that I had been given the like of what so-and-so has been given, so that I might do as he does.'""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAlī ibn Ibrāhīm from Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7831,"(7828) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — narrated to us; Ḥājib ibn Aḥmad — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ḥammād — narrated to us; Abū Muʿāwiyah — from al-Aʿmash — from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd — from Abī Kabshah al-Anmārī, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) struck for us a parable of this world, a parable of four persons from among us:

""A man to whom Allah has given knowledge and He has given him wealth, so he acts according to his knowledge regarding his wealth. And a man to whom Allah has given knowledge but He has not given him wealth, so he says: 'If only Allah, Mighty and Majestic, had given me the like of what so-and-so has been given, I would have done with it as he does.' So they are equal in reward. And a man to whom Allah has given wealth but He has not given him knowledge, so he withholds it from its right and spends it in falsehood. And a man to whom Allah has given neither knowledge nor wealth, so he says: 'If only Allah had given me the like of what so-and-so has been given, I would have done with it as he does.' So they are equal in sin.""

Thus narrated al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Ibn Ḥibbān.]"
7832,"(7829) And Abū al-Ḥasan — Muḥammad ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh — informed us; Bishr ibn Aḥmad al-Isfarāyinī — narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Naṣr — narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Madīnī — narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq ibn Hammām — informed us; Maʿmar — from Manṣūr — from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd — from Ibn Abī Kabshah al-Anmārī — from his father, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) strike a parable of this ummah, a parable of four persons: a man…"" Then he mentioned the ḥadīth with its meaning.

ʿAlī said: And Ibn Abī Kabshah — this is well-known, and he is Muḥammad ibn Abī Kabshah. Another ḥadīth has been narrated from him, meaning from his father, regarding the valley of Thamūd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
7833,"(7830) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ — informed us; Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Akram and Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Muqriʾ — both said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shīrūyah — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā ibn Abī ʿUmar — narrated to us; Marwān ibn Muʿāwiyah — from Yazīd ibn Kaysān — from Abī Ḥāzim al-Ashjaʿī — from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:

""Who among you has begun this day fasting?"" Abū Bakr said: ""I have."" He said: ""Who among you has followed a funeral procession today?"" Abū Bakr said: ""I have."" He said: ""Who among you has fed a poor person today?"" Abū Bakr said: ""I have."" He said: ""Who among you has visited a sick person today?"" Abū Bakr said: ""I have."" Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""These do not gather in a person except that he will enter Paradise.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Abī ʿUmar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7834,"(7831) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Naṣr — Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Fāmiyy — narrated to us; Abū al-ʿAbbās — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAwf al-Ṭāʾī — narrated to us; Ibn al-Muṣaffā — narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd — from al-Mukhtār ibn Fulful — from Anas, who said:

""Be prompt in giving charity, for calamity does not surpass charity.""

It is mawqūf (stopped at the Companion). It was in the book of our shaykh Abū Naṣr al-Fāmiyy as marfūʿ (attributed to the Prophet), but this is a mistake (wahm).

It has also been narrated from Abū Yūsuf al-Qāḍī from al-Mukhtār ibn Fulful as marfūʿ. [Ḍaʿīf.]"
7835,"(7832) Abū ʿAbd Allāh — al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿUmar ibn Burhān al-Ghazzāl, and Abū al-Ḥusayn — ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bushrān al-ʿAdl, and Abū al-Ḥusayn — Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān, and Abū Muḥammad — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār in Baghdad — all said: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār — narrated to us; al-Ḥasan ibn ʿArafah — narrated to us; Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd — from ʿUmārah ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ — from Abī Zurʿah — from Abī Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked: ""Which charity is best?"" He said:

""Let it be known to you: that you give charity while you are healthy and covetous, hoping for long life and fearing poverty. And do not delay until the soul reaches the throat and you say: 'For so-and-so such-and-such, and for so-and-so such-and-such' — and indeed it already belongs to so-and-so.""

Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Jarīr, and al-Bukhārī narrated it from two other routes from ʿUmārah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7836,"(7833) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh — informed us; Abū ʿUthmān — ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī — narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb — informed us; Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd — narrated to us; Sufyān — from Abī Isḥāq — from Abī Ḥabībah, who said: A man bequeathed to me a portion of his wealth to distribute, so I came to Abū al-Dardāʾ and consulted him regarding the poor or the Muhājirūn. He said:

""As for me, I would not abandon the Muhājirūn. I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'The likeness of the one who frees a slave at the time of death is like the one who gives a gift after having eaten his fill.'""

[Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7837,"(7834) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us that 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated to us that Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Shu'bah narrated from Abu Ishaq from Abu Habibah al-Ta'i from Abu al-Darda', who said: I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say: ""The likeness of the one who gives charity or frees a slave at the time of death is like the one who gives a gift after he has already eaten his fill."" [Weak. See the preceding hadith.]"
7838,"(7835) Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran informed us that 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Misri narrated to us that Malik ibn Yahya narrated to us that Yazid ibn Harun informed us that Shu'bah narrated from Zubayd from Murrah that 'Abdullah, regarding the words of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic: {And gives wealth, despite love for it, to relatives, orphans, and the needy}, said: ""Give charity while you are healthy and covetous, hoping for wealth and fearing poverty."" [Hasan]"
7839,"(7836) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us that Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Balawayh narrated to us that Musa ibn Harun narrated to us that Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us that Yahya — (H) and Abu 'Abdullah informed us that Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Bashshar and Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna both said: Yahya, meaning Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us from 'Ubaydullah, meaning Ibn 'Umar, who said: Khubayb ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me from Hafs ibn 'Asim from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Seven will be shaded by Allah, the Most High, in His shade on the day when there shall be no shade except His shade: a just leader; a man who grew up in the worship of Allah; a man whose heart is attached to the mosques; two men who love each other for the sake of Allah, meeting upon it and parting upon it; a man who is sought by a woman of high status and beauty, but he says: 'I fear Allah'; a man who gives charity and conceals it so that his left hand does not know what his right hand has spent; and a man who remembers Allah in solitude and his eyes overflow with tears.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muhammad ibn Bashshar, and Muslim narrated it from Zuhayr ibn Harb and Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna. They all said: From Yahya al-Qattan from 'Ubaydullah: ""His left hand does not know what his right hand has spent."" And the rest of the narrators from Yahya al-Qattan said it thus. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7840,"(7837) Abu 'Amr al-Adib informed us that Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me that al-Hasan ibn Sufyan narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Khallad narrated to us that Yahya narrated to us that 'Ubaydullah narrated to me that Khubayb ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to me from Hafs ibn 'Asim from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ), and he mentioned the hadith, saying in it: ""And a man who gives charity and conceals it so that his left hand does not know what his right hand has spent.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Musaddad from Yahya, thus. And likewise Ahmad ibn Hanbal narrated it from Yahya. And with the same meaning, the rest of the narrators narrated it from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Umar. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
7841,"(7838) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz, Abu Muhammad ibn Abi Hamid al-Muqri', and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi all said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Duri narrated to us that Abu al-Nadr Hisham ibn al-Qasim narrated to us that Warqa' narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar from Sa'id ibn Yasar from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever gives in charity a date's worth from honest earnings — and nothing ascends to Allah except that which is good — then Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, accepts it with His right hand and nurtures it for its owner, just as one of you nurtures his foal, until it becomes like Mount Uhud.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it and said: ""And Warqa' said,"" and he mentioned it. And Muslim narrated it from another chain from Sa'id. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7842,"(7839) Abu Salih ibn Abi Tahir informed us that my grandfather Yahya ibn Mansur al-Qadi narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated to us that Ya'qub, meaning Ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, narrated from Suhayl from his father from Abu Hurayrah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""No one gives in charity a date from honest earnings except that Allah takes it with His right hand and nurtures it, just as one of you nurtures his foal or young camel, until it becomes like a mountain or even greater.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah. And al-Bukhari alluded to the narration of Suhayl in that regard and narrated it as mentioned above. [Sahih. See the preceding hadith.]"
7843,"(7840) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-Fadl ibn Ibrahim narrated to us that Ahmad ibn Salamah narrated to us that Qutaybah ibn Sa'id ibn Jamil ibn Tarif ibn 'Abdullah al-Thaqafi narrated to us that Abu 'Awanah narrated from Simak from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, who said: Ibn 'Umar visited Ibn 'Amir to enquire after his health, and he said: ""O Ibn 'Umar, will you not supplicate for me?"" Ibn 'Umar said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say: 'Allah does not accept any prayer without purification, nor any charity from ill-gotten gains.'"" And you had been in Basrah.

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Qutaybah and others. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7844,"(7841) Abu al-Husayn Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn al-Fadl al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us that Abu 'Amr 'Uthman ibn Ahmad ibn al-Sammak narrated to us that Muhammad ibn 'Ubaydullah ibn al-Munadi narrated to us that Wahb ibn Jarir narrated to us that Shu'bah narrated from al-A'mash from Sulayman ibn Musahhir from Kharashah ibn al-Hurr from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Three whom Allah will not speak to on the Day of Resurrection, nor will He look at them, nor will He purify them, and for them is a painful punishment: the one who reminds of his favours after giving; the one who lets his garment hang below the ankles out of pride; and the one who sells his goods by means of false or sinful oaths.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Ghundar from Shu'bah. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7845,"(7842) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me that Abu Bakr al-Shafi'i narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Ghalib narrated to us that Abu Hufayfah narrated to us that Sufyan narrated from Ja'far ibn Iyas from Sa'id ibn Jubayr from Ibn 'Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: They used to dislike giving gifts to their relatives while they were polytheists, so the verse was revealed: {It is not for you to guide them, but Allah guides whom He wills} until He reached: {And you are not wronged.} Then permission was granted for them. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Nasa'i.]"
7846,"(7843) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf al-Asbahani informed us that Abu Sa'id Ahmad ibn Muhammad ibn Ziyad al-Basri in Makkah narrated to us that Sa'dan ibn Nasr al-Mukharrami narrated to us — (H) and Abu al-Husayn ibn Bishran and Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari both said: Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar narrated to us that Sa'dan narrated to us that Sufyan ibn 'Uyaynah narrated from Hisham ibn 'Urwah from Fatimah bint al-Mundhir from her grandmother Asma' bint Abi Bakr, who said: I asked the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and said: ""My mother has come to me desiring something — should I give to her?"" He said: ""Yes, maintain ties with her."" Thus said Sa'dan from Sufyan. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7847,"(7844) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq informed us that Bishr ibn Mūsā informed us that al-Ḥumaydī informed us that Sufyān informed us that Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us that he heard his father saying: Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr al-Ṣiddīq, may Allah be pleased with both of them, informed me. She said: ""My mother came to me during the era of Quraysh, desiring [to maintain ties]. So I asked the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him: 'Should I maintain ties of kinship with her?' He said: 'Yes.'"" Sufyān said: ""Regarding her, the verse was revealed: {Allah does not forbid you from those who do not fight you...} [al-Mumtaḥinah: 8].""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ḥumaydī, and Muslim extracted it from the ḥadīth of ʿAbdullāh ibn Idrīs and Abī Usāmah from Hishām from his father. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
7848,"(7845) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq and others informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us that al-Rabīʿ informed us that al-Shāfiʿī informed us that Sufyān informed us from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah from his father from his mother, Asmāʾ bint Abī Bakr, may Allah be pleased with both of them. Then he mentioned a narration similar to that of al-Ḥumaydī, without the statement of Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
7849,"(7846) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq informed us, saying: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr al-Qushayrī and ʿImrān ibn Mūsā informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh informed us, saying: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Mūsā informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Isḥāq al-Sarrāj informed us, saying: Suwayd ibn Saʿīd informed us that Ḥafṣ ibn Mayṣarah informed me from Mūsā ibn ʿUqbah from Abī al-Zinād from al-Aʿraj from Abū Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

""A man said: 'I shall certainly give charity tonight.' So he went out with his charity and placed it in the hand of an adulteress. In the morning, the people began saying: 'Charity was given to an adulteress!' So he said: 'O Allah, all praise is to You for [it reaching] an adulteress! I shall certainly give charity tonight.' So he went out with his charity and placed it in the hand of a wealthy person. In the morning, they began saying: 'Charity was given to a wealthy person!' So he said: 'O Allah, all praise is to You for [it reaching] a wealthy person! I shall certainly give charity tonight.' So he went out with his charity and placed it in the hand of a thief. In the morning, they began saying: 'Charity was given to a thief!' So he said: 'O Allah, all praise is to You for [it reaching] an adulteress, a wealthy person, and a thief!' Then he was approached and told: 'As for your charity, it has been accepted. As for the adulteress, perhaps she will refrain from her adultery because of it. Perhaps the wealthy person will take heed and spend from what Allah has given him. Perhaps the thief will refrain from his theft because of it.'""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Suwayd ibn Saʿīd, and al-Bukhārī extracted it from the ḥadīth of Shuʿayb ibn Abī Ḥamzah from Abī al-Zinād. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
7850,"(7847) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAlawī informed us that Abū Ḥāmid Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-Azhar Aḥmad ibn al-Azhar informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf ibn Maʿqil al-Umawī informed us that Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḥārithī al-Kūfī, a righteous man, informed us, saying: Abū Usāmah informed us that Burayd informed me from his grandfather, Abū Burdah, from Abū Mūsā, from the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, who said:

""Indeed, the trustworthy treasurer who gives what he has been commanded to give, completely and in full, with a cheerful soul, until he delivers it to the one for whom he was commanded to give it — he is one of the two who give charity (or: one of those who give charity).""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kurayb from Abī Usāmah, and Muslim narrated it from Abī Kurayb and a group from Abī Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
7851,"(7848) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us that al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAffān informed us that Ibn Numayr informed us from al-Aʿmash from Shaqīq from Masrūq from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

""When a woman feeds [others] from her husband's household without causing any corruption, she shall have her reward, and he shall have the same, and the treasurer shall have the same — each according to what they have earned, and she according to what she has spent.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Numayr from his father, and al-Bukhārī extracted it from another chain from al-Aʿmash. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
7852,"(7849) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us that Ismāʿīl ibn Qutaybah informed us that Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā informed us that Jarīr informed us from Manṣūr from Shaqīq from Masrūq from ʿĀʾishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

""When a woman spends from the food of her household without causing any corruption, she shall have her reward for what she has spent, and her husband shall have his reward for what he has earned, and the treasurer shall have the same — none of them diminishing the reward of the other in any way.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it together from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā. Some of them said from Manṣūr in this ḥadīth: ""from the food of her husband."" And some of them said: ""When she gives charity from her husband's household."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
7853,"(7850) Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī informed us that Abū al-Qāsim ʿUbaydullāh ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Bālawiyah al-Muzakkī informed us that Aḥmad ibn Yūsuf al-Sulamī informed us that ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us that Maʿmar informed us from Hammām ibn Munabbih, who said: This is what Abū Hurayrah informed us, saying: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said:

""A woman should not fast [a voluntary fast] while her husband is present except with his permission, and she should not permit anyone into his house while he is present except with his permission. And whatever she spends from his earnings without his command, then half of the reward is for him.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ from ʿAbd al-Razzāq, and al-Bukhārī extracted the ḥadīth of spending from Yaḥyā ibn Jaʿfar from ʿAbd al-Razzāq. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī extracted it.]"
7854,"(7851) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us that Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us that Abū Dāwūd informed us that Muḥammad ibn Sawwār informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us that Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us that Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbdillāh informed us that Sulaymān informed us, saying: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn Ḥarb informed us that Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd informed us from Ziyād ibn Jubayr ibn Ḥayyah from Saʿd, who said: When the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, took the pledge of allegiance from the women, a distinguished woman stood up, as if she were from the women of Muḍar, and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we are a burden upon our fathers and our sons.""

Abū Dāwūd said: ""And I think he said: 'and our husbands.'"" And in the narration of Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb: ""upon our sons and our husbands."" She said: ""What is lawful for us from their wealth?"" He said: ""Fresh food — you may eat it and give it as a gift.""

In the ḥadīth of Ibn Sawwār, the word ""food"" is not mentioned. Sufyān al-Thawrī corroborated it from Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd extracted it.]"
7855,"(7852) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us that Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Faqīh al-Ṣaffār al-Aṣbahānī informed us that ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed me that my father informed us that ʿAbd al-Malik ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dhimārī informed us from Sufyān, who informed me that Yūnus ibn ʿUbayd informed us from Ziyād ibn Jubayr from Saʿd, that a woman said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we are a burden upon our fathers and our brothers. What is lawful for us from their wealth?"" The Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""From the fresh food that you eat and give as a gift."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
7856,"(7853) And with what Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, that Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us that Abū Dāwūd informed us that Muḥammad ibn Sawwār al-Miṣrī informed us that ʿAbadah informed us from ʿAbd al-Malik from ʿAṭāʾ, from Abū Hurayrah, regarding a woman who gives charity from her husband's household: He said: ""No, except from her provision, and the reward is between them. And it is not lawful for her to give charity from her husband's wealth except with his permission.""

This is the statement of Abū Hurayrah, and he is one of the narrators of those reports. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Abū Dāwūd extracted it.]"
7857,"(7854) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Ḥurṣī informed us; Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Ayyūb al-Ṣighbī informed us; Sahl ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us; Abū Ghassān Mālik ibn Ismāʿīl al-Nahdī narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated from Umm Ḥumayd bint al-ʿAyzār, from her mother Umm ʿAfār, from Thumāma bint Shawwāl, who said: ""I asked the Mothers of the Believers — ʿĀʾisha, Ḥafṣa, and Umm Salama, may Allah be pleased with them — what is permissible for a woman from her husband's household?"" Each of them picked up a stick from the ground and said: ""No, not even the weight of this, except with his permission."" [Very Weak]"
7858,"(7855) Abū al-Ḥasan al-Muqrīʾ informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Isfarāʾinī narrated to us; Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Abī Bakr narrated to us; Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd narrated from Ziyād ibn Lāḥiq, who said: Tamīma bint Salama narrated to me that she came to ʿĀʾisha along with some women of the people of Kūfa. She said: ""A woman among us asked her: 'May a woman take something from her husband's household without his permission?' She became angry and frowned, and she rebuked her for what she had said. She said: 'Do not steal from it gold or silver, and do not take anything from his household.' And she mentioned the hadith."" [Ḥasan]"
7859,"(7856) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us; Shuraḥbīl ibn Muslim al-Khawlānī narrated, having heard Abā Umāma say: ""I was present with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during the Farewell Pilgrimage, and I heard him say…"" and he mentioned the hadith, which includes: ""Beware! It is not permissible for a woman to give anything from her husband's wealth except with his permission."" A man said: ""O Messenger of Allah, not even food?"" He said: ""That is the best of our wealth."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7860,"(7857) Layth ibn Abī Sulaym narrated from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet ﷺ, regarding the right of the husband over his wife, that he said: ""She shall not give anything from his household except with his permission. If she does so, the reward is for him and the sin is upon her.""
Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed us of it; ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us; Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us; Abū Dāwūd narrated to us; Jarīr narrated from Layth, and he mentioned it. [Weak. Narrated by al-Ṭayālisī]"
7861,"(7858) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us; al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān narrated to us; Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shayba narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated from Muḥammad ibn Zayd, from ʿUmayr, the freedman of Abī al-Laḥm, who said: ""I was a slave, and I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ: 'May I give charity from the wealth of my masters?' He said: 'Yes, and the reward between you is equal.'""
Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shayba and others. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
7862,"(7859) Abū Ṣāliḥ, the son of the daughter of Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr the Qāḍī, informed us; my grandfather narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Salama narrated to us; Qutayba ibn Saʿīd al-Thaqafī narrated to us; Ḥātim, meaning Ibn Ismāʿīl al-Madanī, narrated from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd, who said: ""I heard ʿUmayr, the freedman of Abī al-Laḥm, say: 'My master ordered me to cut up meat. A poor man came to me, so I fed him from it. My master came to know of that and beat me. So I went to the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and mentioned that to him. He called him and said: ""Why did you beat him?"" He said: ""He gives my food without my command."" He said: ""The reward is between you both.""'""
Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutayba. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
7863,"(7860) The Imam Abū al-Fatḥ al-ʿUmarī informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Shuraīḥ informed us; Abū al-Qāsim al-Baghawī narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn al-Jaʿd informed us; Ibn Abī Dhiʾb narrated from Dirham, who said: ""My master imposed upon me one dirham every day. So I came to Abū Hurayra, and he said: 'Fear Allah, and fulfill the right of Allah upon you and the right of your masters, for you do not own anything of your wealth or your own blood, except that you place your hand upon an orphan or feed a morsel to a poor person.'""
Among those from whom it was narrated that he permitted him to give something small in charity from his wealth were: Abū Hurayra, Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, Saʿīd ibn Jubayr, al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī, al-Shaʿbī, al-Nakhaʿī, al-Zuhrī, and Makḥūl — except that Makḥūl qualified it by saying that he does so to seek atonement thereby. [Weak. Narrated by Ibn al-Ḥuṣr]"
7864,"(7861) Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us; Abū Muḥammad ibn Ḥayyān narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us; Abū ʿĀmir narrated to us; al-Walīd ibn Muslim narrated to me; ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Ghanīya narrated from al-Ḥakam ibn ʿUtayba, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī al-Hudhayl, who said: ""The people of Kūfa wrote with me some questions to ask Ibn ʿAbbās about. So I sat before him, and a slave came to him and said: 'O Ibn ʿAbbās, I tend sheep for my family. A thirsty person passes by me — may I give him water?' He said: 'No, and then not except with the command of your family.' He said: 'I fear death upon him.' He said: 'Then give him water, and inform your family of that.'"" [Weak]"
7865,"(7862) Abū Manṣūr al-Faqīh, Abū Naṣr ibn Qatāda, and Abū al-Qāsim ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAlī ibn Ḥamdān al-Fārisī said: Abū ʿAmr ibn Nujayd al-Sulamī informed us; Abū Muslim narrated to us; al-Anṣārī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muslim narrated to us; ʿAṭāʾ narrated from Ibn ʿAbbās: ""He was asked about a slave giving something in charity, and he said: 'Allah has set forth a parable: a slave who has no power over anything' — he cannot give anything in charity, except that he be a shepherd of camels, and a man comes to him whose throat has dried up from thirst, and he fears that if he does not give him water he will die — then he may give him water.""
He said: al-Anṣārī narrated to us; Ismāʿīl narrated to us; ʿAṭāʾ narrated from Jābir: ""He was asked about a slave — may he give something in charity? He said: 'He cannot give anything in charity.'"" [Weak]"
7866,"(7863) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us; Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Karīm ibn al-Haytham narrated to us; Abū al-Yamān informed us; Shuʿayb narrated. Nāfiʿ said: ""ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: 'It is not permissible for a slave to spend anything of his wealth or to give it to anyone except with the permission of his master, unless he eats in a reasonable manner or clothes himself.'""
The musnad hadith may be interpreted remotely to mean that the intention of the Prophet ﷺ was to encourage the master to permit his slave to give charity on his behalf, with the reward being shared between them. What its outward indication suggests of permissibility is more appropriate for one who aspires to follow the Sunnah. And with Allah lies success. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy]"
7867,"(7864) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us from his original source: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated to us, from Hishām ibn ʿUrwah, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""That one of you should take his rope, go to the mountain, bring a bundle of firewood on his back, sell it, and thereby become self-sufficient is better for him than asking people, whether they give him or deny him."" al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Mūsā, from Wakīʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7868,"(7865) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: Yaʿlā ibn ʿUbayd narrated to us: Ismāʿīl narrated to us, from Qays.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad, Muḥammad ibn al-Naḍr, and Aḥmad ibn Salamah narrated to us. Muḥammad said: ""informed us,"" and the other two said: ""narrated to us"": Hannād ibn al-Sarī narrated to us: Abū al-Aḥwaṣ narrated to us, from Bayān Abī Bishr, from Qays ibn Abī Ḥāzim, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""That one of you should go out in the morning, gather firewood on his back, give charity from it, and thereby become self-sufficient of people is better for him than asking a man, whether he gives him or denies him. That is because the upper hand is better than the lower hand, and begin with those whom you support.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Hannād ibn al-Sarī, and he also narrated it from the ḥadīth of Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān from Ismāʿīl. al-Bukhārī narrated it from the ḥadīth of al-Aʿraj, from the ḥadīth of Abī Ṣāliḥ, and others, from Abū Hurayrah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7869,"(7866) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʔUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: al-Asfāṭī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl narrated to us: Mālik narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū ʿAmr al-Mustamlī and Muḥammad ibn Shādhān said: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us, from Mālik ibn Anas, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yazīd, from Abū Saʿīd al-Khudrī, that a group of the Anṣār asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, and he gave them. Then they asked him again, and he gave them, until when what he had with him was exhausted, he said: ""Whatever good I have, I will not withhold it from you. Whoever seeks chastity, Allah will make him chaste. Whoever seeks self-sufficiency, Allah will make him self-sufficient. Whoever is patient, Allah will grant him patience. And no gift given to anyone is better or more expansive than patience."" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Qutaybah.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdullāh ibn Yūsuf, from Mālik, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7870,"(7867) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq al-Faqīh informed us: ʿUbayd ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid narrated to us: Ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar ibn Abī Kathīr narrated to us: Sharīk ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Namir narrated to us, from ʿAṭāʾ ibn Yasār and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī ʿAmrah, that they both heard Abū Hurayrah say: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The poor person (miskīn) is not the one who is turned away by one or two dates, or by one or two morsels. Rather, the poor person is the one who restrains himself (from asking). Recite, if you wish: {They do not ask people persistently}.""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam, and Muslim narrated it from Abī Bakr ibn Isḥāq, from Ibn Abī Maryam. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7871,"(7868) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Khashnām ibn al-Ṣiddīq narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Yazīd al-Muqriʾ narrated to us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Ayyūb narrated to us: Sharḥabīl ibn Sharīk narrated to us, from Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ḥublī, from ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAmr ibn al-ʿĀṣ, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Indeed, prosperous is the one who embraced Islam, was provided with sufficient sustenance, and Allah made him content with what He gave him.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, from al-Muqriʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7872,"(7869) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī informed us: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbdullāh informed us: Bashīr ibn Salmān narrated to us, from Sayyār, from Ṭāriq, from Ibn Masʿūd, who said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever is afflicted by poverty and presents it to people, his poverty will not be relieved. And whoever presents it to Allah, Allah will soon grant him wealth, either through an imminent death or through imminent wealth."" [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7873,"(7870) Abū al-Ḥusayn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān in Baghdād informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar ibn Durustawayh narrated to us: Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us: al-Muʿallā ibn Asad narrated to us: Wahb narrated to us, from al-Nuʿmān ibn Rāshid, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muslim, the brother of al-Zuhrī, from Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿUmar, who said: We set out for al-Shām to beg. When we arrived in al-Madīnah, Ibn ʿUmar said to us: ""Did you come to al-Shām to beg? Indeed, I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: 'Begging will continue to be a man's practice until he meets Allah with not a shred of flesh on his face.'""

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ and said: ""al-Muʿallā ibn Asad mentioned it."" And Muslim narrated it in an abridged form from the ḥadīth of Maʿmar, from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muslim. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7874,"(7871) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq said: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaydalānī and Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Qabbānī said: Abū Kurayb narrated to us: Ibn Fuḍayl narrated to us, from ʿUmārah ibn al-Qaʿqāʿ, from Abī Zurʿah, from Abī Hurayrah, who said: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever asks people for their wealth merely to accumulate is asking for a burning coal; let him take little of it or much.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abī Kurayb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7875,"(7872) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us: ʿAbbās al-Dūrī narrated to us: Shabbābah narrated to us: Warqāʾ narrated to us, from ʿAmr ibn Dīnār.

(Ḥ) And Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā al-Muzakkī narrated to us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Shādhān narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from ʿAmr, meaning Ibn Dīnār, from Wahb ibn Munabbih, from his brother, who said: I heard Muʿāwiyah say: the Messenger of Allah ﷺ say: ""Do not be persistent in begging. By Allah, none of you asks me for anything, and I give it to him while I am reluctant, but that blessing is placed in it for him.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Sufyān. Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Ibn Numayr, from Sufyān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7876,"(7873) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm al-Marwazī narrated to us: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us: ʿAbdān informed us: ʿAbdullāh informed us: Yūnus narrated to us, from al-Zuhrī, from ʿUrwah and Ibn al-Musayyab, that Ḥakīm ibn Ḥizām said: I asked the Messenger of Allah ﷺ and he gave me. Then I asked him and he gave me. Then I asked him and he gave me. Then he said: ""O Ḥakīm, indeed this wealth is green and sweet. Whoever takes it with a generous soul will be blessed in it, and whoever takes it with a covetous soul will not be blessed in it, like one who eats but is never satisfied. The upper hand is better than the lower hand."" Ḥakīm said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, by the One who sent you with the truth, I will not ask anyone for anything after you until I depart this world."" He said: And Abū Bakr used to call Ḥakīm to give him gifts, but he would refuse to accept anything from him. Then ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, called him to give him something, but he refused to accept anything from him. So ʿUmar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: ""I bear witness to you, O company of Muslims, concerning Ḥakīm, that I offered him his share of this fayʾ, but he refused to take it."" So Ḥakīm did not ask anyone among the people for anything after the Messenger of Allah ﷺ until he passed away.

al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAbdān, and Muslim narrated it in an abridged form from the ḥadīth of Ibn ʿUyaynah, from al-Zuhrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7877,"(7874) Yahya ibn Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Yahya informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us. (H) And Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah ibn Ya'qub and Abu 'Amr ibn Abi Ja'far informed us, both saying: 'Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Shirawayh narrated to us: Salamah ibn Shabib and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman both narrated to us: Marwan ibn Muhammad narrated to us: Sa'id ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated from Rabi'ah ibn Yazid from Abu Idris al-Khawlani from Abu Muslim al-Khawlani, who said: The beloved and trustworthy one narrated to me — as for his being beloved, he was beloved to me, and as for his being trustworthy, he was trustworthy in my estimation — 'Awf ibn Malik al-Ashja'i, who said: We were in the presence of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) — nine, or eight, or seven of us — and he said: ""Will you not pledge allegiance to the Messenger of Allah?"" We were recently new to having given a pledge, so we said: ""We have already pledged to you, O Messenger of Allah."" Then he said again: ""Will you not pledge allegiance to the Messenger of Allah?"" So we said: ""We have already pledged to you, O Messenger of Allah."" Then he said once more: ""Will you not pledge allegiance to the Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""So we extended our hands and said: 'We have already pledged to you, O Messenger of Allah — so upon what shall we pledge to you?'"" He said: ""(Pledge) that you worship Allah and associate nothing with Him, and (perform) the five prayers, and that you obey"" — and he uttered a word quietly — ""and that you do not ask people for anything."" He said: ""Indeed, some of that group would have his whip fall while he was (riding) and he would not ask anyone to hand it to him."" This is the wording of the hadith of al-Hafiz. Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Salamah ibn Shabib and 'Abd Allah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Darimi. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7878,"(7875) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: Abu Bakr al-Qattan informed us: Ibrahim ibn al-Harith narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Bukayr narrated to us: Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated from Muhammad ibn Qays from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Mu'awiyah from Thawban, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever accepts one (condition) on my behalf, I shall accept Paradise on his behalf."" Thawban said: ""Then I (accept), O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Do not ask people for anything."" He said: ""And often Thawban's whip would fall while he was on his camel, and he would not say to anyone, 'Hand it to me,' until he would dismount and take it himself."" It is also narrated from Abu al-'Aliyah from Thawban. [Sahih. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
7879,"(7876) Abu Bakr ibn Furak informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far narrated to us: Yunus ibn Habib narrated to us: Abu Dawud narrated to us: Shu'bah narrated from 'Abd al-Malik ibn 'Umayr from Zayd ibn 'Uqbah from Samurah that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Asking (for things) are scratches by which a man scars his face. Whoever wishes may preserve his face, and whoever wishes may leave it — except that a man asks in a matter from which he has no recourse, or (he asks) one in authority."" Zayd ibn 'Uqbah said: ""So I narrated it to al-Hajjaj ibn Yusuf, and he said: 'Then ask me, for I am one in authority.'"" [Sahih. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
7880,"(7877) 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd al-Saffar informed us: Ibn Milhan narrated to us: Yahya, meaning Ibn Bukayr, narrated to us: al-Layth narrated from Ja'far ibn Rabi'ah from Bakr ibn Sawadah from Muslim ibn Makhshi, who said: Ibn al-Farasi informed me that al-Farasi said to the Prophet (ﷺ): ""Shall I ask (people for things), O Prophet of Allah?"" He said: ""No. And if you must ask, then ask the righteous."" Abu Dawud narrated it from Qutaybah from al-Layth. [Da'if. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
7881,"(7878) And Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us: 'Ali ibn Ibrahim ibn Mu'awiyah al-Naysaburi informed us: Muhammad ibn Muslim ibn Warah narrated to me: Muhammad ibn Musa ibn A'yan said: ""I found in my father's book from 'Amr ibn al-Harith from Bakr from Muslim ibn Makhshi that al-Farasi narrated to him from his father that he said to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ): 'O Messenger of Allah, shall I ask (people for things)?' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'No. And if you must ask, then ask the righteous.'""

And among the hadiths of Qabisa ibn Mukhariq and others regarding for whom asking is permissible — and the place for that is the Book of the Division of Charitable Gifts. [Da'if. See the preceding.]"
7882,"(7879) Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us: Isma'il ibn Ishaq al-Qadi narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Maslamah al-Qa'nabi narrated from Malik. (H) And Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq al-Muzzi informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub ibn Yusuf narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Shadhan narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Sa'id narrated from Malik from Nafi' from 'Abd Allah ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said — while he was upon the pulpit, mentioning charity and abstaining from begging: ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand. The upper hand is the one that gives in self-sufficiency, and the lower hand is the one that asks.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Qa'nabi, and Muslim narrated it from Qutaybah. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7883,"(7880) And Abu 'Abd Allah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu 'Abd Allah 'Ali ibn 'Abd Allah al-'Attar, companion of al-Hakimi, in Baghdad, informed us: al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad al-Dawri narrated to us: 'Arim, Abu al-Nu'man, narrated to us: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyub from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) delivering a sermon: 'The upper hand is better than the lower hand. The upper hand is the one that spends, and the lower hand is the one that asks.'""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from 'Arim. And 'Abd al-Warith narrated it from Ayyub, and he said in the hadith: ""The upper hand is the one that abstains (al-muta'affifah).""

And likewise Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated it from Musa ibn 'Uqbah from Nafi': ""al-muta'affifah."" [Sahih. See the preceding.]"
7884,"(7881) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us: Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Husayn al-Qattan informed us: 'Ali ibn al-Hasan ibn Abi 'Isa narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn al-Walid al-'Adani narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated to me: Musa ibn 'Uqbah narrated from Nafi' from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""The upper hand is better than the lower hand. The upper hand is the one that abstains (al-muta'affifah), and the lower hand is the one that asks."" [Sahih. See the preceding.]"
7885,"(7882) And Hafs ibn Maynarah narrated it from Musa ibn 'Uqbah, and it was said from him: ""The upper hand is the one that spends (al-munfiqah)."" And 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us: Ahmad ibn 'Ubayd informed us: Ibn Mihran, meaning al-Hasan ibn al-'Abbas ibn Mihran, narrated to us: Suwayd, meaning Ibn Sa'id, narrated to us: Hafs narrated from Musa, and he mentioned it. [Sahih. See the preceding.]"
7886,"(7883) Abu al-Hasan ibn 'Abdan informed us: Sulayman ibn Ahmad informed us: Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us: Ibn Kathir narrated to us: Sufyan narrated from 'Abd Allah ibn Dinar from Ibn 'Umar, who said: ""We used to say that the upper hand is the one that spends."" [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7887,"(7884) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh, Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq, and Abū al-ʿAbbās — Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad al-Shādhīyākhī and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr — narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥakam informed us: Bishr ibn Bakr reported to us from Ibn Jābir, from ʿUrwah ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAṭiyyah, who said: My father narrated to me that his father informed him, saying: I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) among a group of Banū Saʿd ibn Bakr, and I was the youngest of the people. They left me behind with their belongings, then they went to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and took care of their needs. Then he asked: ""Is there anyone left among you?"" They said: ""O Messenger of Allah, a boy from among us whom we left behind with our belongings."" So he commanded them to send me to him, and they brought me to him. When he saw me, he said: ""May Allah enrich you! Do not ask the people for anything, for the upper hand is the giving hand, and the lower hand is the asking hand. And indeed, the wealth of Allah will be asked about and given."" He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) spoke to me in our dialect."
7888,"(7885) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī informed us orally. Abū Saʿīd Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Ziyād al-Baṣrī reported to us in Makkah. Al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us. ʿAbīdah ibn Ḥumayd narrated to me. Abū al-Zuʿrāʾ narrated to me from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, from his father Mālik ibn Naḍlah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The hands are three: the Hand of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, is the uppermost; the hand of the giver which follows it; and the hand of the asker is the lowest. So give the surplus and do not stint regarding yourself.""

Ibrāhīm al-Hajarī also narrated it from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, both as a marfūʿ and mawqūf narration."
7889,"(7886) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us. Abū ʿAlī Ismāʿīl ibn Muḥammad al-Ṣaffār narrated to us. Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us. ʿAlī ibn ʿĀṣim informed us. Ibrāhīm al-Hajarī reported from Abī al-Aḥwaṣ, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The hands are three: the Hand of Allah is the uppermost; the hand of the giver follows it; and the hand of the asker is the lowest until the Day of Resurrection. So guard against asking as much as you are able. And whomever Allah grants goodness, let it be seen upon him. Begin with those whom you support. Give from the surplus, and do not reproach one who is self-sufficient, and do not stint regarding yourself.""

Ibrāhīm ibn Ṭahmān followed it up from al-Hajarī as a marfūʿ narration. And Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn narrated it from Ibrāhīm al-Hajarī as a mawqūf narration."
7890,"(7887) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us. Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us. ʿUbayd ibn Sharīk narrated to us. Yaḥyā, meaning Ibn Bukayr, narrated to us. Al-Layth reported from Yūnus, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, who said: ʿAbd Allāh said: I heard ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāh (may Allah be pleased with him) say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would give me a stipend, and I would say: ""Give it to one who is more in need than me."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Take it. Whatever comes to you of this wealth without you coveting it or asking for it, then take it. And if it does not come to that, then do not let your soul pursue it.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Yaḥyā ibn Bukayr, and Muslim narrated it through another route from Yūnus."
7891,"(7888) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānīʾ narrated to us. Sahl ibn Mihrān al-Baghdādī narrated to us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī narrated to us. Mubārak ibn Faḍālah reported from Thābit al-Bunānī, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Bakr, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Is there anyone among you who has fed a poor person today?"" Abū Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""I entered the mosque and there was a beggar asking. I found a piece of bread in the hand of ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, so I took it and gave it to him."""
7892,"(7889) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhabārī informed us. Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us. Abū Dāwūd narrated to us. Abū al-ʿAbbās al-Qalawrī — meaning ʿAmr ibn al-ʿAbbās, who used to live on the road of Khuzāʿah — narrated to us. Yaʿqūb ibn Isḥāq al-Ḥaḍramī narrated to us from Sulaymān ibn Muʿādh. Muḥammad ibn al-Munkadir narrated to us from Jābir (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not ask by the Countenance of Allah except for Paradise."""
7893,"(7890) Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrak informed us. ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us. Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us. Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us. Abū ʿAwanah reported from al-Aʿmash, from Mujāhid, from Ibn ʿUmar, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever seeks your protection by Allah, grant him protection. Whoever asks you by Allah, give him. Whoever invites you, accept his invitation. Whoever does a kindness to you, recompense him. And if you do not find anything with which to recompense him, then praise him until you know that you have indeed recompensed him."""
7894,"(7891) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī ibn Yazīd al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us. Abū Yaʿlā al-Mawṣilī narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated to us. Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar reported from Abī Mālik al-Ashjaʿī, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Islam is built upon five: the Oneness of Allah, the establishment of Ṣalāh, the giving of Zakāh, the fasting of Ramaḍān, and the Ḥajj.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr. And he added in it that a man said: ""The Ḥajj and the fasting of Ramaḍān?"" He said: ""No — the fasting of Ramaḍān and the Ḥajj. Thus I heard it from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)."""
7895,(7892) ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf informed us. Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā and Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd both said: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Sufyān narrated to us. Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us. Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated it with its addition.
7896,"(7893) Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-Daqqāq, known as Ibn al-Bayyāḍ, in Baghdad, narrated to us. Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān al-Khuraqī narrated to us. Abū Qilābah narrated to us. Abū Zayd al-Harawī narrated to us. Qurrah ibn Khālid reported from Abī Jamrah Naṣr ibn ʿImrān al-Ḍubaʿī, who said: I said to Ibn ʿAbbās: ""I have a jar of sweet nabīdh which I drink, and when I drink much of it and sit with the people, I prolong the gathering, fearing that I may be exposed."" He said to me: ""The delegation of ʿAbd al-Qays came, and he said: 'Welcome to the delegation, not the disgraced ones.' They said: 'O Messenger of Allah, between us and you are the disbelievers of Muḍar, and we cannot reach you except in a sacred month. So command us with an order that we may act upon it and call those behind us to it.' He said: 'I command you to faith. Do you know what faith is? It is the testimony that there is no deity except Allah and that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah, that you establish Ṣalāh, give Zakāh, fast Ramaḍān, and perform Ḥajj to the Sacred House.'"" He said: ""And I think he said: 'And that you give the khums from the war spoils. And I forbid you from drinking from the jar, the gourd vessel, the tarred vessel, and the hollowed stump.'""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Qurrah ibn Khālid."
7897,"(7894) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿUfayr narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn al-Rabīʿ al-Anṣārī narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Numayr narrated from al-Aʿmash: ʿAmr ibn Murrah narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā narrated to us: The Companions of Muḥammad (ﷺ) said: Fasting was prescribed in three stages. The people came to Madīnah and had no prior obligation of fasting. So they used to fast three days of every month until the month of Ramaḍān was made obligatory. They found this excessive and it became difficult upon them. Whoever was able to fast but chose to feed a poor person each day instead was permitted to do so, and this was abrogated by the verse: **""And that you fast is better for you, if only you knew""** (al-Baqarah:184). He said: Then they were commanded to fast. Al-Bukhārī said: Ibn Numayr said: al-Aʿmash narrated to us, and he mentioned part of its meaning in abridged form. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7898,"(7895) Abū Ṣāliḥ ibn Abī Ṭāhir al-ʿAnbarī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Manṣūr al-Qāḍī, my grandfather, narrated to us: ʿUmar ibn Ḥafṣ al-Sudūsī narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: al-Masʿūdī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Murrah narrated from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Abī Laylā from Muʿādh ibn Jabal (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: Fasting was prescribed in three stages, and he mentioned the hadith. He said: As for the state of fasting, when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to Madīnah, he began fasting three days of every month, and he fasted ʿĀshūrāʾ. He fasted for seventeen months, from the month of Rabīʿ to the month of Rabīʿ, until Ramaḍān. Then Allah, Blessed and Exalted, obligated the month of Ramaḍān upon him and revealed: **""Prescribed for you is fasting, as it was prescribed for those before you""** (al-Baqarah:183), and he mentioned the remainder of the hadith. This is a mursal narration; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān did not meet Muʿādh ibn Jabal. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
7899,"(7896) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr ibn Sābiq al-Khawlānī narrated to us: It was recited to ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb: ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith informed you from Bukayr ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn al-Ashajj from Yazīd ibn Abī ʿUbayd from Salamah ibn al-Akwʿ, who said: During the month of Ramaḍān in the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), whoever wished fasted and whoever wished broke his fast and gave a ransom of feeding a poor person, until the verse was revealed: **""So whoever among you witnesses the month, let him fast it""** (al-Baqarah:185). Muslim narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿAmr ibn Sawwād from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim]"
7900,"(7897) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū ʿAmr al-Mustamlī narrated to us: Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Bakr ibn Muḍar narrated from ʿAmr ibn al-Ḥārith from Bukayr from Yazīd, the freedman of Salamah ibn al-Akwʿ, from Salamah ibn al-Akwʿ, who said: When this verse was revealed — **""And upon those who are able [to fast, but with hardship] a ransom of feeding a poor person""** (al-Baqarah:184) — whoever among us wished would break his fast and give a ransom, until the verse after it was revealed which abrogated it. Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥ from Qutaybah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
7901,"(7898) Abū ʿAmr Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Adīb narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Ismāʿīlī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah and Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah both narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Thaqafī narrated from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: This verse — **""a ransom of feeding poor persons""** — was abrogated by the verse after it: **""So whoever among you witnesses the month, let him fast it.""** Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7902,"(7899) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd al-Nasawī narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Shākir narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us: ʿAyāsh narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Aʿlā narrated from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, that he recited: **""a ransom of feeding poor persons""** and said: It is abrogated. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding]"
7903,"(7900) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥamshādh narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥīm ibn ʿAmmār ibn Dunūqā narrated to us. (Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to me: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Jawharī in Baghdād narrated to us: Abū Jaʿfar Aḥmad ibn Mūsā al-Shaṭawī narrated to us. Both said: Muḥammad ibn Sābiq narrated to us: Isrāʾīl narrated from Abī Isḥāq from al-Barāʾ, who said: A man from among the Companions of Muḥammad (ﷺ), when he was fasting and the time for breaking the fast arrived but he fell asleep before breaking his fast, would not eat that night nor that day until evening. Qayṣ ibn Ṣirmah was fasting, and when the time for breaking the fast came, he went to his wife and said: Do you have any food? She said: No, but let me go and seek some — and he had been working that day on his land. Sleep overcame him, and his wife came. When she saw him, she said: What a loss for you! He woke up, and before the day was half gone, he fainted. She mentioned that to the Prophet (ﷺ), and this verse was revealed: **""Permitted for you on the night of the fasting is intimacy with your wives. They are a garment for you and you are a garment for them""** (al-Baqarah:187). They rejoiced greatly at it. **""And eat and drink until the white thread of dawn becomes distinct to you from the black thread [of night]""** (al-Baqarah:187). The wording is from the ḥadīth of al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥamshādh. Al-Bukhārī narrated it in his Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd Allāh ibn Mūsā from Isrāʾīl. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7904,"(7901) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Thābit ibn Shabbūyah narrated to me: ʿAlī ibn Ḥusayn ibn Wāqid narrated from his father from Yazīd al-Naḥwī from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, regarding the verse: **""O you who believe, prescribed for you is fasting, as it was prescribed for those before you""** (al-Baqarah:183). The people, in the time of the Prophet (ﷺ), when they prayed al-ʿAtamah (ʿIshāʾ), food, drink, and women became forbidden upon them, and they would fast until the next evening. A man betrayed himself and had intercourse with his wife after having prayed ʿIshāʾ without breaking his fast. Allah, the Mighty and Majestic, wished to make this an ease for those who remained, a concession and a benefit. So He said: **""Allah knew that you were betraying yourselves, so He turned to you and pardoned you""** (al-Baqarah:187). This was among the things by which Allah benefited the people, granting them ease and facilitation. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7905,"(7902) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn Marzūq narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated from ʿAmr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard Ibn Abī Laylā, and he mentioned the hadith. He said: Our companions narrated to us that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to Madīnah, he commanded them to fast three days. Then Ramaḍān was revealed. They were a people unaccustomed to fasting, and fasting was difficult upon them. Whoever did not fast would feed sixty poor persons. Then this verse was revealed: **""So whoever among you witnesses the month, let him fast it""** (al-Baqarah:185). The concession remained for the sick and the traveler, and they were commanded to fast. He said: Our companions also narrated that when a man broke his fast and then fell asleep before eating, he would not eat until the next morning. ʿUmar came and desired his wife, but she said: I have fallen asleep. He thought she was making an excuse, so he went to her. A man from the Anṣār came seeking food, and they said: Let us warm something for you, but he fell asleep. When they woke up, this verse was revealed concerning them: **""Permitted for you on the night of the fasting is intimacy with your wives""** (al-Baqarah:187). [Ṣaḥīḥ. Meaning of the preceding]"
7906,"(7903) Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad al-Zaʿfarānī narrated to us: ʿĀṣim ibn ʿAlī narrated to us: Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us: Nāfiʿ ibn Mālik narrated from his father from Ṭalḥah ibn ʿUbayd Allāh, that a Bedouin came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) with disheveled hair and said: O Messenger of Allah, tell me what Allah has obligated upon me of prayer. He said: **""The five prayers, unless you wish to volunteer something.""** He said: Tell me what Allah has obligated upon me of fasting. He said: **""The fasting of the month of Ramaḍān, unless you wish to volunteer something.""** He said: Tell me what Allah has obligated me of zakāh. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) informed him of the laws of Islam. He said: By the One who honored you, I shall not volunteer anything, nor shall I diminish anything that Allah has obligated upon me. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: **""He has succeeded, by his father, if he is truthful. He will enter Paradise, by Allah, if he is truthful.""** Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in their Ṣaḥīḥayn from Qutaybah from Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī]"
7907,"(7904) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us, Ibn Nājiyah informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Maʿshar informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿid al-Mālinī and Abū Manṣūr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī al-Dāmghānī both informed us, saying: Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿAdī informed us, ʿAlī ibn Saʿīd informed us, Muḥammad ibn Abī Maʿshar informed us, my father narrated to me, from Saʿīd al-Maqburī, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not say 'Ramadan' alone, for indeed Ramadan is one of the names of Allah. Rather, say 'the month of Ramadan.'""

Al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Khāzin similarly narrated it from Abī Maʿshar.

Abū Maʿshar is Najīḥ al-Sindī — Yaḥyā ibn Maʿīn declared him weak, and Yaḥyā al-Qaṭṭān would not narrate from him, whereas ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī would narrate from him. And Allah knows best.

It has also been narrated from Abī Maʿshar, from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb, as his own statement, and that is more likely. [Munkar. Narrated by Ibn ʿAdī.]"
7908,"(7905) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Fanjawayh al-Dīnawarī informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf ibn Aḥmad ibn Mālik informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz al-Baghawī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Bakkār ibn al-Rayyān informed us, Abū Maʿshar narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Kaʿb, who said: ""Do not say 'Ramadan,' for indeed Ramadan is one of the names of Allah, the Mighty and Majestic. Rather, say 'the month of Ramadan.'""

That has also been narrated from Mujāhid and al-Ḥasan al-Baṣrī, though the chain to them is weak. Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bukhārī, in his Ṣaḥīḥ, cited evidence permitting the unrestricted use of the term. [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by Ibn Ḥātim.]"
7909,"(7906) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us.

(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hānī informed us, both saying: Muḥammad ibn Nuʿaym informed us, Qutaybah informed us, Ismāʿīl ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us, from Abū Suhayl ibn Mālik, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When Ramadan arrives, the gates of Paradise are opened, the gates of the Fire are closed, and the devils are chained up.""

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim both narrated it in the two Ṣaḥīḥs from Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd. Al-Bukhārī said: And the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever fasts Ramadan…"" and he said: ""Do not precede Ramadan [by fasting]."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7910,"(7907) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī informed us, Ibn Abī Maryam informed us, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb informed me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr narrated to me, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, from Ḥafṣah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever does not resolve to fast before dawn, there is no fast for him.""

It was also narrated by ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb, from Ibn Lahīʿah and Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7911,"(7908) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr both informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb informed me, Ibn Lahīʿah and Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to me, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr ibn ʿAmr ibn Ḥazm, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, from his father, from Ḥafṣah, the wife of the Prophet (ﷺ), that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever does not resolve to fast together with the dawn, there is no fast for him."" Thus he said.

It was narrated by Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ from Ibn Wahb, and he said: ""before dawn.""

This is a hadith over which there is disagreement regarding al-Zuhrī — both in its chain of narration and in its attribution to the Prophet (ﷺ). ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr established its chain and raised it [to the Prophet], and he is among the reliable and precise narrators. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
7912,"(7909) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, saying: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Abī Bakr raised it, and he is among the reliable narrators who raise [hadiths to the Prophet].

Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn ibn Dāwūd al-ʿAlawī and Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — by dictation and by reading to them — both saying: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān informed us, Abū al-Azhar informed us, ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us, Ibn Jurayj informed us, from Ibn Shihāb, from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, from Ḥafṣah, that the Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Whoever does not make the intention to fast during the night, there is no fast for him.""

Maʿmar narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from his father, from Ḥafṣah, as her own statement. And it was said from him: from al-Zuhrī, from Ḥamzah ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from his father, from Ḥafṣah.

Yūnus narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, from Ibn ʿUmar, as his own statement. And ʿUqayl narrated it from al-Zuhrī, from Sālim, that ʿAbd Allāh and Ḥafṣah both said that. And it was said otherwise. And Mālik narrated it as well. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
7913,"(7910) Abū Aḥmad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-ʿAdl informed us, Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Muzakkī informed us, Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-ʿAbdī informed us, Ibn Bukayr informed us, Mālik narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: ""No one's fast is valid except the one who resolved to fast before dawn."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7914,"(7911) He said: And Mālik narrated to us, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿĀʾishah and Ḥafṣah (may Allah be pleased with both of them), the same as that. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Mālik.]"
7915,"(7912) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū Jaʿfar al-Baghdādī in Naysābūr informed us, Abū al-Zinbāʿ Rūḥ ibn al-Faraj al-Miṣrī informed me, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbād informed us, al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah informed me, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated to me, from Yaḥyā ibn Saʿīd al-Anṣārī, from ʿAmrah bint ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Saʿ ibn Zurārah, from ʿĀʾishah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""Whoever does not make the intention to fast before the rising of dawn, there is no fast for him.""

Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh informed us, saying: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Dāraquṭnī said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbād is the sole narrator of this from al-Mufaḍḍal with this chain, and all of them are reliable. [Munkar. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
7916,"(7913) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed me, Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us, Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Naṣr al-Imām informed us, Abū Kāmil al-Fuḍayl ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Jaḥdarī informed us, ʿAbd al-Wāḥid ibn Ziyād informed us, Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿUbayd Allāh informed us, ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah narrated to me, from ʿĀʾishah, the Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to me one day: ""O ʿĀʾishah, do you have anything [to eat]?"" She said: I said: ""O Messenger of Allah, we have nothing."" He said: ""Then I am fasting."" And he mentioned the hadith.

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū Kāmil. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7917,"(7914) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us — Sahl ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us — Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah al-Baṣrī narrated to us — Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to us. And Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Faqīh in Ṭabarān informed us — Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh narrated to us — ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us — Sufyān narrated from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā, from ʿĀʾishah bint Ṭalḥah, from ʿĀʾishah Umm al-Muʾminīn, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, who said: The Prophet of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, was fond of a certain food. One day he came and said: ""Do you have any of that food?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Then I am fasting.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Muḥammad ibn Kathīr. In the narration of Rūḥ, she said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, used to come to us and say: ""Do you have any lunch?"" If I said: ""No,"" he would say: ""Then I am fasting.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See before it.]"
7918,"(7915) And Wakīʿ ibn al-Jarrāḥ narrated it from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā, and he said in the ḥadīth: She said: The Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, came to me one day and said: ""Do you have anything?"" We said: ""No."" He said: ""Then I am fasting.""

And with this wording, Muslim extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ.

Abū Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf informed us — Abū Aḥmad ibn ʿĪsā and Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yazīd narrated to us — they said: Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Muslim ibn al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us — Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah narrated to us — Wakīʿ narrated, and he mentioned it.

And Yaʿlī ibn ʿUbayd narrated similarly from Ṭalḥah ibn Yaḥyā: ""Then I am fasting.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Extracted by Muslim.]"
7919,"(7916) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Fūrāk narrated to us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb narrated to us — Abū Dāwūd narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Muʿādh narrated from Simāk, from ʿIkrimah, from ʿĀʾishah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhā, who said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, came to me one day and said: ""Do you have anything?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Then I shall fast.""

And this is a sound (ṣaḥīḥ) chain of narration.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Nasāʾī.]"
7920,"(7917) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us — Sahl ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us — Rūḥ narrated to us — Shuʿbah narrated from Qatādah, from Anas ibn Mālik: that Abū Ṭalḥah used to come to his family at mid-morning and say: ""Do you have any lunch?"" If they said: ""No,"" he would fast that day and say: ""Then I am fasting.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Extracted by Ibn Shaybah.]"
7921,"(7918) Abū Naṣr ibn Qatādah and Abū al-Ḥasan ibn Abī al-Maʿrūf al-Faqīh informed us — they said: Abū ʿAmr ibn Najīd informed us — Abū Muslim narrated to us — Abū ʿĀṣim narrated from Ibn Abī Dhʾb, from ʿUthmān ibn Najīḥ, from Ibn al-Musayyib, who said: I saw Abū Hurayrah making rounds in the market, then coming to his family and saying: ""Do you have anything?"" If they said: ""No,"" he would say: ""Then I am fasting.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Extracted by Abū Nuʿaym.]"
7922,"(7919) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us — ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar narrated to us — Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us — Ḥammād, meaning Ibn Zayd, narrated from Ayyūb, from Abī Qilābah, who said: Umm al-Dardāʾ narrated to me that Abū al-Dardāʾ used to come after the morning and say: ""Do you have any lunch?"" If he did not find any, he would say: ""Then I am fasting.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Its narrators are trustworthy (thiqāt).]"
7923,"(7920) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us — Abū Ṭāhir al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us — Abū Qilābah narrated to us — Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us — Sufyān narrated.

(Ḥ) And Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us — al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī reported it as a narration from Bishr ibn al-Sarī and others, from Sufyān al-Thawrī, from al-Aʿmash, from Ṭalḥah ibn Muṣarrif, from Saʿd ibn ʿUbaydah, from Abī ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī: that Ḥudhayfah, raḍiya Allāhu ʿanhu, the fasting became clear to him after the sun had passed its zenith, so he fasted.

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this narration. Extracted by al-Ṭabarī.]"
7924,"(7921) And Abū Saʿīd narrated to us — Abū al-ʿAbbās informed us — al-Rabīʿ said: al-Shāfiʿī said, narrating from Abī Muʿāwiyah, from al-Aʿmash, from ʿUmārah, from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Yazīd, from ʿAbd Allāh, who is Ibn Masʿūd, who said: ""Each of you has the option (to eat or fast) so long as he has not eaten or drunk.""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""They, meaning the Iraqis, do not hold this view. They claim that one is not considered fasting until he makes the intention to fast before the zawāl (passing of the sun from the zenith). As for us, we say: the one who voluntarily fasts may make the intention to fast whenever he wishes.""

[Ḍaʿīf. Extracted by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
7925,"(7922) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, that is al-Shaybānī, narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Shādān al-Aṣamm narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr narrated to us — Ismāʿīl narrated from Ayyūb.

(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī narrated to us — Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Naṣr and Jaʿfar ibn Muḥammad narrated to us — they said: Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā narrated to us, he said: I read before Mālik, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, from the Prophet, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, that he mentioned Ramaḍān and said: ""Do not fast until you see the new moon, and do not break the fast until you see it. If it is obscured over you, then estimate it.""

In the narration of al-Qaʿnabī: that the Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, mentioned Ramaḍān and said: ""If it is obscured over you…""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, and Muslim narrated it from Yaḥyā ibn Yaḥyā.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Extracted by al-Bukhārī.]"
7926,"(7923) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb, that is al-Shaybānī, narrated to us — Muḥammad ibn Shādān al-Aṣamm narrated to us — ʿAlī ibn Ḥujr narrated to us — Ismāʿīl narrated from Ayyūb.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Muqriʾ informed us — al-Ḥasan ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq narrated to us — Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us — Sulaymān ibn Ḥarb narrated to us — Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated from Ayyūb, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The Messenger of Allah, ṣallallāhu ʿalayhi wa sallam, said: ""The month is only twenty-nine days, so do not fast until you see it, and do not break the fast until you see it. If it is obscured over you, then estimate it.""

Ḥammād added in his narration from Ayyūb: Nāfiʿ said: When twenty-nine days of Shaʿbān had passed, the new moon would be looked for. If it was seen, then that was it. If it was not seen, and no clouds or haze obstructed the sighting, he would wake up in a state of non-fasting. If clouds or haze obstructed the sighting, he would wake up fasting. He would break the fast with the people and would not resort to this calculation.

He said: And Ibn ʿAwn said: I mentioned the practice of Ibn ʿUmar to Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, and it did not please him.

Muslim extracted it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Zuhayr ibn Ḥarb from Ismāʿīl ibn ʿUlayyah, without the practice of Ibn ʿUmar."
7927,"(7924) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us that Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman narrated to us that 'Abdullah ibn Wahb informed me that Yunus reported from Ibn Shihab, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break your fast. And if it is obscured from you, then estimate it for it.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it from the hadith of 'Uqayl from al-Zuhri, and Muslim narrated it from Harmalah from Ibn Wahb.

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7928,"(7925) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Ahmad ibn 'Abdan informed us that Ahmad ibn 'Ubaid al-Saffar narrated to us that Ahmad ibn 'Abdullah al-Narsi narrated to us that Rawh ibn 'Ubadah narrated to us that Malik reported from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar, from Ibn 'Umar, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The month is twenty-nine days. Do not fast until you see it, and do not break your fast until you see it. And if it is obscured from you, then estimate it for it.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from al-Qa'nabi from Malik, except that he said: ""Then complete the count as thirty."" Thus I found it in my copy.

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7929,"(7926) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and others informed us, saying: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us that al-Shafi'i informed us that Malik informed us, and he mentioned it with its like. And he said: ""And if it is obscured from you, then complete the count as thirty.""

The narration of the group from Malik is upon the first wording. And Malik narrated this hadith in al-Muwatta' upon the first wording, then he narrated after it his hadith from Thawr ibn Zayd al-Dili from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abbas that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mentioned Ramadan and mentioned the hadith, and said in it: ""And if it is obscured from you, then complete the count as thirty."""
7930,"(7927) Abu Ahmad al-Mihrajani informed us that Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Ibrahim narrated to us that Ibn Bukayr narrated it. It is as though he mentioned both hadiths together, and the scribe erred, so part of the text of the second hadith entered into the first chain of narration. Although the narration of al-Shafi'i and al-Qa'nabi from him on the authority of al-Bukhari is preserved, it is possible that Malik narrated it with both wordings together. And Allah knows best.

Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar something close to the first narration from Malik.

[Sahih. Narrated by Malik.]"
7931,"(7928) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, saying: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us that Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us that Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us that Isma'il ibn Ja'far reported from 'Abdullah ibn Dinar that he heard Ibn 'Umar say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The month is twenty-nine nights. Do not fast until you see it, and do not break your fast until you see it, unless it is obscured from you. And if it is obscured from you, then estimate it for it.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya.

[Sahih. This is the wording of Muslim.]"
7932,"(7929) Abu 'Ali al-Rudhbari informed us that Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated to us that Abu Dawud narrated to us that Humayd ibn Mas'adah narrated to us that 'Abd al-Wahhab narrated to me that Ayyub said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz wrote to the people of Basra: ""It has reached us that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said""—and he mentioned something similar to the hadith of Ibn 'Umar from the Prophet (ﷺ). He added: ""And the best way to estimate it is that we saw the crescent of Sha'ban on such and such a date, and fasting, if Allah wills, is on such and such a date, unless you see the crescent before that.""

The Sheikh said: What indicates the correctness of what 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz mentioned are the other narrations from the Prophet (ﷺ) in this chapter, among them from Ibn 'Umar.

[Sahih. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
7933,"(7930) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq informed us that Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn 'Abdullah informed us that Muhammad ibn 'Ubaid narrated to us that 'Asim ibn Muhammad reported from his father, from 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""The month is like this, and like this, and like this""—three times, with his hands. Then, on the third occasion, he tucked in his thumb. ""And if it is obscured from you, then complete thirty.""

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7934,"(7931) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us that Abu Hamid ibn Bilal narrated to us that Abu al-Azhar narrated to us that 'Abd al-Majid ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad narrated to us that my father narrated to us from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, Allah, Blessed and Exalted, has made the crescents markers of time. So when you see it, fast; and when you see it, break your fast. And if it is obscured from you, then estimate it—complete thirty.""

And among the established narrations in this chapter from other than Ibn 'Umar are those from Abu Hurayrah from the Prophet (ﷺ).

[Hasan. Narrated by Ibn Khuzaymah.]"
7935,"(7932) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me that 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi narrated to us that Ibrahim ibn al-Husayn narrated to us that Adam narrated to us that Shu'bah narrated to us that Muhammad ibn Ziyad narrated to me. He said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said—or Abu al-Qasim (ﷺ) said: ""Fast upon seeing it, and break your fast upon seeing it. And if the month is obscured from you, then count thirty days""—meaning: count Sha'ban as thirty.

Al-Bukhari narrated it in al-Sahih from Adam ibn Abi Iyas, except that he said in the hadith: ""And if it is obscured from you, then complete the count of Sha'ban as thirty.""

[Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7936,"(7933) Abu Zakariyya Ibrahim ibn Ibrahim informed us that Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani narrated to us that Yahya ibn Muhammad narrated to us that 'Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh ibn Mu'adh narrated to us that my father narrated to us that Shu'bah reported from Muhammad ibn Ziyad. He said: I heard Abu Hurayrah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Fast upon seeing it, and break your fast upon seeing it. And if the month is obscured from you, then count thirty.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from 'Ubaydullah ibn Mu'adh.

[Sahih. See the preceding.]"
7937,"(7934) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us: Abu Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us: Muhammad ibn 'Abd al-Wahhab and Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated to us, saying: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us: Ibrahim ibn Sa'd informed us, from Ibn Shihab, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it [the crescent of Shawwal], break the fast. If it is obscured from you, then fast thirty days.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya. [Sahih. See its predecessor.]"
7938,"(7935) Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Yusuf narrated to us: Abu Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani al-Hafiz narrated to us: Ahmad ibn Salamah al-Bazzaz narrated to us: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Bishr al-'Abdi narrated to us: 'Ubayd Allah informed us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), that he mentioned the crescent and said: ""Fast upon seeing it, and break the fast upon seeing it. If it is obscured from you, then count thirty.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah, from Muhammad ibn Bishr."
7939,"(7936) Abu Bakr Muhammad ibn al-Hasan ibn Furak informed us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us: Yunus ibn Habib informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: Shu'bah informed us: 'Amr ibn Murrah informed us, saying: I heard Abu al-Bakhtari say: We sighted the crescent of Ramadan while we were at Dhat 'Irq, so we sent a man to Ibn 'Abbas to ask him. Ibn 'Abbas said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed, Allah has extended it [the month] for its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from the hadith of Ghundar, from Shu'bah. It was also narrated by 'Ikrimah and Muhammad ibn Hunayn from Ibn 'Abbas regarding the completion of the count as thirty, with the same meaning. Others besides them also narrated it. [Sahih. Muslim.]"
7940,"(7937) Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Isma'il al-Tabrani narrated to us therein: 'Abd Allah ibn Ahmad ibn Mansur al-Tusi narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'igh narrated to us: Rawh narrated to us: Zakariyya ibn Ishaq narrated to us: Abu al-Zubayr informed us that he heard Jabir ibn 'Abd Allah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break the fast. If it is obscured from you, then count thirty days."" [Sahih. Narrated by Ahmad.]"
7941,"(7938) Abu Bakr ibn Furak narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Ja'far informed us: Yunus ibn Habib informed us: Abu Dawud informed us: 'Imran al-Qattan narrated to us, from Qatadah, from al-Hasan, from Abu Bakrah, who said: The Prophet (ﷺ) said: ""Fast upon seeing it, and break the fast upon seeing it. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count of thirty days."" [Sahih. Narrated by al-Tabrani.]"
7942,"(7939) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us: Abu Abdullah Muhammad ibn 'Abd Allah al-Saffar narrated to us: Abu Isma'il Muhammad ibn Isma'il narrated to us: 'Abd Allah ibn Salih informed me: Mu'awiyah ibn Salih informed me, from 'Abd Allah ibn Abi Qays, who said: I heard 'A'ishah say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to meticulously count the days of the crescent of Sha'ban in a way he did not do for any other month. Then he would fast Ramadan upon seeing its crescent. If it was obscured from him, he would count thirty days, and then he would fast.

'Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi similarly narrated it from Mu'awiyah ibn Salih. [Hasan. Narrated by Abu Dawud.]"
7943,"(7940) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Ishaq al-Faqih informed us: Isma'il ibn Qutaybah narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Count the crescent of Sha'ban for [the purpose of] Ramadan."" [Hasan. Narrated by al-Tirmidhi.]"
7944,"(7941) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us: 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Makhlad narrated to us: Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj Abu al-Husayn narrated to us: Yahya ibn Yahya narrated to us, and he mentioned it.

And he added therein: ""And do not mix [the fast] with Ramadan unless it coincides with a fast that one of you was already observing, so let him fast that fast. And fast upon seeing it, and break the fast upon seeing it. If it is obscured from you, then indeed the count is not obscured from you."" [Hasan. See its predecessor.]"
7945,"(7942) Abu Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Abi Tahir al-Daqqaq informed us in Baghdad: Abu Muhammad 'Abd Allah ibn Ibrahim ibn Ayyub al-Bazzaz narrated to us: Ibrahim ibn 'Abd Allah ibn Muslim al-Basri narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us.

(And) Abu al-Hasan 'Ali ibn Muhammad al-Muqri' informed us: al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us: Yusuf ibn Ya'qub al-Qadi informed us: Muslim ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Hisham al-Dastawa'i narrated to us: Yahya ibn Abi Kathir informed us, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah, from the Prophet (ﷺ), who said: ""None of you should precede Ramadan by fasting a day or two, except for a man who has been observing a [regular] fast, so let him fast that fast.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Muslim ibn Ibrahim, and Muslim narrated it through another chain from Hisham. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7946,"(7943) Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz narrated to us: Abu Bakr Ahmad ibn Ishaq ibn Ayyub al-Faqih informed us, dictating: Bishr ibn Musa narrated to us: Yahya ibn Bishr al-Hariri narrated to us: Mu'awiyah ibn Salam narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Abu Salamah, that Abu Hurayrah informed him that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do not precede Ramadan by a day or two, except for a man who has been observing a [regular] fast, so let him fast it."" Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya ibn Bishr.

It was also narrated by Ayyub, al-Awza'i, 'Ali ibn al-Mubarak, Ma'mar, Shayban, Husayn al-Mu'allim, Hammam, and Aban, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, with a narration similar to that of Hisham and Mu'awiyah.

And Muhammad ibn 'Amr narrated it from Abu Salamah. [Sahih. See its predecessor.]"
7947,"(7944) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Dāwūd al-Razzāz informed us — read aloud to him from the original of his book in Baghdad — Abū ʿAmr ʿUthmān ibn Aḥmad al-Daqqāq informed us, dictating: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib informed us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb ibn Aṭāʾ informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr informed us, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet ﷺ, who said: ""Do not precede the month by a day or two, except that which coincides with a fast that one of you would [normally] observe. Fast upon its sighting and break fast upon its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count of thirty, and then break fast.""

It has also been narrated from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAbbās, Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān, Ṭalq ibn ʿAlī, and others, from the Prophet ﷺ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7948,"(7945) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ of Naysābūr and Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Isḥāq al-Bazzāz in Baghdad informed us, both saying: Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Fākihī in Makkah informed us: Abū Yaḥyā ibn Abī Masarrat informed us: ʿAbd al-Salām ibn ʿĀṣim al-Rāzī in Makkah informed us: Abū Zuhayr ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Maghrāʾ informed us: Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq informed us, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Mālik ibn Abī ʿĀmir, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, may Allah be pleased with him, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do not precede this month. Fast upon its sighting and break fast upon its sighting. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count of thirty.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain. Reported by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7949,"(7946) Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān al-ʿAdl in Baghdad informed us: Abū Jaʿfar Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr ibn al-Bakhtarī informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us: Zakariyyā ibn Isḥāq informed us: ʿAmr ibn Dīnār informed us that Muḥammad ibn Ḥunayn informed him that he heard Ibn ʿAbbās saying: ""I am amazed at those who fast before Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah ﷺ only said: 'When you see the crescent, fast; and when you see it, break fast. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count of thirty.'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ as per al-Bayhaqī. Reported by al-Nasāʾī.]"
7950,"(7947) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Ḥāmid ibn Bilāl informed us: Abū al-Azhar informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bakr al-Sahmī informed us, from Ḥātim, that is, Ibn Abī Ṣaghīrah, from Simāk, meaning Ibn Ḥarb, who said: I entered upon ʿIkrimah on a day when it was unclear to me whether it was from Ramadan or from Shaʿbān. I woke up fasting, saying: ""If it is from Ramadan, it will not have preceded me; and if it is from Shaʿbān, it would be a voluntary fast."" I then entered upon ʿIkrimah while he was eating bread, greens, and milk. He said: ""Come to the meal."" I said: ""I am fasting."" He said: ""I swear by Allah, you must break it."" I said: ""Glory be to Allah!"" He said: ""I swear by Allah, you must break it."" When I saw that he would not make an exception, I broke my fast. The next morning I went with some food while I was full, and I said: ""Bring it [the narration]."" He said: ""I heard Ibn ʿAbbās saying: 'I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""Fast upon its sighting and break fast upon its sighting. If a cloud or haze comes between you and it, then complete the count, and do not anticipate the month — do not anticipate Ramadan with a day from Shaʿbān.""'""

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Nasāʾī.]"
7951,"(7948) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī informed us: Ḥusayn informed us, from Zāʾidah, from Simāk, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do not anticipate the month by fasting a day or two, except that which one of you would [normally] observe as a fast. And do not fast until you see it; then fast until you see it. If a cloud cover obstructs it, then complete the count of thirty, and then break fast. The month is [sometimes] twenty-nine [days].""

Abū Dāwūd said: Ḥātim ibn Abī Ṣaghīrah, Shuʿbah, and al-Ḥasan ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated it from Simāk with the same meaning, but they did not say ""and then break fast."" The Shaykh said: Abū ʿAwānah narrated it from Simāk, and he made the completion of the count pertain to Shaʿbān.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7952,"(7949) Abū Bakr ibn Fūrak informed him: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us: Abū ʿAwānah informed us, from Simāk, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Fast Ramadan upon its sighting and break fast upon its sighting. If a cloud or mist comes between you and it, then complete the month of Shaʿbān as thirty, and do not anticipate Ramadan with a fast of a day from Shaʿbān.""

It is as though he mentioned the ruling in both respects together, and each one of them narrated one aspect of it.

[Ḥasan. Reported by al-Ṭayālisī.]"
7953,"(7950) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Ṣabbāḥ al-Bazzāz informed us: Jarīr ibn ʿAbd al-Ḥamīd al-Ḍabbī informed us, from Manṣūr, from Ribʿī ibn Ḥirāsh, from Ḥudhayfah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do not anticipate the month until you see the crescent or complete the count; then fast until you see the crescent or complete the count."" Jarīr connected it, mentioning Ḥudhayfah in it, and he is a reliable authority.

Al-Thawrī and a group narrated it from Manṣūr, from Ribʿī, from some of the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, from the Prophet ﷺ.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7954,"(7951) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb al-Farrāʾ informed us: Muḥāḍir ibn al-Mūwariʿ informed us: Hishām ibn Ḥassān informed us, from Qays ibn Ṭalq, from his father Ṭalq, who said: I heard a man asking the Prophet ﷺ about the day concerning which there is doubt — some of them saying, ""This is from Shaʿbān,"" and some saying, ""This is from Ramadan."" The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Do not fast until you see the crescent. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count of thirty.""

[Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain.]"
7955,"(7952) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Bālawayh informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah informed us: Abū Khālid al-Aḥmar informed us, from ʿAmr ibn Qays al-Malāʾī, from Abū Isḥāq, from Ṣilah ibn Zufar, who said: We were with ʿAmmār ibn Yāsir when a roasted sheep was brought. He said: ""Eat."" Some of the people withdrew, saying: ""I am fasting."" ʿAmmār said: ""Whoever fasts the Day of Doubt has disobeyed Abū al-Qāsim ﷺ.""

Al-Bukhārī reported its text in the translation of the chapter.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. Reported by al-Bukhārī as a connected narration.]"
7956,"(7953) Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Bazzāz al-Ṭūsī therein informed us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us: al-Thawrī informed us, from Abī ʿAbbād, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Prophet ﷺ prohibited fasting a day before Ramadan, and [he also prohibited fasting on] the Day of Sacrifice (al-Aḍḥā), the Day of Breaking Fast (al-Fiṭr), and the Days of Tashrīq — three days after the Day of Sacrifice (Yawm al-Naḥr).

Abū ʿAbbād is ʿAbd Allāh ibn Saʿīd al-Maqburī, who is not strong.

[Munkar.]"
7957,"(7954) Abū Muḥammad al-Ḥasan ibn ʿAlī ibn al-Mu'ammal al-Māsarjisī informed us; he said: Abū ʿUthmān ʿAmr ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Baṣrī narrated to us.
(Ḥ) And Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzakkī informed us; he said: Abū ʿAbd Allāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us. Both said: Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us; Jaʿfar ibn ʿAwn informed us; ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAbd Allāh al-Masʿūdī informed us from Hilāl, from ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUkaym, who said: When the night of doubt arrived in Ramaḍān, ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) would stand after praying Maghrib and then say: ""Indeed, this is a month whose fasting Allāh has made obligatory upon you, but He has not made its standing (in prayer) obligatory upon you. So whoever among you is able to stand (in prayer), let him do so, for it is among the supererogatory acts of goodness that Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic, has commanded. And whoever is unable, let him sleep on his bed. And let no one say: 'If so-and-so fasts, I will fast, and if so-and-so stands (in prayer), I will stand.' Rather, whoever fasts or stands, let him do so for the sake of Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic. Minimize idle speech in the houses of Allāh, the Mighty and Majestic. And let each one of you know that he is in a state of prayer so long as he is waiting for the prayer. Beware! Let no one among you anticipate the month. Fast upon sighting it (the new moon) and break fast upon sighting it. If it is obscured from you, then count Shaʿbān as thirty days. Then do not break fast until the night descends upon the hilltops."" [Weak]"
7958,"(7955) Abū al-Fatḥ Hilāl ibn Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar al-Ḥaffār in Baghdād informed us; he said: al-Ḥusayn ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us; Ibrāhīm ibn Mushajjir narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us from Mujāld, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with him), that he used to deliver a sermon when Ramaḍān arrived, and then say: ""This is the blessed month whose fasting Allāh has made obligatory, but He has not made its standing (in prayer) obligatory. Let a man beware of saying: 'I will fast if so-and-so fasts, and I will break fast if so-and-so breaks fast.' Indeed, fasting is not from food and drink, but rather from falsehood, vain talk, and idle speech. Beware! Do not anticipate the month. When you see the crescent, fast, and when you see it (at the end), break fast. If it is obscured from you, then complete the count."" He used to say this after the Fajr prayer and the ʿAṣr prayer. [Very weak. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7959,"(7956) Abū al-Fatḥ informed us; he said: al-Ḥusayn narrated to us; Ibrāhīm narrated to us; Hushaym narrated to us from Mujāld, from al-Shaʿbī, from Masrūq, that ʿUmar (may Allāh be pleased with him) used to say the same as that. [Very weak]"
7960,"(7957) Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us; he said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Ṣūfī narrated to us; Ḥamzah ibn Muḥammad al-Kātib narrated to us; Nuʿaym ibn Ḥammād narrated to us; Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us from Mujāld, from ʿĀmir, that ʿUmar and ʿAlī (may Allāh be pleased with them both) used to prohibit fasting on the day of doubt in Ramaḍān. [Weak]"
7961,"(7958) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Fanjawayh al-Dīnawarī informed us; he said: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Mājah al-Qazwīnī narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Mandah narrated to us; al-Ḥusayn ibn Ḥafṣ narrated to us; Isrāʾīl narrated to us from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Ḥakīm al-Ḥaḍramī, who said: I heard Ibn ʿUmar say: ""If I were to fast the entire year, I would certainly break fast on that day of doubt in Ramaḍān.""
And al-Thawrī narrated it from ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz, who said: ""I saw Ibn ʿUmar commanding a man to break fast on the day of doubt."""
7962,"(7959) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad ibn Fanjawayh al-Dīnawarī informed us; he said: Mūsā ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn ʿAbd Allāh narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn Māhān narrated to us; ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Ṭanāfisī narrated to us; Wakīʿ narrated to us; Abū al-Ḍurays ʿUqbah ibn ʿAmmār narrated to us from ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿĀbis al-Nakhaʿī, from his father, who said: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Masʿūd said: ""That I should miss a day of Ramaḍān and then make it up is more beloved to me than to add to it a day that is not part of it."" [Good. Narrated by Ibn Abī Shaybah]"
7963,"(7960) Abū ʿAbd Allāh ibn Fanjawayh informed us; he said: Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Mājah narrated to us; Muḥammad ibn Ayyūb informed us; Abū Salamah narrated to us; Hammām narrated to us; Qatādah said: ""They differed concerning a day about which it was not known whether it was from Ramaḍān or from Shaʿbān. So we came to Anas and found him sitting, eating his morning meal.""
And it has been narrated from Ḥudhayfah ibn al-Yamān that he used to prohibit fasting on the day of doubt, and from Ibn ʿAbbās that he used to say: ""Separate,"" meaning between the fasting of Ramaḍān and Shaʿbān, by breaking fast. [Sound. Ibn Mājah]"
7964,"(7961) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us; he said: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Abū Muslim narrated to us; Sulaymān ibn Dāwūd narrated to us; ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad narrated to us from al-ʿAlāʾ ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When half of Shaʿbān has passed, refrain from fasting until Ramaḍān enters."" [Denounced. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7965,"(7962) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us; he said: I heard Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh say: I heard Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Qutaybah al-Ṭūsī say: I heard Qutaybah ibn Saʿīd say: I heard ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad say: ""ʿAbbād ibn Kathīr came to us in Madīnah and went to the gathering of al-ʿAlāʾ. He took him by the hand, made him stand, and then said: 'O Allāh, indeed this one narrates from his father, from Abū Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) said: ""When half of Shaʿbān has passed, do not fast.""'"" So al-ʿAlāʾ said: ""O Allāh, indeed my father narrated to me from Abū Hurayrah, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), the same.""
Abū Dāwūd narrated it from Qutaybah. Then Abū Dāwūd said: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal said: ""This is a ḥadīth munkar (denounced)."" He also said: ""ʿAbd al-Raḥmān used not to narrate it."" [Denounced. See the preceding]"
7966,"(7963) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbd al-Jabbār al-Sukkarī informed us; he said: Ismāʿīl al-Ṣaffār narrated to us; Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr narrated to us; ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us; Maʿmar narrated to us from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allāh (peace and blessings be upon him) forbade that the month of Ramaḍān be anticipated by fasting one or two days, except for a man who was already fasting a regular fast, in which case it would coincide with his usual fast. [Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd]"
7967,"(7964) Abū Bakr ibn Furak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd al-Ṭayālisī informed us: Hishām, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) did not fast any month of the year except Shaʿbān. Indeed, he would fast the entirety of Shaʿbān.

Al-Bukhārī and Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from the ḥadīth of Hishām al-Dastawāʾī.

And Abū al-Naṣr narrated it from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""I did not see him fasting more in any month than in Shaʿbān.""

And Ibn Abī Labīd narrated it from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""He would fast all of Shaʿbān; he would fast Shaʿbān except a little.""

And Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr narrated it from Abū Salamah, from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: ""He would fast Shaʿbān except a little — nay, he would fast all of it."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7968,"(7965) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān informed us: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī informed us, from Sufyān, from Manṣūr.

(Ḥ) And Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan ibn Furak informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Yūnus ibn Ḥabīb informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from Manṣūr, from Sālim ibn Abī al-Jaʿd, from Abū Salamah, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) did not fast two months consecutively except Shaʿbān and Ramaḍān.

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Shuʿbah. And in the narration of Sufyān, she said: ""I did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) fasting for two consecutive months except that he would join Shaʿbān with Ramaḍān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Tirmidhī.]"
7969,"(7966) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal informed us: Muḥammad ibn Jaʿfar informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from Tawbah al-ʿAnbarī, from Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm, from Abū Salamah, from Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her), from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him), that he did not fast any complete month of the year except Shaʿbān, which he would join with Ramaḍān. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7970,"(7967) Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb al-Shaybānī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Būshanjī informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ informed us: Mahdī ibn Maymūn informed us: Ghaylān ibn Jarīr informed us, from Muṭarrif, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him — or to a man while he was listening: ""Did you fast anything from the end of this month?"" The man said: ""No, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Then when you break your fast, fast two days in its place.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Ṣalt ibn Muḥammad, from Mahdī. And Muslim narrated it from ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Asmāʾ. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Bukhārī.]"
7971,"(7968) ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār informed us: Hishām ibn ʿAlī informed us: Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl Abū al-Nuʿmān informed us: Mahdī ibn Maymūn informed us, mentioning it with his chain of narration, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to a man: ""Did you fast anything from the end of this month?"" He said: ""No"" — meaning Shaʿbān. He said: ""Then when you break your fast, fast one day or two days.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Nuʿmān. He said: And Thābit narrated from Muṭarrif, from ʿImrān, from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him): ""From the end of Shaʿbān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding.]"
7972,"(7969) Abū Naṣr Aḥmad ibn ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad al-Fāmī informed us: Abū ʿAbdullāh Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Sufyān informed us: Hudbah ibn Khālid informed us: Ḥammād ibn Salamah informed us, from Thābit, from Muṭarrif, from ʿImrān ibn Ḥuṣayn, that the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) said to him — or to a man: ""Did you fast anything from the end of Shaʿbān?"" He said: ""No."" He said: ""Then when you break your fast, fast two days.""

Muslim narrated it in the Ṣaḥīḥ from Haddāb ibn Khālid. [Ṣaḥīḥ. The wording is Muslim's.]"
7973,"(7970) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn al-ʿAlāʾ al-Zubaydī informed us from his book: al-Walīd ibn Muslim informed us: ʿAbdullāh ibn al-ʿAlāʾ informed us, from Abū al-Azhar al-Mughīrah ibn Farwah, who said: Muʿāwiyah stood among the people at Dayr Misḥal, which is at the gate of Ḥimṣ, and said: ""O people, we have seen the moon on such-and-such a day, and I am proceeding with fasting. So whoever wishes to do so, let him do so."" Thereupon Mālik ibn Hubayrah al-Sabaʾī stood up and said: ""O Muʿāwiyah, is this something you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him), or is it something from your own opinion?"" He said: ""I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings be upon him) say: 'Fast the month and its end (sirrah).'""

Abū ʿAlī informed us: Abū Bakr informed us: Abū Dāwūd informed us: Sulaymān ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Dimashqī informed us in this ḥadīth, and al-Walīd said: ""I heard Abū ʿAmr — meaning al-Awzāʿī — say: 'Its end (sirrah) means its beginning.'""

He said: And Abū Dāwūd informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Wāḥid informed us: Abū Mushir informed us, saying: Saʿīd ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz used to say: ""Its end (sirrah) means its beginning.""

The Sheikh said: And others narrated it from al-Awzāʿī that he said: ""Its end (sirrah) means its last part,"" and this is the correct view. He intended by it the one or two days in which the moon is hidden before the day of doubt, or he intended fasting the last part of the month together with the day of doubt, if that coincided with his customary practice of fasting at the end of every month. And it was also said that he intended by its ""end"" (sirrah) its middle part. And the ""end"" (sirr) of everything is its innermost part. Accordingly, he intended the White Days (Ayyām al-Bīḍ). And Allah knows best. [Ḥasan. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7974,"(7971) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — informed us: al-Ḥasan ibn Mukram informed us: Yazīd ibn Hārūn informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, from—

(Ḥ) And Abū Naṣr Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṭūsī informed us there: ʿAbdullāh ibn Aḥmad ibn Manṣūr al-Ṭūsī informed us: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl al-Ṣāʾigh informed us: Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah informed us: Shuʿbah informed us, saying: I heard Yazīd ibn Khumayr from ʿAbdullāh ibn Abī Mūsā, a client of Banū Naṣr, that he asked ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) about the day in which the people are in doubt. She said: ""That I should fast a day of Shaʿbān is more beloved to me than that I should break fast a day of Ramaḍān.""

This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Rūḥ. And in the narration of Yazīd: ""About the month when it is obscured,"" and he did not say ""a client of Banū Naṣr."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
7975,"(7972) Abū ʿAbdullāh ibn Fanjawayh al-Dīnawarī informed us at al-Dāmaghān: ʿUbaydullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shanbah informed us: Muḥammad — meaning al-Ḥaḍramī — informed us: ʿUthmān informed us: Yaḥyā ibn Ḍurays informed us: Hishām ibn ʿUrwah informed us, from Fāṭimah bint al-Mundhir, from Asmāʾ (may Allah be pleased with her), that she used to fast the day that was in doubt from Ramaḍān.

He said: And ʿUthmān informed us: Zayd ibn Ḥubāb informed us: Muʿāwiyah ibn Ṣāliḥ informed us, from Abū Maryam, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: ""That I should fast the day that is in doubt from Shaʿbān is more beloved to me than that I should break fast a day of Ramaḍān.""

This was narrated from Abū Hurayrah with this chain of narration. And the narration of Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) regarding the prohibition of anticipating Ramaḍān, except that it coincides with a fast that one habitually observes, is more authentic than that.

As for what has been narrated from ʿAlī (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding that, he said it in the context of a man's testimony to having seen the moon, and that will be addressed, Allah the Most High willing.

As for the position of Ibn ʿUmar regarding that, we have mentioned it previously.

And the narration of Yazīd ibn Hārūn indicates that the position of ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) regarding this is like the position of Ibn ʿUmar concerning fasting — when the month is overcast and not clear. And adherence to the established Sunnah and what the majority of the Companions and the generality of the people of knowledge follow is more appropriate for us. And with Allah lies success."
7976,"(7973) Abū ʿAbdullāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us: Abū al-Bakhtarī ʿAbdullāh ibn Muḥammad ibn Shākir informed us: al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Juʿfī informed us: Zāʾidah informed us, from Simāk ibn Ḥarb, from ʿIkrimah, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet (peace and blessings be upon him) and said: ""Indeed, I have seen the moon"" — meaning the moon of Ramaḍān. He said: ""Do you bear witness that there is no god but Allah?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Do you bear witness that Muḥammad is the Messenger of Allah?"" He said: ""Yes."" He said: ""O Bilāl, announce to the people that they should fast tomorrow."" [Ṣaḥīḥ in other chains. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7977,"(7974) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, he said: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, he said: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, he said: Muḥammad ibn Bikkār ibn al-Rayyān narrated to us, he said: al-Walīd, meaning Ibn Abī Thawr, narrated to us from Simāk, and he mentioned it similarly, except that he did not say, ""meaning the crescent of Ramaḍān."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
7978,"(7975) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, he said: al-Ḥasan ibn Ḥalīm informed us, he said: Abū al-Muwajjih informed us, he said: ʿAbdān informed us, he said: al-Faḍl ibn Mūsā narrated to us, he said: Sufyān al-Thawrī narrated to us from Simāk from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: A Bedouin came to the Prophet ﷺ on the night of the crescent of Ramaḍān and said, ""O Messenger of Allah, I have seen the crescent."" He said, ""Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah?"" He said, ""Yes."" He then called out, ""Fast.""
And similarly it was narrated from Abū ʿĀṣim from al-Thawrī as a connected (mawṣūl) narration, while others narrated it from al-Thawrī as a mursal narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
7979,"(7976) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, he said: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, he said: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, he said: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, he said: Ḥammād narrated to us from Simāk from ʿIkrimah: They were uncertain about the crescent of Ramaḍān on one occasion, and they intended not to stand in prayer and not to fast. Then a Bedouin came from al-Ḥārah and testified that he had seen the crescent. He was brought to the Prophet ﷺ, who said, ""Do you testify that there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah?"" He said, ""Yes,"" and he testified that he had seen the crescent. So he commanded Bilāl, and he called out among the people that they should stand in prayer and fast. Abū Dāwūd said: A group narrated it from Simāk from ʿIkrimah as a mursal narration, and no one mentioned ""standing in prayer"" except Ḥammād ibn Salamah. The Shaykh said: The hadith of Ḥammād was narrated by Abū Dāwūd from Mūsā from Ḥammād as a mursal narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7980,"(7977) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us in al-Mustadrak, he said: Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn Salamah al-ʿAnzī narrated to us, he said: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us, he said: Mūsā ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, he said: Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated to us from Simāk from ʿIkrimah from Ibn ʿAbbās, and he mentioned it as a connected narration with its like, except that he did not say ""on one occasion."" [Ṣaḥīḥ for other than it. See the meaning.]"
7981,"(7978) Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī informed us, he said: Abū Bakr ibn Dāsah informed us, he said: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us, he said: Maḥmūd ibn Khālid and ʿAbdullāh ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Samarqandī narrated to us — and I am more precise in his hadith — they said: Marwān, meaning ibn Muḥammad, narrated to us from ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb from Yaḥyā ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn Sālim from Abī Bakr ibn Nāfiʿ from his father from Ibn ʿUmar, who said: The people sighted the crescent, and I informed the Messenger of Allah ﷺ that I had seen it, so he fasted and commanded the people to fast.
Abū ʿAbd al-Raḥmān al-Sulamī informed us, he said: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ said: Marwān ibn Muḥammad is the sole narrator of it from Ibn Wahb, and he is trustworthy (thiqah). The Shaykh said: This hadith is counted among the singular narrations (afrād) of Marwān ibn Muḥammad al-Dimashqī; al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated it from him. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
7982,"(7979) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated it to us, he said: Muḥammad ibn Ṣāliḥ ibn Hāniʾ narrated to us, he said: Muḥammad ibn Ismāʿīl ibn Mihrān narrated to us, he said: Hārūn ibn Saʿīd al-Aylī narrated to us, he said: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Yaḥyā informed me, and he mentioned it with its like, except that he said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ fasted and commanded the people to fast.""
And Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmari al-Ubullī, Abū Ismāʿīl — and he is weak in hadith — narrated from Misʿar ibn Kidām and Abū ʿAwanah from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Mayṣarah from Ṭāwūs, who said: I witnessed Madīnah, and Ibn ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās were there. A man came to its governor and testified before him to having sighted the crescent of Ramaḍān. So he asked Ibn ʿUmar and Ibn ʿAbbās about his testimony, and they ordered him to accept it, and they said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ accepted the testimony of one man regarding the sighting of the crescent of Ramaḍān."" They said: ""The Messenger of Allah ﷺ did not accept testimony for breaking the fast except the testimony of two men."" [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
7983,"(7980) Abū Naṣr ʿUmar ibn ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn ʿUmar ibn Qatādah al-Anṣārī narrated it to us, he said: Abū Aḥmad al-Ḥusayn ibn ʿAlī al-Tamīmī informed us, he said: Abū ʿAbdillāh Muḥammad ibn Makhlad ibn Ḥafṣ al-Dūrī informed us in Baghdād, he said: Yaḥyā ibn ʿAyyāsh narrated to us, he said: Ḥafṣ ibn ʿUmari, Abū Ismāʿīl al-Ubullī, narrated to us, he said: Abū ʿAwanah and Misʿar narrated to us, and he mentioned it. And this is not something upon which proof should be established, and what has preceded is sufficient. [Bāṭil. Narrated by al-Ṭabarānī.]"
7984,"(7981) Abū Zakariyyā Yaḥyā ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Muḥammad ibn Yaḥyā informed us, he said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb informed us, he said: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us, he said: al-Shāfiʿī informed us, he said: ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Muḥammad al-Dārawardī narrated to us from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh ibn ʿAmr ibn ʿUthmān from his mother Fāṭimah bint Ḥusayn: A man testified before ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, to having sighted the crescent of Ramaḍān, so he fasted. And I think he said: ""And he commanded the people to fast."" And he said: ""I would prefer to fast one day from Shaʿbān than to break the fast one day from Ramaḍān."" [Ḍaʿīf. Narrated by al-Shāfiʿī.]"
7985,"(7982) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr informed us, they said: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, he said: Hārūn ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, he said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī narrated to us from Sufyān from Manṣūr from Abū Wāʾil, who said: The letter of ʿUmar came to us while we were at Khāniqīn: ""The crescents are some larger than others. If you see the crescent during the day, do not break your fast until evening comes, unless two Muslim men testify that they sighted it the previous evening."" Thus a group narrated it from Sufyān al-Thawrī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Narrated by al-Dāraquṭnī.]"
7986,"(7983) Muʾammal ibn Ismāʿīl narrated it from Sufyān and added in it: ""If you see the crescent at the beginning of the day, do not break your fast until two upright men testify that they sighted it the previous evening.""
Abū Bakr ibn al-Ḥārith al-Faqīh narrated it to us, he said: ʿAlī ibn ʿUmar al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, he said: Abū Bakr al-Naysābūrī narrated to us, he said: Ḥājib ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, he said: Muʾammal ibn Ismāʿīl narrated to us, he said: Sufyān narrated to us, he said: Manṣūr narrated to me, and he mentioned it.
ʿAlī said: Abū Bakr said to us: ""If Muʾammal preserved it, then it is gharīb, and the imām ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Mahdī contradicted him.""
The Shaykh said: These words were narrated by Shuʿbah from Sulaymān ibn Mihrān al-Aʿmash from Abū Wāʾil. [Ṣaḥīḥ. See the preceding hadith.]"
7987,"(7984) Abu Bakr ibn al-Harith al-Faqih informed us; 'Ali ibn 'Umar al-Hafiz informed us; Abu Bakr al-Naysaburi informed us; Yusuf ibn Sa'id ibn Muslim informed me; Hajjaj ibn Muhammad informed us.

(He said) And Abu Bakr narrated to us; Ahmad ibn Sa'id ibn Sakhr narrated to us; al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us.

(He said) And Abu Bakr narrated to us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim Abu Umayyah, al-'Abbas ibn Muhammad, and Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn al-Junayd said: Rawh narrated to us; they said: Shu'bah narrated to us from Sulayman from Abu Wa'il, who said: The letter of 'Umar reached us at Khaniqin stating that some crescents are greater than others. So when you see the crescent in the early part of the day, do not break your fast until two witnesses testify that they saw it the previous day.

It was also narrated by Hammad ibn Salamah from al-A'mash, just as Shu'bah narrated it.

And Ibrahim al-Nakha'i narrated it as a mursal narration contrary to that. [Sahih. See the hadith before it.]"
7988,"(7985) Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Jabbar al-Sukkari informed us in Baghdad; Isma'il ibn Muhammad al-Saffar informed us; Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us; 'Abd al-Razzaq informed us; al-Thawri narrated from Mughirah from Shibak from Ibrahim, who said: 'Umar wrote to 'Utbah ibn Farqad: ""When you see the crescent during the day before the sun reaches its zenith, then break your fast for the completion of thirty days. And when you see it after the sun has passed its zenith, do not break your fast until you complete the fast."" Thus narrated Ibrahim al-Nakha'i as a munqati' narration, and the hadith of Abu Wa'il is more authentic than that. [Da'if. Narrated by 'Abd al-Razzaq.]

(7986) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Bahr ibn Nasr informed us; it was read to Ibn Wahb: Yunus ibn Yazid informed you from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn 'Abdullah that some people saw the crescent of Fitr during the day, so 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar completed his fast until nightfall and said: ""No, until it is seen from where it is seen at night."" [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7989,"(7986) Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and Abu Bakr ibn al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Bahr ibn Nasr informed us; it was read to Ibn Wahb: Yunus ibn Yazid informed you from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn 'Abdullah that some people saw the crescent of Fitr during the day, so 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar completed his fast until nightfall and said: ""No, until it is seen from where it is seen at night."" [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7990,"(7987) 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Salamah narrated it from Ibn Shihab from Salim, who said: Ibn 'Umar used to say: ""Indeed, some people break their fast when they see the crescent during the day, and it is not permissible for you to break your fast until you see it at night from where it is seen.""

Abu Nasr Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Isma'il al-Tusi informed me; 'Abdullah ibn Ahmad ibn Mansur informed us; Muhammad ibn Isma'il al-Sa'ig informed us; Rawh informed us; 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn Abi Salamah narrated it.

And we have received narrations to that effect from 'Uthman ibn 'Affan and 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with them both. [Sahih. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
7991,"(7988) Abu Nasr 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz ibn 'Umar ibn Qatadah informed us; Abu al-Hasan Muhammad ibn al-Husayn ibn Mansur al-Tajir informed us orally; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Sa'id al-BushanjI informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; al-Layth ibn Sa'd and Ibn Wahb narrated from Yahya ibn Ayyub from 'Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr from Ibn Shihab from Salim ibn 'Abdullah from his father from Hafsah from the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, who said: ""Whoever does not intend the fast before dawn, there is no fast for him."" [Sahih. Its authentication has preceded in the hadith.]"
7992,"(7989) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Zakariyya ibn Abi Ishaq and others informed us; they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; al-Rabi' ibn Sulayman informed us; al-Shafi'i informed us; Malik narrated from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'mar from Abu Yunus, the freed slave of 'A'ishah, from 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that a man said to the Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, while she was listening: ""I wake up in a state of janabah and I wish to fast."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""I also wake up in a state of janabah and I wish to fast, so I perform ghusl and then I fast that day."" The man said: ""You are not like us; Allah has forgiven your past and future sins."" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, became angry and said: ""By Allah, I hope to be the most fearful of Allah among you and the most knowledgeable of you regarding what I observe of taqwa.""

Isma'il ibn Ja'far corroborated it from 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, and from that perspective Muslim narrated it. [Sahih. Narrated by Muslim.]"
7993,"(7990) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us; Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub informed us; Muhammad ibn Nu'aym informed us; Qutaybah ibn Sa'id informed us.

(He said) And Abu al-Hasan al-Miqri' al-MihrajanI informed us therein; al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ishaq informed us; Yusuf ibn Ya'qub informed us; Abu al-Rabi' informed us; they said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far informed us; 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ma'mar ibn Hazm informed us that Abu Yunus, the freed slave of 'A'ishah, informed him from 'A'ishah that a man came to the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, to seek his verdict, while 'A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, was listening from behind the door. He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the prayer time overtakes me while I am in a state of janabah, so should I fast?"" The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, said: ""And the prayer time overtakes me while I am in a state of janabah, so I fast."" He said: ""You are not like us; Allah has forgiven your past and future sins."" He said: ""By Allah, I hope to be the most fearful of Allah among you and the most knowledgeable of you regarding what I observe of taqwa.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Qutaybah ibn Sa'id. [Sahih. See the hadith before it.]"
7994,"(7991) Muhammad ibn 'Abdullah al-Hafiz informed me; Abu al-Nadr al-Faqih informed us; 'Uthman ibn Sa'id al-Darimi informed us; Yahya ibn Yahya narrated from Malik ibn Anas, from what he read to him, from 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id ibn Qays from Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham from 'A'ishah and Umm Salamah, the two wives of the Prophet, peace and blessings be upon him, that they both said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to wake up in a state of janabah from sexual intercourse, not from a wet dream, and then he would fast. [Sahih. Narrated by Malik.]"
7995,"(7992) Abu Ahmad al-MihrajanI informed us; Abu Bakr ibn Ja'far al-MuzakkI informed us; Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-'AbdI informed us; Ibn Bukayr informed us; Malik narrated it with the same meaning. He added in its text: ""in Ramadan, and then he would fast.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Yahya ibn Yahya and mentioned his words ""in Ramadan."" [Sahih. See the hadith before it.]"
7996,"(7993) Abu 'Abdullah al-Hafiz and Abu Muhammad 'Abdullah ibn Yusuf informed us; Abu Muhammad said: he narrated to us, and Abu 'Abdullah said: Abu 'Abdullah Muhammad ibn Ya'qub al-Shaybani informed us; Husayn ibn Hasan ibn Muhajir informed us; Harun ibn Sa'id al-Ayli informed us; Ibn Wahb informed us; 'Amr ibn al-Harith informed me from 'Abd Rabbihi ibn Sa'id from 'Abdullah ibn Ka'b al-Himyari that Abu Bakr ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated to him that Marwan sent him to Umm Salamah to ask her about a man who wakes up in a state of janabah—should he fast? She said: The Messenger of Allah, peace and blessings be upon him, used to wake up in a state of janabah from sexual intercourse, not from a wet dream, and then he would neither break his fast nor make it up.

Muslim narrated it in al-Sahih from Harun ibn Sa'id. [Sahih. Narrated by al-Bukhari.]"
7997,"(7994) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Wahb narrated to me: Yūnus informed me, from Ibn Shihāb, from ʿUrwah ibn al-Zubayr and Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would be overtaken by the dawn in Ramaḍān while he was in a state of janābah, without having had a wet dream, and he would then perform ghusl and fast."" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Aḥmad ibn Ṣāliḥ, and Muslim narrated it from Ḥarmalah, both of them from Ibn Wahb. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7998,"(7995) Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Ḥasan Aḥmad ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbdūs informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: al-Qaʿnabī narrated from what he read to Mālik.
(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us among the last of them, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān informed us: al-Shāfiʿī informed us: Mālik informed us, from Sumayy, the freedman of Abū Bakr, that he heard Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān say: ""My father and I were with Marwān ibn al-Ḥakam, who was the governor of al-Madīnah, and it was mentioned to him that Abū Hurayrah was saying: 'Whoever wakes up in a state of janābah has broken his fast for that day.' So Marwān said: 'I swear upon you, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, that you must go to the two Mothers of the Believers, ʿĀʾishah and Umm Salamah, and ask them about this.'"" Abū Bakr said: ""So ʿAbd al-Raḥmān went and I went with him until we entered upon ʿĀʾishah. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān greeted her and said: 'O Mother of the Believers, we were with Marwān and it was mentioned to him that Abū Hurayrah said: ""Whoever wakes up in a state of janābah has broken his fast for that day.""' ʿĀʾishah said: 'It is not as Abū Hurayrah said, O ʿAbd al-Raḥmān. Do you turn away from what the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to do?' ʿAbd al-Raḥmān said: 'No, by Allah.' ʿĀʾishah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhā) said: 'Then I bear witness that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to wake up in a state of janābah from intercourse, without having had a wet dream, and then he would fast that day.' Then we left until we entered upon Umm Salamah and asked her about it, and she said the same as ʿĀʾishah had said. Then we went out until we came to Marwān, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān told him what they had said. Marwān said: 'I swear upon you, O Abū Muḥammad, that you must mount my mount at the gate and go to Abū Hurayrah and inform him of this.' So ʿAbd al-Raḥmān mounted and I mounted with him until we came to Abū Hurayrah. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān spoke with him for a while and then mentioned this to him. Abū Hurayrah said: 'I have no knowledge of this; rather, an informant informed me.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from al-Qaʿnabī, incorporated within his narration from Abū al-Yamān, from Shuʿayb, from al-Zuhrī, from Abū Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, with the meaning of this ḥadīth, except that in his ḥadīth he said: 'Thus al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās narrated to me, and he is more knowledgeable.' [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
7999,"(7996) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Shaybānī informed us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Razzāq informed us: Ibn Jurayj informed me: ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Abī Bakr ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān informed me, from Abū Bakr, meaning his father, who said: ""I heard Abū Hurayrah say in his discourse: 'Whoever is overtaken by the dawn while in a state of janābah, let him not fast.'"" He said: ""So I mentioned that to ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn al-Ḥārith, his father, and he rejected that. So ʿAbd al-Raḥmān set out and I set out with him until we entered upon ʿĀʾishah and Umm Salamah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhumā), and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān asked them about it. He said: 'Both of them said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to wake up in a state of janābah without having had a wet dream, and then he would fast.""' He said: ""Then we set out until we entered upon Marwān, and ʿAbd al-Raḥmān mentioned that to him. Marwān said: 'I adjure you that you go to Abū Hurayrah and retract what he says.' He said: 'So we came to Abū Hurayrah, and Abū Bakr was present for all of that. ʿAbd al-Raḥmān mentioned it to him, and Abū Hurayrah said: 'I heard that from al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās, and I did not hear it from the Prophet (ﷺ).'"" He said: ""So Abū Hurayrah retracted what he had been saying about that. I said to ʿAbd al-Malik: 'Did they both say: ""In Ramaḍān""?' He said: 'Thus he would wake up in a state of janābah without having had a wet dream, and then he would fast.'"" Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Muḥammad ibn Rāfiʿ. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
8000,"(7997) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās, al-Aṣamm, narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn Abī Ṭālib narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wahhāb, Ibn ʿAṭāʾ, informed us: Saʿīd, meaning Ibn Abī ʿArūbah, informed us, from Qatādah, from Saʿīd ibn al-Musayyib, that Abū Hurayrah (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu) retracted from his statement before his death. [Ḍaʿīf]"
8001,"(7998) He said: And ʿAbd al-Wahhāb informed us: ʿUmar ibn Qays al-Makkī informed us, who said: ʿAṭāʾ said: ""Abū Hurayrah retracted from his statement a good retraction, meaning regarding the one who wakes up in a state of janābah and has not performed ghusl.""
And it has been narrated to us from Abū Bakr ibn al-Mundhir that he said: ""The best of what I have heard regarding this is that it is carried upon the matter of naskh (abrogation). That is, intercourse was initially forbidden upon the fasting person at night after sleeping, just as food and drink were forbidden. Then when Allah, the Mighty and Sublime, permitted intercourse until the rising of the dawn, it became permissible for the one in a state of janābah, if he woke up before performing ghusl, to fast that day, due to the lifting of the prohibition. So Abū Hurayrah used to give fatwā based on what he heard from al-Faḍl ibn ʿAbbās according to the initial ruling, and he was unaware of the abrogation. Then when he heard the ḥadīth of ʿĀʾishah and Umm Salamah, he turned to it."""
8002,"(7999) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ in Naysābūr and Abū al-Ḥasan Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb ibn Aḥmad al-Faqīh in Ṭabarān narrated to us, saying: Abū al-Naḍr Muḥammad ibn Muḥammad ibn Yūsuf al-Faqīh informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī narrated to us: ʿAmr ibn ʿAwn al-Wāsiṭī narrated to us: Hushaym informed us, from Ḥuṣayn, from al-Shaʿbī, from ʿAdī ibn Ḥātim, who said: ""When the verse was revealed: {Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread of the dawn}, I took two cords, one white and one black, and placed them beneath my pillow. I would get up at night and look, but they would not become distinct to me. When I went to the Prophet (ﷺ) in the morning and informed him, he smiled and said: 'Indeed your pillow is wide! That is only the whiteness of the day from the blackness of the night.'"" Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Ḥajjāj ibn Minhāl, from Hushaym, and Muslim narrated it from another route from Ḥuṣayn. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
8003,"(8000) Abū ʿAlī al-Ḥusayn ibn Muḥammad al-Rūdhbārī informed us: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd al-Dārimī informed us: Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam narrated to us: Abū Ghassān narrated to us: Abū Ḥāzim narrated to me, from Sahl ibn Saʿd (raḍiyAllāhu ʿanhu), who said: ""This verse was revealed: {Eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread}, and {of the dawn} had not yet been revealed. He said: 'When men intended to fast, one of them would tie a black thread and a white thread to his leg. He would continue to eat and drink until their colours became distinct to him. Then Allah, the Most High, revealed after that: {of the dawn}, so they knew that He thereby meant the night and the day.'""
Ibn Abī Maryam said: ""And Ibn Abī Ḥāzim narrated to me, from his father, from Sahl ibn Saʿd, something similar to it.""
Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Saʿīd ibn Abī Maryam with both chains of narration together, and Muslim narrated it from Muḥammad ibn Sahl and Abū Bakr ibn Isḥāq, from Ibn Abī Maryam, with the first chain of narration. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
8004,"(8001) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Makhlad ibn Jaʿfar al-Daqqāq informed us: Yūsuf ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Abū al-Rabīʿ narrated to us: Ḥammād ibn Zayd narrated to us: ʿAbdullāh ibn Sawādah al-Qushayrī narrated to us, from his father, from Samurah ibn Jundub, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Let not the adhān of Bilāl deceive you regarding your suḥūr, nor the whiteness of the elongated horizon, like this, until it spreads like this.'"" Ḥammād gestured with his hand, meaning horizontally.
Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Abū al-Rabīʿ al-Zahrānī. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
8005,"(8002) Abū Zakariyyā ibn Abī Isḥāq al-Muzzakkī and Abū Bakr Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Baḥr ibn Naṣr narrated to us, saying: It was read to Ibn Wahb: ""Ibn Abī Dhiʾb informed you, from al-Ḥārith ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān, from Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Thawbān, that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'There are two dawns. As for the one that is like the tail of a wolf, it does not permit anything nor forbid anything. As for the one that spreads across the horizon, it permits the prayer and forbids the food.'"" This is a mursal narration, and it has been narrated as mawṣūl with the mention of Jābir ibn ʿAbdillāh in it. [Ṣaḥīḥ]"
8006,"(8003) Abū Saʿd Saʿīd ibn Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Shuʿaybī informed us: Abū al-Ḥusayn ʿAbdullāh ibn Ibrāhīh ibn Jaʿfar al-Bazzāz al-Zaynabī in Baghdād informed us: Aḥmad ibn ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Marzūq ibn Abī ʿAwf informed us: ʿAmr al-Nāqid informed us: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us.
(Ḥ) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us: Abū al-Naḍr al-Faqīh informed us: Abū Bakr Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq ibn Khuzaymah informed us: Muḥammad ibn ʿAlī ibn Muḥriz al-Baghdādī in Miṣr narrated to us with a gharīb report: Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from ʿAṭāʾ, from Ibn ʿAbbās, who said: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The dawn is two dawns. As for the first, it does not forbid the food nor permit the prayer. As for the second, it forbids the food and permits the prayer.'"" This is the wording of the ḥadīth of Ibn Muḥriz. And in the narration of ʿAmr al-Nāqid: ""The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The dawn is two dawns: a dawn in which food is permitted and prayer is forbidden, and a dawn in which prayer is permitted and food is forbidden.'"" Abū Aḥmad al-Zubayrī narrated it with a chain of narration (asnadah), and others narrated it from al-Thawrī as a mawqūf statement attributed to Ibn ʿAbbās. [Ṣaḥīḥ from others]"
8007,"(8004) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us — 'Ali bin Hamshadh al-'Adl narrated to us — Bishr bin Musa narrated to us — al-Humaydi narrated to us — Sufyan narrated to us — Hisham bin 'Urwah narrated to us — my father informed me, saying: I heard 'Asim bin 'Umar narrating from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""When the night approaches from here and the day departs from here, and the sun has set, then the fasting person has broken his fast.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from al-Humaydi, and Muslim recorded it through other chains from Hisham bin 'Urwah. [Sahih — reported by al-Bukhari]"
8008,"(8005) Abu Muhammad 'Abdillah bin Yusuf al-Isbahani informed us — Abu 'Abdillah Muhammad bin Ya'qub al-Shaybani narrated to us — Muhammad bin 'Amr al-Harashi narrated to us — Yahya bin Yahya narrated to us — Hushaym informed us — from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani — from 'Abdillah bin Abi Awfa, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah ﷺ during a journey in the month of Ramadan. When the sun set, he said: ""O so-and-so, come down and prepare a drink for us."" He said: ""O Messenger of Allah, daylight still remains upon you."" He said: ""Come down and prepare a drink for us."" So he came down and prepared a drink for him. The Prophet ﷺ drank it, then said with his hand: ""When the sun sets from here and the night comes from here, then the fasting person has broken his fast.""

Muslim narrated it in his Sahih from Yahya bin Yahya, and al-Bukhari recorded it through other chains from al-Shaybani. [Sahih — reported by al-Bukhari]"
8009,"(8006) Abu 'Abdillah al-Hafiz informed us — Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad bin Ya'qub narrated to us — Bahr bin Nasr bin Sabiq al-Khulani narrated to us — Bishr bin Bakr narrated to us — 'Abd al-Rahman bin Yazid bin Jabir narrated to us — from Sulaym bin 'Amir, Abu Yahya al-Kala'i, who said: Abu Umamah al-Bahili narrated to me, saying: I heard the Messenger of Allah ﷺ saying: ""While I was sleeping, two men came to me, took hold of my upper arms, and brought me to a rugged mountain. They said to me: 'Climb.' I said: 'I am not able to do so.' They said: 'We shall make it easy for you.' So I climbed until I reached the summit of the mountain, when I heard loud, severe voices. I said: 'What are these voices?' They said: 'This is the wailing of the people of the Fire.' Then I was taken further, and lo, I came upon people suspended by their heels, their mouths torn open, and their mouths flowing with blood. I said: 'Who are these?' He said: 'These are those who used to break their fast before the time of their fast had become permissible.'""

[Sahih — reported by al-Hakim]"
8010,"(8007) Abu Nasr bin Qatadah informed us — Abu Mansur al-'Abbas bin al-Falad al-Nadrawi informed us — Ahmad bin Najdah narrated to us — Sa'id bin Mansur narrated to us — Hushaym narrated to us — Khalid and Mansur narrated to us — from Ibn Sirin — from Yahya bin al-Jazzar, who said: Ibn Mas'ud was asked about a man who took the pre-dawn meal (suhur) while he believed that night still persisted, though the dawn had already risen. He said: ""Whoever ate at the beginning of the day, let him also eat at its end.""

[Sahih — its narrators are trustworthy (thiqat)]"
8011,"(8008) He said: And Sa'id narrated to us — Hushaym informed us — Mansur informed us — from Ibn Sirin, that he said the same as that.

[Sahih — its narrators are trustworthy (thiqat)]"
8012,"(8009) He said: And al-Hasan said: ""He should complete his fast, and there is nothing upon him.""

[Sahih — its chain has been previously mentioned]"
8013,"(8010) He said: And Sa'id narrated to us — 'Umar bin 'Abd al-Wahid, one of the people of Damascus, narrated to us — from al-Nu'man bin al-Mundhir al-Ghassani — from Makhul, who said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri was asked about a man who took the pre-dawn meal (suhur) while he believed that night still persisted, though the dawn had already risen. He said: ""If it was during the month of Ramadan, he should complete the fast and make up a day in its place. And if it was outside the month of Ramadan, let him eat at its end, for he has already eaten at its beginning.""

And it has been narrated to us from Sa'id bin Jubayr a statement similar to that of Ibn Sirin, and from Mujahid a statement similar to that of al-Hasan. And the view of the one who says he must make up the day is more correct, given what has passed of the evidence establishing the obligation of fasting from the time of the rising of the dawn, together with what we have narrated in this chapter from the narrations. And with Allah lies the granting of success. [Da'if]"
8014,"(8011) Abu 'Amr Muhammad bin 'Abdillah al-Adib informed us — Abu Bakr al-Isma'ili informed me — al-Hasan bin Sufyan informed us — Ibn Numayr narrated to us — Abu Usamah narrated to us — from Hisham bin 'Urwah — from Fatimah — from Asma', who said: We broke our fast during the time of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ on a cloudy day, and then the sun appeared to us. I said to Hisham: ""Were they then ordered to make it up?"" He said: ""There was no alternative but to do so.""

Al-Bukhari narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Bakr bin Abi Shaybah, from Abu Usamah. [Sahih — reported by al-Bukhari]"
8015,"(8012) Abu Bakr Ahmad bin al-Hasan al-Qadi informed us — among the last of them, they said: Abu al-'Abbas Muhammad bin Ya'qub narrated to us — al-Rabi' bin Sulayman informed us — al-Shafi'i informed us — Malik informed us — from Zayd bin Aslam — from his brother Khalid bin Aslam, that 'Umar bin al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, broke his fast in Ramadan on a cloudy day, believing that evening had come and the sun had set. Then a man came to him and said: ""O Commander of the Faithful, the sun has risen!"" 'Umar said: ""The matter is simple, and we have exercised our judgment (ijtihad).""

Al-Shafi'i said: ""That is, he should make up a day in its place."" And upon this meaning did Malik bin Anas also narrate it.

And it was narrated by Sufyan bin 'Uyaynah from Zayd bin Aslam, from his brother, from his father, from 'Umar. And it was narrated from two other directions from 'Umar, interpreted as pertaining to making up the day. [Sahih — reported by Malik]"
8016,"(8013) Abu Tahir al-Faqih informed us — Abu 'Uthman al-Basri narrated to us — Muhammad bin 'Abd al-Wahhab informed us — Ya'la bin 'Ubayd narrated to us — Sufyan narrated —

And Abu al-Husayn bin al-Faddal al-Qattan in Baghdad informed us — 'Abdillah bin Ja'far bin Durustawayh narrated to us — Ya'qub bin Sufyan narrated to us — Abu Nu'aym narrated to us — Sufyan narrated — from Jabalat bin Suhaym — from 'Ali bin Hanzhalah — from his father, who said: We were in the presence of 'Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, when a bowl of food was brought to him in the month of Ramadan. The mu'adhdhin said: ""The sun has risen!"" He said: ""May Allah protect us from your evil! We did not send you as a shepherd for the sun. Rather, we sent you only as a caller to the Prayer. O people! Whoever among you has already broken his fast, then making up a day is an easy matter. And whoever has not, let him complete his fast.""

This is the wording of the hadith of Abu Nu'aym. And in the narration of Ya'la: ""We came with food, and he broke his fast, and said: 'How easy it is to make up a day! And whoever does not, let him complete his fast.'"" [Sahih — reported by 'Abd al-Razzaq]"
8017,"(8014) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, Ādam informed us, Shuʿbah narrated from Jabalah ibn Suḥaym, who said: I heard ʿAlī ibn Ḥanẓalah narrating from his father, and his father was a companion of ʿUmar. He said: I was with ʿUmar during Ramaḍān, and he broke his fast and the people broke their fast. Then the mu'adhdhin ascended to give the call to prayer and said: ""O people, this sun has not yet set."" ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""May Allah protect us from your evil! We did not appoint you as a shepherd."" Then ʿUmar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: ""Whoever among you has broken his fast, let him make up a day in its place.""

Abū Isḥāq al-Shaybānī also narrated it with the same meaning from ʿAlī ibn Ḥanẓalah, and it was narrated through another chain from ʿUmar.

[Ṣaḥīḥ. See its predecessor.]"
8018,"(8015) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Rajāʾ informed us, Isrāʾīl narrated from Ziyād, meaning Ibn ʿAllāqah, from Bishr ibn Qays, from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: I was with him one evening during Ramaḍān, and it was a cloudy day. He thought that the sun had set, so ʿUmar drank and gave me to drink. Then they looked at it on the slope of the mountain, and ʿUmar said: ""By Allah, we do not care; we shall make up a day in its place.""

Al-Walīd ibn Abī Thawr also narrated it from Ziyād with the same meaning.

The convergence of these narrations from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) regarding making up the fast is evidence of the error of the narration of Zayd ibn Wahb concerning the abandonment of making up the fast, which is in what follows. [Ṣaḥīḥ in other than this chain.]"
8019,"(8016) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn al-Faḍl al-Qaṭṭān informed us, ʿAbdullāh ibn Jaʿfar informed us, Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān informed us, ʿUbaydullāh ibn Mūsā narrated from Shaybān from al-Aʿmash from al-Musayyib ibn Rāfiʿ from Zayd ibn Wahb, who said: While we were sitting in the mosque of Madīnah during Ramaḍān and the sky was overcast, we thought that the sun had set and that we had reached the evening. So vessels of milk were brought out to us from the house of Ḥafṣah. ʿUmar drank and we drank. We had not long remained when the clouds parted and the sun appeared. Some of us said to others: ""Let us make up this day."" ʿUmar heard that and said: ""By Allah, we shall not make it up, and we have not inclined toward any sin.""

Thus did Shaybān narrate it.

Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth and Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated it from al-Aʿmash from Zayd ibn Wahb. Yaʿqūb ibn Sufyān al-Fārisī used to criticize Zayd ibn Wahb on account of this narration, which contradicts the earlier narrations, and he counted it among those matters in which he was opposed. Zayd is trustworthy, but error is not impossible. Allah protects us from stumbling and sins by His grace and the vastness of His mercy. [Munkar. Al-Fasawī narrated it.]"
8020,"(8017) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us, Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Isḥāq al-Ṣaghānī narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated to us, Yūsuf ibn Muḥammad ibn Yazīd ibn Ṣayfī ibn Ṣuhayb, the companion of the Prophet (peace be upon him), narrated to us, Shuʿayb ibn ʿAmr ibn Sulaym al-Anṣārī narrated to us — and he had reached one hundred and fifteen years of age — who said: We broke our fast with Ṣuhayb al-Khayr — I and my father — during the month of Ramaḍān on a cloudy and rainy day. While we were having our evening meal, the sun appeared. Ṣuhayb said: ""This is sustenance from Allah. Complete your fast until the night, and make up a day in its place."" [Ḍaʿīf. Al-Bukhārī narrated it.]"
8021,"(8018) By way of evidence, Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn Ḥamdān al-Jallāb in Hamadhān informed us, Abū Ḥātim and Ibrāhīm ibn Naṣr al-Rāziyyān both said: Abū al-Walīd al-Ṭayālisī narrated to us, al-Layth ibn Saʿd narrated from Bukayr ibn ʿAbdullāh ibn al-Ashajj from ʿAbd al-Malik ibn Saʿīd ibn Suwayd al-Anṣārī from Jābir ibn ʿAbdullāh from ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb (may Allah be pleased with him) that he said: ""I felt desire one day and kissed while I was fasting. I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) and said: 'I have done a great thing today — I kissed while I was fasting.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'What do you think if you had rinsed your mouth with water while you were fasting?' He said: 'I said: 'There is no harm in that.' The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: 'Then what is the matter?'""

Al-Shāfiʿī said: ""If he swallows it after dawn, he must make up a day in its place."" The Sheikh said: [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Ḥākim narrated it.]"
8022,"(8019) Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbaydullāh al-Narsī narrated to us, Rūḥ ibn ʿUbādah narrated to us.

(Ḥ) And Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān informed us, Abū Jaʿfar al-Razzāz narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn Aḥmad al-Riyāḥī narrated to us, Rūḥ narrated to us, Ḥammād narrated from Muḥammad ibn ʿAmr from Abū Salamah from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace be upon him) that he said: ""When any one of you hears the call and the vessel is in his hand, let him not set it down until he has fulfilled his need from it."" [Ḥasan. Abū Dāwūd narrated it.]"
8023,"(8020) He said: And Ḥammād ibn Salamah narrated from ʿAmmār ibn Abī ʿAmmār from Abū Hurayrah from the Prophet (peace be upon him) a similar narration. Al-Riyāḥī said in his narration and added therein: ""And the mu'adhdhins used to give the call when the dawn appeared."" Others also narrated this from Ḥammād.

This, if it is authentic, is understood by the generality of the scholars of knowledge to mean that the Prophet (peace be upon him) knew that the caller used to call before the rising of the dawn, so that his drinking would occur just before the rising of the dawn. The statement of the narrator, ""And the mu'adhdhins used to give the call when the dawn broke,"" may be a disconnected report from someone below Abū Hurayrah, or it may be a report concerning the second call to prayer. The statement of the Prophet (peace be upon him), ""When any one of you hears the call and the vessel is in his hand,"" refers to the first call, so that it may be in agreement with what we have mentioned. [Ḥasan. See its predecessor.]"
8024,"(8021) Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Jarīr and al-Muʿtamir ibn Sulaymān narrated from Sulaymān al-Taymī from Abū ʿUthmān al-Nahdī from ʿAbdullāh ibn Masʿūd from the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him), who said: ""Let not the call of Bilāl prevent any one of you from his pre-dawn meal, for he calls only to wake those of you who are sleeping and to return those of you who are standing in prayer.""

Jarīr said in his narration: ""Not like this,"" — and he indicated lengthwise — ""but like this,"" — and he indicated breadthwise — ""for the dawn is that which spreads horizontally, not that which stretches vertically.""

Muslim narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from Isḥāq ibn Ibrāhīm, and al-Bukhārī narrated it through various routes from al-Taymī. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Al-Bukhārī narrated it.]"
8025,"(8022) And Abū ʿAbdillāh al-Ḥāfiẓ informed us, Abū al-Faḍl Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm informed us, Aḥmad ibn Salamah informed us, Isḥāq ibn Manṣūr informed us, Abū Usāmah narrated from ʿUbaydullāh ibn ʿUmar from Nāfiʿ from Ibn ʿUmar, and from al-Qāsim from ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with them both), who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said: ""Indeed, Bilāl gives the call at night, so eat and drink until you hear the call of Ibn Umm Maktūm.""

Al-Bukhārī narrated it in al-Ṣaḥīḥ from ʿUbayd ibn Ismāʿīl, and Muslim narrated it from Abū Bakr ibn Abī Shaybah, both from Abū Usāmah. [Ṣaḥīḥ. Agreed upon.]"
8026,"(8023) And ʿAlī ibn Aḥmad ibn ʿAbdān informed us, Aḥmad ibn ʿUbayd al-Ṣaffār narrated to us, Muḥammad ibn al-Faḍl ibn Jābir narrated to us, Saʿīd ibn Sulaymān narrated from Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth from Ashʿath from Abū Hubayrah from his grandfather Shaybān, who said: I entered the mosque and gave the call to prayer, and then withdrew to a corner. The Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) said to me: ""Abā Yaḥyā!"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""Come near — have some breakfast."" I said: ""I intend to fast."" He said: ""And I intend to fast, but our mu'adhdhin has something wrong with his eye, or something — he gave the call before the dawn rose."" Thus did Ḥafṣ narrate it.

Sharīk narrated it from Ashʿath from Yaḥyī ibn ʿAbbād al-Anṣārī, who is Abū Hubayrah, from his father from his grandfather.

This narration is unique to Ashʿath ibn Sawwār. If it is authentic, it is as though the call of Ibn Umm Maktūm occurred before the dawn, and the Messenger of Allah (peace be upon him) did not refrain from eating. Upon this which we have mentioned, the reports converge and do not differ. And with Allah lies the success. [Ḥasan in other than this chain. Al-Ṭabarānī narrated it.]"
8027,"(8024) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ and Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās — al-Aṣamm — narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to me: al-Layth ibn Saʿd informed me that Nāfiʿ narrated to him that ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar used to say: ""If the call to prayer is given while a man is with his wife, that does not prevent him from fasting. When he intends to fast, he should rise, perform ghusl, and then complete his fast.""
[Sound. Its narrators are trustworthy.]"
8028,"(8025) Abū Bakr — Aḥmad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Qāḍī — and others narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbāh — Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb — narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: al-Shāfiʿī narrated to us, saying: ""Whoever vomits deliberately while fasting, qaḍāʾ (making up the fast) is obligatory upon him, and whoever is overcome by vomiting, there is no qaḍāʾ upon him."" And with this chain Mālik informed us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar.
[Sound up to al-Shāfiʿī.]"
8029,"(8026) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Subʿī, Abū Saʿīd ibn Abī ʿAmr, and Abū Naṣr Manṣūr ibn al-Ḥusayn al-Mufassir narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿUmar and Mālik ibn Anas narrated to us, from Nāfiʿ, from Ibn ʿUmar, that he said: ""Whoever is overcome by vomiting, there is no qaḍāʾ upon him, and whoever induces vomiting must make it up.""
[Sound.]"
8030,"(8027) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ, Abū al-Ḥasan al-Subʿī, and Abū Naṣr Manṣūr ibn al-Ḥusayn al-ʿAnazī narrated to us, saying: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: al-Rabīʿ ibn Sulaymān narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Wahb narrated to me: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated from —

(Ḥ) And Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us: Abū Bakr al-Qaṭṭān narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Yazīd al-Sulamī narrated to us: Shaddād ibn Ḥakīm narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus — and Abū al-Khayr Jāmiʿ ibn Aḥmad al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us: Abū Ṭāhir Muḥammad ibn al-Ḥasan al-Muḥammadābādhī narrated to us: ʿUthmān ibn Saʿīd narrated to us: Musaddad narrated to us: ʿĪsā ibn Yūnus narrated to us: Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated to us, from Muḥammad ibn Sīrīn, from Abū Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""Whoever is overcome by vomiting while fasting, there is no qaḍāʾ upon him, but whoever induces vomiting must make it up.""
[Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
8031,"(8028) Abū ʿAbd Allāh al-Ḥāfiẓ narrated to us: Abū al-ʿAbbās Muḥammad ibn Yaʿqūb narrated to us: Ibrāhīm ibn Abī Dāwūd al-Burlusī narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd Yaḥyā ibn Sulaymān al-Juʿfī narrated to us: Ḥafṣ ibn Ghiyāth narrated to us: Hishām ibn Ḥassān narrated it with the same meaning.

Hishām ibn Ḥassān al-Qurdūsī is the sole narrator of this. Abū Dāwūd recorded it in the Sunan, but some of the ḥuffāẓ do not consider it preserved (maḥfūẓ). Abū Dāwūd said: I heard Aḥmad ibn Ḥanbal say: ""There is nothing to this."" I say: It has also been narrated through another weak chain from Abū Hurayrah as a marfūʿ hadith, and it has been narrated from Abū Hurayrah that he said regarding vomiting: ""It does not break the fast.""

And something to that effect has been narrated from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, as his own statement.
[Sound. See what preceded it.]"
8032,"(8029) Abū Muḥammad ʿAbd Allāh ibn Yūsuf al-Aṣbahānī narrated to us: Abū Saʿīd ibn al-Aʿrābī narrated to us: Saʿdān ibn Naṣr narrated to us: Abū Muʿāwiyah narrated to us, from Ḥajjāj, from Abū Isḥāq, from al-Ḥārith, from ʿAlī, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: ""If a man eats forgetfully while he is fasting, it is merely provision that Allah has granted him. If he vomits deliberately while fasting, then qaḍāʾ is obligatory upon him. And if he is overcome by vomiting, there is no qaḍāʾ upon him.""
[Weak.]"
8033,"(8030) As for the hadith that ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad ibn ʿAbd Allāh ibn Bishrān narrated to us in Baghdad: Abū al-Ḥasan ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm ibn Jannād narrated to us: Abū Maʿmar ʿAbd Allāh ibn ʿAmr ibn Abī al-Ḥajjāj narrated to us: ʿAbd al-Wārith narrated to us: Ḥusayn al-Muʿallim narrated to us, from Yaḥyā ibn Abī Kathīr, who said: ʿAbd al-Raḥmān ibn ʿAmr al-Awzāʿī narrated to me, from Yaʿīsh ibn al-Walīd ibn Hishām, who narrated to him that his father narrated to him, saying: Maʿdān ibn Ṭalḥah narrated to me that Abū al-Dardāʾ informed him that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ vomited and broke his fast. He (Maʿdān) said: I met Thawbān, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ, in the mosque of Damascus and said to him: ""Abū al-Dardāʾ informed me that the Messenger of Allah ﷺ vomited and broke his fast."" He said: ""He spoke the truth, and I poured water for his wuḍūʾ.""

This hadith differs in its chain of narration. If it is sound, it is interpreted to mean that he vomited deliberately, and that he ﷺ was fasting a voluntary fast. It has also been narrated through another chain from Thawbān.
[Sound. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
8034,"(8031) Abū Ṭāhir al-Faqīh narrated to us: Abū al-Ḥasan al-Ṭarāʾifī narrated to us: ʿUthmān al-Dārimī narrated to us: Muslim ibn Ibrāhīm narrated to us: Shuʿbah narrated to us, from Abī al-Jūdī, from Balj, from Abī Shaybah al-Mahrī, who said: We said to Thawbān: ""Narrate to us from the Messenger of Allah ﷺ."" He said: ""I saw the Messenger of Allah ﷺ vomit and break his fast.""
[Sound for other than it. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
8035,"(8032) Abū al-Ḥusayn ibn Bishrān narrated to us: ʿAlī ibn Muḥammad al-Miṣrī narrated to us: Yaḥyā ibn ʿUthmān ibn Ṣāliḥ narrated to us: My father narrated to us: ʿAbd Allāh ibn Lahīʿah and al-Mufaḍḍal ibn Faḍālah narrated to us, saying: Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb narrated to us, from Abī Marzūq, from Ḥanash ibn ʿAbd Allāh, from Faḍālah ibn ʿUbayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ woke up fasting, then vomited and broke his fast. He was asked about that, and he said: ""I vomited deliberately."" Similarly, Yaḥyā ibn Ayyūb narrated it from Yazīd ibn Abī Ḥabīb, and it too is interpreted as referring to deliberate vomiting.
[Sound. Narrated by Aḥmad.]"
8036,"(8033) As for the hadith that Abū ʿAlī al-Rūdhbārī narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Bakr narrated to us: Abū Dāwūd narrated to us: Muḥammad ibn Kathīr narrated to us: Sufyān narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from a man from his companions, from a man from the Companions of the Prophet ﷺ, who said: The Messenger of Allah ﷺ said: ""The one who vomits does not break his fast, nor does the one who experiences a wet dream, nor does the one who is cupped.""

This hadith, if confirmed, is interpreted to mean the case of one who is overcome by vomiting.
[Weak. Narrated by Abū Dāwūd.]"
